"""
    ROUTER

The process-global `HTTP.Router` every `@htmx` route registers on. [`serve`](@ref)
listens with it; [`dispatch`](@ref) and [`record!`](@ref) call it in-process.
"""
const ROUTER = HTTP.Router()

# The GET routes on `ROUTER` minus the resume-only GETs of mutation paths
# (`_register_get_route!`), so a plain GET that reaches a resume-only GET is
# answered by whatever `ROUTER` would have matched without it.
struct _NoGetRoute end
const _GET_ROUTER = HTTP.Router(_ -> _NoGetRoute(), _ -> _NoGetRoute())

"""
    Verb{V}

Singleton type used as the **first positional argument** of every route IP
emitted by `@get`/`@post`/`@put`/`@patch`/`@delete`/`@ws`/`@sse`. Routes are pure
sugar over DynamicObjects' indexable properties:

    @get foo(x::Int) = body            # source

    compute_property(::T, ::Val{:foo}, ::Verb{:GET}, x::Int) = body   # emitted

The verb being part of the dispatch signature lets a single property name
host an arbitrary number of HTTP-method handlers (`@get foo() = …` and
`@post foo() = …` coexist), and lets verb-keyed route IPs share a name with
non-verb-keyed (data) IPs without collision. The route registrar threads
`Verb{V}()` into the IP call at request-handling time.

Bare-form route LHS (`@get foo = body`) is rejected at macro-expansion
time — every route must be a call form (`@get foo() = body`) so DO can
register it as an indexable property.
"""
struct Verb{V} end

# Forward declaration: `@htmx struct` (defined further down in this file at
# `RecordingRoutes`, `AppContext`, `TestRoutes`, `EditorRoutes`) emits a
# fully-qualified method definition `HTMXObjects.query_url(self::T; …) = …`
# during its expansion. Julia 1.10 errors with `UndefVarError: query_url not
# defined` when adding a method to `M.f` if `f` doesn't yet exist in `M` —
# even though the same form `f(args) = body` would create the binding when
# unqualified. The full `query_url` implementation is defined later in the
# file alongside the `@query_url` macro; this `function …end` just creates
# the binding so the in-file `@htmx struct` blocks can attach methods.
function query_url end

"""
    option_wire_value(value)

Return the stable machine value used to carry an option through URL segments,
query parameters, and form controls. Values exposing a `key` property use that
property by default; every other value keeps the established `string(value)`
spelling. Extend this function for an option type whose stable identity lives
somewhere other than `key`.

The returned value is stringified and URL/HTML escaped by the caller. Its
spelling must be unique within each live option domain so a submission resolves
to exactly one existing option object.
"""
option_wire_value(value) =
    hasproperty(value, :key) ? getproperty(value, :key) : string(value)

_option_wire_string(value) = string(option_wire_value(value))

"""
    _url_segment(value) -> String

`value`'s wire identity ([`option_wire_value`](@ref)) as ONE URL path segment.
Every path segment the framework builds from a value goes through here: indexed
`@include` mount prefixes (inline and external), `@query_url` positional
arguments, and `Resource` list links. The request side decodes each segment
exactly once (`_percent_decode_lenient`), so the value round-trips to the same
child.

Only the bytes that would otherwise end, split or reinterpret the segment are
percent-encoded: `/` (segment separator), `?` and `#` (start of query and
fragment), `%` (the escape introducer, so a literal `a%20b` is not decoded to
`a b`), `\\` (browsers read it as `/` in http(s) paths), and ASCII controls and
space (rejected by URI parsing). Every other byte — unreserved and
sub-delimiter characters, `:`, `@`, non-ASCII UTF-8 — stays literal, so a value
free of the escaped bytes keeps the byte-identical URL, `__prefix__`, semantic
result-target id and static recording file name it always had.
"""
function _url_segment(value)
    s = _option_wire_string(value)
    any(_url_segment_escapes, codeunits(s)) || return s
    io = IOBuffer()
    for b in codeunits(s)
        _url_segment_escapes(b) ?
            print(io, '%', uppercase(string(b; base=16, pad=2))) :
            write(io, b)
    end
    String(take!(io))
end

_url_segment_escapes(b::UInt8) =
    b <= 0x20 || b == 0x7f || b in (UInt8('#'), UInt8('%'), UInt8('/'), UInt8('?'), UInt8('\\'))

"""
    serve(; host="127.0.0.1", port=8080, async=false, parallel=false, revise=nothing,
          middleware=[], check_origin=nothing, access_log=<timed default>,
          operation_log=<default>, runtime_tracking=true, compress=true, kwargs...)

Start an HTTP server for the routes registered on [`ROUTER`](@ref). When
`async=false` (the default), blocks until interrupted and calls
[`terminate`](@ref) on exit; `async=true` returns the running `HTTP.Server`.

- `middleware` — request middleware (`handler -> (req -> response)`), applied
  outermost first.
- `check_origin` — optional WebSocket handshake policy, called as
  `(request, origin) -> Bool` (or `(request) -> Bool`) on the original upgrade
  request. `origin` is `nothing` when the header is absent. With `nothing`,
  HTTP.jl's default policy applies. A reverse proxy that terminates HTTPS can
  supply a callback that accepts only its exact public origin; ordinary request
  middleware cannot change the request used by the WebSocket upgrade.
- `access_log` — an `(io, req) -> nothing` formatter written once per request
  (`req.context[:response]` holds the response), or `nothing` to disable the
  log. The default line is `time - ip:port - "GET /path HTTP/1.1" 200 12.3ms`.
- `operation_log` — an `(io, record)` formatter written once for every polled
  operation (one that outlived the `:auto` grace period) when it ends, or
  `nothing` to disable it. `record` is an [`HTMXObjects.OperationRecord`](@ref).
  The default line is
  `time - operation GET /path?x=1 ok compute=12.4s answered=477.0s polls=23`.
  An access-log line covers one request, and a slow page is many of them; this
  line states the whole operation: how it ended (`ok`, `failed`, `stalled`,
  `abandoned`, `evicted`, `unclaimed`), how long its computation ran, how long the client
  took to receive the answer, and how many polls reached it. Records are
  logged at `Info` with `_group=:operation`. The setting is process-wide
  ([`HTMXObjects.OPERATION_LOG`](@ref)).
- `runtime_tracking=true` (the default) installs [`track_requests`](@ref)
  outside `middleware`, recording in-flight and finished requests with their
  handling times in [`runtime_tracker`](@ref) for the [`RuntimeRoutes`](@ref)
  dev dashboard. Pass `false` to skip it. Long-running `@queued` operations are
  recorded as jobs either way.
- `compress=true` (the default) gzips text responses for clients that accept
  gzip (see below). Pass `false` to send every body as the route produced it.
- `prestart=true` (the default) starts a page's load-triggered fragments with
  the page (see below). Pass `false` to leave each one to the browser's request.
- `operation_stream=true` (the default) gives each page one event stream that
  announces the end of its pollers' operations (see below). Pass `false` to
  leave every poller to its own polls.
- `revise` — `:lazy` applies pending Revise revisions before each request;
  `:eager` also applies them in the background as soon as a file changes. Both
  need `using Revise` before the app is loaded.
- Remaining keyword arguments are passed to `HTTP.listen!`.

A response whose body is held in memory (bytes or a string) is sent with a
`Content-Length` computed after every middleware ran, unless it already carries
`Content-Length` or `Transfer-Encoding`, answers a HEAD request, or has a status
without content (1xx, 204, 304). The client then has the complete response as
soon as the body is written. On HTTP.jl 1.x such a response would otherwise go
out chunked, and its last chunk is written only when the connection task
resumes. A streamed body (an `IO`) keeps HTTP.jl's own framing.

With `compress=true`, such a body of at least 1 KiB whose `Content-Type` is
text (`text/*`, JSON, JavaScript, XML or SVG) is gzipped when the request's
`Accept-Encoding` admits gzip. The response then carries `Content-Encoding:
gzip`, the compressed `Content-Length`, and a weak `ETag` in place of a strong
one. Every such response carries `Vary: Accept-Encoding`, compressed or not,
so a cache keeps the two forms apart. A client that sends no
`Accept-Encoding` (plain `curl`, for example) gets the body unchanged. Left
alone: HEAD answers, `206` and `Content-Range` answers, responses that
already carry `Content-Encoding` or `Cache-Control: no-transform`, a streamed
`IO` body, and WebSocket upgrades. An event stream (an `@sse` route, and the
page's operation stream with the answers it pushes) is gzipped as one stream
for a client that admits gzip, each write flushed through the compressor so
an event arrives as soon as it is sent; it carries `Vary: Accept-Encoding`
too. [`dispatch`](@ref) and static recording do not go through `serve` and
always see the uncompressed body.

With `prestart=true`, a response a browser receives — a page navigation
(`Accept: text/html`) or an htmx request — starts the operations of its
load-triggered fragments while it is being rendered: every element with an
`hx-get` to this server whose `hx-trigger` is only `load` (as
[`lazy`](@ref) renders), unless it or an enclosing element carries `hx-vals`,
`hx-vars`, `hx-include`, `hx-headers` or `hx-request`, which only the browser
can evaluate. Each such request runs through `middleware` with the page
request's headers (cookies and authorization included), and the fragment's
`hx-get` gains the operation's token, so the browser's request joins the
running operation instead of starting it. Only a read that the route's
[`OperationPolicy`](@ref) answers by polling is started; any other route, a
`revealed` or `intersect` trigger, and a request that differs from the
prestart are left to the browser. At most 24 fragments of one response and 96
not yet requested across the server are started; an operation no browser asks
for within ten minutes is dropped and logged as `unclaimed`. `middleware`
receives each prestart request as well, through its own composition without
the access log, the runtime tracker or Revise.

With `operation_stream=true`, a page whose `:auto` pollers are running holds
one event stream for them, so a poller's result arrives when its computation
ends instead of at its next poll. Each running poller `serve` renders is
marked `data-htmxo-operation-stream`; the shell's runtime opens the stream as a
GET of the page's own address with `__htmxo_operations=<token>,…` (up to 32
operations, the rest poll as before) and `Accept: text/event-stream`. The
server answers it after `middleware` and before routing, so an auth gateway
sees it like any request and no route is needed. It sends `ready` with an
operation's token the moment that computation finishes or fails, and `gone`
for an operation it no longer holds; once every operation has been sent it
closes. On `ready` the poller sends its poll at once, so the answer takes the
path a poll's does (its swap, its 286, its recorded error). While the stream
is open a poller polls its progress tree at most once a second. A stream that
drops or is refused leaves its pollers polling at their own pace, and the
runtime reopens it with backoff.

When the page's Treebars script can apply pushed answers (Treebars ≥ `2b6a0f7`,
`treebarUpdatePoller`), the stream carries the answers themselves: once a
second the server renders the running poller's answer by polling the operation
through `middleware` and the router with the stream request's headers (its
`Referer` as the page's address), and sends it as `progress`; when the
computation ends it renders the final answer the same way and sends it as
`result`. The page's poller then sends no polls at all: it waits for the
stream's first answer, and polls once a second only if none has arrived three
seconds after the stream opened. `middleware` sees each
of these renders as a GET of the poll address with `req.context[:htmxo_push]`
set. A final answer that
needs htmx's own response handling (a status other than 286, an `HX-*`
header, a recorded error's id, a toast) is sent as `ready` instead, and the
poll the page then sends is answered with the answer the stream rendered, so
nothing is computed twice.

`parallel` chooses where each request is handled:
- `false` (default) — on HTTP.jl's own connection task. HTTP.jl 2 spawns that
  task on the `:interactive` threadpool (Julia uses `:default` when there are no
  interactive threads), so requests on different connections already run
  concurrently across a sized interactive pool. HTTP.jl 1.x handles every request
  on one thread; `serve` warns when that leaves several interactive threads unused.
- `true` — spawned on the `:default` threadpool.
- `:interactive` — spawned on the `:interactive` threadpool, leaving `:default`
  free for heavy computation. With HTTP.jl 2 this is the default's placement plus
  one extra spawn per request; with HTTP.jl 1.x it is what puts requests on the
  interactive pool. Launch julia with e.g. `julia -t 8,4` for 8 computation
  threads and 4 request-handling threads.

With exactly one interactive thread, `false` (on HTTP.jl 2) and `:interactive`
both handle requests one at a time; with none, `:interactive` requests run on
`:default`. `serve` warns about both `:interactive` cases.

A route's background operation (see [`OperationPolicy`](@ref)) starts on the pool
that handles its request, so with requests on `:interactive` pages keep answering
while application compute saturates `:default`. A `@queued` route declares heavy
computation and runs it on `:default` instead, through [`configure_job_queue!`](@ref).

**Footgun:** `julia -tauto,auto` and `JULIA_NUM_THREADS=auto,auto` both resolve the
second slot to `1`, not "match default" — `:interactive` stays at 1 thread even
with `auto`. To size both pools equally, compute shell-side:
`julia -t\$(nproc),\$(nproc)`.
"""
function serve(; host="127.0.0.1", port=8080, async=false, parallel=false,
        revise=nothing, middleware=[], check_origin::Union{Nothing,Function}=nothing,
        access_log=_timed_access_log, operation_log=_default_operation_log,
        runtime_tracking::Bool=true, compress::Bool=true, prestart::Bool=true,
        operation_stream::Bool=true, kwargs...)
    kwargs = _drop_oxygen_kwargs(kwargs)
    revise in (nothing, :none, :lazy, :eager) ||
        throw(ArgumentError("`revise` must be nothing, :lazy or :eager, got $(repr(revise))"))
    Revise = revise in (:lazy, :eager) ? _revise_module() : nothing
    revise in (:lazy, :eager) && Revise === nothing &&
        error("`serve(; revise=$(repr(revise)))` needs Revise: run `using Revise` before loading the app.")

    OPERATION_LOG[] = operation_log
    tracker = runtime_tracking ? runtime_tracker() : nothing
    _PRESTART_APP[] = prestart ? _prestart_pipeline(middleware) : nothing
    _OPERATION_STREAM[] = operation_stream
    _OPERATION_PUSH_APP[] = operation_stream ? _inner_pipeline(middleware, ROUTER) : nothing
    handle = _stream_handler(
        _served_middleware(_request_pipeline(middleware, access_log, Revise,
                                             tracker; operation_stream)),
        check_origin, compress)
    pool, warning = _request_threadpool(parallel, Threads.nthreads(:interactive),
        Threads.nthreads(:default), pkgversion(HTTP))
    warning === nothing || @warn warning
    pool === nothing || (handle = _spawning_stream_handler(handle, pool))

    server = HTTP.listen!(handle, host, port; kwargs...)
    _SERVER[] = server
    revise === :eager && (_EAGER_REVISE_STOP[] = _start_eager_revise(Revise))
    @info "Serving on http://$host:$port"
    async && return server
    try
        wait(server)
    catch err
        err isa InterruptException ||
            @error "Server stopped" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
    finally
        terminate()
    end
    return server
end

# The server started by `serve`, and the stop flag of its `revise=:eager` task.
const _SERVER = Ref{Any}(nothing)
const _EAGER_REVISE_STOP = Ref{Any}(nothing)

"""Stop the HTTP server started by [`serve`](@ref)."""
function terminate()
    stop = _EAGER_REVISE_STOP[]
    stop === nothing || (stop[] = true)
    _EAGER_REVISE_STOP[] = nothing
    server = _SERVER[]
    _SERVER[] = nothing
    _PRESTART_APP[] = nothing
    _OPERATION_STREAM[] = false
    _OPERATION_PUSH_APP[] = nothing
    server !== nothing && isopen(server) && close(server)
    return nothing
end

# Marks a request as one `serve` received, which is the only kind whose
# response prestarts its fragments (`_prestart_wanted`).
_served_middleware(handler) = function (req::HTTP.Request)
    req.context[:htmxo_served] = true
    handler(req)
end

# `serve` keywords that only configured Oxygen, which HTMXObjects no longer
# uses. Warn and drop them rather than fail an existing launch script.
const _OXYGEN_SERVE_KWARGS = (:docs, :metrics, :show_banner, :serialize,
    :catch_errors, :show_errors, :docs_path, :schema_path, :external_url,
    :prefix, :context, :handler)

function _drop_oxygen_kwargs(kwargs)
    dropped = [k for k in keys(kwargs) if k in _OXYGEN_SERVE_KWARGS]
    isempty(dropped) && return kwargs
    @warn "Ignoring `serve` keyword(s) $(join(dropped, ", ")): they configured Oxygen, which HTMXObjects no longer uses."
    return (; (k => v for (k, v) in pairs(kwargs) if !(k in _OXYGEN_SERVE_KWARGS))...)
end

# The request pipeline, outermost first: access log → runtime `tracker` →
# Revise → caller `middleware` → fallback → `ROUTER`.
function _request_pipeline(middleware, access_log, Revise, tracker=nothing;
        operation_stream::Bool=false)
    app = _fallback_middleware(
        operation_stream ? _operation_stream_router(ROUTER) : ROUTER)
    for m in reverse(middleware)
        app = m(app)
    end
    Revise === nothing || (app = _revise_middleware(app, Revise))
    tracker === nothing || (app = track_requests(app; tracker))
    access_log === nothing || (app = _access_log_middleware(app, access_log))
    return app
end

# Thrown by a `@ws` route once its upgraded session ends. The connection now
# belongs to the finished WebSocket, so no HTTP response may be written: the
# exception unwinds the request pipeline and `_stream_handler` swallows it.
struct _WebSocketClosed <: Exception end

# Innermost middleware. `@htmx` routes always return an `HTTP.Response`; this
# coerces anything else a hand-registered handler returns, and turns an
# escaped exception into a logged 500 instead of a dropped connection.
function _fallback_middleware(handler)
    function (req::HTTP.Request)
        try
            response = handler(req)
            return response isa HTTP.Response ? response : to_response(response)
        catch err
            err isa _WebSocketClosed && rethrow()
            @error "Unhandled error serving $(req.method) $(req.target)" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
            body = "500: Internal Server Error"
            return HTTP.Response(500, ["Content-Type" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
                                       "Content-Length" => string(sizeof(body))], body)
        end
    end
end

# `revise=:lazy`/`:eager`: apply pending revisions before handling a request,
# then call into the newest world so the revised methods run.
function _revise_middleware(handler, Revise)
    function (req::HTTP.Request)
        isempty(Revise.revision_queue) || Revise.revise()
        return Base.invokelatest(handler, req)
    end
end

# `revise=:eager`: revise as soon as Revise sees a file change instead of on the
# next request. Returns the flag `terminate` sets to stop the task.
function _start_eager_revise(Revise)
    stop = Ref(false)
    errormonitor(@async while true
        wait(Revise.revision_event)
        reset(Revise.revision_event)
        stop[] && break
        Revise.revise()
    end)
    return stop
end

# Stream-level entry point: exposes the raw stream to the request pipeline (a
# WebSocket upgrade needs it) and hands off to HTTP.jl's request adapter.
# `compress`: `serve(; compress)`.
function _stream_handler(app, check_origin=nothing, compress::Bool=true)
    function (stream::HTTP.Stream)
        handle = HTTP.streamhandler(function (req::HTTP.Request)
            req.context[:stream] = stream
            req.context[:ws_check_origin] = check_origin
            # An event stream writes its own body; `_serve_sse` compresses it.
            req.context[:htmxo_compress] = compress
            response = app(req)
            compress && (response = _compress_buffered(req, response))
            return _frame_buffered!(req, response)
        end)
        try
            handle(stream)
        catch err
            err isa _WebSocketClosed || rethrow()
        end
        return nothing
    end
end

# A buffered response leaves `serve` with an explicit `Content-Length`. Without
# one, HTTP.jl 1.x frames it `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`: the handler task
# writes the head and the body, and the terminating chunk follows only when the
# connection task resumes after that task returns. A scheduler stall in between
# leaves the client holding the whole body of a response that never ends. This
# runs after every middleware, on the response HTTP.jl is about to write (and
# would otherwise mark chunked itself), so the length is that of the body that
# goes out. HTTP.jl 2 already frames these bodies with the same length. A
# response that is already framed, a streamed body of unknown length, a HEAD
# answer, and a status that carries no content (1xx, 204, 304) keep HTTP.jl's
# own framing.
function _frame_buffered!(req::HTTP.Request, response::HTTP.Response)
    len = _buffered_length(response.body)
    (len === nothing || req.method == "HEAD" || !_carries_content(response.status) ||
     HTTP.hasheader(response, "Content-Length") ||
     HTTP.hasheader(response, "Transfer-Encoding")) && return response
    HTTP.setheader(response, "Content-Length" => string(len))
    return response
end
# Not a response: HTTP.jl's adapter reports it.
_frame_buffered!(req, response) = response

_buffered_length(body::AbstractVector{UInt8}) = length(body)
_buffered_length(body::AbstractString) = ncodeunits(body)
_buffered_length(body) = nothing

_carries_content(status::Integer) = !(100 <= status < 200 || status == 204 || status == 304)

# `serve(; compress=true)`: the response to send for `response`, gzipped when
# the client admits gzip. Runs after every middleware, before
# `_frame_buffered!`, so the compressed length is the one that is framed. An
# eligible response (a text body of at least `_COMPRESS_MIN_BYTES` held in
# memory, see `_compression_eligible`) gets `Vary: Accept-Encoding` whether or
# not this client takes gzip, so a cache never hands one client's form to
# another. A compressed answer is a NEW response: a route may return a
# response it keeps (a cached page, a constant), and rewriting its body or
# headers in place would compress it for every later request too.
function _compress_buffered(req::HTTP.Request, response::HTTP.Response)
    _compression_eligible(req, response) || return response
    bytes = _buffered_bytes(response.body)
    (bytes === nothing || length(bytes) < _COMPRESS_MIN_BYTES) && return response
    gzip = _accepts_gzip(HTTP.header(req, "Accept-Encoding", ""))
    compressed = gzip ? transcode(CodecZlib.GzipCompressor, bytes) : nothing
    # Highly random text can come out larger; the original is the better answer.
    compressed !== nothing && length(compressed) >= length(bytes) && (compressed = nothing)
    headers = Pair{String,String}[]
    vary = String[]
    for (k, v) in response.headers
        name = lowercase(k)
        # The identity length; framing computes the compressed one.
        compressed !== nothing && name == "content-length" && continue
        name == "vary" && (push!(vary, v); continue)
        name == "etag" && compressed !== nothing && !startswith(v, "W/") && (v = "W/" * v)
        push!(headers, String(k) => String(v))
    end
    push!(headers, "Vary" => _vary_with_accept_encoding(join(vary, ", ")))
    compressed === nothing && return HTTP.Response(response.status, headers, bytes;
        request=response.request)
    push!(headers, "Content-Encoding" => "gzip")
    return HTTP.Response(response.status, headers, compressed; request=response.request)
end
# Not a response: HTTP.jl's adapter reports it.
_compress_buffered(req, response) = response

const _COMPRESS_MIN_BYTES = 1024

# Whether `response` is one `serve` may compress for `req`, before its body and
# the client's `Accept-Encoding` are looked at: a GET-like answer whose status
# carries a complete body, with a text `Content-Type`, not encoded, ranged or
# framed already, and not marked `no-transform` (RFC 9111 §5.2.2.6).
function _compression_eligible(req::HTTP.Request, response::HTTP.Response)
    req.method == "HEAD" && return false
    (_carries_content(response.status) && response.status != 206) || return false
    for name in ("Content-Encoding", "Content-Range", "Transfer-Encoding")
        HTTP.hasheader(response, name) && return false
    end
    occursin("no-transform", lowercase(HTTP.header(response, "Cache-Control", ""))) &&
        return false
    return _compressible_type(HTTP.header(response, "Content-Type", ""))
end

# Text media types worth compressing; images, archives and fonts are compressed
# already or not text.
function _compressible_type(content_type::AbstractString)
    mime = lowercase(strip(first(split(content_type, ';'))))
    return startswith(mime, "text/") || mime in _COMPRESSIBLE_TYPES ||
        endswith(mime, "+json") || endswith(mime, "+xml")
end
const _COMPRESSIBLE_TYPES = ("application/json", "application/javascript",
    "application/xml", "image/svg+xml")

# The bytes of a body held in memory, in a form `transcode` takes, or `nothing`
# for a streamed one. HTTP.jl 2 keeps them in a `BytesBody`; converting it
# copies the unread bytes without consuming them.
_buffered_bytes(body::Union{Vector{UInt8},Base.CodeUnits{UInt8}}) = body
_buffered_bytes(body::AbstractVector{UInt8}) = Vector{UInt8}(body)
_buffered_bytes(body::AbstractString) = codeunits(String(body))
function _buffered_bytes(body)
    isdefined(HTTP, :BytesBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :BytesBody)) &&
        return Vector{UInt8}(body)
    return nothing
end

# Whether an `Accept-Encoding` value admits gzip (RFC 9110 §12.5.3): its own
# entry, or else a `*` entry, with a nonzero q-value. No header, no entry, or
# a malformed q-value: no.
function _accepts_gzip(accept::AbstractString)
    gzip_q = star_q = nothing
    for entry in split(accept, ',')
        coding, params... = split(entry, ';')
        coding = lowercase(strip(coding))
        q = 1.0
        for param in params
            key, value = (split(param, '='; limit=2)..., "")[1:2]
            lowercase(strip(key)) == "q" && (q = something(tryparse(Float64, strip(value)), 0.0))
        end
        coding in ("gzip", "x-gzip") && (gzip_q = q)
        coding == "*" && (star_q = q)
    end
    return something(gzip_q, star_q, 0.0) > 0
end

# A `Vary` value naming `Accept-Encoding` besides what `vary` names already.
function _vary_with_accept_encoding(vary::AbstractString)
    fields = filter(!isempty, strip.(split(vary, ',')))
    any(f -> f == "*" || lowercase(f) == "accept-encoding", fields) && return join(fields, ", ")
    return join([fields; "Accept-Encoding"], ", ")
end

# `serve(; parallel)`: the threadpool each request is spawned on (`nothing`: it
# runs on HTTP.jl's own connection task) and the startup warning for a setting
# that leaves threads unused (`nothing`: none), given the `interactive` and
# `default` threadpool sizes and HTTP.jl's version `http`. HTTP.jl 2 spawns each
# connection's task on `:interactive` (Julia falls back to `:default` when that
# pool is empty); HTTP.jl 1.x serves every connection on the listener's thread.
function _request_threadpool(parallel, interactive::Integer, default::Integer, http::VersionNumber)
    if parallel === :interactive
        interactive == 0 && return :interactive,
            "`parallel=:interactive` without interactive threads: requests run on the :default threadpool. Launch julia with e.g. \"julia -t 8,4\" to add interactive threads for request handling."
        interactive == 1 && return :interactive,
            "Only 1 interactive thread available, so requests are handled one at a time. Launch julia with e.g. \"julia -t 8,4\" to add more interactive threads for request handling."
        return :interactive, nothing
    elseif parallel === true
        return :default, default <= 1 ?
            "`parallel=true` with only 1 thread available. Launch julia with e.g. \"julia -t auto\" to handle requests on several threads." :
            nothing
    end
    http < v"2" && interactive > 1 && return nothing,
        "HTTP.jl $http handles every request on one thread with `parallel=false`, leaving $interactive interactive threads unused. Pass `parallel=:interactive`, or use HTTP.jl 2, which runs connections across the interactive threadpool."
    return nothing, nothing
end

# `parallel=true`/`:interactive`: handle each request on a task spawned on `pool`.
# `wait` reports the task's failure as a `TaskFailedException`; HTTP.jl gets the
# task's own error instead, as it does without the task, because that error is
# how it tells a client that hung up (logged at Debug) from a server fault.
# Thrown inside the `catch`, it keeps the wrapper, and with it the task's
# backtrace, beneath it on the exception stack.
function _spawning_stream_handler(handle, pool::Symbol)
    function (stream)
        try
            wait(Threads.@spawn pool handle(stream))
        catch err
            err isa TaskFailedException && throw(err.task.exception)
            rethrow()
        end
    end
end

# --- Access log ------------------------------------------------------------

function _access_log_middleware(handler, format)
    function (req::HTTP.Request)
        req.context[:t0] = time()
        response = nothing
        try
            response = handler(req)
            return response
        catch err
            err isa _WebSocketClosed && (response = HTTP.Response(101))
            rethrow()
        finally
            req.context[:response] = something(response, HTTP.Response(500))
            try
                @info sprint(format, req) _group=:access
            catch err
                @warn "`access_log` formatter failed" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
            end
        end
    end
end

"""
    _timed_access_log(io, req)

Default `access_log` formatter: `time - ip:port - "METHOD target HTTP/x.y" status`
followed by the request's handling time with SI units (e.g. ` 78.9ms`,
` 1.23s`, ` 5.0min`).
"""
function _timed_access_log(io::IO, req::HTTP.Request)
    response = Base.get(req.context, :response, nothing)
    print(io,
        _access_log_timestamp(), " - ",
        _peer_string(Base.get(req.context, :stream, nothing)), " - \"",
        req.method, " ", req.target, " HTTP/", _http_version(req), "\" ",
        response === nothing ? "-" : response.status,
    )
    t0 = Base.get(req.context, :t0, nothing)
    t0 === nothing || print(io, " ", fmt_time(time() - t0))
end

# --- Operation log -----------------------------------------------------------

"""
    HTMXObjects.OperationRecord

How one polled operation ended, as handed to the `operation_log` formatter of
[`serve`](@ref) (see [`HTMXObjects.OPERATION_LOG`](@ref)). A polled operation is
a route operation that outlived the `:auto` grace period, so the client
followed it with polls; one that answered within the grace period is only its
request's access-log line.

- `method`, `target` — the request that started it. `target` is its path and
  query as the access log shows it, without the poll markers and the operation
  token, and with the values of credential-like parameters replaced by `…`.
- `outcome` — `:ok` (it answered its result), `:failed` (it answered a
  failure), `:stalled` (it answered `OperationStalled` at its stall bound),
  `:abandoned` (no request reached it for ten minutes, so it was dropped),
  `:evicted` (dropped to make room for newer operations) or `:unclaimed` (the
  server started it for a page's load-triggered fragment, see
  [`serve`](@ref)'s `prestart`, and no browser ever asked for it).
- `started_at` — when it started, in Unix seconds.
- `compute` — seconds from its start until the computation finished or failed,
  measured on the server; `NaN` while it still runs.
- `answered` — seconds from its start until the client received the answer that
  ended it; `NaN` for `:abandoned`, `:evicted` and `:unclaimed`. Clients poll at their own
  pace (a browser slows timers in background tabs), so `answered` can exceed
  `compute` by however long the client was not asking.
- `last_seen` — seconds from its start until the last request that reached it.
- `polls` — follow-up requests that reached it (a page load's first fetch
  included).
"""
struct OperationRecord
    method::String
    target::String
    outcome::Symbol
    started_at::Float64
    compute::Float64
    answered::Float64
    last_seen::Float64
    polls::Int
end

"""
    HTMXObjects.OPERATION_LOG

The process-wide operation log formatter (`Ref`): `(io, record::OperationRecord)
-> nothing`, written once per polled operation when it ends, or `nothing` to
write no records. [`serve`](@ref) sets it from its `operation_log` keyword
(default on). It is `nothing` until then, so in-process [`dispatch`](@ref) calls
and other servers write no records unless they set it.
"""
const OPERATION_LOG = Ref{Any}(nothing)

"""
    _default_operation_log(io, record)

Default `operation_log` formatter:
`time - operation METHOD target outcome compute=… answered=… polls=…`, with
seconds to one decimal and `-` for an unknown value. An operation that was not
answered (`:abandoned`, `:evicted`) adds `last_poll=…`, the age at its last
request; an `:unclaimed` one was never requested, so it has none.
"""
function _default_operation_log(io::IO, r::OperationRecord)
    print(io, _access_log_timestamp(), " - operation ", r.method, " ", r.target,
        " ", r.outcome, " compute=", _operation_log_seconds(r.compute),
        " answered=", _operation_log_seconds(r.answered),
        " polls=", r.polls)
    isnan(r.answered) && r.outcome !== :unclaimed &&
        print(io, " last_poll=", _operation_log_seconds(r.last_seen))
end

_operation_log_seconds(seconds::Real) =
    isnan(seconds) ? "-" : string(round(seconds; digits=1), "s")

# Same split as HTTP.jl's `$time_iso8601` log variable: Windows' C runtime
# `strftime` does not support `%F`/`%T`/`%z`, so format without a zone there.
const _ACCESS_LOG_WINDOWS_DATEFORMAT = Dates.DateFormat("yyyy-mm-dd\\THH:MM:SS")

function _access_log_timestamp(windows::Bool=Sys.iswindows())
    if windows
        return Dates.format(Dates.now(), _ACCESS_LOG_WINDOWS_DATEFORMAT)
    end
    return Libc.strftime("%FT%T%z", time())
end

# HTTP 1.x requests carry a `version`; HTTP 2.x replaced it with `proto_major`/`proto_minor`.
_http_version(req::HTTP.Request) = hasproperty(req, :version) ?
    "$(req.version.major).$(req.version.minor)" :
    "$(req.proto_major).$(req.proto_minor)"

# Client address of a server stream as `"ip:port"`, or `"-"`. HTTP 2.x exposes
# it through `HTTP.peeraddr`, HTTP 1.x through `Sockets.getpeername`.
function _peer_string(stream)
    stream === nothing && return "-"
    try
        if isdefined(HTTP, :peeraddr)
            addr = getproperty(HTTP, :peeraddr)(stream)
            return addr === nothing ? "-" : string(addr)
        end
        ip, port = Sockets.getpeername(stream)
        return "$ip:$(Int(port))"
    catch
        return "-"
    end
end

# --- Static files ----------------------------------------------------------

"""
    staticfiles(folder, mountdir="static"; headers=[], loadfile=nothing)

Serve every file under `folder` at `/<mountdir>/<relative path>`; an
`index.html` also answers at its directory's path. Files are read once, when
mounted. `headers` are added to every response, and `loadfile(path)` replaces
`read(path)`.
"""
staticfiles(folder::AbstractString, mountdir::AbstractString="static";
        headers::Vector=[], loadfile=nothing) =
    _mount_files(folder, mountdir) do path
        body = _load_file(path, loadfile)
        _ -> _file_response(path, body, headers)
    end

"""
    dynamicfiles(folder, mountdir="static"; headers=[], loadfile=nothing)

Like [`staticfiles`](@ref), but re-reads each file from disk on every request.
"""
dynamicfiles(folder::AbstractString, mountdir::AbstractString="static";
        headers::Vector=[], loadfile=nothing) =
    _mount_files(folder, mountdir) do path
        _ -> _file_response(path, _load_file(path, loadfile), headers)
    end

function _mount_files(handler_for, folder, mountdir)
    prefix = strip(mountdir, '/')
    for (dir, _, files) in walkdir(folder), file in files
        path = joinpath(dir, file)
        rel = join(splitpath(relpath(path, folder)), "/")
        route = isempty(prefix) ? "/$rel" : "/$prefix/$rel"
        handler = handler_for(path)
        _register_get_route!(route, handler)
        if file == "index.html"
            dir_route = String(chopsuffix(route, "/index.html"))
            _register_get_route!(isempty(dir_route) ? "/" : dir_route, handler)
        end
    end
    return nothing
end

_load_file(path, loadfile) = loadfile === nothing ? read(path) : loadfile(path)

# A fresh response per request: HTTP.jl mutates the response it writes, and a
# middleware reading `String(body)` would steal a shared byte buffer.
function _file_response(path, body, headers)
    bytes = body isa AbstractString ? Vector{UInt8}(body) : copy(body)
    response = HTTP.Response(200, headers; body=bytes)
    HTTP.setheader(response, "Content-Type" => _static_content_type(path))
    HTTP.setheader(response, "Content-Length" => string(length(bytes)))
    return response
end

const _STATIC_CONTENT_TYPES = Dict(
    "html" => "text/html; charset=utf-8", "htm" => "text/html; charset=utf-8",
    "css" => "text/css; charset=utf-8", "js" => "text/javascript; charset=utf-8",
    "mjs" => "text/javascript; charset=utf-8", "json" => "application/json; charset=utf-8",
    "map" => "application/json; charset=utf-8", "txt" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
    "md" => "text/markdown; charset=utf-8", "csv" => "text/csv; charset=utf-8",
    "xml" => "application/xml", "svg" => "image/svg+xml", "png" => "image/png",
    "jpg" => "image/jpeg", "jpeg" => "image/jpeg", "gif" => "image/gif",
    "webp" => "image/webp", "avif" => "image/avif", "ico" => "image/x-icon",
    "woff" => "font/woff", "woff2" => "font/woff2", "ttf" => "font/ttf",
    "otf" => "font/otf", "pdf" => "application/pdf", "wasm" => "application/wasm",
    "mp4" => "video/mp4", "webm" => "video/webm", "mp3" => "audio/mpeg",
    "wav" => "audio/wav", "ogg" => "audio/ogg",
)

_static_content_type(path) = Base.get(_STATIC_CONTENT_TYPES,
    lowercase(lstrip(last(splitext(path)), '.')), "application/octet-stream")

# Append a new value to an existing query-parameter slot. String slot becomes
# a 2-element vector; vector slot grows in place.
_form_append(existing::String, v) = [existing, v]
_form_append(existing::AbstractVector, v) = (push!(existing, v); existing)

# --- Form-encoded parsing (multi-value aware) ---

# Hex digit value of one ASCII byte, or -1 when it isn't one. Used by the
# lenient percent-decoder below to test a `%XY` escape without throwing.
_hexval(b::UInt8) =
    UInt8('0') <= b <= UInt8('9') ? Int(b - UInt8('0')) :
    UInt8('a') <= b <= UInt8('f') ? Int(b - UInt8('a')) + 10 :
    UInt8('A') <= b <= UInt8('F') ? Int(b - UInt8('A')) + 10 : -1

"""
    _percent_decode_lenient(s) -> String

Percent-decode `s` per RFC 3986 (`%` HEXDIG HEXDIG), emitting a malformed
escape as the literal `%` it was written as instead of throwing. This is the
browser / `URLSearchParams` behaviour.

Why it matters: this decoder runs on data the framework merely *guessed* was a
form. `HTTP.URIs.unescapeuri` throws on any `%` not followed by two hex digits
— `ArgumentError: invalid base 16 digit` on a non-hex digit, `EOFError` on a
truncated escape at the end of the string. Julia's `%` operator, a printf `%s`,
`?q=50%` typed into a URL bar, any compressed/binary byte — and the throw lands
in the argument extractor, *before* the handler runs, so it surfaces as a bare
500 whose log points at URI parsing. From the caller's side that reads as a
corrupt payload rather than as a Content-Type negotiation problem
(snag `bodyparams-form-525c2ab8`).

Relationship to `unescapeuri`, measured rather than assumed. Identical on all
484 well-formed `%XY` escapes, on every string containing no `%`, and on 153646
randomly generated strings that `unescapeuri` accepts — multi-byte UTF-8
included, since both reassemble it from decoded bytes. The two differ on
exactly **264** two-character sequences after a `%`: `unescapeuri` delegates to
`parse(UInt8, c1c2; base=16)`, which tolerates surrounding ASCII whitespace, so
one hex digit beside a raw space/tab/newline (`"%a "`, `"% 9"`) currently
decodes to a control byte in `0x00`–`0x0f`. Those now decode to the literal
three characters. Such input is already non-conforming twice over — a bare `%`
*and* raw whitespace inside a form value, which a real encoder writes as `%25`
and `%20`/`%09` — and the byte it produced was an accident of `parse`, not URI
semantics. Everything else that used to 500 now round-trips the `%` literally.
"""
function _percent_decode_lenient(s::AbstractString)
    occursin('%', s) || return String(s)
    b = codeunits(s)
    n = length(b)
    out = IOBuffer(sizehint=n)
    i = 1
    while i <= n
        c = b[i]
        if c == UInt8('%') && i + 2 <= n
            hi = _hexval(b[i + 1])
            lo = _hexval(b[i + 2])
            if hi >= 0 && lo >= 0
                write(out, UInt8(16 * hi + lo))
                i += 3
                continue
            end
        end
        write(out, c)
        i += 1
    end
    String(take!(out))
end

# Parse a form-encoded string ("a=1&b=2&b=3") into a multi-value-preserving
# Dict. Shared by `queryparams` (URL query string) and `formparams` (request
# body). Query and `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies use the same
# wire format, so one parser suffices.
#
# Never throws on malformed input: a bad percent escape degrades to a literal
# '%' (see `_percent_decode_lenient`). A parameter *source* must not be able to
# fail the request — it runs speculatively, ahead of the handler, on a body the
# caller may never have meant as a form.
function _parse_form_encoded(s::AbstractString)
    isempty(s) && return Dict{String, Union{String, Vector{String}}}()
    d = Dict{String, Union{String, Vector{String}}}()
    # '+' represents a space in form-encoded data. Percent-decoding alone does
    # not cover it, so translate '+' → ' ' before unescaping. Without this,
    # values produced by JS `URLSearchParams.toString()` (which form-encodes
    # spaces as '+') round-trip spaces as literal '+' characters and break
    # downstream lookups.
    _form_unescape(t) = _percent_decode_lenient(replace(t, '+' => ' '))
    for part in split(s, "&", keepempty=false)
        kv = split(part, "=", limit=2)
        k = _form_unescape(kv[1])
        v = length(kv) >= 2 ? _form_unescape(kv[2]) : ""
        if haskey(d, k)
            d[k] = _form_append(d[k], v)
        else
            d[k] = v
        end
    end
    d
end

"""
    queryparams(req::HTTP.Request) -> Dict{String, Union{String, Vector{String}}}

Parse query parameters from the request URL. Unlike `HTTP.queryparams` (which
returns `Dict{String,String}` and drops duplicate keys), this preserves all
values: single-value keys map to a `String`, duplicate keys map to a
`Vector{String}`.

    queryparams("http://x/?a=1&b=2&b=3")  #=> Dict("a" => "1", "b" => ["2", "3"])

Malformed percent-escapes degrade to a literal `%` rather than throwing, so a
hand-typed `?q=50%` is a value, not a 500 (see `_percent_decode_lenient`).
"""
queryparams(req::HTTP.Request) = _parse_form_encoded(HTTP.URI(req.target).query)

"""
    formparams(req::HTTP.Request) -> Dict{String, Union{String, Vector{String}}}

Parse a urlencoded request body into a multi-value-preserving Dict. The
`formparams` analogue of `queryparams` — repeated body fields (`image=a&image=b`)
become a `Vector{String}` instead of collapsing to the last value, which is
what [`formdata`](@ref) (a thin wrapper over `HTTP.queryparams`) does. Used by
the `@post`/`@put`/`@patch` argument extractor so a `Vector`-typed kwarg
receives every posted value.

Reads a non-destructive copy of the request body; an empty body yields an empty
dict. Never throws — a body that isn't really urlencoded yields junk keys at
worst, never a 500 (see `_percent_decode_lenient`).
"""
_request_body_bytes(req::HTTP.Request) = _message_body_bytes(req)

function _message_body_bytes(message::Union{HTTP.Request,HTTP.Response})
    # HTTP 1.x exposes `payload(message)` and stores ordinary request bodies as
    # byte vectors. HTTP 2.x replaced it with explicit body objects; BytesBody
    # conversion copies only the unread bytes and therefore keeps argument
    # extraction non-destructive in both worlds.
    if isdefined(HTTP, :payload)
        return copy(getproperty(HTTP, :payload)(message))
    end
    body = message.body
    if isdefined(HTTP, :BytesBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :BytesBody))
        return Vector{UInt8}(body)
    elseif isdefined(HTTP, :EmptyBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :EmptyBody))
        return UInt8[]
    elseif body isa AbstractVector{UInt8}
        return copy(body)
    elseif body isa AbstractString
        return Vector{UInt8}(codeunits(body))
    end
    # A streaming body cannot be copied without consuming it. Server-side form
    # requests arrive buffered in both supported HTTP worlds; for a custom
    # programmatic request, preserve the non-destructive contract and expose no
    # body-sourced kwargs rather than draining its stream.
    return UInt8[]
end

function formparams(req::HTTP.Request)
    # Only an `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body is parseable here — it's
    # the wire format `_parse_form_encoded` understands. A `multipart/form-data`
    # (file upload), JSON, or other binary body must NOT be fed to the
    # urlencoded parser at all: its bytes are not key/value pairs, so parsing
    # them can only invent keys. Such handlers read the raw body themselves
    # (e.g. `HTTP.parse_multipart_form`); any kwargs they declare fall back to
    # `queryparams`. Parse only when the content-type says urlencoded, or is
    # absent (a headerless client posting a urlencoded body).
    #
    # This gate is a CORRECTNESS filter, not a safety net: a caller that
    # mislabels a binary payload as urlencoded (curl's `--data-binary` default,
    # for one) walks straight through it. That case must therefore be
    # survivable in the parser itself, which is why `_parse_form_encoded` is
    # non-throwing (snag `bodyparams-form-525c2ab8`).
    ct = lowercase(HTTP.header(req, "Content-Type", ""))
    isempty(ct) || occursin("application/x-www-form-urlencoded", ct) ||
        return Dict{String, Union{String, Vector{String}}}()
    # `String(::Vector{UInt8})` takes ownership and empties the vector, so the
    # compatibility helper above always hands it a detached copy.
    _parse_form_encoded(String(_request_body_bytes(req)))
end

"""
    formdata(req::HTTP.Request) -> Dict{String, String}

Parse a urlencoded request body into a `Dict`; a repeated field keeps only its
last value. See [`formparams`](@ref) for the multi-value form.
"""
formdata(req::HTTP.Request) = HTTP.queryparams(String(_request_body_bytes(req)))

"""
    Upload

One uploaded part of a `multipart/form-data` request body, as bound to a route
kwarg by the POST/PUT/PATCH argument extractor. `filename` is the client-supplied
name, `contenttype` the part's declared MIME type, `data` the raw bytes, `name`
the form field name. Declare `@post upload(; file)` and the handler receives an
`Upload` for `file` — no need to call `HTTP.parse_multipart_form` yourself.

Declare the argument as `file::Upload` (or `files::Vector{Upload}` for several)
and generated operation forms render an `<input type="file">` (`multiple` for
the vector) and submit as `multipart/form-data`. A file travels only in a
request body, so such an argument belongs on a POST, PUT or PATCH route.
"""
struct Upload
    name::String
    filename::String
    contenttype::String
    data::Vector{UInt8}
end
Base.show(io::IO, u::Upload) =
    print(io, "Upload(", repr(u.filename), ", ", repr(u.contenttype), ", ", length(u.data), " bytes)")

"""
    MultilineText(text)
    MultilineText()

Free-form text that may span several lines — a note, a comment, a description.
Declare a route argument or fixed field as `MultilineText`
(`@post flag(; note::MultilineText)`) and generated operation forms render it
as a `<textarea>` instead of the single-line `<input>` an ordinary `String`
gets. The route body receives a `MultilineText`: an `AbstractString` backed by
a `String`, which `String(note)` returns unchanged. A submitted value's line
breaks arrive as `\\n`, whether the browser sent `\\r\\n`, `\\r` or `\\n`.

An optional argument needs a `MultilineText` default (`note::MultilineText =
MultilineText()`), because Julia checks a keyword's declared type. A string
literal default, `MultilineText("…")`, also prefills the textarea; any other
default expression leaves it blank, and a blank submission uses the default.
"""
struct MultilineText <: AbstractString
    value::String
    MultilineText(text::AbstractString) = new(String(text))
    MultilineText() = new("")
end
Base.codeunit(::MultilineText) = UInt8
Base.ncodeunits(text::MultilineText) = ncodeunits(text.value)
Base.codeunit(text::MultilineText, i::Integer) = codeunit(text.value, i)
Base.isvalid(text::MultilineText, i::Integer) = isvalid(text.value, i)
Base.iterate(text::MultilineText) = iterate(text.value)
Base.iterate(text::MultilineText, i::Integer) = iterate(text.value, i)
Base.print(io::IO, text::MultilineText) = print(io, text.value)
Base.String(text::MultilineText) = text.value

# Multi-value append for repeated multipart field names. Value-type-agnostic
# (works for `Upload` and `String` parts alike), mirroring `_form_append`.
_mp_append(existing::AbstractVector, v) = (push!(existing, v); existing)
_mp_append(existing, v) = Any[existing, v]

"""
    multipartparams(req::HTTP.Request) -> Dict{String, Any}

Parse a `multipart/form-data` body into a field-name → value map: a file part
(one carrying a filename) becomes an [`Upload`](@ref); a plain text part becomes
a `String`. Repeated field names collect into a `Vector`. Returns an empty dict
when the body isn't valid multipart. Non-destructive — `HTTP.parse_multipart_form`
reads `req.body` through its own buffer, leaving the request body intact.
"""
function multipartparams(req::HTTP.Request)
    d = Dict{String, Any}()
    parts = HTTP.parse_multipart_form(req)
    parts === nothing && return d
    for p in parts
        fname = p.filename === nothing ? "" : p.filename
        val = isempty(fname) ? String(read(p)) : Upload(p.name, fname, p.contenttype, read(p))
        d[p.name] = haskey(d, p.name) ? _mp_append(d[p.name], val) : val
    end
    d
end

"""
    bodyparams(req::HTTP.Request) -> AbstractDict

The request-body parameter source for the POST/PUT/PATCH argument extractor:
a `multipart/form-data` body routes to [`multipartparams`](@ref) (text fields as
`String`, file fields as [`Upload`](@ref)); everything else routes to
[`formparams`](@ref) (which itself only parses an
`application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body and returns an empty dict otherwise).
This is what lets a declared kwarg bind from either body shape through the one
shared `_lookup_param` path — `@post f(; x)` for a urlencoded field and
`@post upload(; file)` for a file both Just Work.
"""
function bodyparams(req::HTTP.Request)
    ct = lowercase(HTTP.header(req, "Content-Type", ""))
    occursin("multipart/form-data", ct) && return multipartparams(req)
    formparams(req)
end

# Streaming route macros and the handle variable their bodies receive.
const _STREAM_BODY_VARS = Dict(Symbol("@ws") => :__ws__, Symbol("@sse") => :__sse__)

"""
    _wrap_stream_bodies!(struct_expr)

Pre-process the struct body: for any `@ws` / `@sse` property, wrap the RHS in
`(__ws__) -> RHS` / `(__sse__) -> RHS`. This lets users write
`@ws feed = begin ... __ws__ ... end` (or `@sse` with `__sse__`) and have the
connection handle available as a variable, while DynamicObjects stores a
callable `(handle) -> body`.
"""
function _wrap_stream_bodies!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa Expr || continue
        # Walk through nested macrocall layers to find @ws / @sse
        expr = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && !haskey(_STREAM_BODY_VARS, expr.args[1])
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) || continue
        var = _STREAM_BODY_VARS[expr.args[1]]
        # Found the marker — the inner expression is an assignment: name = rhs
        inner = expr.args[end]
        inner isa Expr || continue
        if inner.head == :(=)
            rhs = inner.args[2]
            inner.args[2] = Expr(:(->), var, rhs)
        end
    end
    struct_expr
end

"""
    _warn_legacy_name!(struct_expr, legacy::Symbol, replacement::Symbol)

Scan the struct body for top-level property definitions named `legacy` and
emit a deprecation `@warn` pointing at `replacement`. Skips route-marked
properties (so `@get legacy(...)` — a user route literally named `legacy` —
is not flagged). The warning includes `file:line` from the nearest
`LineNumberNode` so the fix is mechanical.
"""
# Resolve the bound property name from the LHS of a `name = ...` (or
# `name(...) = ...`, `name[...] = ...`, `name::T = ...`) inside an `@htmx`
# struct body. Returns `nothing` when the LHS doesn't bind a plain symbol.
_as_symbol(s::Symbol) = s
_as_symbol(_) = nothing
_property_lhs_name(_) = nothing
_property_lhs_name(lhs::Symbol) = lhs
function _property_lhs_name(lhs::Expr)
    (lhs.head === :call || lhs.head === :ref) && return _as_symbol(first(lhs.args))
    lhs.head === :(::) && length(lhs.args) >= 1 && return _property_lhs_name(lhs.args[1])
    nothing
end

# Return-annotated route definitions have an outer `::ReturnType` around the
# call-form LHS. Keep that annotation intact for DynamicObjects descriptor
# inference while route-specific transforms work on the inner call.
_route_call_lhs(_) = nothing
function _route_call_lhs(lhs::Expr)
    Meta.isexpr(lhs, (:call, :ref)) && return lhs
    Meta.isexpr(lhs, :(::)) && length(lhs.args) == 2 || return nothing
    inner = lhs.args[1]
    Meta.isexpr(inner, (:call, :ref)) ? inner : nothing
end

# Name of a kwarg slot in a `:parameters` expr — bare `Symbol` or `:kw` Expr.
_kwarg_name(_) = nothing
_kwarg_name(s::Symbol) = s
_kwarg_name(e::Expr) = e.head === :kw ? e.args[1] : nothing

# Symbol name from a typed param shape: `n` or `n::T`.
_typed_param_name(_) = nothing
_typed_param_name(s::Symbol) = s
function _typed_param_name(e::Expr)
    e.head === :(::) && length(e.args) >= 1 || return nothing
    _as_symbol(e.args[1])
end

# Split a `@param`-style LHS into `(name, type_or_nothing)`. Returns
# `(nothing, nothing)` if the LHS is not a recognized shape.
_split_param_lhs(_) = (nothing, nothing)
_split_param_lhs(lhs::Symbol) = (lhs, nothing)
function _split_param_lhs(lhs::Expr)
    lhs.head === :(::) && length(lhs.args) == 2 || return (nothing, nothing)
    name = _as_symbol(lhs.args[1])
    isnothing(name) ? (nothing, nothing) : (name, lhs.args[2])
end

function _warn_legacy_name!(struct_expr, legacy::Symbol, replacement::Symbol)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    lnn = nothing
    for arg in body.args
        if arg isa LineNumberNode
            lnn = arg
            continue
        end
        arg isa Expr || continue
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) && arg.args[1] in route_macros
            continue
        end
        inner = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(inner, :macrocall)
            inner = inner.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(inner, :(=)) || continue
        name = _property_lhs_name(inner.args[1])
        if name === legacy
            loc = isnothing(lnn) ? "" : " (near $(lnn.file):$(lnn.line))"
            @warn "Deprecated: `$legacy` is a legacy framework property name — rename to `$replacement`$loc. The legacy name still works but will be emitted as a warning on every macro expansion."
        end
    end
    struct_expr
end

_warn_legacy_page_name!(struct_expr) = _warn_legacy_name!(struct_expr, :page, :__page__)

# Attribute names that should be `__prefix__`-aware (and therefore should
# not be hardcoded root-absolute strings). `hx-*` attributes get
# underscore-to-hyphen translation by HTMX's node builder; we match the underscore form
# as written in source.
const _URL_BEARING_ATTRS = (
    :href, :src, :action, :formaction,
    :hx_get, :hx_post, :hx_put, :hx_patch, :hx_delete,
    :hx_post_url, :hx_target, :sse_connect,
)

# Cheaply detect if an expression is a hardcoded root-absolute URL string —
# i.e. `"/foo"` or `"/foo/$id"`-style interpolation that begins with `/`.
# External (`http://…`, `//…`, `mailto:`), protocol-relative, or anchor URLs
# (`#…`) are fine; only literal absolute paths are flagged.
_is_hardcoded_root_url(expr::AbstractString) = startswith(expr, "/") && !startswith(expr, "//")
_is_hardcoded_root_url(expr::Expr) = Meta.isexpr(expr, :string) && !isempty(expr.args) &&
    _is_root_str_prefix(expr.args[1])
_is_hardcoded_root_url(_) = false
_is_root_str_prefix(s::AbstractString) = startswith(s, "/") && !startswith(s, "//")
_is_root_str_prefix(_) = false

"""
    _warn_hardcoded_url_in_attrs!(struct_expr)

Walk the struct body looking for `href="/foo"` / `hx_get="/foo"` /
`src="/foo"` etc. — root-absolute URL string literals on URL-bearing
attributes. Such URLs bypass `__prefix__` (so they break under any
non-root mount, base-path deploy, or `record!` target) and bypass
`__route__` (so updating a route's path requires hunting all literal
references). Suggest `__self__/"…"` (cross-route) or `__route__`
(self-link) instead.

Warning, not error: the hardcoded form still works for root-mounted
local dev. Apps adopt the prefix-aware form gradually.
"""
function _warn_hardcoded_url_in_attrs!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    type_name = _struct_type_name(struct_expr)
    seen = Set{Tuple{Symbol,Any}}()  # dedupe identical (attr, value) pairs across passes

    function walk(node, lnn)
        if node isa Expr
            for arg in node.args
                if arg isa LineNumberNode
                    lnn = arg
                elseif arg isa Expr
                    if Meta.isexpr(arg, :kw) && length(arg.args) == 2 &&
                       arg.args[1] in _URL_BEARING_ATTRS &&
                       _is_hardcoded_root_url(arg.args[2])
                        attr = arg.args[1]
                        val = arg.args[2]
                        key = (attr, val)
                        if !(key in seen)
                            push!(seen, key)
                            shown = val isa AbstractString ? repr(val) :
                                    Meta.isexpr(val, :string) ? "string interp starting with `$(val.args[1])…`" :
                                    string(val)
                            loc = isnothing(lnn) ? "" : " (near $(lnn.file):$(lnn.line))"
                            @warn """
@htmx struct $type_name: hardcoded root-absolute URL `$shown` on `$attr`$loc — bypasses `__prefix__` (breaks under non-root mounts and static deploys). Prefer:
  `$attr=__self__/"<rest>"`           — cross-route URL, `__prefix__`-aware
  `$attr=__route__`                   — same URL as this handler call
  `$attr=string(__self__/"<rest>")`   — if the surrounding ctx wants a String
"""
                        end
                    end
                    walk(arg, lnn)
                end
            end
        end
        lnn
    end

    walk(body, nothing)
    struct_expr
end

"""
    _warn_redundant_req_decl!(struct_expr)

Warn when the user explicitly writes `__req__ = nothing`. `_inject_dunder_props!`
adds this line automatically when no `__req__` is declared, so an explicit
declaration is redundant noise.
"""
function _warn_redundant_req_decl!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    lnn = nothing
    for arg in body.args
        if arg isa LineNumberNode
            lnn = arg
            continue
        end
        arg isa Expr || continue
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) && arg.args[1] in route_macros
            continue
        end
        inner = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(inner, :macrocall)
            inner = inner.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(inner, :(=)) || continue
        lhs = inner.args[1]
        lhs === :__req__ || continue
        rhs = inner.args[2]
        rhs === :nothing || (rhs isa QuoteNode && rhs.value === nothing) || continue
        loc = isnothing(lnn) ? "" : " (near $(lnn.file):$(lnn.line))"
        @warn "Redundant: `__req__ = nothing` is injected automatically by `@htmx` — remove this line$loc."
    end
    struct_expr
end

"""
    _mounted_prefix(parent, step)

Derive an `@htmx` object's absolute `__prefix__` from its parent and the
parent-relative `__mount_step__` recorded at its mount site.

`step === nothing` means the object is not mounted through an `@include` (it is
a root, or a standalone construction), so its prefix is `""` unless the struct
body or a constructor kwarg supplies one. An `:index` mount records `""` and
therefore inherits the parent's prefix unchanged — the same URL-collapse rule
`_route_path` and `_nested_prefix_and_step` apply.

Deriving rather than baking in at construction is what makes the prefix survive
`DynamicObjects.remount`: remount deliberately masks a retained `__prefix__` and
recomputes it from the child's own body (see `_remount_child` — "`__prefix__` is
route-relative, so mask the retained value and let the child's own generated
property recompute it from its new `__parent__`"). An inline `@include` child
already satisfied that contract, because `_convert_include_to_struct!` gives it
a `__prefix__` body reading `__parent__.__prefix__`. An EXTERNAL `@include`
child did not: its prefix arrived as a constructor kwarg, so recomputation fell
back to the type default `""` and every URL built from it lost the mount.
"""
function _mounted_prefix(parent, step)
    step === nothing && return ""
    parent === nothing && return String(step)
    hasproperty(parent, :__prefix__) || return String(step)
    string(getproperty(parent, :__prefix__)) * step
end

"""
    _inject_dunder_props!(struct_expr)

Ensure that `@htmx` struct bodies always declare `__parent__`, `__mount_step__`,
`__prefix__`, `__req__`, `__appdata__`, `__route__`, `__on_error__`, and
`__error_texts__` as properties so route bodies can reference them and so the
`__parent__` chain threads request context, appdata, the mount prefix, the
per-request route URL, the error hook, and the error answers' texts down through
`@include`d sub-structs.

Defaults:
- `__parent__ = nothing` — set by `@include` desugar to point at the enclosing struct.
- `__mount_step__ = nothing` — the PARENT-RELATIVE URL step this object is
  mounted at (`"/childname"`, `""` for an `:index` mount, or a step carrying
  interpolated index arguments). `nothing` means "not mounted through an
  `@include`" (a root, or a standalone construction). `_inject_include_prefix!`
  supplies it at the mount site.
- `__prefix__ = _mounted_prefix(__parent__, __mount_step__)` — the absolute
  mount path (no trailing `/`; `""` at the root). It is DERIVED from the parent
  rather than baked in at construction so that it stays correct when the object
  is re-parented — `DynamicObjects.remount` deliberately masks a retained
  `__prefix__` and recomputes it, which a construction-time constant cannot
  survive (a retained semantic root then served root-relative URLs from its
  second request on).
- `__req__` / `__appdata__` / `__on_error__` / `__error_texts__` — fall through
  `__parent__` if present, else `nothing`. `__req__` is supplied by HTMXO at request-handler
  construction. `__appdata__` is supplied by the user — the conventional pattern
  is to override the default inside the root `@htmx struct` body
  (`__appdata__ = APP_DATA` with `const APP_DATA = AppData()` at module scope) so
  the singleton is part of the struct's definition, not of `route!`'s API.
  `__on_error__` is the optional post-error side-effect hook (see
  `_default_error_render`); defining it once on the root `@htmx struct` body
  propagates it to every route — including `@include`d upstream structs the app
  cannot edit. `__error_texts__` is the [`ErrorTexts`](@ref) the error articles
  are written in (`nothing` = English); `__error_texts__ = error_texts(:de)` on
  the root reaches every route the same way.
- `__route__ = ""` — defaults to empty; `_register_route_handler` sets it to
  the current request's path (query string stripped, **mount-prefix-aware**:
  the request `__prefix__` is prepended when a path-stripping proxy removed it,
  so `__route__` is the full externally-visible path and stays consistent with
  `__prefix__`) when constructing the struct for a real request, so route
  bodies can write `hx_get=__route__` / `href=__route__` — and
  `query_url(__self__;…)`, which builds on `__route__` — instead of recomputing
  `__self__/"name/\$id"`. For non-request constructions (e.g., `route!` at
  module init) it stays `""`.

The legacy short names `req` / `appdata` are no longer recognised.
"""
function _inject_dunder_props!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    has_req = false
    has_appdata = false
    has_parent = false
    has_prefix = false
    has_mount_step = false
    has_route = false
    has_on_error = false
    has_error_texts = false
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa Expr || continue
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) && arg.args[1] in route_macros
            continue
        end
        inner = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(inner, :macrocall)
            inner = inner.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(inner, :(=)) || continue
        name = _property_lhs_name(inner.args[1])
        name === :__req__      && (has_req = true)
        name === :__appdata__  && (has_appdata = true)
        name === :__parent__   && (has_parent = true)
        name === :__prefix__   && (has_prefix = true)
        name === :__mount_step__ && (has_mount_step = true)
        name === :__route__    && (has_route = true)
        name === :__on_error__ && (has_on_error = true)
        name === :__error_texts__ && (has_error_texts = true)
    end
    prepend = Any[]
    has_parent   || push!(prepend, :(__parent__ = nothing))
    has_mount_step || push!(prepend, :(__mount_step__ = nothing))
    has_prefix   || push!(prepend, :(__prefix__ =
        $(GlobalRef(@__MODULE__, :_mounted_prefix))(__parent__, __mount_step__)))
    has_req      || push!(prepend, :(__req__      = isnothing(__parent__) ? nothing : __parent__.__req__))
    has_appdata  || push!(prepend, :(__appdata__  = isnothing(__parent__) ? nothing : __parent__.__appdata__))
    has_route    || push!(prepend, :(__route__    = isnothing(__parent__) ? "" : __parent__.__route__))
    has_on_error || push!(prepend, :(__on_error__ = isnothing(__parent__) ? nothing : __parent__.__on_error__))
    has_error_texts || push!(prepend, :(__error_texts__ = isnothing(__parent__) ? nothing : __parent__.__error_texts__))
    if !isempty(prepend)
        body.args = vcat(prepend, body.args)
    end
    struct_expr
end

# Find inline struct properties: prop = struct Name ... end
# Returns list of (prop_name, child_struct_name) pairs.
function _find_inline_structs(struct_expr)
    parent_name = _struct_type_name(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    result = Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}[]
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa Expr || continue
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :(=)) && Meta.isexpr(arg.args[2], :struct)
            lhs = arg.args[1]
            Meta.isexpr(lhs, (:call, :ref)) && (lhs = lhs.args[1])
            Meta.isexpr(lhs, :(::)) && (lhs = lhs.args[1])
            child_name = arg.args[2].args[2]  # struct Name from `prop = struct Name ... end`
            # DO renames child to Parent_ChildName
            gen_name = Symbol(parent_name, "_", child_name)
            let n = _as_symbol(lhs); isnothing(n) || push!(result, (n, gen_name)) end
        end
    end
    result
end

# Find @include external struct properties:
#   @include prop = ExternalStruct(; ...)              # classic, non-indexed
#   @include prop(args…) = ExternalStruct(args…; …)    # indexed include
# Returns list of (prop_name, type_name_expr, index_params) tuples where
# `index_params` is a `Vector{Symbol}` (empty for non-indexed).
"""
    _unwrap_short_form_body(rhs) -> Expr

Undo the `:block` the parser wraps a short-form method body in.

`f(x) = g(x)` and `f(x) = begin g(x) end` parse to the SAME head, so an
`@include mount(idx) = Child(idx)` is indistinguishable from a block-form
mount by head alone. The payload discriminates: one statement (LineNumberNodes
aside) is the parser's wrapping and unwraps to the bare call; several
statements is a body the author wrote and is returned untouched.

Delegates to DynamicObjects when available so both sides of the same
declaration agree by construction rather than by two copies of one rule.
"""
function _unwrap_short_form_body(rhs)
    if isdefined(DynamicObjects, :_unwrap_short_form_body)
        return getproperty(DynamicObjects, :_unwrap_short_form_body)(rhs)
    end
    Meta.isexpr(rhs, :block) || return rhs
    body = [a for a in rhs.args if !(a isa LineNumberNode)]
    length(body) == 1 ? only(body) : rhs
end

"""
    _is_inline_router_body(rhs) -> Bool

Is this `@include` rhs a body the AUTHOR wrote, rather than the `:block` the
parser wrapped a short-form method body in?

`_unwrap_short_form_body` discriminates on statement COUNT, which is the whole
truth in DynamicObjects' vocabulary — a mount's rhs is a constructor call, so a
lone statement can only be the parser's doing. It is not the whole truth in
HTMXObjects': a sub-router that declares exactly ONE route

    @include index(id::String) = begin
        @delete index() = ...
    end

is a one-statement block the author wrote. Unwrapping it leaves a bare
`@delete` macrocall as the rhs — neither `:block` nor `:call`, so
`_convert_include_to_struct!` falls through both branches, the `@include`
survives into real macro expansion, and Julia reports
`UndefVarError: @delete not defined` at the route line. The verbs are
`@htmx`-consumed MARKERS, never exported macros, so that error names a symbol
that is not supposed to exist by then and points nowhere near the include that
swallowed it.

A route macro or a nested `@include` settles it: neither can ever be the short
form of an external mount, because you cannot mount a struct by declaring a
route. Anything else (a call, an assignment) stays with the count heuristic.
"""
function _is_inline_router_body(rhs)
    Meta.isexpr(rhs, :block) || return false
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    for a in rhs.args
        expr = a
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall)
            name = expr.args[1]
            (name in route_macros || name === Symbol("@include")) && return true
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
    end
    false
end

function _find_include_externals(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    result = Tuple{Symbol, Any, Vector{Symbol}}[]
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa Expr || continue
        expr = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && expr.args[1] != Symbol("@include")
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && expr.args[1] == Symbol("@include") || continue
        inner = expr.args[end]
        Meta.isexpr(inner, :(=)) || continue
        lhs = inner.args[1]
        rhs = inner.args[2]
        # The INDEXED form `@include mount(key::T) = Child(key)` is a short-form
        # function definition, and the parser wraps a short form's body in a
        # `begin` block carrying its LineNumberNode. The value form
        # `@include mount = Child(...)` is a plain assignment and is not
        # wrapped. Unwrap before the shape check, or every indexed mount is
        # silently skipped here — and skipping is invisible: the struct still
        # compiles, DynamicObjects' own `_nested_struct_type` (returning its
        # memoizing wrapper, whose meta declares no routes) is the only method
        # left standing, and the child's routes simply never register.
        # Only an indexed mount — a `:call` LHS — can have had its rhs wrapped
        # by the parser. With a plain Symbol LHS a block is always a genuine
        # inline sub-router and unwrapping it would silently reinterpret it.
        # A body that DECLARES a route (or a nested `@include`) is the author's
        # even at one statement — same rule as `_convert_include_to_struct!`,
        # and the two must agree or one classifies a sub-router as a mount.
        Meta.isexpr(lhs, :call) && !_is_inline_router_body(rhs) &&
            (rhs = _unwrap_short_form_body(rhs))
        # RHS should be a call like ExternalStruct(; __req__, ...) — extract the type.
        Meta.isexpr(rhs, :call) || continue
        type_expr = rhs.args[1]
        prop_name, index_params = if lhs isa Symbol
            (lhs, Symbol[])
        elseif Meta.isexpr(lhs, :call) && lhs.args[1] isa Symbol
            ips = Symbol[]
            for a in lhs.args[2:end]
                Meta.isexpr(a, :parameters) && continue   # kwargs
                bare = Meta.isexpr(a, :kw) ? a.args[1] : a
                push!(ips, Meta.isexpr(bare, :(::)) ? bare.args[1] : bare)
            end
            (lhs.args[1], ips)
        else
            continue
        end
        push!(result, (prop_name, type_expr, index_params))
    end
    result
end

# Extract type name from a struct expression.
function _struct_type_name(struct_expr)
    type_name = struct_expr.args[2]
    Meta.isexpr(type_name, :(<:)) && (type_name = type_name.args[1])
    Meta.isexpr(type_name, :(curly)) && (type_name = type_name.args[1])
    type_name
end

# Default: no inline struct properties.
_inline_struct_props(::Type) = ()

"""
    _child_struct_type(T, ::Val{name}) -> Type or nothing

The type whose `meta` declares the routes of the child mounted at `name`.

`@htmx` records what it knows from each `@include` declaration by emitting a
`_nested_struct_type` method — the hook DynamicObjects documents for exactly
this. Reading goes through `nested_object_type`, which combines that with
inline `@struct` children and DO's own `@include` externals and applies the
DynamicObject guard: `_nested_struct_type` alone misses externals declared
under a plain `@dynamicstruct`, and the unguarded analysis paths happily answer
`Int` for `port::Int = 8080`, which a route walker must never follow.

Falls back to the raw hook when DynamicObjects is too old to export the
accessor.
"""
function _child_struct_type(T, v::Val)
    if isdefined(DynamicObjects, :nested_object_type)
        name = typeof(v).parameters[1]
        return Base.invokelatest(
            getproperty(DynamicObjects, :nested_object_type), T, name)
    end
    _nested_struct_type(T, v)
end

# Map a route-macro symbol to the verb short symbol used in `Verb{V}` and
# `_http_verbs`. `Symbol("@get")` → `:GET`; `Symbol("@ws")` → `:WEBSOCKET`
# (the special case — the macro name `@ws` is shorter than the
# "WEBSOCKET" label `_register_handler` maps to a GET upgrade route).
function _verb_short(verb::Symbol)
    verb === Symbol("@ws") && return :WEBSOCKET
    s = String(verb)
    startswith(s, "@") ? Symbol(uppercase(s[2:end])) : verb
end

"""
    _convert_include_to_struct!(struct_expr)

Pre-process `@include` lines:

- `@include prop = begin...end` is rewritten to `prop = struct _Include_prop ... end`
  so DO's inline-struct machinery wires `__parent__=__self__` automatically.
- `@include prop = ExternalStruct(; ...)` keeps the external call but its kwargs
  are augmented with `__parent__=__self__, __mount_step__="/prop"` so the
  sub-struct receives the request/appdata chain (via `__parent__`) and the
  parent-relative URL step its `__prefix__` composes with the parent's
  (`_mounted_prefix`). User-provided values for these kwargs win; we only fill
  them in if absent.
"""
function _convert_include_to_struct!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    for (i, arg) in enumerate(body.args)
        arg isa Expr || continue
        # Walk through nested macrocall layers to find @include
        expr = arg
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && expr.args[1] != Symbol("@include")
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
        Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && expr.args[1] == Symbol("@include") || continue
        inner = expr.args[end]
        Meta.isexpr(inner, :(=)) || continue
        lhs = inner.args[1]
        rhs = inner.args[2]
        prop_name, index_params, lhs_idx_exprs = if lhs isa Symbol
            (lhs, Symbol[], Any[])
        elseif Meta.isexpr(lhs, :call) && lhs.args[1] isa Symbol
            ips = Symbol[]
            idx_exprs = Any[]
            for a in lhs.args[2:end]
                Meta.isexpr(a, :parameters) && continue
                push!(ips, Meta.isexpr(a, :(::)) ? a.args[1] : a)
                push!(idx_exprs, a)   # preserve type annotations
            end
            (lhs.args[1], ips, idx_exprs)
        else
            continue
        end
        # `@include mount(idx…) = Child(idx…)` is a short-form METHOD
        # definition, so the parser wraps its rhs in a `:block` — the same head
        # `= begin … end` produces. Head alone cannot tell the two apart; the
        # payload can. One statement (LineNumberNodes aside) means the parser
        # did the wrapping, so unwrap it and let the call form below handle it.
        # Several statements is a real body and stays an inline child.
        #
        # Without this, an indexed external mount was converted into an INLINE
        # child whose body is the call `Child(idx)`, which makes `Child` a
        # property of the generated wrapper rather than the type to mount — the
        # `Any`-typed field with no routes behind it. DynamicObjects had the
        # identical bug on its side of the same declaration.
        #
        # ONLY for an indexed mount: `@include name = begin … end` has a plain
        # Symbol LHS, was never wrapped by the parser, and is a real inline
        # sub-router — unwrapping a single-statement one would reinterpret it
        # as an external mount.
        #
        # And not even for every indexed mount: a body that DECLARES a route or
        # a nested `@include` is the author's however few statements it has, so
        # `_is_inline_router_body` overrides the count heuristic there.
        if !isempty(index_params) && !_is_inline_router_body(rhs)
            rhs = _unwrap_short_form_body(rhs)
            # Persist the classification into the AST DynamicObjects receives.
            # Mutating the unwrapped call below is not enough: without replacing
            # the parser's outer one-statement block, DO still sees an inline
            # sub-router and rejects it under its plain `@dynamicstruct` pass.
            inner.args[2] = rhs
        end
        if Meta.isexpr(rhs, :block)
            # Convert to: prop[(args…)] = struct _Include_prop ... end
            # __prefix__ extends the parent's prefix with the include name
            # plus any index_params interpolated at runtime — same shape as
            # what `_inject_include_prefix!` does for the call form.
            # `:index` is dropped from the URL segment (mirrors `_route_path`
            # and `_nested_prefix_and_step`), so an `@include index(x) = …`
            # inside `/skills` produces children at `/skills/{x}`, not
            # `/skills/index/{x}`. Each index value is one `_url_segment`, the
            # same wire identity and encoding as the call form.
            struct_name = Symbol("_Include_", prop_name)
            prefix_expr = prop_name === :index ?
                :(__parent__.__prefix__) :
                :(__parent__.__prefix__ * "/" * $(string(prop_name)))
            url_segment = GlobalRef(@__MODULE__, :_url_segment)
            for ip in index_params
                prefix_expr = :($prefix_expr * "/" * $url_segment($ip))
            end
            pushfirst!(rhs.args, :(__prefix__ = $prefix_expr))
            child_struct = Expr(:struct, false, struct_name, rhs)
            body.args[i] = isempty(index_params) ?
                :($prop_name = $child_struct) :
                Expr(:(=), Expr(:call, prop_name, lhs_idx_exprs...), child_struct)
        elseif Meta.isexpr(rhs, :call)
            # Only inject __prefix__ (HTMXO-specific). Leave @include in place
            # for DO to handle __parent__ and __status__ wiring. For indexed
            # includes, the runtime prefix interpolates the per-call arg values.
            _inject_include_prefix!(rhs, prop_name; index_params)
        end
    end
    struct_expr
end

"""
    _inject_verb_in_route_lhs!(struct_expr)

For every `@get`/`@post`/`@put`/`@patch`/`@delete`/`@ws`/`@sse` macrocall in the
struct body, rewrite the call-form LHS to inject
`__verb__::HTMXObjects.Verb{V}` as the **first positional argument**, where
`V` is the verb short symbol (`:GET`, `:POST`, `:WEBSOCKET`, `:SSE`, …). After this
rewrite, DynamicObjects' `@dynamicstruct` sees:

    @get foo(x::Int) = body          # source
    foo(__verb__::HTMXObjects.Verb{:GET}, x::Int) = body   # after rewrite (LHS)

and emits `compute_property(::T, ::Val{:foo}, __verb__::Verb{:GET}, x::Int) = body`.
The route registrar threads `Verb{V}()` into `prop(Verb{V}(), idx_vals…; kw…)`
at request-handling time, so verb dispatch is purely a method-table lookup.
The arg is named (`__verb__`) so route bodies can forward the verb to
sub-router routes — e.g. `__self__.pipeline.index(__verb__)` to delegate
from `@get index()` to an `@include`d sub-router's `@get index()`.

Bare-form LHS is impossible here — `_extract_route_info` already rejected it.
The injection is idempotent guarded: if the first positional arg already has
a `::Verb{…}` annotation (e.g. when re-running on a Revise re-eval),
this is a no-op.
"""
function _inject_verb_in_route_lhs!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa Expr || continue
        expr = arg
        verb = Symbol("")
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall)
            expr.args[1] in route_macros && (verb = expr.args[1])
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
        verb === Symbol("") && continue
        Meta.isexpr(expr, :(=)) || continue
        lhs = expr.args[1]
        lhs = _route_call_lhs(lhs)
        lhs === nothing && continue
        verb_short = _verb_short(verb)
        verb_type = Expr(:(::), :__verb__, Expr(:curly, GlobalRef(@__MODULE__, :Verb), QuoteNode(verb_short)))
        # Skip past `:parameters` if present (must come immediately after
        # the called name); insertion goes after both the name and any
        # leading `:parameters` block so positional args stay positional.
        insert_pos = 2
        if length(lhs.args) >= 2 && Meta.isexpr(lhs.args[2], :parameters)
            insert_pos = 3
        end
        # Idempotency: if a `::Verb{…}` is already at insert_pos, skip.
        if length(lhs.args) >= insert_pos
            existing = lhs.args[insert_pos]
            if Meta.isexpr(existing, :(::))
                ann = existing.args[end]
                if Meta.isexpr(ann, :curly) && length(ann.args) >= 1 &&
                   (ann.args[1] === :Verb || (ann.args[1] isa GlobalRef && ann.args[1].name === :Verb))
                    continue
                end
            end
        end
        insert!(lhs.args, insert_pos, verb_type)
    end
    struct_expr
end

"""
    _inject_include_prefix!(call_expr, prop_name; index_params=Symbol[])

Mutate the kwargs of `SomeStruct(args...; ...)` so that
`__mount_step__="/prop_name"[ * "/" * _url_segment(arg)…]` is present (without
overriding any user-provided value). For an indexed include
(`@include prop(x, y) = External(x, y)`), the `index_params` are interpolated
into the step at runtime so each invocation of the property gets the URL
segment for its current arg values — percent-encoded by `_url_segment`, so a
value containing `/`, `?`, `#` or `%` stays one segment that routes back to the
same child.

The recorded step is PARENT-RELATIVE; the child's injected `__prefix__` body
composes it with `__parent__.__prefix__` at access time (see `_mounted_prefix`).
Passing the composed absolute prefix here instead would not survive
`DynamicObjects.remount`, which masks a retained `__prefix__` and recomputes it
from the child's own body.

`__parent__` and `__status__` are handled by DynamicObjects' `@include`
processing.
"""
function _inject_include_prefix!(call_expr, prop_name; index_params::Vector{Symbol}=Symbol[])
    params_idx = findfirst(a -> Meta.isexpr(a, :parameters), call_expr.args)
    if params_idx === nothing
        params = Expr(:parameters)
        insert!(call_expr.args, 2, params)
    else
        params = call_expr.args[params_idx]
    end
    has_prefix = any(kw -> _kwarg_name(kw) in (:__prefix__, :__mount_step__),
                     params.args)
    if !has_prefix
        # `:index` drops the URL segment — mirrors `_route_path` and
        # `_nested_prefix_and_step`. So `@include index(x) = External(x)`
        # mounts children at `/parent/{x}`, not `/parent/index/{x}`.
        step_expr = prop_name === :index ? "" : "/" * string(prop_name)
        url_segment = GlobalRef(@__MODULE__, :_url_segment)
        for ip in index_params
            step_expr = :($step_expr * "/" * $url_segment($ip))
        end
        push!(params.args, Expr(:kw, :__mount_step__, step_expr))
    end
    call_expr
end

# Default: no @param properties declared.
_param_names(::Type) = ()

"""
    _convert_params!(struct_expr) -> Vector{Symbol}

Find `@param name::T = default` lines in a `@htmx` struct body and rewrite them
to plain derived-property assignments that call
`_extract_param(_req_of(__self__), ...)` at property-access time. A parameter
with a matching `@options` declaration instead calls `_extract_option_param`
with the bound option evaluator, so serialized machine values can resolve
against the live domain before scalar parsing. Supports:

    @param vessels::Vector{String} = ["Tablet-20"]
    @param fit_key::String                             # required, errors on miss
    @param note = "hi"                                 # untyped, with default
    @param begin
        vessels::Vector{String} = ["Tablet-20"]
        n_bootstrap::String     = "10"
    end
    @param (; fit_key, top_chains, max_draws) = __parent__   # delegate to parent

The delegation form registers each listed name as a param on this struct (so
`query_url(__self__)` serializes them) and resolves their value at access time
via `source.<name>` — typically `__parent__.<name>` — rather than extracting
from the request directly. See `_rewrite_param_delegation`.

A `@relevant(name) = condition` line declares when the `@param` `name` applies.
It becomes the property `__relevant_<name>__ = condition`, and the parameter
extracts its submitted value only while that property is `true`: otherwise it
is its default, whatever was submitted. A `@relevant` parameter therefore needs
a default.

Returns the ordered list of declared param names for later `_param_names`
emission, and the `@relevant` conditions by name for `_param_relevance`.
"""
function _convert_params!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    option_names = Set{Symbol}()
    relevance = Pair{Symbol,Any}[]
    for arg in body.args
        name = _option_parameter_name(arg)
        name === nothing || push!(option_names, name)
        declared = _relevance_declaration(arg)
        declared === nothing || push!(relevance, declared)
    end
    relevant_names = Set{Symbol}(first.(relevance))
    length(relevant_names) == length(relevance) || throw(ArgumentError(string(
        "@htmx: a `@param` has two `@relevant` declarations: ",
        join(unique(n for n in first.(relevance) if count(==(n), first.(relevance)) > 1), ", "))))
    names = Symbol[]
    new_args = Any[]
    process_line! = inner -> begin
        delegated = _rewrite_param_delegation(inner)
        if delegated !== nothing
            for (n, assign) in delegated
                n in relevant_names && throw(ArgumentError(string(
                    "@htmx: `@relevant($n)` names a `@param` that delegates to another ",
                    "struct; declare the condition where `$n` is extracted from the request")))
                push!(names, n)
                push!(new_args, assign)
            end
            return
        end
        rewritten = _rewrite_param_line(inner, option_names, relevant_names)
        rewritten === nothing && return
        push!(names, rewritten[1])
        push!(new_args, rewritten[2])
    end
    for arg in body.args
        declared = _relevance_declaration(arg)
        if declared !== nothing
            # The condition, as a property of its own: DynamicObjects reads its
            # dependencies, and a page renders the control as it evaluates.
            push!(new_args, Expr(:(=), _relevance_property(first(declared)), last(declared)))
            continue
        end
        if !(arg isa Expr)
            push!(new_args, arg); continue
        end
        # Peel a `Core.@doc "str" @param …` wrapper — Julia's parser wraps
        # `"doc"\n@param x = …` inside a struct body, making arg.args[1] a
        # GlobalRef(Core,:@doc) rather than Symbol("@param"), which the bare
        # check below would miss. Capture the wrapper so it can be re-attached
        # on the lowered property, letting DynamicObjects capture info.doc.
        doc_wrapper = nothing
        param_arg = arg
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall)
            mname = arg.args[1]
            if (mname isa GlobalRef ? mname.name : mname) === Symbol("@doc") &&
               length(arg.args) >= 4
                doc_wrapper = arg
                param_arg = arg.args[end]
            end
        end
        if Meta.isexpr(param_arg, :macrocall) && param_arg.args[1] === Symbol("@param")
            # Strip the LineNumberNode that follows the macro name
            payload = [a for a in param_arg.args[2:end] if !(a isa LineNumberNode)]
            # Block form: @param begin ... end → expand each line
            is_block = length(payload) == 1 && Meta.isexpr(payload[1], :block)
            if is_block
                # Block form: outer @doc wrapper is ambiguous across multiple params —
                # don't re-attach (each block-line param gets info.doc=nothing). Deferred.
                for inner in payload[1].args
                    inner isa LineNumberNode && (push!(new_args, inner); continue)
                    process_line!(inner)
                end
            else
                # Single-line form: @param vessels::T = default
                # Multiple payload elements are a tuple literal (comma-separated)
                n_before = length(new_args)
                for inner in payload
                    process_line!(inner)
                end
                # Single-param with outer @doc: re-attach the wrapper on the one
                # newly-pushed rewrite so DO captures the doc string onto info.doc.
                if !isnothing(doc_wrapper) && length(new_args) - n_before == 1
                    new_args[end] = Expr(:macrocall, doc_wrapper.args[1:end-1]..., new_args[end])
                end
            end
        else
            push!(new_args, arg)
        end
    end
    body.args = new_args
    for (name, _) in relevance
        name in names || throw(ArgumentError(string(
            "@htmx: `@relevant($name)` names no `@param` of this struct; ",
            "declare `@param $name = <default>` next to it")))
    end
    names, relevance
end

# `@relevant(name) = condition` and `@relevant name = condition`, as
# `name => condition`; `nothing` for any other line.
function _relevance_declaration(arg)
    if Meta.isexpr(arg, :(=), 2) && Meta.isexpr(arg.args[1], :macrocall)
        lhs = arg.args[1]
        mname = lhs.args[1]
        (mname isa GlobalRef ? mname.name : mname) === Symbol("@relevant") ||
            return nothing
        payload = Any[x for x in lhs.args[2:end] if !(x isa LineNumberNode)]
        length(payload) == 1 && only(payload) isa Symbol || throw(ArgumentError(
            "@htmx: write `@relevant(name) = condition` with the name of one `@param`"))
        return only(payload) => arg.args[2]
    end
    Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) || return nothing
    mname = arg.args[1]
    (mname isa GlobalRef ? mname.name : mname) === Symbol("@relevant") ||
        return nothing
    payload = Any[x for x in arg.args[2:end] if !(x isa LineNumberNode)]
    length(payload) == 1 && Meta.isexpr(only(payload), :(=), 2) &&
        only(payload).args[1] isa Symbol || throw(ArgumentError(
            "@htmx: write `@relevant(name) = condition` with the name of one `@param`"))
    only(payload).args[1] => only(payload).args[2]
end

_relevance_property(name::Symbol) = Symbol("__relevant_", name, "__")

# Default: no `@relevant` declarations.
_param_relevance(::Type) = (;)

# Read the parameter name from either supported declaration spelling before
# DynamicObjects lowers it:
#
#     @options node = catalogue
#     @options(node) = catalogue
function _option_parameter_name(arg)
    if Meta.isexpr(arg, :(=), 2) && Meta.isexpr(arg.args[1], :macrocall)
        lhs = arg.args[1]
        mname = lhs.args[1]
        (mname isa GlobalRef ? mname.name : mname) === Symbol("@options") ||
            return nothing
        payload = Any[x for x in lhs.args[2:end] if !(x isa LineNumberNode)]
        return length(payload) == 1 && only(payload) isa Symbol ? only(payload) : nothing
    end
    Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) || return nothing
    mname = arg.args[1]
    (mname isa GlobalRef ? mname.name : mname) === Symbol("@options") ||
        return nothing
    payload = Any[x for x in arg.args[2:end] if !(x isa LineNumberNode)]
    length(payload) == 1 && Meta.isexpr(only(payload), :(=), 2) || return nothing
    lhs = only(payload).args[1]
    lhs isa Symbol ? lhs : nothing
end

# Parse a delegation form `@param (; a, b, c) = source` — register a/b/c as
# params on this struct and resolve their values via `source.a` / `source.b`
# / `source.c` at property-access time. Typical use: `@param (; a, b) = __parent__`
# so the child both (a) serializes these keys through `query_url(__self__)` and
# (b) mirrors whatever value the parent resolved them to.
# Returns `Vector{Tuple{Symbol, Expr}}` or `nothing` if the expression isn't a
# delegation form.
# TODO: assert at macro-expand time that `source`'s type declares each name as
# an `@param`, so a parent-side rename fails loudly rather than silently
# shadowing the child's default extraction path.
function _rewrite_param_delegation(expr)
    Meta.isexpr(expr, :(=), 2) || return nothing
    lhs, source = expr.args
    Meta.isexpr(lhs, :tuple) && length(lhs.args) == 1 || return nothing
    params = lhs.args[1]
    Meta.isexpr(params, :parameters) || return nothing
    out = Tuple{Symbol, Expr}[]
    for n in params.args
        name_sym = _typed_param_name(n)
        name_sym === nothing && return nothing
        push!(out, (name_sym, Expr(:(=), name_sym, Expr(:., source, QuoteNode(name_sym)))))
    end
    out
end

# Parse a single `name::T = default` / `name = default` / `name::T` / `name` form
# and return `(name_sym, rewritten_assignment)` or `nothing` if unrecognized.
function _rewrite_param_line(expr, option_names=Set{Symbol}(), relevant_names=Set{Symbol}())
    default_expr = nothing
    lhs = expr
    if Meta.isexpr(expr, :(=))
        lhs = expr.args[1]
        default_expr = expr.args[2]
    end
    name_sym, type_expr = _split_param_lhs(lhs)
    name_sym === nothing && return nothing
    t = type_expr === nothing ? :nothing : type_expr
    req_expr = :($(_req_of)(__self__))
    relevant = name_sym in relevant_names
    relevant && default_expr === nothing && throw(ArgumentError(string(
        "@htmx: `@param $name_sym` has a `@relevant` condition and no default; give it ",
        "the default it takes where it does not apply (`@param $name_sym::T = default`)")))
    # A `@relevant` parameter's extractor takes its condition first; the
    # extractor stays the outermost call, which `_reflect_local_param` reads.
    extractor, args = if name_sym in option_names
        relevant ? (_extract_relevant_option_param,
                    Any[_relevance_property(name_sym), :(__self__.__options__), req_expr]) :
                   (_extract_option_param, Any[:(__self__.__options__), req_expr])
    else
        relevant ? (_extract_relevant_param, Any[_relevance_property(name_sym), req_expr]) :
                   (_extract_param, Any[req_expr])
    end
    append!(args, (QuoteNode(name_sym), t))
    default_expr === nothing || push!(args, default_expr)
    rhs = Expr(:call, extractor, args...)
    (name_sym, Expr(:(=), name_sym, rhs))
end

# Parse a single `name::T = default` / `name = default` / `name::T` / `name` form
# and return `(name_sym, rewritten_assignment)` for @header, or `nothing` if unrecognized.
# Produces `name = _extract_header(_req_of(__self__), :name, T, default)`.
function _rewrite_header_line(expr)
    default_expr = nothing
    lhs = expr
    if Meta.isexpr(expr, :(=))
        lhs = expr.args[1]
        default_expr = expr.args[2]
    end
    name_sym, type_expr = _split_param_lhs(lhs)
    name_sym === nothing && return nothing
    t = type_expr === nothing ? :nothing : type_expr
    req_expr = :($(_req_of)(__self__))
    rhs = default_expr === nothing ?
        :($(_extract_header)($req_expr, $(QuoteNode(name_sym)), $t)) :
        :($(_extract_header)($req_expr, $(QuoteNode(name_sym)), $t, $default_expr))
    (name_sym, Expr(:(=), name_sym, rhs))
end

"""
    _convert_headers!(struct_expr) -> Vector{Symbol}

Find `@header name::T = default` lines in a `@htmx` struct body and rewrite them
to plain derived-property assignments that call
`_extract_header(_req_of(__self__), ...)` at property-access time. The header key
is derived from the kwarg name by replacing `_` with `-` (so `x_kb_agent_id`
binds HTTP header `X-KB-Agent-ID`; HTTP.jl header lookup is case-insensitive).
Supports single-line and block forms identical to `@param`.

Returns the ordered list of declared header names for threading to child structs.
"""
function _convert_headers!(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    names = Symbol[]
    new_args = Any[]
    process_line! = inner -> begin
        rewritten = _rewrite_header_line(inner)
        rewritten === nothing && return
        push!(names, rewritten[1])
        push!(new_args, rewritten[2])
    end
    for arg in body.args
        if !(arg isa Expr)
            push!(new_args, arg); continue
        end
        if Meta.isexpr(arg, :macrocall) && arg.args[1] === Symbol("@header")
            payload = [a for a in arg.args[2:end] if !(a isa LineNumberNode)]
            if length(payload) == 1 && Meta.isexpr(payload[1], :block)
                for inner in payload[1].args
                    inner isa LineNumberNode && (push!(new_args, inner); continue)
                    process_line!(inner)
                end
            else
                for inner in payload
                    process_line!(inner)
                end
            end
        else
            push!(new_args, arg)
        end
    end
    body.args = new_args
    names
end

function _htmx_transform(struct_expr; reroute=true, parent_params=Symbol[], parent_headers=Symbol[], is_child=false, kwargs...)
    # Capture externals BEFORE _convert_include_to_struct! strips the @include
    # wrapper from `@include prop = ExternalStruct(...)` lines (the begin-block
    # form turns into an inline struct, but the call form is left as a bare
    # assignment, so _find_include_externals would no longer recognize it).
    include_externals = _find_include_externals(struct_expr)
    route_info = _extract_route_info(struct_expr)

    # With `Verb{V}` injected as the first positional argument of every route
    # IP, same-name routes on different verbs and same-name `@include` members
    # are all distinguished by method dispatch — no internal-name mangling.
    # We only reject exact same-`(name, verb)` duplicates, which would emit
    # identical `compute_property(::T, ::Val{name}, ::Verb{V}, …)` methods
    # that overwrite each other.
    let seen = Set{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}}(), dups = Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}[]
        for ri in route_info
            key = (ri.prop_name, ri.verb)
            key in seen && push!(dups, key)
            push!(seen, key)
        end
        isempty(dups) || error("""
            @htmx struct $(_struct_type_name(struct_expr)): duplicate route definitions $(unique(dups)) — same property name AND same verb. Each (name, verb) pair must be unique because both produce identical `compute_property(::T, ::Val{name}, ::Verb{V}, …)` methods.

            Don't write:

                @get foo() = list_view()
                @get foo() = other_view()    # ← rejected (same verb)

            Either rename one, or merge into a single route with a positional/kwarg switch:

                @get foo(slug::String="") = isempty(slug) ? list_view() : item_view(slug)
            """)
    end

    # Inject `::HTMXObjects.Verb{V}` as the first positional arg of every
    # route LHS so DO emits a verb-keyed `compute_property` method.
    _inject_verb_in_route_lhs!(struct_expr)

    _convert_include_to_struct!(struct_expr)

    _wrap_stream_bodies!(struct_expr)
    _warn_legacy_page_name!(struct_expr)
    reroute && _warn_redundant_req_decl!(struct_expr)
    reroute && _warn_hardcoded_url_in_attrs!(struct_expr)
    reroute && _inject_dunder_props!(struct_expr)
    own_params, own_relevance = _convert_params!(struct_expr)
    own_headers = _convert_headers!(struct_expr)
    # Merge: parent params/headers first (preserve order), then own, deduplicated.
    param_names = Symbol[]
    for n in parent_params
        n in param_names || push!(param_names, n)
    end
    for n in own_params
        n in param_names || push!(param_names, n)
    end
    header_names = Symbol[]
    for n in parent_headers
        n in header_names || push!(header_names, n)
    end
    for n in own_headers
        n in header_names || push!(header_names, n)
    end
    inline_props = _find_inline_structs(struct_expr)

    block = DynamicObjects.dynamicstruct(struct_expr;
        child_handler=s -> _htmx_transform(s; reroute=false, parent_params=param_names, parent_headers=header_names, is_child=true),
        is_child, kwargs...)
    type_name = _struct_type_name(struct_expr)
    @assert Meta.isexpr(block, :escape)
    # Emit one `_extract_args(::Type{T}, ::Val{name}, ::Verb{V}, …)` method
    # per route entry. Keying on `Verb{V}` lets multiple routes share the
    # same property name (different verbs) without colliding.
    for ri in route_info
        push!(block.args[1].args, _generate_extract_args(type_name, ri.prop_name, ri.verb, ri.pos_params, ri.kw_params))
    end
    # Emit the child-type methods for @include externals.
    # (Inline `@struct` children are emitted by DO's macro itself, on the same
    # generic, so HTMXO only needs to add the @include-external methods here.)
    #
    # The INDEXED form goes on HTMXObjects' own `_include_child_type`, NOT on
    # `_nested_struct_type`: DynamicObjects already defines the latter for an
    # indexed mount, returning its memoizing wrapper. Defining ours on the same
    # signature would overwrite DO's — which is both a precompilation error
    # ("method overwriting is not permitted") and wrong, since the wrapper is
    # what actually constructs and caches the child per index. We need a second
    # answer, not a replacement: the wrapper for construction, the child type
    # for walking routes.
    _type_fname = Expr(:., DynamicObjects, QuoteNode(:_nested_struct_type))
    for (prop, type_expr, _index_params) in include_externals
        push!(block.args[1].args, Expr(:(=),
            Expr(:call, _type_fname, :(::Type{$type_name}), :(::Val{$(QuoteNode(prop))})),
            type_expr))
    end
    # Emit _param_names method if this struct declared any @param properties
    if !isempty(param_names)
        _pn_fname = Expr(:., @__MODULE__, QuoteNode(:_param_names))
        push!(block.args[1].args, Expr(:(=),
            Expr(:call, _pn_fname, :(::Type{$type_name})),
            Tuple(param_names)))
    end
    # Emit `_param_relevance`: each `@relevant` condition as written, which a
    # generated form evaluates in the browser where it can.
    if !isempty(own_relevance)
        _pr_fname = Expr(:., @__MODULE__, QuoteNode(:_param_relevance))
        push!(block.args[1].args, Expr(:(=),
            Expr(:call, _pr_fname, :(::Type{$type_name})),
            Expr(:tuple, Expr(:parameters,
                (Expr(:kw, name, QuoteNode(condition)) for (name, condition) in own_relevance)...))))
    end
    # Emit _inline_struct_props for backward compat
    all_nested = Symbol[p for (p, _) in inline_props]
    if !isempty(all_nested)
        _fname = Expr(:., @__MODULE__, QuoteNode(:_inline_struct_props))
        push!(block.args[1].args, Expr(:(=),
            Expr(:call, _fname, :(::Type{$type_name})),
            Tuple(all_nested)))
    end
    # Emit `Base.:/(::T, ::AbstractString)` so route bodies can write
    # `__self__/"plots"` and get the fully-qualified URL relative to this
    # struct's mount point. `__prefix__` is threaded through construction:
    # the root receives it from `route!`, and `@include` desugar passes
    # `__prefix__=__self__.__prefix__ * "/childname"` to each nested sub-struct.
    push!(block.args[1].args, :(
        Base.:/(self::$type_name, p::AbstractString) =
            self.__prefix__ * "/" * lstrip(p, '/')
    ))
    _qurl_fname = Expr(:., @__MODULE__, QuoteNode(:query_url))
    push!(block.args[1].args, :(
        $(_qurl_fname)(self::$type_name; overrides...) =
            $(_qurl_fname)(self.__route__, self; overrides...)
    ))
    # Emit `Base.print(io, ::T)` so route bodies can write
    # `href=__self__` (the struct's index URL — `__prefix__` for non-root
    # mounts, `"/"` for the root). HTMX stringifies attribute values via
    # `print(io, val)`, so this is what makes `href=__self__` resolve to a
    # bare URL rather than the struct's default Julia repr.
    push!(block.args[1].args, :(
        Base.print(io::IO, self::$type_name) =
            print(io, isempty(self.__prefix__) ? "/" : self.__prefix__)
    ))
    # Wrap _reroute!(T) in Base.invokelatest so it always dispatches at the
    # latest world. Without this, on Revise re-eval the call site captures the
    # world age before the freshly-emitted `meta(::Type{T})` /
    # `_nested_struct_type(::Type{T}, …)` methods are visible, producing
    # `MethodError: meta(::Type{T}) (method too new …)` from `_register_routes`.
    reroute && push!(block.args[1].args, :($Base.invokelatest($(_reroute!), $type_name)))
    block
end

_route_macros() = Set([Symbol("@get"), Symbol("@post"), Symbol("@put"), Symbol("@patch"), Symbol("@delete"), Symbol("@ws"), Symbol("@sse")])

# Extract route property info from the struct body AST at macro expansion time.
# Returns [(prop_name::Symbol, verb::Symbol, positional_params, kwargs_params), ...]
# where verb is the route macro symbol (e.g. Symbol("@get")), and
#   positional_params = [(name::Symbol, type_expr_or_nothing), ...]
#   kwargs_params     = [(name::Symbol, type_expr_or_nothing, has_default::Bool), ...]
function _extract_route_info(struct_expr)
    body = struct_expr.args[3]
    route_macros = _route_macros()
    routes = @NamedTuple{prop_name::Symbol, verb::Symbol, pos_params::Vector, kw_params::Vector}[]
    lnn = nothing
    for arg in body.args
        arg isa LineNumberNode && (lnn = arg; continue)
        arg isa Expr || continue
        # Walk macrocall layers to find route markers
        expr = arg
        has_route = false
        verb = Symbol("")
        while Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall)
            if expr.args[1] in route_macros
                has_route = true
                verb = expr.args[1]
            end
            # macrocall args[2] is often a LineNumberNode
            expr.args[2] isa LineNumberNode && (lnn = expr.args[2])
            expr = expr.args[end]
        end
        has_route || continue
        # expr is the inner assignment: name(...) = rhs
        Meta.isexpr(expr, :(=)) || continue
        lhs = expr.args[1]
        pos_params = Tuple{Symbol, Any}[]
        kw_params = Tuple{Symbol, Any, Bool}[]
        call_lhs = _route_call_lhs(lhs)
        if call_lhs !== nothing
            prop_name = call_lhs.args[1]
            Meta.isexpr(prop_name, :(::)) && (prop_name = prop_name.args[1])
            for idx in call_lhs.args[2:end]
                if Meta.isexpr(idx, :parameters)
                    for kw_arg in idx.args
                        if Meta.isexpr(kw_arg, :kw)
                            kw_name_expr = kw_arg.args[1]
                            kw_type = _macro_extract_type(kw_arg)
                            kw_name = first(DynamicObjects.extractnames(kw_name_expr))
                            push!(kw_params, (kw_name, kw_type, true))
                        else
                            kw_type = _macro_extract_type(kw_arg)
                            kw_name = first(DynamicObjects.extractnames(kw_arg))
                            push!(kw_params, (kw_name, kw_type, false))
                        end
                    end
                else
                    p_type = _macro_extract_type(idx)
                    p_name = first(DynamicObjects.extractnames(idx))
                    push!(pos_params, (p_name, p_type))
                end
            end
        else
            # Bare-form route LHS (`@get name = body`) is rejected at
            # macro-expansion time. Every route must be a call form
            # (`@get name() = body`) so DO can register it as an indexable
            # property dispatching on the `::Verb{V}` first positional arg.
            # Without that, two routes for the same name (e.g. `@get foo`
            # and `@post foo`) would emit identical
            # `compute_property(::T, ::Val{:foo})` methods and shadow each
            # other; and a route + a same-named non-verb IP could not coexist.
            bare_name = lhs isa Symbol ? lhs :
                        (Meta.isexpr(lhs, :(::)) && lhs.args[1] isa Symbol ? lhs.args[1] : :unknown)
            error("""
                @htmx struct $(_struct_type_name(struct_expr)): bare-form route `$(verb) $(bare_name) = …` is not allowed. Every route must be a call form so it can be registered as an indexable property dispatching on `::Verb{V}` as the first positional argument.

                Don't write:

                    $(verb) $(bare_name) = body

                Write:

                    $(verb) $(bare_name)() = body

                The empty parentheses make `$(bare_name)` an indexable property; the route registrar then invokes it as `prop(Verb{:$(_verb_short(verb))}())` so multiple HTTP verbs and non-verb IPs can share the name `:$(bare_name)` without collision.
                """)
        end
        push!(routes, (prop_name=prop_name, verb=verb, pos_params=pos_params, kw_params=kw_params))
    end
    routes
end

# Extract type annotation from a parameter AST node (macro-time version of _extract_type).
function _macro_extract_type(idx)
    expr = Meta.isexpr(idx, :kw) ? idx.args[1] : idx
    Meta.isexpr(expr, :(::)) && length(expr.args) == 2 ? expr.args[2] : nothing
end

# Generate an `_extract_args(::Type{T}, ::Val{name}, ::Verb{V}, …)` method
# for one route. Keying on `Verb{V}` (instead of the legacy `__method__`
# string arg) lets multiple routes with the same property name but different
# verbs each get their own typed parameter parser.
#
# `verb` is the route macro symbol (e.g. `Symbol("@get")`); we map it to the
# verb short symbol (`:GET`) for the `::Verb{V}` annotation.
function _generate_extract_args(type_name, prop_name, verb, pos_params, kw_params)
    verb_short = _verb_short(verb)

    # Build positional param conversion statements (by URL segment position).
    # Segments arrive percent-encoded; decode each one ONCE here, before
    # `_convert_param` — never inside it, which also serves already-decoded
    # query/form values (snag `htmxo-path-param-79355e13`).
    pos_stmts = Expr[]
    for (i, (pname, ptype)) in enumerate(pos_params)
        segment_expr = :($(_percent_decode_lenient)(__parts__[__base_segments__ + $i]))
        convert_call = ptype === nothing ?
            :($(_convert_param)($segment_expr, nothing)) :
            :($(_convert_submitted)($segment_expr, $ptype, $(QuoteNode(pname))))
        push!(pos_stmts, :($i <= __n_params__ && push!(__idx__, $convert_call)))
    end

    # Build kwarg conversion statements — delegate per-key lookup to `_lookup_param`
    # so @param, @get, @post, etc. share one parsing path.
    kw_stmts = Expr[]
    for (kname, ktype, has_default) in kw_params
        type_expr = ktype === nothing ? :nothing : ktype
        lookup_call = :($(_lookup_param)(__src__, __fallback__, $(QuoteNode(kname)), $type_expr))
        if has_default
            push!(kw_stmts, quote
                let __v__ = $lookup_call
                    __v__ === $(_NO_DEFAULT) || push!(__kw__, $(QuoteNode(kname)) => __v__)
                end
            end)
        else
            push!(kw_stmts, quote
                let __v__ = $lookup_call
                    __v__ === $(_NO_DEFAULT) && throw($(MissingRequiredParam)($(QuoteNode(kname))))
                    push!(__kw__, $(QuoteNode(kname)) => __v__)
                end
            end)
        end
    end

    # Source selection is fixed at codegen time: GET/DELETE/WEBSOCKET/SSE pull
    # from queryparams, the rest from bodyparams (with queryparams fallback).
    # bodyparams routes by Content-Type: urlencoded → formparams (multi-value
    # preserved, repeated `image=a&image=b` → `Vector{String}`), multipart →
    # multipartparams (file fields → `Upload`, text fields → `String`).
    _is_query_verb = verb_short in (:GET, :DELETE, :WEBSOCKET, :SSE)

    # …and the sources are computed ONLY when the route declares kwargs to bind.
    # A route with no kwargs (`@post stalecheck()`) has nothing to look them up
    # for, so reading and parsing the request body for it is pure waste — and,
    # before this, waste that could FAIL: the parse ran ahead of the handler, so
    # a caller's raw payload could 500 a route that never asked for one body
    # field, and the app had no way to opt out by changing what it declared
    # (snag `bodyparams-form-525c2ab8`). Positional params are read from
    # `__parts__` (URL segments) and never touch `__src__`/`__fallback__`, so
    # omitting these assignments is unobservable for every other route shape.
    _src_expr = if isempty(kw_stmts)
        quote end
    elseif _is_query_verb
        quote
            __src__ = $(queryparams)(__req__)
            __fallback__ = nothing
        end
    else
        quote
            __src__ = $(bodyparams)(__req__)
            __fallback__ = $(queryparams)(__req__)
        end
    end

    _M = @__MODULE__
    _fname = Expr(:., _M, QuoteNode(:_extract_args))
    _verb_ann = Expr(:(::), Expr(:curly, GlobalRef(_M, :Verb), QuoteNode(verb_short)))
    _call = Expr(:call, _fname,
        :(::Type{$type_name}), :(::Val{$(QuoteNode(prop_name))}), _verb_ann,
        :__req__, :(__base_segments__::Int), :(__n_params__::Int))
    _body = quote
        __parts__ = split(split(__req__.target, "?")[1], "/", keepempty=false)
        $_src_expr
        __idx__ = Any[]
        $(pos_stmts...)
        __kw__ = Pair{Symbol,Any}[]
        $(kw_stmts...)
        (__idx__, __kw__)
    end
    Expr(:(=), _call, _body)
end

# Dispatch target for generated route arg extraction methods.
function _extract_args end


"""
    @htmx struct MyApp ... end

Wraps `@dynamicstruct` and appends a `_reroute!` call so that Revise-triggered
re-evaluation automatically re-registers routes without a server restart.
`@ws` property bodies are automatically wrapped in `(__ws__) -> body`, and `@sse`
bodies in `(__sse__) -> body` (see [`SSEStream`](@ref)). Both accept the same
path-param + kwargs surface as GET — `@ws feed(id::Int; q="") = body` mounts at
`/feed/{id}` and the handler receives the typed `id` (same `_convert_param` path as
GET) plus `q` from the upgrade request's query string.
Inline `prop = struct ... end` definitions are processed as nested route structs.
"""
macro htmx(args...)
    isempty(args) && error("@htmx: missing struct definition.")
    kwargs = Dict{Symbol,Any}(DynamicObjects._parse_macro_opt(a) for a in args[1:end-1])
    _htmx_transform(args[end]; kwargs...)
end

"""
    HTMLDocument(root::Node)

A complete HTML document: the `<html>` element `root` plus the `<!DOCTYPE html>`
preamble that must precede it in the serialized bytes. This is what [`htmx`](@ref)
returns — and therefore what [`pico_page`](@ref) and every `__page__` built on
them return.

A doctype is not an element, so it cannot live inside the `HTMX.Node` tree; it is
emitted by this type's `show(::IO, ::MIME"text/html", …)` method, immediately
ahead of `root`. Serializing a `HTMLDocument` (via `repr("text/html", …)`, the
response pipeline, or as a child of another node) therefore always yields a
standards-mode document.

Without the doctype a browser renders the page in **quirks mode**, which is not
only a legacy box-model detail — libraries refuse to run in it. KaTeX's
`katex.render(tex, el)` throws `ParseError: KaTeX doesn't work in quirks mode`
outright, and `throwOnError: false` does *not* downgrade that (the option covers
parse errors; the quirks-mode check is a precondition).

`root` stays a plain `Node`, so the recording pipeline
([`static_transform`](@ref)) walks the document exactly as it walked the bare
`<html>` node before.

Every other projection reads `root` too, wherever the document sits (returned
from a route or nested in a node): a `?plain` / `?markdown` /
`Accept: text/markdown` read is the Markdown of the `<html>` tree, and `?error`
keeps only its error-tagged nodes, still behind the doctype.
"""
struct HTMLDocument
    root::Node
end

Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", doc::HTMLDocument) =
    (print(io, "<!DOCTYPE html>\n"); show(io, m, doc.root); nothing)
# The doctype carries no content, so the Markdown and error projections are the
# root's. Without these, HTMX's catch-all Markdown `show` prints the struct's
# repr, and `filter_errors` passes the whole page through as its errors.
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/markdown", doc::HTMLDocument) = show(io, m, doc.root)
filter_errors(doc::HTMLDocument) = let root = filter_errors(doc.root)
    isnothing(root) ? nothing : HTMLDocument(root)
end
_filter_errors_child(doc::HTMLDocument) = filter_errors(doc)
_strip_md_chrome(doc::HTMLDocument) = _strip_md_chrome(doc.root)
_strip_md_chrome_child(doc::HTMLDocument) = _strip_md_chrome(doc)
_rescue_semantic!(out, doc::HTMLDocument) = _rescue_semantic!(out, doc.root)

# --- Vendored head assets ----------------------------------------------------
#
# `htmx(...; assets=:vendor)` serves the page shell's JS/CSS from same-origin
# `/vendor/...` routes instead of the CDN, so a standalone app runs fully
# offline (air-gapped). The bytes live in the exact npm release tarballs
# pinned in Artifacts.toml — byte-identical to the CDN files at the pinned
# versions — and download lazily on the first `vendorfiles()` call.
#
# The version constants single-source both surfaces: they are the `htmx()`
# CDN defaults AND the vendored pins. Bumping one means bumping the matching
# Artifacts.toml entry in the same commit, after re-verifying byte-identity
# against the CDN URL with `cmp`.
const _HTMX_VERSION = "2.0.8"
const _HTMX_SSE_VERSION = "2.2.4"
const _HTMX_WS_VERSION = "2.0.4"
const _HTMX_PRELOAD_VERSION = "2.1.2"
const _HYPERSCRIPT_VERSION = "0.9.14"
# Vendor pin only: the CDN surface stays `nothing` (`htmx()`) / floating
# `"2"` (`pico_page`), so vendor mode serves pico solely at this exact pin.
const _PICO_VERSION = "2.1.1"
# KaTeX typesets the math `render_markdown` marks (`math_script`). The shell
# only names where it lives; the runtime fetches it when a page shows math.
const _KATEX_VERSION = "0.19.0"
# PrismJS highlights code (`syntax_script`). As with KaTeX, the shell only
# names where it lives; the runtime fetches it when a page shows code.
const _PRISM_VERSION = "1.30.0"
const _PRISM_CORE = "components/prism-core.min.js"
const _PRISM_AUTOLOADER = "plugins/autoloader/prism-autoloader.min.js"

# pkg => (path inside its artifact, served filename, pinned version).
# KaTeX is a directory: its stylesheet finds its fonts relative to itself, so
# it is served as `katex/…` with the `fonts/` beside it (`_vendor_files`).
# PrismJS is one too: its autoloader fetches each language's grammar from the
# `components/` directory beside its own `plugins/`, so it is served as
# `prism/…` in the npm package's layout.
# The artifact itself resolves through `_vendor_artifact` below — the
# `artifact"..."` macro needs a literal, so one method per asset.
const _VENDOR_ASSETS = (
    htmx=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist", "htmx.min.js"),
        file="htmx.min.js", version=_HTMX_VERSION),
    sse=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist", "sse.min.js"),
        file="sse.min.js", version=_HTMX_SSE_VERSION),
    ws=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist", "ws.min.js"),
        file="ws.min.js", version=_HTMX_WS_VERSION),
    preload=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist", "preload.min.js"),
        file="preload.min.js", version=_HTMX_PRELOAD_VERSION),
    hyperscript=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist", "_hyperscript.min.js"),
        file="_hyperscript.min.js", version=_HYPERSCRIPT_VERSION),
    pico=(relpath=joinpath("package", "css", "pico.min.css"),
        file="pico.min.css", version=_PICO_VERSION),
    katex=(relpath=joinpath("package", "dist"),
        file="katex", version=_KATEX_VERSION),
    prism=(relpath="package", file="prism", version=_PRISM_VERSION),
)

_vendor_artifact(::Val{:htmx}) = artifact"htmx"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:sse}) = artifact"htmx-ext-sse"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:ws}) = artifact"htmx-ext-ws"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:preload}) = artifact"htmx-ext-preload"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:hyperscript}) = artifact"hyperscript"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:pico}) = artifact"pico"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:katex}) = artifact"katex"
_vendor_artifact(::Val{:prism}) = artifact"prismjs"

# Single validation point for the `assets` mode, so an unknown mode fails
# even when every version kwarg is `nothing` and no URL is built.
function _check_assets_mode(assets)
    assets === :cdn || assets === :vendor || assets isa AbstractString ||
        error("htmx: unknown assets mode $(repr(assets)) — use :cdn, :vendor, or a mount-path string.")
    return nothing
end

# The same-origin mount a vendor `assets` mode serves from.
_vendor_prefix(assets) = assets === :vendor ? "/vendor" : rstrip(assets, '/')

# One head-asset URL for the requested `assets` mode. `:cdn` keeps the
# passed-through CDN URL untouched; `:vendor` (or a custom mount-prefix
# string) serves the pinned file same-origin. A non-pinned version under
# vendor mode is a loud error, never a silent CDN fallback. `versioned`
# (a linked runtime) appends the pin, so the browser may cache the file
# for good.
function _head_asset_src(pkg::Symbol, version, cdn_src, assets; versioned::Bool=false)
    assets === :cdn && return cdn_src
    _check_assets_mode(assets)
    spec = _VENDOR_ASSETS[pkg]
    version == spec.version ||
        error("htmx(...; assets=:vendor): vendored $pkg is pinned at $(spec.version), got $version — pass the pinned version or stay on assets=:cdn.")
    src = _vendor_prefix(assets) * "/" * spec.file
    return versioned ? src * "?v=" * spec.version : src
end

# The head node for one asset: pico is a stylesheet, every other vendored
# library a script. Shared by the `htmx()` shell and `vendor_head`.
_head_asset_node(::Val{:pico}, src) = h.link(rel="stylesheet", href=src)
_head_asset_node(::Val, src) = h.script(src=src)
# A head asset's address and its `?query` (`""` without one).
function _split_query(src)
    i = findfirst('?', src)
    isnothing(i) ? (src, "") : (src[1:prevind(src, i)], src[i:end])
end
# KaTeX is not loaded by the head: `src` names its directory, and the
# template holds the two files `math_script` adds once the page shows math.
# Template content is inert, so a page without math fetches nothing, and a
# static export rewrites its `href`/`src` like any other address. A pinned
# `?v=` (a linked shell) rides on each file, not on the directory.
function _head_asset_node(::Val{:katex}, src)
    dir, query = _split_query(src)
    h.template(h.link(rel="stylesheet", href=dir * "/katex.min.css" * query),
        h.script(src=dir * "/katex.min.js" * query); id="htmxo-katex")
end
# PrismJS likewise: the template names its core and the autoloader, which
# `syntax_script` adds in that order once the page shows code; the autoloader
# then fetches each language's grammar from `components/` beside `plugins/`.
function _head_asset_node(::Val{:prism}, src)
    dir, query = _split_query(src)
    h.template(h.script(src=dir * "/" * _PRISM_CORE * query),
        h.script(src=dir * "/" * _PRISM_AUTOLOADER * query); id="htmxo-prism")
end

# htmx extensions register themselves against a loaded htmx, so they need
# htmx loaded before them.
const _VENDOR_EXTENSIONS = (:sse, :ws, :preload)

# The requested vendored packages, validated and put in load order (the
# `_VENDOR_ASSETS` order: htmx before its extensions). An unknown name is a
# loud error, never a skipped file.
function _vendor_packages(packages)
    requested = Set{Symbol}(Symbol(p) for p in packages)
    unknown = setdiff(requested, keys(_VENDOR_ASSETS))
    isempty(unknown) || throw(ArgumentError(
        "unknown vendored package(s) " * join(repr.(sort!(collect(unknown))), ", ") *
        "; known: " * join(repr.(keys(_VENDOR_ASSETS)), ", ")))
    Symbol[pkg for pkg in keys(_VENDOR_ASSETS) if pkg in requested]
end

"""
    vendorfiles(mountdir="vendor"; headers=[])

Serve the vendored [`htmx`](@ref) head assets — the exact pins behind
`assets=:vendor` — at `/<mountdir>/<file>`, next to [`staticfiles`](@ref):

```julia
vendorfiles()                                  # → GET /vendor/htmx.min.js, …
__page__(content) = htmx(content; assets=:vendor)
```

KaTeX, which typesets Markdown math ([`render_markdown`](@ref)), is served
as a directory, `/<mountdir>/katex/katex.min.css`, `…/katex.min.js` and the
`…/katex/fonts/` its stylesheet loads. PrismJS, which highlights code
([`syntax_script`](@ref)), is a directory too, in its npm layout:
`/<mountdir>/prism/components/prism-core.min.js`,
`…/prism/plugins/autoloader/prism-autoloader.min.js` and every language's
grammar, `…/prism/components/prism-<language>.min.js`.

The first call downloads the pinned npm tarballs from Artifacts.toml (about
5.2 MB, cached in the depot); further calls re-read from disk. Files
are served with [`staticfiles`](@ref)-style content types. A custom
`mountdir` (or a second mount elsewhere) pairs with the matching
`assets="/<mountdir>"` prefix on [`htmx`](@ref)/[`pico_page`](@ref).

The same mount also serves HTMXObjects' own page runtime at
`/<mountdir>/htmxo/<name>.css|js`, which `htmx(...; runtime=:linked)` links
instead of inlining it. Every response carries an `ETag`; a request whose
`?v=` names the current version (the URLs a linked shell emits) is cacheable
for a year as `immutable`, any other revalidates (`no-cache`). A
`Cache-Control` in `headers` wins over both.
"""
function vendorfiles(mountdir::AbstractString="vendor"; headers::Vector=[])
    prefix = strip(mountdir, '/')
    route(file) = isempty(prefix) ? "/$file" : "/$prefix/$file"
    for pkg in keys(_VENDOR_ASSETS)
        version = _VENDOR_ASSETS[pkg].version
        for (file, path) in _vendor_files(pkg)
            body = read(path)
            handler = req -> _vendor_file_response(req, path, body, version, headers)
            _register_get_route!(route(file), handler)
        end
    end
    # The runtime is rendered per request, so a Revise edit or a Treebars
    # extension loaded after this call is served as the shell links it.
    for name in keys(_runtime_blocks())
        file = _runtime_file(name)
        handler = req -> let text = _runtime_text(name)
            _vendor_file_response(req, file, text, _runtime_version(text), headers)
        end
        _register_get_route!(route("htmxo/" * file), handler)
    end
    return nothing
end

# One vendor-mount file. The version doubles as the ETag; `?v=<version>`
# marks the URL as content-addressed, so the browser keeps it without
# revalidating until the shell links a new version.
function _vendor_file_response(req, path, body, version, headers)
    etag = "\"$version\""
    response = _etag_matches(HTTP.header(req, "If-None-Match", ""), etag) ?
        HTTP.Response(304, headers; body=UInt8[]) : _file_response(path, body, headers)
    HTTP.setheader(response, "ETag" => etag)
    HTTP.hasheader(response, "Cache-Control") ||
        HTTP.setheader(response, "Cache-Control" =>
            _requested_version(req) == version ?
                "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" : "no-cache")
    return response
end

_requested_version(req) = get(HTTP.queryparams(HTTP.URI(req.target)), "v", nothing)

_etag_matches(if_none_match, etag) =
    any(t -> t == "*" || t == etag || t == "W/" * etag,
        strip.(split(if_none_match, ',')))

# The pinned file inside its (lazily downloaded) artifact.
_vendor_file(pkg::Symbol) = joinpath(_vendor_artifact(Val(pkg)), _VENDOR_ASSETS[pkg].relpath)

# Every file a package serves, as `served path => file`, in a stable order:
# one for a library, KaTeX's stylesheet, script and fonts for `:katex`, and
# PrismJS's core, autoloader and every language grammar for `:prism`.
_vendor_files(pkg::Symbol) = _vendor_files(Val(pkg))
_vendor_files(::Val{P}) where {P} = [_VENDOR_ASSETS[P].file => _vendor_file(P)]
function _vendor_files(::Val{:katex})
    dir, served = _vendor_file(:katex), _VENDOR_ASSETS[:katex].file
    fonts = sort!(readdir(joinpath(dir, "fonts")))
    [[served * "/" * f => joinpath(dir, f) for f in ("katex.min.css", "katex.min.js")];
     [served * "/fonts/" * f => joinpath(dir, "fonts", f) for f in fonts]]
end
function _vendor_files(::Val{:prism})
    dir, served = _vendor_file(:prism), _VENDOR_ASSETS[:prism].file
    [[served * "/" * f => joinpath(dir, split(f, '/')...) for f in (_PRISM_CORE, _PRISM_AUTOLOADER)];
     [served * "/components/" * f => joinpath(dir, "components", f) for f in _prism_languages(dir)]]
end
# The language grammars the autoloader can fetch: every minified component
# but the core, which the template loads itself.
_prism_languages(dir=_vendor_file(:prism)) = sort!(filter(
    f -> startswith(f, "prism-") && endswith(f, ".min.js") && f != basename(_PRISM_CORE),
    readdir(joinpath(dir, "components"))))

"""
    copy_vendorfiles(dir; packages=(:htmx, :sse, :ws, :preload, :hyperscript, :pico, :katex, :prism), runtime=true) -> Vector{String}

Copy the vendored head assets — the same files [`vendorfiles`](@ref)
serves — into `dir` (created if missing), for a static bundle that has no
routes to serve them from. `packages` selects which pinned libraries to copy;
`:katex` is the directory `dir/katex/` (stylesheet, script and fonts) that
typesets Markdown math on pages whose `assets` prefix names `dir`, and
`:prism` the directory `dir/prism/` (core, autoloader and every language's
grammar) that highlights their code.
With `runtime=true` (the default) HTMXObjects' own page runtime is copied
too, to `dir/htmxo/<name>.css|js` — the files a `runtime=:linked` page links
(see [`htmx`](@ref)), including the Treebars poll assets while that
extension is loaded — so `StaticExport(; runtime=:linked)` pages can link one
shared copy instead of carrying the runtime inline. The written paths come
back with the libraries first, in load order. Existing files are overwritten,
and the copies are ordinary writable files (the artifact store is
read-only). Like `vendorfiles`, the first call downloads the pinned npm
tarballs.

Pair it with [`vendor_head`](@ref) (or `htmx(...; assets=<same relative
prefix>)`) for head tags that address the copies relative to the page:

```julia
copy_vendorfiles(joinpath(bundle, "assets", "vendor"); packages=(:htmx, :pico))
page = HTMLDocument(h.html(
    h.head(h.meta(charset="utf-8"), vendor_head("assets/vendor"; packages=(:htmx, :pico))...),
    h.body(content)))
```
"""
function copy_vendorfiles(dir::AbstractString; packages=keys(_VENDOR_ASSETS), runtime::Bool=true)
    pkgs = _vendor_packages(packages)
    mkpath(dir)
    libraries = String[]
    for pkg in pkgs, (file, path) in _vendor_files(pkg)
        dest = joinpath(dir, split(file, '/')...)
        mkpath(dirname(dest))
        write(dest, read(path))
        push!(libraries, dest)
    end
    runtime || return libraries
    mkpath(joinpath(dir, "htmxo"))
    [libraries; [let dest = joinpath(dir, "htmxo", _runtime_file(name))
                     write(dest, _runtime_text(name))
                     dest
                 end for name in _available_runtime_names()]]
end

"""
    vendor_head(base="vendor"; packages=(:htmx,)) -> Tuple

Head nodes loading the vendored libraries `packages` from `base`: a
`<script src=…>` per library and a stylesheet `<link>` for `:pico`, in load
order (htmx before its extensions). `base` is used as given, so a relative
prefix such as `"assets/vendor"` addresses files [`copy_vendorfiles`](@ref)
wrote next to the page, and a rooted one (`"/vendor"`) matches a
[`vendorfiles`](@ref) mount. Splat the result into a hand-built `h.head(…)`.

An extension (`:sse`, `:ws`, `:preload`) without `:htmx` is an
`ArgumentError`. The tags load the libraries only: `:preload` still needs
`hx-ext="preload"` on an ancestor and [`preload_runtime_js`](@ref), which the
[`htmx`](@ref) shell adds for you. `:katex` loads nothing by itself: it is the
inert `<template id="htmxo-katex">` naming `<base>/katex/`, from which
[`math_script`](@ref) loads KaTeX once the page shows math, so a hand-built
head that wants typeset math adds `math_script()` and `math_styles()` too.
`:prism` is likewise the inert `<template id="htmxo-prism">` naming
`<base>/prism/`, from which [`syntax_script`](@ref) loads PrismJS once the
page shows code; a hand-built head adds `syntax_script()` and
`syntax_styles()` with it.
"""
function vendor_head(base::AbstractString="vendor"; packages=(:htmx,))
    pkgs = _vendor_packages(packages)
    extensions = filter(in(_VENDOR_EXTENSIONS), pkgs)
    isempty(extensions) || :htmx in pkgs || throw(ArgumentError(
        "vendor_head: htmx extension(s) " * join(repr.(extensions), ", ") *
        " need :htmx in `packages` — an extension must load after htmx"))
    prefix = rstrip(base, '/')
    Tuple(_head_asset_node(Val(pkg),
              (isempty(prefix) ? "" : prefix * "/") * _VENDOR_ASSETS[pkg].file)
          for pkg in pkgs)
end

# --- Page runtime: inline, or linked from the vendor mount ---
#
# The `htmx()` shell carries HTMXObjects' own CSS/JS runtime, plus the
# Treebars poll assets while that extension is loaded. Each block has a
# stable name and a producer returning ONE attribute-free `<style>`/`<script>`
# of `Raw` text, so the same bytes ship either inline (the default) or as the
# file `vendorfiles` serves at `<mount>/htmxo/<name>.<ext>`. The shell decides
# which blocks a page carries; this table only names them. Producers resolve
# at call time, so the file may name functions defined further down.
_runtime_blocks() = (
    preload_runtime = (:js, preload_runtime_js),
    theme = (:css, htmxo_theme),
    pico_bridge = (:css, pico_bridge),
    request_feedback_style = (:css, request_feedback_style),
    request_feedback_script = (:js, request_feedback_script),
    compose_box_styles = (:css, compose_box_styles),
    compose_box_script = (:js, compose_box_script),
    toast_styles = (:css, toast_styles),
    toast_script = (:js, toast_script),
    live_thread_styles = (:css, live_thread_styles),
    live_thread_script = (:js, live_thread_script),
    utility_styles = (:css, htmxo_utility_styles),
    tabset_styles = (:css, tabset_styles),
    tabset_script = (:js, tabset_script),
    comparison_styles = (:css, comparison_styles),
    editor_styles = (:css, editor_styles),
    comparison = (:js, comparison_js),
    stream_probe = (:js, stream_probe_script),
    redirect_history = (:js, redirect_history_script),
    modifier_click = (:js, modifier_click_script),
    live_region = (:js, live_region_script),
    treebars_styles = (:css, () -> _polling_page_asset(1)),
    treebars_script = (:js, () -> _polling_page_asset(2)),
    live_refresh = (:js, live_refresh_script),
    auto_terminal = (:js, auto_terminal_script),
    mutation_poll = (:js, mutation_poll_script),
    operation_stream = (:js, operation_stream_script),
    sent_ahead = (:js, sent_ahead_script),
    semantic_refresh = (:js, semantic_refresh_script),
    semantic_follow = (:js, semantic_follow_script),
    show_when = (:js, show_when_script),
    choice_list = (:js, choice_list_script),
    url_params = (:js, url_params_script),
    url_anchor = (:js, url_anchor_script),
    glossary = (:js, glossary_script),
    math_styles = (:css, math_styles),
    math_script = (:js, math_script),
    syntax_styles = (:css, syntax_styles),
    syntax_script = (:js, syntax_script),
    # The table runtime. `htmx(...; tables=true)` carries it together with
    # `sortable_table_styles`, `caption_style` and `download_table` below.
    table_runtime = (:js, _table_runtime_js),
    # Page helpers an app may place itself, usually in `extra_head`. The
    # shell leaves out a copy whose code it already carries and links the
    # rest (`_shell_extra_head`).
    sortable_table = (:js, sortable_table_js),
    sortable_table_styles = (:css, sortable_table_styles),
    caption_style = (:css, caption_style),
    master_detail = (:js, master_detail_js),
    download_table = (:js, download_table_js),
    gallery_styles = (:css, htmxo_gallery_styles),
)

# The blocks whose text exists in this process: the Treebars poll assets only
# while that extension is loaded.
_available_runtime_names() = isempty(_polling_page_assets()) ?
    filter(name -> !(name in (:treebars_styles, :treebars_script)), keys(_runtime_blocks())) :
    keys(_runtime_blocks())

_runtime_block(name::Symbol) = last(getfield(_runtime_blocks(), name))()
_runtime_file(name::Symbol) = string(name, '.', first(getfield(_runtime_blocks(), name)))

_runtime_tag(::Val{:css}) = :style
_runtime_tag(::Val{:js}) = :script

# The block's text — exactly what the inline element holds. Any other shape
# would render differently inline than linked, so it is refused loudly.
function _runtime_text(name::Symbol)
    node = _runtime_block(name)
    expected = _runtime_tag(Val(first(getfield(_runtime_blocks(), name))))
    node isa Node && HTMX.tag(node) === expected && isempty(HTMX.attrs(node)) &&
        all(child -> child isa Raw, HTMX.children(node)) || throw(ArgumentError(
            "page runtime block $(repr(name)) must be an attribute-free <$expected> of Raw text; got $(repr(MIME"text/html"(), node))"))
    join(String(child) for child in HTMX.children(node))
end

# Content-addressed: any change to a block's text changes its URL.
_runtime_version(text::AbstractString) = string(hash(text); base=16)

# One block as the shell carries it: the element itself (inline), or a link
# to its versioned file on the vendor mount. The marker lets a static export
# put the element back (`_static_walk`), so exported pages stay standalone.
_runtime_head(name::Symbol, ::Nothing) = _runtime_block(name)
function _runtime_head(name::Symbol, mount::AbstractString)
    src = string(mount, "/htmxo/", _runtime_file(name), "?v=", _runtime_version(_runtime_text(name)))
    _runtime_link(Val(first(getfield(_runtime_blocks(), name))), src, name)
end
_runtime_link(::Val{:css}, src, name) = h.link(rel="stylesheet", href=src, data_htmxo_runtime=string(name))
_runtime_link(::Val{:js}, src, name) = h.script(src=src, data_htmxo_runtime=string(name))

_runtime_heads(mount, names...) = map(name -> _runtime_head(name, mount), names)

# The named runtime block an element is exactly — an attribute-free
# `<style>`/`<script>` whose `Raw` text equals that block's — or `nothing`.
_runtime_block_name(node, texts) = nothing
function _runtime_block_name(node::Node, texts)
    HTMX.tag(node) in (:style, :script) && isempty(HTMX.attrs(node)) &&
        all(child -> child isa Raw, HTMX.children(node)) || return nothing
    get(texts, (HTMX.tag(node), join(String(child) for child in HTMX.children(node))), nothing)
end

# The shell blocks whose code a runtime block consists of: the block itself,
# or for a page helper that bundles shell code, those blocks.
_runtime_parts(name::Symbol) = _runtime_parts(Val(name))
_runtime_parts(::Val{name}) where {name} = (name,)
_runtime_parts(::Val{:sortable_table}) = (:table_runtime, :comparison)
_runtime_parts(::Val{:master_detail}) = (:comparison,)

# `extra_head` as the shell carries it. An element that is exactly a named
# runtime block — an app's own `sortable_table_js()`, say — is left out when
# the shell already carries its code (`carried`, the shell's block names);
# otherwise, linked, it becomes its link in the same place, like the shell's
# blocks. Anything else stays as given.
function _shell_extra_head(extra_head, mount, carried)
    texts = nothing
    filter(!isnothing, map(extra_head) do node
        node isa Node && HTMX.tag(node) in (:style, :script) || return node
        # Built once, and only for a head that has a candidate element.
        isnothing(texts) && (texts = Dict(
            (_runtime_tag(Val(first(getfield(_runtime_blocks(), name)))), _runtime_text(name)) => name
            for name in _available_runtime_names()))
        name = _runtime_block_name(node, texts)
        isnothing(name) ? node :
            all(in(carried), _runtime_parts(name)) ? nothing :
            isnothing(mount) ? node : _runtime_head(name, mount)
    end)
end

# `runtime` → the mount the shell links its runtime from, or `nothing` to
# inline it. Linking needs a same-origin mount, which `assets` names.
_runtime_mount(runtime, assets) = _runtime_mount(Val(runtime), assets)
_runtime_mount(::Val{:inline}, assets) = nothing
function _runtime_mount(::Val{:linked}, assets)
    assets === :cdn && error("htmx(...; runtime=:linked) serves the page runtime from the vendor mount, which `assets` names — pass assets=:vendor or a mount path (paired with vendorfiles), or keep runtime=:inline.")
    _vendor_prefix(assets)
end
_runtime_mount(::Val{R}, assets) where {R} =
    error("htmx: unknown runtime mode $(repr(R)) — use :inline or :linked.")

"""
    htmx(body...; htmx_version="2.0.8", sse_version="2.2.4", ws_version="2.0.4", hyperscript_version="0.9.14", preload_version="2.1.2", pico_version=nothing, katex_version="0.19.0", prism_version="1.30.0", assets=:cdn, runtime=:inline, feedback=true, thread=true, tables=true, extra_head=(), error_texts=nothing, lang=nothing, title=nothing)

Generate a full HTML page with HTMX and optionally Hyperscript/PicoCSS loaded from CDN.
Pass `nothing` to any version kwarg to skip that library.
`lang` is the page's language as a BCP 47 tag (`"de"`, `"de-CH"`, `"en-GB"`),
written as `<html lang>`: screen readers pronounce the page in it and CSS
`hyphens: auto` hyphenates by it. `title` is the page's `<title>`. Left at
`nothing`, each takes the `__lang__` / `__title__` that a struct on the
route's mount chain declares (the innermost declaration wins), so a subtree
can give its own pages a language and title the root's shell does not know;
with neither, the page has no `lang` attribute and no `<title>`. A string that
is not a language tag, or a title that is not a non-empty string, throws an
`ArgumentError`, and so does a title alongside an `h.title` in `extra_head` (a
page has one `<title>`).
`sse_version` is htmx's SSE extension, which [`sse_region`](@ref) needs; it
loads only alongside the shell's own htmx (an extension must follow htmx).
`ws_version` is htmx's WebSocket extension, which `@ws` routes need on the
client (a `ws-connect` element); it loads only alongside the shell's own
htmx for the same reason. The shell always carries [`stream_probe_script`](@ref),
so a ws or sse stream refused by an auth gateway reloads the page on the
gateway's `HX-Refresh` instead of reconnecting forever. It also carries
[`modifier_click_script`](@ref): a Ctrl/Cmd- or Shift-click on an htmx link
with a real `href` opens that address in a new tab or window, as on any link.
Set `feedback=false` to disable automatic request feedback (pulsating borders, success/error flash).
Set `thread=false` to leave out the [`live_thread`](@ref) runtime.

The shell carries the table runtime, so a table from [`render_table`](@ref),
[`sortable_table`](@ref) or [`master_detail_table`](@ref) works with nothing
added to the page: sorting, search, row selection, tree rows with Expand all /
Collapse all, regrouping, hover linking, the "⬇ CSV" button and the caption
layout. These are [`sortable_table_js`](@ref) (whose master/detail half the
shell carries anyway), [`sortable_table_styles`](@ref),
[`download_table_js`](@ref) and [`caption_style`](@ref). Set `tables=false` to
leave them out of pages without tables.

`extra_head` follows the shell's own head. An element in it that is exactly
one of these page helpers, or [`master_detail_js`](@ref), as the function
returns it, is left out while the shell carries its code, so a page that
still lists them does not ship them twice. A changed or attributed copy stays.
`error_texts`, an [`ErrorTexts`](@ref) such as `error_texts(:de)`, is the
language of the failed-request toasts ([`toast_script`](@ref)) and of the
status line over a part that a running request will replace
([`request_feedback_script`](@ref)); pass the root's `__error_texts__` from
`__page__` so they match the error articles.
`nothing` (the default) keeps them English.
`katex_version` is the KaTeX release that typesets Markdown math
([`render_markdown`](@ref) marks it, [`math_script`](@ref) renders it). The
shell never loads KaTeX itself: it carries an inert template naming KaTeX's
stylesheet and script, which the runtime adds the first time the page shows
math, on load or after a swap, so a page without math fetches nothing. Pass
`nothing` to leave the template out; math then shows as its TeX source, unless
the page loads KaTeX itself (`window.katex`), which the runtime then uses.
`prism_version` is the PrismJS release that highlights code: every
`<code class="language-…">` on the page, such as a [`SemanticCode`](@ref) or a
Markdown code fence that names its language, in the page itself and in every
fragment htmx swaps in ([`syntax_script`](@ref)). The shell carries an inert
template naming PrismJS's core and its autoloader, which the runtime adds the
first time the page shows such code; the autoloader then fetches the grammar
of each language the page shows, so a page without code fetches nothing.
Pass `nothing` to leave the template out; code then stays plain, unless the
page loads PrismJS itself (`window.Prism`), which the runtime then uses.
Pass `overlay=true` to load the KB-app overlay bar (`<KB_ORIGIN>/overlay/bar.js`);
it defaults to `false` — the bundle is served only by the KB app, so standalone
pages omit the fetch.

`preload_version` loads htmx's [`preload` extension](https://htmx.org/extensions/preload/),
enables it page-wide and adds [`preload_runtime_js`](@ref). It is inert until an
element carries a `preload` attribute — see the `preload` keyword of
[`nav_sidebar`](@ref), [`htmx_tabset`](@ref), [`tabset`](@ref),
[`htmxo_breadcrumb`](@ref) and [`hx_link`](@ref) — and speculative requests do
work only on routes marked `@preload`.

`assets=:vendor` serves the same files same-origin from `/vendor/...`
(byte-identical to the CDN files at the pinned versions) instead of the CDN,
so the page loads fully offline — mount them with [`vendorfiles`](@ref) and
pass each library at its pinned version (a non-pinned version errors loudly).
A string mounts elsewhere: `assets="/static/vendor"` pairs with
`vendorfiles("static/vendor")`. The string is used as given, so a relative
prefix (`assets="assets/vendor"`) addresses the files relative to the page —
for a static bundle whose copies [`copy_vendorfiles`](@ref) wrote.

`runtime=:linked` moves HTMXObjects' own page runtime — the CSS and JS the
shell otherwise inlines into every page (request feedback, live threads,
tabs, comparison/master-detail, tables, stream probe, live regions, and the
Treebars poll assets while that extension is loaded) — out of the page into files on
the same vendor mount, e.g. `<mount>/htmxo/live_region.js?v=<hash>`. The
`?v=` is the content version, and the vendored library URLs gain their pins
the same way, so a browser keeps the whole head for good and a changed
runtime is a new URL. It needs a vendor mount — `assets=:vendor` or a mount
path, served by [`vendorfiles`](@ref) — and errors under `assets=:cdn`. Each
block keeps its place and its own element, so load order and script
isolation are unchanged. The page helpers an app adds through `extra_head` —
[`sortable_table_js`](@ref), [`sortable_table_styles`](@ref),
[`caption_style`](@ref), [`master_detail_js`](@ref),
[`download_table_js`](@ref) and [`htmxo_gallery_styles`](@ref), exactly as
they return — are linked the same way, in place, unless the shell already
carries them; any other `extra_head` element stays as given. A static export
or recording ([`static_transform`](@ref)) puts the runtime back inline, so
exported pages stay standalone; `StaticExport(; runtime=:linked)` keeps the
links instead, for a bundle that carries the files once
([`copy_vendorfiles`](@ref)). Behind a
path-stripping proxy, pass the request mount with the prefix:
`assets=__prefix__ * "/vendor"` in a root `__page__`. The default
`runtime=:inline` leaves the page byte-identical.

Returns an [`HTMLDocument`](@ref) — the `<html>` element together with the
`<!DOCTYPE html>` preamble, so the page renders in standards mode.
"""
function htmx(args...;
    head = h.head,
    body = h.body,
    htmx_version        = _HTMX_VERSION,
    sse_version         = _HTMX_SSE_VERSION,
    ws_version          = _HTMX_WS_VERSION,
    hyperscript_version = _HYPERSCRIPT_VERSION,
    preload_version     = _HTMX_PRELOAD_VERSION,
    pico_version        = nothing,
    katex_version       = _KATEX_VERSION,
    prism_version       = _PRISM_VERSION,
    assets              = :cdn,
    runtime             = :inline,
    feedback             = true,
    compose              = true,
    thread               = true,
    tables               = true,
    # Opt-in: the bar bundle is a KB-app-only route (`KB_ORIGIN/overlay/bar.js`),
    # so standalone pages must not fetch it by default.
    overlay              = false,
    extra_head          = (),
    treebars_assets     = true,
    error_texts         = nothing,
    lang                = nothing,
    title               = nothing,
)
    _check_assets_mode(assets)
    declared = _page_meta()
    isnothing(lang) && (lang = declared.lang)
    isnothing(title) && (title = declared.title)
    titles = _title_nodes(title)
    isempty(titles) || (any(_is_title_node, extra_head) && throw(ArgumentError(string(
        "htmx: the page has a title (", repr(title), ", from `title=` or a `__title__` ",
        "declaration) and `extra_head` carries an `h.title` as well; a page has one ",
        "<title>. Drop the `h.title` from `extra_head` and set the app's default ",
        "title as `__title__` on the root struct, or pass it as `title=`."))))
    mount = _runtime_mount(runtime, assets)
    versioned = !isnothing(mount)
    cdn = []
    isnothing(htmx_version)        || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:htmx), _head_asset_src(:htmx, htmx_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx.org@$(htmx_version)/dist/htmx.min.js", assets; versioned)))
    isnothing(htmx_version) || isnothing(sse_version) || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:sse), _head_asset_src(:sse, sse_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx-ext-sse@$(sse_version)/dist/sse.min.js", assets; versioned)))
    isnothing(htmx_version) || isnothing(ws_version) || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:ws), _head_asset_src(:ws, ws_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx-ext-ws@$(ws_version)/dist/ws.min.js", assets; versioned)))
    # The extension registers itself on load, so it must follow htmx.
    preload = !isnothing(htmx_version) && !isnothing(preload_version)
    preload && push!(cdn,
        _head_asset_node(Val(:preload), _head_asset_src(:preload, preload_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx-ext-preload@$(preload_version)/dist/preload.min.js", assets; versioned)),
        _runtime_head(:preload_runtime, mount))
    isnothing(hyperscript_version) || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:hyperscript), _head_asset_src(:hyperscript, hyperscript_version, "https://unpkg.com/hyperscript.org@$(hyperscript_version)", assets; versioned)))
    isnothing(pico_version)        || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:pico), _head_asset_src(:pico, pico_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@picocss/pico@$(pico_version)/css/pico.min.css", assets; versioned)))
    # jsdelivr, not cdnjs: cdnjs was measured serving KaTeX 0.16.21's fonts
    # one byte short, which browsers reject as invalid font data.
    isnothing(katex_version)       || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:katex), _head_asset_src(:katex, katex_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@$(katex_version)/dist", assets; versioned)))
    isnothing(prism_version)       || push!(cdn, _head_asset_node(Val(:prism), _head_asset_src(:prism, prism_version, "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@$(prism_version)", assets; versioned)))
    # `hx-ext` on `<html>` rather than `<body>`: htmx collects extensions from
    # every ancestor, and a caller-supplied `body` keeps its own `hx-ext`.
    html_attrs = (; _lang_attrs(lang)..., (preload ? (; hx_ext="preload") : (;))...,
        _error_texts_attrs(error_texts)...)
    html = isempty(html_attrs) ? h.html : h.html(; html_attrs...)
    # The runtime blocks the shell carries, before and after the overlay
    # bootstrap.
    shell_blocks = (
        # Theme defaults: declared at zero specificity inside `@layer htmxo`
        # so any subsequent `:root { --htmxo-...: ... }` (host-supplied or
        # via `pico_bridge` / `vitepress_bridge`) wins automatically.
        :theme,
        (isnothing(pico_version) ? () : (:pico_bridge,))...,
        (feedback ? (:request_feedback_style, :request_feedback_script) : ())...,
        (compose ? (:compose_box_styles, :compose_box_script) : ())...,
        (thread ? (:live_thread_styles, :live_thread_script) : ())...)
    page_blocks = (:utility_styles, :tabset_styles,
        # A tabset's open tab follows the page URL; inert without one.
        :tabset_script,
        :comparison_styles, :editor_styles,
        # Route-named success toasts and `window.htmxoToast`; inert
        # until a response or a script raises one.
        :toast_styles, :toast_script,
        # Comparison panes and master/detail rows share the lazy runtime.
        :comparison,
        # A refused stream hides its HTTP status: this probe lets an auth
        # gateway's `HX-Refresh` reload the page instead of the stream
        # retrying forever. Inert until a stream fails.
        :stream_probe,
        # A followed redirect records its destination where an
        # `hx-push-url` names the requested page; inert otherwise.
        :redirect_history,
        # A Ctrl/Cmd- or Shift-click on an htmx link opens its `href`
        # in a new tab or window, as on any link; inert otherwise.
        :modifier_click,
        # Discover-mode live regions keep one reconnecting stream behind
        # this runtime; inert on pages without one.
        :live_region,
        # Semantic shared context groups follow their live declared
        # domains; inert on pages without a refreshable group.
        :semantic_refresh,
        # A `presentation=:tabs` page's results follow their inputs;
        # inert on pages without one.
        :semantic_follow,
        # Widgets built with `show_when=` and the controls of `@relevant`
        # inputs show only while they apply; inert on pages without one.
        :show_when,
        # A `choice_list` (a generated choice among many options) opens,
        # searches and chooses; inert on pages without one.
        :choice_list,
        # `url_params` elements follow the page address and write
        # their values into it; inert on pages without one.
        :url_params,
        # The address's `#id` reaches an element an htmx swap inserts
        # after the page loaded; inert on an address without one.
        :url_anchor,
        # A glossary term's definition opens beside it, kept on screen
        # and outside scrolling tables; inert on pages without terms.
        :glossary,
        # Markdown math (`render_markdown`) is typeset by KaTeX, which
        # this runtime loads from the `katex_version` template the
        # first time the page shows math; inert on pages without any.
        :math_styles, :math_script,
        # Code that names its language is highlighted by PrismJS, which
        # this runtime loads from the `prism_version` template the first
        # time the page shows such code; inert on pages without any.
        :syntax_styles, :syntax_script,
        # The tables' sort, search, selection, tree, regroup, linking and
        # CSV controls with their styles and captions; inert on pages
        # without a table.
        (tables ? (:table_runtime, :sortable_table_styles, :caption_style, :download_table) : ())...,
        # Poller quietness by default: the Treebars stylesheet + script
        # ride every shell while the extension is loaded (no-op without
        # Treebars), ahead of `extra_head` so apps can still override.
        # A manual install alongside stays harmless but redundant.
        _operation_page_blocks(treebars_assets)...)
    carried = ((preload ? (:preload_runtime,) : ())..., shell_blocks..., page_blocks...)
    HTMLDocument(html(
        head(
            h.meta(charset="utf-8"),
            h.meta(name="viewport", content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"),
            h.meta(name="color-scheme", content="light dark"),
            titles...,
            cdn...,
            _runtime_heads(mount, shell_blocks...)...,
            # Thin-hook bootstrap: load the relocated overlay bundle from
            # `KB_ORIGIN/overlay/bar.js` instead of inlining `<style>`+`<script>`.
            # The bar is `position:fixed`, so a deferred async load has no
            # layout-shift to guard (no skeleton/placeholder needed).
            (overlay ? (h.script(""; src = KB_ORIGIN * "/overlay/bar.js", defer = true),) : ())...,
            _runtime_heads(mount, page_blocks...)...,
            _shell_extra_head(extra_head, mount, carried)...,
        ),
        body(args...),
    ))
end

# The `<html>` attribute that carries the page's language. A BCP 47 tag is a
# two- or three-letter ISO 639 language plus optional subtags of letters and
# digits (script, region, variant), so a language name such as "German" is
# refused; a page without one carries no attribute, so its output is unchanged.
const _HTML_LANG = r"^[A-Za-z]{2,3}(-[A-Za-z0-9]{1,8})*$"
_lang_attrs(::Nothing) = (;)
_lang_attrs(lang::AbstractString) = occursin(_HTML_LANG, lang) ? (; lang=String(lang)) :
    throw(ArgumentError(string("htmx: lang must be a BCP 47 language tag such as \"de\" or ",
        "\"de-CH\", or nothing for no lang attribute; got ", repr(lang))))
_lang_attrs(lang) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    "htmx: lang must be a BCP 47 language tag string such as \"de\", or nothing; got ", repr(lang))))

# The page's `<title>`: none without one, so an untitled page is unchanged.
_title_nodes(::Nothing) = ()
_title_nodes(title::AbstractString) = isempty(title) ?
    throw(ArgumentError("htmx: title must be a non-empty string, or nothing for no <title>; got \"\"")) :
    (h.title(String(title)),)
_title_nodes(title) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    "htmx: title must be a non-empty string, or nothing for no <title>; got ", repr(title))))
_is_title_node(node) = node isa Node && HTMX.tag(node) === :title

# The page's language and title as the route's mount chain declares them:
# `__lang__` / `__title__` on any struct from the root to the leaf, the
# innermost declaration winning, so a subtree mounted under an English root
# can make its own pages German. The shell reads them as its `lang` / `title`
# defaults. A dynamic scope rather than keywords, because the shell is a plain
# function that the root's `__page__` calls, and neither sees the leaf; the
# response handler sets it around the whole answer, error page included.
# `__progress_pause__` rides along for Treebars, which reads it while it
# renders a poller or a live board (`page_progress_pause`). `route` and
# `prefix` are the request's own address and mount prefix, which `pico_page`
# reads to mark the nav entry of the page being read.
const _NO_PAGE_META = (; lang=nothing, title=nothing, progress_pause=nothing, glossary=nothing,
    route=nothing, prefix=nothing)
const _PAGE_META = ScopedValues.ScopedValue{NamedTuple}(_NO_PAGE_META)

_page_meta() = _PAGE_META[]
_with_page_meta(f, meta) = ScopedValues.with(f, _PAGE_META => meta)

"""
    HTMXObjects.page_lang() -> Union{String,Nothing}

The language of the answer being produced: the BCP 47 tag that the route's
mount chain declares with `__lang__` (the innermost declaration wins), or the
one the answer is asked for in. `dispatch(…; lang)` wins over the declaration;
[`to_markdown_string`](@ref)`(x; lang)` and a [`pdf_report`](@ref) of a value
set it while they render. It is `nothing` when none of them sets one, as
outside a route.

It holds while the route's property runs and while its answer is rendered (a
poller, the page shell, an error page), and in the work the route runs on other
tasks: an `:auto` or `@queued` operation's body and a task the route spawns. So
code that writes words of its own can write them in the page's language.
Treebars' progress trees and the `:auto` pollers do, through `treebar_texts`.
"""
page_lang() = _page_meta().lang

"""
    HTMXObjects.page_progress_pause() -> Union{Bool,Nothing}

Whether the progress displays of the answer being produced offer a pause
control: the `__progress_pause__` that the route's mount chain declares (the
innermost declaration wins), or `nothing` when it declares none, as outside a
route. `false` asks for no pause control; `true` asks for one.

A visitor-facing app declares `__progress_pause__ = false` once on its root
struct. Pausing a poller only freezes what the page shows while the work runs
on, which a visitor reads as a stuck page. Treebars' `:auto` pollers and live
boards read the value and leave out their ❚❚ / Pause control; `nothing` shows
it, as before.

It holds where [`HTMXObjects.page_lang`](@ref) holds: while the route's
property runs and while its answer is rendered (an `:auto` poller and its poll
answers, the page shell, an error page), and in the work the route runs on
other tasks.
"""
page_progress_pause() = _page_meta().progress_pause

# Read and checked before the route body runs, so a bad declaration is the
# route's error, named after its struct, rather than a failure inside the shell.
function _page_meta_of(objects)
    lang = title = progress_pause = glossary = nothing
    for obj in objects
        declared = _page_declaration(obj, :__lang__)
        isnothing(declared) || (lang = _page_lang_value(obj, declared))
        declared = _page_declaration(obj, :__title__)
        isnothing(declared) || (title = _page_title_value(obj, declared))
        declared = _page_declaration(obj, :__progress_pause__)
        isnothing(declared) || (progress_pause = _page_progress_pause_value(obj, declared))
        declared = _page_declaration(obj, :__glossary__)
        isnothing(declared) || (glossary = _page_glossary_value(obj, declared))
    end
    (; lang, title, progress_pause, glossary)
end

_page_declaration(obj, name) = hasproperty(obj, name) ? getproperty(obj, name) : nothing

_page_lang_value(obj, lang::AbstractString) = occursin(_HTML_LANG, lang) ? String(lang) :
    _page_declaration_error(obj, :__lang__, "a BCP 47 language tag such as \"de\" or \"de-CH\"", lang)
_page_lang_value(obj, lang) =
    _page_declaration_error(obj, :__lang__, "a BCP 47 language tag string such as \"de\"", lang)
_page_title_value(obj, title::AbstractString) = isempty(title) ?
    _page_declaration_error(obj, :__title__, "a non-empty string", title) : String(title)
_page_title_value(obj, title) = _page_declaration_error(obj, :__title__, "a non-empty string", title)
_page_progress_pause_value(obj, pause::Bool) = pause
_page_progress_pause_value(obj, pause) = _page_declaration_error(obj, :__progress_pause__, "true or false", pause)

_page_declaration_error(obj, name, wanted, got) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    "`", name, "` on ", nameof(typeof(obj)), " must be ", wanted,
    ", or nothing to keep the enclosing page's; got ", repr(got))))

# A language a caller asks for what it renders (`to_markdown_string`,
# `dispatch`), in place of the answer's own: a BCP 47 tag, or `nothing`.
_check_lang(::Nothing, who) = nothing
_check_lang(lang::AbstractString, who) = occursin(_HTML_LANG, lang) ? String(lang) :
    throw(ArgumentError(string(who, ": lang must be a BCP 47 language tag such as \"de\" or ",
        "\"de-CH\", or nothing to keep the answer's; got ", repr(lang))))
_check_lang(lang, who) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    who, ": lang must be a BCP 47 language tag string such as \"de\", or nothing; got ", repr(lang))))

_with_page_lang(f, ::Nothing) = f()
_with_page_lang(f, lang::AbstractString) = _with_page_meta(f, (; _page_meta()..., lang=String(lang)))

# A `dispatch(…; lang)` names the answer's language itself, over the chain's.
_page_meta_of(objects, req::HTTP.Request) =
    _page_meta_with_lang(_page_meta_of(objects), get(req.context, :htmxo_lang, nothing))
_page_meta_with_lang(meta, ::Nothing) = meta
_page_meta_with_lang(meta, lang::String) = (; meta..., lang)
# Beside the declarations, the address the request names: its route with the
# mount prefix (`__route__`) and the prefix itself.
_page_meta_of(objects, req::HTTP.Request, context) =
    (; _page_meta_of(objects, req)..., route=context.route, prefix=context.prefix)

"""
    pico_page(content; brand=nothing, nav=(), nav_label=nothing, footer=nothing, skip_link=true, class="container", assets=:cdn, pico_version=<"2" from the CDN, the pinned release under vendor assets>, lang=nothing, kwargs...)

The page frame of a Pico-CSS app on [`htmx`](@ref): a header with the app's
home link and its main navigation, `content` in `<main class="container">`,
and a footer. Replaces the repeated boilerplate

    __page__(content) = htmx(h.main(class="container")(content); pico_version="2")

with

    __page__(content) = pico_page(content)
    # or with a title:
    __page__(content) = pico_page(content; title="My App")

and a whole site frame with

    __page__(content) = let base = __prefix__
        pico_page(content; brand="Strukturdaten Berlin" => base * "/",
                  nav=["Karte" => base * "/", "Methodik" => base * "/modell",
                       private("Angebot" => base * "/angebot")],
                  footer=h.small("Datenbasis: …"))
    end

`brand` is the header's home link, `label => href` (the label in bold), or
an element placed as it is. `nav` lists the main navigation: each
`label => href` pair is a link, a [`private`](@ref) pair a link that public
answers leave out, and any other element (an `h.li` of the app's own) is
placed as it is. Together they form
`<header class="container"><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><ul>brand</ul><ul>nav</ul></nav></header>`;
without either, the page has no header.

The link to the page being read carries `aria-current="page"`, which a
screen reader announces and the page shows (bold and underlined). Nothing is
passed for it: the shell reads the request's address, including the prefix a
proxy forwards. An entry is the page being read when its `href` is that
address or an address below it (`/modell` on `/modell/annahmen`), and of
several such entries the longest; the app's home (`/`, or the mount prefix)
only on itself. Its `href` is compared without a query and with
percent-encoding undone; an `href` with a `#fragment`, a relative one or
another site's never is. Outside a request no entry is marked.

`nav_label` names the navigation for screen readers: "Main navigation" by
default, "Hauptnavigation" on a German page (the `lang` keyword or the
route's `__lang__`). `skip_link=true` puts a "Skip to content" ("Zum Inhalt
springen") link first on a page with a header: it shows when the keyboard
reaches it and moves to `<main id="main">`. A string is its text instead,
and `false` leaves it out. `footer` is placed in `<footer class="container">`,
or as it is when it is an `h.footer` itself. `class` is the class of the
header, `main` and footer.

`pico_version` defaults to Pico's floating `"2"` from the CDN and to the
pinned release under vendor assets (`assets=:vendor` or a mount path), which
serve only that pin. Every other keyword (`extra_head`, `runtime`, `tables`,
`error_texts`, `title`, the library versions, …) goes to [`htmx`](@ref):
`pico_page(content; assets=:vendor, runtime=:linked)` is an offline page
with a cacheable head, and `pico_page(content; lang="de",
error_texts=__error_texts__)` a German one. Left out, `lang` and `title` come
from the route's `__lang__` / `__title__` declarations, as for
[`htmx`](@ref).
"""
function pico_page(content; brand=nothing, nav=(), nav_label=nothing, footer=nothing,
        skip_link=true, class="container", assets=:cdn, pico_version=_pico_page_version(assets),
        lang=nothing, kwargs...)
    texts_lang = isnothing(lang) ? _page_meta().lang : lang
    header = _site_header(brand, nav, nav_label, class, texts_lang)
    skip = isempty(header) ? () : _skip_link(skip_link, texts_lang)
    main = isempty(skip) ? h.main(class=class)(content) : h.main(class=class, id=_MAIN_ID)(content)
    htmx(skip..., header..., main, _site_footer(footer, class)...;
        pico_version, assets, lang, kwargs...)
end

# Vendor assets serve Pico only at its pin; the CDN keeps the floating "2".
_pico_page_version(assets) = assets === :cdn ? "2" : _PICO_VERSION

# The words of the page frame, by the page's language (`_lang_entry`).
const _NAV_LABELS = (en = "Main navigation", de = "Hauptnavigation")
const _SKIP_LINK_TEXTS = (en = "Skip to content", de = "Zum Inhalt springen")
# The skip link's target.
const _MAIN_ID = "main"

# The header: the brand's home link and the main navigation. None without either.
function _site_header(brand, nav, nav_label, class, lang)
    isnothing(brand) && isempty(nav) && return ()
    label = isnothing(nav_label) ? _lang_entry(_NAV_LABELS, lang) : nav_label
    lists = (_brand_list(brand)..., (isempty(nav) ? () : (h.ul(_site_nav_items(nav)...),))...)
    (h.header(class=class)(h.nav(lists...; aria_label=label)),)
end

_brand_list(::Nothing) = ()
_brand_list(brand::Pair) = (h.ul(h.li(h.a(h.strong(first(brand)); href=last(brand)))),)
_brand_list(brand) = (h.ul(h.li(brand)),)

_skip_link(on::Bool, lang) = on ? _skip_link(_lang_entry(_SKIP_LINK_TEXTS, lang), lang) : ()
_skip_link(text::AbstractString, lang) = (h.a(text; href="#" * _MAIN_ID, class="htmxo-skip-link"),)

_site_footer(::Nothing, class) = ()
_site_footer(footer::Node, class) =
    HTMX.tag(footer) === :footer ? (footer,) : (h.footer(class=class)(footer),)
_site_footer(footer, class) = (h.footer(class=class)(footer),)

# --- Route registration and recording ---

const _html_response = s -> HTTP.Response(200, ["Content-Type" => "text/html; charset=utf-8"], body=s)

"""
    generic_html(val) -> Node or ""

Structured, escape-safe HTML for an ordinary Julia value whose type defines no
`show(::IO, ::MIME"text/html", …)` method. This is the framework's *generic
presentation contract*: a route may return plain data (a `NamedTuple` of
results, a `Dict`, a count) and still render in HTML/HX mode, the same way
`?plain` markdown mode already renders any value via `show(::MIME"text/markdown")`.

Shapes, in dispatch order:
- `nothing` → `""` (empty body, not an error)
- `NamedTuple` / `AbstractDict` whose values are all equal-length vectors
  (a column table) → a plain `h.table`
- other `NamedTuple` / `AbstractDict` → an `h.dl` of `dt`/`dd` pairs, values
  rendered recursively
- `AbstractMatrix` → an `h.table` of one `tr` per matrix row, preserving shape
  and row-major reading order
- anything else → `show(::MIME"text/plain")` text, in `h.pre(h.code(…))` when
  it spans lines and `h.span(…)` when it does not

A `Node` is always returned (never a bare `String`), because `auto` passes a
top-level `AbstractString` through as trusted HTML — data must reach the
client as a `Node` child so HTMX's escape-by-default applies. Rich rendering
stays opt-in: define `show(::IO, ::MIME"text/html", ::MyType)` — or reach for
[`render_table`](@ref) — and that wins over this fallback.
"""
generic_html(::Nothing) = ""
generic_html(val) = let text = sprint(show, MIME"text/plain"(), val)
    occursin('\n', text) ? h.pre(h.code(text)) : h.span(text)
end
generic_html(val::NamedTuple) = _generic_mapping_html(keys(val), values(val))
generic_html(val::AbstractDict) = _generic_mapping_html(keys(val), values(val))

# A 2-D array of plain data is a grid, not a fragment sequence. Cells are
# stringified like the column-table branch above rather than recursed through
# `_html_value`, so a numeric grid stays a plain table of numbers; either way
# the text reaches the client as a Node child and is escaped.
generic_html(val::AbstractMatrix) = h.table(h.tbody(
    [h.tr([h.td(string(val[i, j])) for j in axes(val, 2)]...) for i in axes(val, 1)]...,
))

# A column table is a mapping whose values are all vectors of one length —
# checked structurally rather than via `Tables.istable` so the branch a caller
# gets is predictable from the value alone.
_is_column_table(vals) = !isempty(vals) && all(v -> v isa AbstractVector, vals) &&
    allequal(map(length, collect(vals)))

function _generic_mapping_html(ks, vs)
    kv = collect(vs)
    _is_column_table(kv) && return h.table(
        h.thead(h.tr([h.th(string(k)) for k in ks]...)),
        h.tbody([h.tr([h.td(string(col[i])) for col in kv]...)
                 for i in eachindex(first(kv))]...),
    )
    items = Any[]
    for (k, v) in zip(ks, kv)
        push!(items, h.dt(string(k)))
        push!(items, h.dd(_html_value(v)))
    end
    h.dl(items...)
end

# --- Semantic presentation nodes ---

"""
    SemanticNode

Abstract parent for reusable presentation values that sit above any one markup
format. Built-in semantic nodes have peer HTML, Hyperview HXML, Markdown, and
plain-text projections.

Those are projections of the NODE, reached with `show(io, mime, node)` or
`repr(mime, node)`. HTML and Markdown are additionally reachable through a
route: a semantic node survives markdown chrome-stripping (see
[`_strip_md_chrome`](@ref)), so it renders in `?plain` / `?markdown` reads even
when mounted inside a generated `<form>`. The response pipeline negotiates no
`application/vnd.hyperview+xml` arm, so the HXML projection is node-level only.
"""
abstract type SemanticNode end

"""
    SemanticCard(title, children...; anchor="")

A titled semantic summary. Children may be any other semantic node, another
renderable value, or plain data.

`anchor`, when non-empty, becomes the HTML `id` and, in Markdown, the title
heading's identifier (`### Title {#anchor}`) — a document address, so a card
can be cited from elsewhere, also in a [`pdf_report`](@ref).

In HTML the title is the card's `<header>`, not a heading, so a card opens no
level of the HTML outline. In Markdown it is a heading one level below the
[`SemanticSection`](@ref) holding the card, and `###` outside any section,
where a card sits below the page's `##` headings; the card's own content,
nested sections included, continues one level below it.
"""
struct SemanticCard <: SemanticNode
    title::String
    children::Vector{Any}
    anchor::String
    SemanticCard(title::AbstractString, children::AbstractVector; anchor="") =
        new(String(title), Any[children...], String(anchor))
    SemanticCard(title::AbstractString, children...; anchor="") =
        new(String(title), Any[children...], String(anchor))
end

"""`SemanticFields(; facts...)` stores labelled facts for peer format rendering."""
struct SemanticFields <: SemanticNode
    pairs::Vector{Pair{Symbol,Any}}
end

SemanticFields(; kwargs...) = SemanticFields(
    Pair{Symbol,Any}[Symbol(key) => value for (key, value) in pairs(kwargs)])

# A language is one word: the HTML `language-` class and the Markdown fence's
# info string. Source text given first would otherwise render as the info
# string, with the language as the code.
function _semantic_code_language(language)
    name = string(language)
    any(isspace, name) && throw(ArgumentError(string(
        "SemanticCode(language, text): the language must be one word, such as :julia or :stan; got ",
        repr(name), ". The language comes first, the source text second.")))
    Symbol(name)
end

"""
    SemanticCode(language, text; anchor="")

Store source code without choosing markup. `language` is converted to a
`Symbol` and becomes the HTML language class / Markdown fence info string. It
is one word, given first: a language containing whitespace, such as source
text passed in its place, throws an `ArgumentError`.

`anchor`, when non-empty, becomes the HTML `id`, so a code block that is cited
from elsewhere has a document address. In Markdown the block is wrapped in a
fenced div carrying it (`::: {#anchor}`), so a link to it resolves in a
[`pdf_report`](@ref) too.
"""
struct SemanticCode <: SemanticNode
    language::Symbol
    text::String
    anchor::String
    SemanticCode(language, text; anchor="") =
        new(_semantic_code_language(language), String(text), String(anchor))
end

"""
    SemanticStatus(state::Symbol; detail="", label=string(state))

Store status meaning rather than styling. `:ok` / `:available`, `:warn` /
`:blocked`, `:fail`, and `:pending` receive distinct text glyphs; any other
symbol remains valid and uses a neutral glyph. The state stays data: it picks
the glyph and fills the HTML/HXML `data-status` attribute.

`label` is the word written after the glyph in every peer format, the state's
own name unless given. An app in another language, or one whose states are
domain outcomes, keeps a recognised state and its glyph and names it in its
own words:

```julia
SemanticStatus(:warn; label="im Projekt zu klären", detail="3 Ortsteile")
# HTML: <span … data-status="warn"><strong>! im Projekt zu klären</strong>…
# Markdown: ! **im Projekt zu klären** — 3 Ortsteile
# plain text: [im Projekt zu klären] 3 Ortsteile
```

A blank `label` throws an `ArgumentError`: plain text shows no glyph, so the
status would say nothing there.
"""
struct SemanticStatus <: SemanticNode
    state::Symbol
    detail::String
    label::String  # the word after the glyph
    function SemanticStatus(state::Symbol; detail="", label=string(state))
        label = String(label)
        # Plain text carries the status in this word alone.
        all(isspace, label) && throw(ArgumentError(
            "SemanticStatus(:$state): `label` is blank; omit it to show the state's name"))
        new(state, String(detail), label)
    end
end

"""
    SemanticTexts

The words the semantic vocabulary writes itself, besides the caller's own
content: `unavailable` is the marker of a [`SemanticUnavailable`](@ref), in
every peer format, and `decimal` is the decimal mark of a
[`SemanticArtifact`](@ref)'s file size (`1.4 MB`, `1,4 MB`). Build one with
[`semantic_texts`](@ref) and pass it as `texts=`.
"""
struct SemanticTexts
    unavailable::String  # the marker of a declined computation
    decimal::String      # the decimal mark of the numbers the vocabulary writes
end

const _SEMANTIC_TEXT_LOCALES = (
    en = (unavailable = "unavailable", decimal = "."),
    de = (unavailable = "nicht verfügbar", decimal = ","),
)

"""
    semantic_texts(locale::Symbol=:en; texts...)

The [`SemanticTexts`](@ref) for `locale` (`:en` or `:de`), with any of its texts
replaced by keyword:

```julia
const DE = semantic_texts(:de)
SemanticUnavailable("Die Auswertung war beim Export noch nicht berechnet."; texts=DE)
# HTML: ∅ nicht verfügbar — Die Auswertung …; Markdown: ∅ **nicht verfügbar** — …
SemanticUnavailable("…"; texts=semantic_texts(:de; unavailable="entfällt"))
SemanticArtifact("bericht.pdf", "application/pdf"; target="/datei/bericht.pdf",
    size=1_468_006, texts=DE)  # PDF · 1,4 MB
```

An unknown locale or text name, or a placeholder in a text (semantic texts take
none), throws an `ArgumentError`.
"""
semantic_texts(locale::Symbol=:en; texts...) =
    _locale_texts(SemanticTexts, :semantic_texts, _SEMANTIC_TEXT_LOCALES, (;), locale; texts...)

"""
    SemanticUnavailable(reason; texts=semantic_texts())

A DECLINED computation: content that was not produced, and why. The reason is
held as data, so every peer format states it rather than leaving a slot the
reader has to interpret. The marker word that precedes it (`unavailable`) comes
from `texts`, a [`SemanticTexts`](@ref): `texts=semantic_texts(:de)` writes
`nicht verfügbar` in every peer format.

Deliberately NOT a [`SemanticStatus`](@ref), which reports a state the system is
IN. A refusal is not a state: nothing is warning, failing or pending, so every
status symbol asserts something untrue — and `SemanticStatus(:warn)` type-checks
while saying exactly the wrong thing. This is the same distinction the
vocabulary already draws between [`SemanticLink`](@ref) and
[`SemanticAction`](@ref): one HTML idiom, two meanings, and the meaning is what
must survive into the other formats.

Its HTML peer is a `p` — a refusal REPLACES a block of content, where a status
annotates something inline — carrying the framework-owned
`htmxo-semantic-unavailable` class, which is where an app hangs its own styling
rather than burying the meaning back in a call-site `class=`.
"""
struct SemanticUnavailable <: SemanticNode
    reason::String
    texts::SemanticTexts
    SemanticUnavailable(reason; texts::SemanticTexts=semantic_texts()) =
        new(String(reason), texts)
end

"""
    SemanticProse(markdown; math=true)

A run of prose, authored as Markdown. The Markdown and plain-text projections
are the source itself; the HTML projection renders it through
[`render_markdown`](@ref) (CommonMark plus bare-URL linking and TeX math),
which escapes inline markup, so prose cannot smuggle HTML through. Its
`\$…\$` / `\$\$…\$\$` math is typeset in the browser; `math=false` shows every
`\$` as text.
"""
struct SemanticProse <: SemanticNode
    markdown::String
    math::Bool
    SemanticProse(markdown; math::Bool=true) = new(String(markdown), math)
end

"""
    SemanticTable(source)

Tabular data held as DATA. `source` is any Tables.jl-compatible source — a
`NamedTuple` of equal-length vectors, a `DataFrame`, a `TreeArrays.TreeData` —
gated by `Tables.istable`, so a scalar, string, vector or dict is not silently
mangled into a one-row table but shown through its own projection instead.

Deliberately not `DataFrame`-shaped: a `TreeData` is already a lazy Tables.jl
source and is strictly richer, so materializing one into a frame just to render
it loses information for nothing.
"""
struct SemanticTable <: SemanticNode
    source
end

"""
    SemanticPlot(layer)

A plot held as its specification rather than its picture. `layer` is normally an
AlgebraOfVega spec/layer; it is projected through its OWN `show` methods, so
HTMXObjects names no plotting package and the element costs no dependency.
"""
struct SemanticPlot <: SemanticNode
    layer
end

"""
    SemanticMetric(label, value; unit="")

One labelled measurement, keeping the unit as DATA instead of burying it in the
formatted string. HTML has no element for this.
"""
struct SemanticMetric <: SemanticNode
    label::String
    value
    unit::String
end

SemanticMetric(label, value; unit="") =
    SemanticMetric(String(label), value, String(unit))

# `SemanticLink` and `SemanticAction` declare INNER constructors for the same
# reason the containers below do, and the trap is easier to miss here: a struct
# with one typed and one untyped field auto-generates BOTH an exact
# `(String, Any)` constructor and a converting `(Any, Any)` one. An outer
# `SemanticLink(label, target) = SemanticLink(String(label), target)` is itself
# `(Any, Any)`, so it OVERWRITES the converting constructor — which is a hard
# `ERROR: Method overwriting is not permitted during Module precompilation`.
# Declaring the arm inside the struct suppresses the auto-generated pair instead.

"""
    SemanticLink(label, target; external=false, code=false)

A navigation target held as data. Renders as an ordinary anchor in HTML and as
a real link in Markdown, rather than as a bare stringified URL.

With `external=true` the link leaves the app: it opens in a new tab
(`target="_blank"`, `rel="noopener"`), carries a trailing `↗` glyph, and keeps
that marker in the Markdown (`[label ↗](target)`) and plain (`label ↗ (target)`)
peers. A [`StaticExport`](@ref) still hands its address to `url_map` like any
`href`, so an in-app address opened in a new tab is mapped into the bundle; an
address outside the app gets `nothing` back and stays as given.

With `code=true` the label is an identifier — a repo slug, a branch name, a
SHA — and keeps its code styling: `<a …><code>label</code> ↗</a>` in HTML and
``[`label` ↗](target)`` in Markdown. The glyph stays OUTSIDE the code span in
both: it marks the exit, it is not part of the identifier. Plain text has no
code spans, so that peer is unchanged.
"""
struct SemanticLink <: SemanticNode
    label::String
    target
    external::Bool
    code::Bool
    SemanticLink(label, target; external=false, code=false) =
        new(String(label), target, Bool(external), Bool(code))
end

"""
    SemanticAction(label, target)

An OPERATION offered to the reader, as opposed to a [`SemanticLink`](@ref)'s
plain navigation. The HTML projection carries the `hx-*` attributes that make it
an in-place HTMX swap; every other format degrades to an ordinary link, which is
the point — the meaning stays readable where `hx-get` is not.
"""
struct SemanticAction <: SemanticNode
    label::String
    target
    SemanticAction(label, target) = new(String(label), target)
end

"""
    SemanticArtifact(name, mime, bytes=nothing; target=nothing, label=nothing,
                     size=nothing, texts=semantic_texts())

A downloadable payload: a name, a MIME type, and a PLACE to fetch it from.
`bytes` stays positional and optional because an artifact is sometimes a payload
the route already holds and sometimes just a name for what a URL will produce.

With no `target` the artifact addresses itself by `name` — the pre-`target`
behaviour, which could only ever resolve by accident, kept so an in-memory
payload still renders.

- `name` is the file name the download is saved under. Its extension, in
  capitals, is the format every peer names: `XLSX`, `PDF`, `ZIP`.
- `label` is the text shown in place of the name (`"Bericht als PDF"`); the
  name then becomes the link's tooltip.
- `size` is the file's size in bytes. It defaults to the size of `bytes` when
  they are a string or a byte vector, and is written short in every peer:
  `820 KB`, `1.4 MB` (1024 bytes to a KB, one decimal below 10), with the
  decimal mark of `texts`, a [`SemanticTexts`](@ref) (`semantic_texts(:de)`
  writes `1,4 MB`).

In HTML it is one download control: a download icon, the label, and the
format and size in a `small`, inside an `<a class="htmxo-semantic-artifact"
download type=…>`. A Pico page (`htmx(...; pico_version)`, `pico_page`) draws
it as a slate outlined button, through [`pico_bridge`](@ref). Markdown writes
`[label](target) (XLSX, 1.4 MB)` and plain text `label [XLSX, 1.4 MB]
(target)`; a name without an extension names the MIME type there instead.
"""
struct SemanticArtifact <: SemanticNode
    name::String
    mime::String
    bytes
    target
    label::String
    size::Union{Nothing,Int}
    texts::SemanticTexts
    SemanticArtifact(name, mime, bytes=nothing; target=nothing, label=nothing,
                     size=nothing, texts::SemanticTexts=semantic_texts()) =
        new(String(name), String(mime), bytes, target,
            String(something(label, name)), _semantic_artifact_size(size, bytes), texts)
end

# A size given by keyword is a byte count; without one, a payload the artifact
# holds as text or bytes has the size of its bytes, and any other has none.
_semantic_artifact_size(size::Integer, bytes) = size >= 0 ? Int(size) :
    throw(ArgumentError(string("SemanticArtifact: size is a byte count; got ", size)))
_semantic_artifact_size(size, bytes) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    "SemanticArtifact: size is a byte count (an integer); got ", repr(size))))
_semantic_artifact_size(::Nothing, bytes::AbstractString) = ncodeunits(bytes)
_semantic_artifact_size(::Nothing, bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8}) = length(bytes)
_semantic_artifact_size(::Nothing, bytes) = nothing

# The containers declare INNER constructors on purpose. With a `Vector{Any}`
# field, Julia's auto-generated converting constructor and an outer `children...`
# method are mutually ambiguous, and a single non-vector child resolves to the
# converting constructor, which then cannot convert one element to a
# `Vector{Any}`. Declaring both arms inside the struct suppresses the
# auto-generated one and makes dispatch unambiguous.
#
# The vector arm takes `AbstractVector`, NOT `Vector{Any}`, and that is
# load-bearing: `collect(...)` and a comprehension both NARROW, so
# `SemanticSection("t", collect(metrics))` hands over a `Vector{SemanticMetric}`.
# Against a `Vector{Any}` arm that misses, falls through to the varargs arm, and
# silently wraps the whole vector as ONE child — a container that renders a
# single mangled entry instead of N. Caught by running it, not by reading it.

"""
    SemanticSection(title, children...; anchor="", level=nothing)

A document-level division — it opens a heading level, unlike
[`SemanticCard`](@ref), which is a self-contained summary.

The level follows the nesting: a section's title is `<h2>` / `##` at the top
of a document, below its title, and one level deeper inside each enclosing
section, through any markup in between (an `h.div` or a card holding the inner
section). `<h6>` / `######` is the deepest; a section nested deeper keeps it.

`level` (1–6) fixes the level the section opens at, wherever it renders; the
sections inside it follow from there. `level=1` makes the section the page's
title: a page made of one section, such as a glossary page
(`glossary_section(g; level=1)`), then has its `<h1>` / `#` and its sections
at `<h2>` / `##`.

`anchor`, when non-empty, becomes the HTML `id` and, in Markdown, the title
heading's identifier (`## Title {#anchor}`), so a link to `#anchor` jumps to
the section in the browser and in a [`pdf_report`](@ref) alike.
"""
struct SemanticSection <: SemanticNode
    title::String
    children::Vector{Any}
    anchor::String
    level::Union{Nothing,Int}
    SemanticSection(title::AbstractString, children::AbstractVector; anchor="", level=nothing) =
        new(String(title), Any[children...], String(anchor), _semantic_section_level(level))
    SemanticSection(title::AbstractString, children...; anchor="", level=nothing) =
        new(String(title), Any[children...], String(anchor), _semantic_section_level(level))
end

# A section's `level`: none (it follows the nesting), or one of HTML's six.
_semantic_section_level(::Nothing) = nothing
_semantic_section_level(level::Integer) = 1 <= level <= 6 ? Int(level) :
    throw(ArgumentError("SemanticSection: level must be 1 to 6, not $level"))
_semantic_section_level(level) =
    throw(ArgumentError("SemanticSection: level must be an integer from 1 to 6 or `nothing`, not $(repr(level))"))

"""
    SemanticGroup(children...)

An untitled run of siblings — the container to reach for when children belong
together but the grouping itself carries no label.
"""
struct SemanticGroup <: SemanticNode
    children::Vector{Any}
    SemanticGroup(children::AbstractVector) = new(Any[children...])
    SemanticGroup(children...) = new(Any[children...])
end

"""
    SemanticDisclosure(summary, children...)

Content the reader OPENS — not content that is hidden. The distinction is per
format and is exactly why this cannot be an `h.details` wrapper around semantic
children: HTML collapses it, but Markdown and plain text have no viewport to
collapse into, so their correct projection is the label followed by the whole
body. Wrapping semantic children in markup instead would force the entire
subtree down the HTML→Markdown translation path and lose every peer projection
underneath it.

A disclosure with nothing to open renders nothing in any format, its label
included. That covers a disclosure with no children, one holding only `nothing`,
and one whose [`private`](@ref) content has no stand-in for a public reader.
In Markdown it also covers a body that held only form controls. Groups and
vectors of such children count as empty too. A static export also leaves out
a disclosure whose controls it removed (`controls=:remove`).
"""
struct SemanticDisclosure <: SemanticNode
    summary::String
    children::Vector{Any}
    SemanticDisclosure(summary::AbstractString, children::AbstractVector) =
        new(String(summary), Any[children...])
    SemanticDisclosure(summary::AbstractString, children...) =
        new(String(summary), Any[children...])
end

"""
    SemanticAlternatives(default, ("label" => view)...)

Several views of the same content — a verbatim source and its rendered
preview, a summary and its full body. The first view is the DEFAULT: it
renders directly, and it is the ONLY view in the Markdown, plain and HXML
peers. Every later view renders collapsed inside a native `<details>` with its
label as the `<summary>` — view-switching with no client JavaScript. A later
view with nothing to show its reader, such as [`private`](@ref) content without
a stand-in for a public reader, is left out together with its label.

Later views MUST be `label => view` pairs (the label heads the collapsed
section); anything else is an `ArgumentError`, as is an empty call. To show a
literal `Pair` as a view, wrap it (e.g. `Any[pair]`).
"""
struct SemanticAlternatives <: SemanticNode
    views::Vector{Any}
    function SemanticAlternatives(views::AbstractVector)
        isempty(views) && throw(ArgumentError(
            "SemanticAlternatives needs at least one view"))
        for v in views[2:end]
            v isa Pair || throw(ArgumentError(
                "SemanticAlternatives views after the first must be `label => view` pairs, got $(typeof(v))"))
        end
        new(Any[views...])
    end
    SemanticAlternatives(views...) = SemanticAlternatives(Any[views...])
end

"""
    semantic_card(value) -> SemanticCard or nothing

Return the reusable semantic card owned by `value`. Extend this for a domain
value used with `operation_form(...; presentation=:cards)`. Values that are
already `SemanticCard`s pass through; the default has no rich card.
"""
semantic_card(card::SemanticCard) = card
semantic_card(_) = nothing

_semantic_status_glyph(state::Symbol) = state === :ok || state === :available ? "✓" :
    state === :warn || state === :blocked ? "!" :
    state === :fail ? "✗" : state === :pending ? "…" : "•"

function _semantic_fields_node(fields::SemanticFields)
    nodes = Any[]
    for (key, value) in fields.pairs
        push!(nodes, h.dt(string(key)))
        push!(nodes, h.dd(value))
    end
    h.dl(nodes...; class="htmxo-semantic-fields")
end

_semantic_code_node(code::SemanticCode) = h.pre(
    h.code(code.text; class="language-$(code.language)");
    _semantic_anchor_attrs(code.anchor)...)

function _semantic_status_node(status::SemanticStatus)
    detail = isempty(status.detail) ? Any[] : Any[h.small(" $(status.detail)")]
    h.span(
        h.strong(string(_semantic_status_glyph(status.state), " ", status.label)),
        detail...;
        class="htmxo-semantic-status", data_status=string(status.state))
end

# A refusal REPLACES a block of content, so its peer is a `p` rather than the
# `span` a status annotates inline with. The marker word is emitted rather than
# left to the reason: a bare reason would make the Markdown and plain peers
# byte-identical to a `SemanticProse` of the same string, losing the meaning in
# exactly the formats the vocabulary exists to carry it into — the same reason a
# status prints its state even when `detail` restates it. An empty reason emits
# no dangling separator, matching the status detail.
function _semantic_unavailable_node(unavailable::SemanticUnavailable)
    reason = isempty(unavailable.reason) ? Any[] :
        Any[h.small(" — $(unavailable.reason)")]
    h.p(h.strong("∅ $(unavailable.texts.unavailable)"), reason...;
        class="htmxo-semantic-unavailable")
end

# Splatted rather than branched at each call site, so an unaddressed element
# emits no attribute at all instead of `id=""`.
_semantic_anchor_attrs(anchor::AbstractString) =
    isempty(anchor) ? (;) : (; id=anchor)

# The Markdown peer of an anchor: Pandoc's identifier attribute, so a link to
# `#anchor` resolves where the Markdown is rendered. Typst refuses a link to a
# label that does not exist, so without it `pdf_report` failed on every
# in-document link. Pandoc's `#id` shorthand takes letters, digits and `_.:-`;
# any other id is written `id="…"`, which Pandoc reads to the same identifier.
_markdown_anchor_attr(anchor::AbstractString) =
    occursin(r"^[\p{L}\p{N}_.:-]+$", anchor) ? "{#$anchor}" :
    "{id=\"" * replace(anchor, '\\' => "\\\\", '"' => "\\\"", r"\R" => " ") * "\"}"

# Appended to a heading line: the heading carries its element's anchor.
_markdown_heading_anchor(anchor::AbstractString) =
    isempty(anchor) ? "" : " " * _markdown_anchor_attr(anchor)

_semantic_metric_text(metric::SemanticMetric) = isempty(metric.unit) ?
    string(metric.value) : string(metric.value, " ", metric.unit)

# An artifact with no target addresses itself by name — the pre-`target`
# behaviour, kept so an in-memory payload still renders.
_semantic_artifact_href(artifact::SemanticArtifact) =
    isnothing(artifact.target) ? artifact.name : string(artifact.target)

# The file name's extension in capitals (`XLSX`), or "" for a name without one.
_semantic_artifact_format(artifact::SemanticArtifact) =
    uppercase(lstrip(last(splitext(artifact.name)), '.'))

# A byte count written short, 1024 bytes to a KB, with one decimal below 10:
# `512 B`, `820 KB`, `1.4 MB`.
function _semantic_artifact_size_text(bytes::Int, texts::SemanticTexts)
    bytes < 1024 && return string(bytes, " B")
    value, unit = bytes / 1024, 1
    while value >= 1024 && unit < length(_SEMANTIC_ARTIFACT_UNITS)
        value, unit = value / 1024, unit + 1
    end
    digits = round(value; digits=1) < 10 ? 1 : 0
    string(_format_fixed(value, digits, texts.decimal, ""), " ", _SEMANTIC_ARTIFACT_UNITS[unit])
end
const _SEMANTIC_ARTIFACT_UNITS = ("KB", "MB", "GB", "TB")

# What every peer says about the file besides its label: its format and its
# size. A name without an extension has no format to name; the text peers name
# the MIME type instead (`mime`, already in their spelling), while HTML keeps it
# in the link's `type` attribute and passes `mime=nothing`.
function _semantic_artifact_facts(artifact::SemanticArtifact, mime)
    format = _semantic_artifact_format(artifact)
    facts = String[]
    isempty(format) ? isnothing(mime) || push!(facts, mime) : push!(facts, format)
    isnothing(artifact.size) || push!(facts, _semantic_artifact_size_text(artifact.size, artifact.texts))
    facts
end

"""
    _semantic_table_columns(source) -> (names, columns) or nothing

Resolve a [`SemanticTable`](@ref)'s source through Tables.jl, or `nothing` when
it is not a table and must be shown through its own projection instead.

`Tables.istable` is the gate rather than a duck-typed `propertynames` check:
measured against the real dependency it is `true` for a `NamedTuple` of vectors,
a `DataFrame` and a `TreeArrays.TreeData`, and `false` for a `NamedTuple` of
scalars, a scalar, a `String`, a `Vector` and a `Dict` — so a non-table is never
mangled into a spurious one-row table.
"""
function _semantic_table_columns(source)
    Tables.istable(source) || return nothing
    columns = Tables.columns(source)
    names = collect(Tables.columnnames(columns))
    isempty(names) && return nothing
    (names, Any[Tables.getcolumn(columns, name) for name in names])
end

# --- Markdown renderer with inline text rules -------------------------------
#
# `render_markdown` parses with CommonMark.jl (the spec GitHub-Flavored
# Markdown renders) and walks the AST into `h` nodes. It replaces the stdlib
# `Markdown` pipeline the prose peer used before, which predates CommonMark
# and misreads prose — most damagingly, an intra-word underscore in an
# identifier (`data_path`) is taken as emphasis and the `_` is *deleted*, so
# the identifier renders italicised and uncopyable. CommonMark forbids
# intra-word `_` emphasis, so identifiers stay verbatim.
#
# The walk generalizes the KB's `src/kb_markdown.jl` (same joined-run and
# in-link machinery; the KB-only pre-parse linkifiers, LaTeX rule, mentions
# and decorations stay there) and keeps its three guarantees:
#
# - adjacent `Text` children render as ONE run: CommonMark cuts a visually
#   single run of text at every inline trigger it tried (`_`, `&`, `!`, …),
#   so a URL such as `https://x/a_b?c=1&d=2` arrives as several `Text` nodes
#   and matching per node would link a fragment of it;
# - rules run only on runs OUTSIDE links/images, code spans and fences — an
#   `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML, and the author's link already says
#   where the text points;
# - rules that match nothing, or return `nothing`, leave the text alone.
#
# Escaping rides on `h`: ordinary `String` children and attributes escape
# exactly once, so `Text`/`Code`/`CodeBlock` literals pass through unchanged
# and raw HTML stays visible text instead of injecting live markup.

"""
    MarkdownRenderer

Abstract renderer for [`render_markdown`](@ref): an app subtypes it and adds
[`markdown_node`](@ref) methods for only the CommonMark node kinds it cares
about, and every other kind falls through to the stock method. The walk
recurses through the SAME renderer at every depth — children, joined text
runs, tight-list items, table cells — so an override applies inside lists,
quotes and tables.
"""
abstract type MarkdownRenderer end

"""
    DefaultMarkdownRenderer()

The stock [`MarkdownRenderer`](@ref): CommonMark plus the GFM table,
strikethrough and task-list extensions rendered into plain `h` nodes, exactly
as [`render_markdown`](@ref) behaved before renderers existed. `render_markdown`
uses it unless the caller passes `renderer=`.
"""
struct DefaultMarkdownRenderer <: MarkdownRenderer end

"""
    MarkdownRule(pattern, build)

One inline text rule for [`render_markdown`](@ref): `pattern` is matched
against each joined text run outside links, code and fences, and
`build(match::RegexMatch, context)` builds the replacement — a node, a
`String`, or a vector of nodes and strings spliced in place.

`build` returns `nothing` (or an explicitly empty vector) to leave the
matched text alone; the scan then resumes after the match START, so an
overlapping later match can still fire. A pattern that matches the empty
string never fires — the scan steps past it instead of looping on it.

`context` is whatever the caller passed to `render_markdown`: a mutable
value (usually a `Dict`) lets rules accumulate render-scoped state, for
example keys a later batched request decorates.
"""
struct MarkdownRule
    pattern::Regex
    build::Function
    MarkdownRule(pattern::Regex, build::Function) = new(pattern, build)
end

# GFM's trailing-punctuation rule for a bare URL: sentence punctuation after
# a URL belongs to the sentence, and a closing `)`/`]` belongs to the URL
# only when the URL itself opened it (`(see https://x/a_(b))` keeps one).
function _md_trim_url_tail(url::AbstractString)
    u = String(url)
    while !isempty(u)
        c = u[end]
        if c in ('.', ',', ':', ';', '!', '?', '\'', '"', '*', '_', '~')
            u = u[1:prevind(u, lastindex(u))]
        elseif (c == ')' && count(==(')'), u) > count(==('('), u)) ||
               (c == ']' && count(==(']'), u) > count(==('['), u))
            u = u[1:prevind(u, lastindex(u))]
        else
            break
        end
    end
    u
end

# The `MARKDOWN_URL_RULE` builder: the trimmed URL becomes the link, and any
# trimmed tail is re-emitted as text (it was part of the match but is not part
# of the URL).
function _md_build_url_link(m::RegexMatch, context)
    matched = String(m.match)
    url = _md_trim_url_tail(matched)
    link = h.a(url; href=url)
    ncodeunits(url) == ncodeunits(matched) && return link
    Any[link, String(SubString(matched, ncodeunits(url) + 1))]
end

"""
    MARKDOWN_URL_RULE

The stock [`MarkdownRule`](@ref): a bare `http(s)://` URL becomes an anchor
whose label is the URL itself. Trailing sentence punctuation is trimmed off
the link (GFM's rule) and re-emitted as text.

This is the default [`render_markdown`](@ref) registry, and it composes with
app registries: `render_markdown(src; rules=[MY_RULE, MARKDOWN_URL_RULE])`.
"""
const MARKDOWN_URL_RULE = MarkdownRule(
    r"(?<![\w/])https?://[^\s<>\"'`]+", _md_build_url_link)

# Splice one rule-builder result into a run's parts: a vector's elements join
# the run, anything else joins as one child.
_md_push_rule_out!(parts, out::AbstractVector) = append!(parts, out)
_md_push_rule_out!(parts, out) = push!(parts, out)

# Run the rule registry over one joined text run. Earliest match across rules
# wins; ties go to the earliest rule. Returns the run unchanged when nothing
# fires, so a rule-free render splices plain strings, never empty spans.
function _md_apply_rules(str::AbstractString, rules, context)
    s = String(str)
    isempty(rules) && return s
    parts = Any[]
    i = firstindex(s)
    pos = firstindex(s)
    while pos <= lastindex(s)
        found_rule = nothing
        found_match = nothing
        for rule in rules
            m = match(rule.pattern, s, pos)
            isnothing(m) && continue
            if isnothing(found_match) || m.offset < found_match.offset
                found_rule = rule
                found_match = m
            end
        end
        isnothing(found_match) && break
        m = found_match
        if isempty(m.match)
            pos = m.offset > lastindex(s) ? m.offset + 1 : nextind(s, m.offset)
            continue
        end
        out = found_rule.build(m, context)
        if isnothing(out) || (out isa AbstractVector && isempty(out))
            pos = nextind(s, m.offset)
            continue
        end
        m.offset > i && push!(parts, s[i:prevind(s, m.offset)])
        _md_push_rule_out!(parts, out)
        i = m.offset + ncodeunits(m.match)
        pos = i
    end
    isempty(parts) && return s
    i <= lastindex(s) && push!(parts, s[i:end])
    h.span(parts...)
end

# Child nodes of a CommonMark AST node, in document order.
function _md_children(n::CommonMark.Node)
    out = CommonMark.Node[]
    c = n.first_child
    while c !== CommonMark.NULL_NODE
        push!(out, c)
        c = c.nxt
    end
    out
end

# True when `n` is, or sits inside, a Link/Image. Text there must never gain
# a link of its own: an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML, and the
# author's link already says where the text points.
function _md_in_link(n::CommonMark.Node)
    while n !== CommonMark.NULL_NODE
        n.t isa Union{CommonMark.Link,CommonMark.Image} && return true
        isdefined(n, :parent) || return false
        n = n.parent
    end
    false
end

"""
    markdown_children(renderer, node, rules, context) -> Vector

Render `node`'s children with joined text runs, ready to splat into an `h`
parent. Adjacent `Text` children render as ONE run (see
[`MarkdownRenderer`](@ref)); a `Backslash` node is a zero-width escape marker
— the escaped char arrives as the following `Text` — so joining skips over it
rather than splitting the run. Rules therefore see escape-RESOLVED text and
cannot tell `\\#12` from `#12`.

This is the recursion helper for [`markdown_node`](@ref) override authors:
custom node methods render their subtree through it, so overrides apply at
every depth — inside lists, quotes and tables.
"""
function markdown_children(
        r::MarkdownRenderer, n::CommonMark.Node, rules, context)
    out = Any[]
    run = IOBuffer()
    pending = false
    in_link = _md_in_link(n)
    for c in _md_children(n)
        if c.t isa CommonMark.Text
            print(run, c.literal)
            pending = true
            continue
        elseif c.t isa CommonMark.Backslash
            continue
        end
        if pending
            push!(out, markdown_text_run(
                r, String(take!(run)), in_link, rules, context))
            pending = false
        end
        push!(out, markdown_node(r, c, rules, context))
    end
    pending && push!(out, markdown_text_run(
        r, String(take!(run)), in_link, rules, context))
    out
end

"""
    markdown_text_run(renderer, run, in_link, rules, context)

Render one joined text run. The stock method leaves runs inside links alone
and runs the rule registry over every other run; override it to treat runs
differently — for example to link `@mentions` in link text while never
adding another `<a>`.
"""
function markdown_text_run(r::MarkdownRenderer, s::AbstractString,
                           in_link::Bool, rules, context)
    in_link ? String(s) : _md_apply_rules(s, rules, context)
end

"""
    markdown_node(renderer, node, rules, context)
    markdown_node(renderer, node_kind, node, rules, context)

Render one AST node into an `h` node (or a bare `String`, for text). The
three-argument form dispatches on the container payload type `n.t` — one
method per CommonMark node kind, so an unhandled kind is loud, never silent.

Override authors add methods on their own
[`MarkdownRenderer`](@ref) subtype for only the kinds they care about, for
example `markdown_node(r::MyRenderer, ::CommonMark.Code, n, rules, context)`;
every other kind falls through to the stock method below. Render subtrees
through [`markdown_children`](@ref) (and single nodes through the
three-argument form) so the SAME renderer — and every override — applies at
every depth.
"""
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, n::CommonMark.Node, rules, context) =
    markdown_node(r, n.t, n, rules, context)

markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Document, n, rules, context) =
    h.div(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Paragraph, n, rules, context) =
    h.p(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.BlockQuote, n, rules, context) =
    h.blockquote(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Item, n, rules, context) =
    h.li(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.ThematicBreak, n, rules, context) =
    h.hr()
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Emph, n, rules, context) =
    h.em(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Strong, n, rules, context) =
    h.strong(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Text, n, rules, context) =
    markdown_text_run(r, n.literal, _md_in_link(n), rules, context)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Code, n, rules, context) =
    h.code(n.literal)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.SoftBreak, n, rules, context) = " "
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.LineBreak, n, rules, context) =
    h.br()
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Backslash, n, rules, context) = ""
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Strikethrough, n, rules, context) =
    h.del(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.Heading, n, rules, context) =
    getproperty(h, Symbol("h", clamp(t.level, 1, 6)))(
        markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)

# Fenced or indented code block — the content is in `n.literal` and stays
# byte-literal. The fence's info string (e.g. ```julia) is preserved as a
# `language-<lang>` class on the `<code>` element, the highlight.js
# convention, so a client highlighter runs the declared language instead of
# auto-detect. Untyped fences keep a bare `<pre><code>`.
function markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.CodeBlock, n, rules, context)
    info = strip(String(t.info))
    lang = isempty(info) ? "" : lowercase(first(split(info)))
    isempty(lang) ? h.pre(h.code(n.literal)) :
        h.pre(h.code(n.literal; class="language-$(lang)"))
end

# Ordered vs bullet list off the parsed list metadata. Honor the TIGHT flag:
# CommonMark keeps a `Paragraph` node inside EVERY item even in a tight list,
# so the default `Item` renderer (which wraps each Paragraph in `<p>`) would
# render every list loose — even one the author wrote tight. In a tight list
# each item's Paragraph contributes its inline content with NO `<p>`, matching
# the stock CommonMark HTML writer.
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.List, n, rules, context) =
    (t.list_data.type === :ordered ? h.ol : h.ul)(
        (t.list_data.tight ? _md_tight_item(r, c, rules, context) :
            markdown_node(r, c, rules, context)
         for c in _md_children(n))...)

# One item in a TIGHT list. A `Paragraph` child contributes its inline content
# directly (no `<p>` wrapper); any other block child (a nested sublist, a code
# block) renders normally through `markdown_node`, which recurses and honors that
# nested list's OWN tight flag. A `List`'s children are always `Item`s.
function _md_tight_item(
        r::MarkdownRenderer, item::CommonMark.Node, rules, context)
    kids = Any[]
    item.t isa CommonMark.TaskItem && push!(kids, _md_task_box(item.t))
    for c in _md_children(item)
        if c.t isa CommonMark.Paragraph
            append!(kids, markdown_children(r, c, rules, context))
        else
            push!(kids, markdown_node(r, c, rules, context))
        end
    end
    h.li(kids...)
end

# Task-list items show a static checkbox GLYPH — a form `<input>` would be
# interactive chrome, dropped from `?plain` — plus the item's content. The
# class is a styling hook for apps, like the semantic vocabulary's.
_md_task_box(t::CommonMark.TaskItem) =
    h.span(t.checked ? "☑ " : "☐ "; class="htmxo-md-task")
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.TaskItem, n, rules, context) =
    h.li(_md_task_box(t), markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)

# Author links and images render as written; rules never run inside them (see
# `_md_in_link`). A link/image title rides along only when the author gave
# one — an empty `title=""` is noise.
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.Link, n, rules, context) =
    h.a(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...; href=String(t.destination),
        _md_title_attrs(t.title)...)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.Image, n, rules, context) =
    h.img(; src=String(t.destination), alt=_md_plain_text(n),
        _md_title_attrs(t.title)...)
_md_title_attrs(title) = isempty(title) ? (;) : (; title=String(title))

# Plain-text flatten of a subtree — for an `<img alt>`, where only text is
# allowed. Keeps `Text` leaves of the entering walk.
_md_plain_text(n::CommonMark.Node) =
    join(c.literal for (c, entering) in n if entering && c.t isa CommonMark.Text)

# GFM pipe tables — node types come from the `TableRule` extension the parser
# enables. `TableCell.header` distinguishes `<th>` from `<td>`; a set column
# alignment renders as an inline `text-align`, so it holds with no app CSS.
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.Table, n, rules, context) =
    h.table(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.TableHeader, n, rules, context) =
    h.thead(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.TableBody, n, rules, context) =
    h.tbody(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.TableRow, n, rules, context) =
    h.tr(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.TableCell, n, rules, context) =
    (t.header ? h.th : h.td)(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...;
        _md_cell_align_attrs(t.align)...)
_md_cell_align_attrs(align::Symbol) =
    align === :none ? (;) : (; style="text-align: $(String(align))")

# --- TeX math ---
#
# The parser decides what is math, so a browser never has to guess: the rule
# captures the bytes between the delimiters VERBATIM, before escape or
# emphasis processing can touch them (`\_` stays `\_`, `\\` stays `\\`), and
# code spans and fences are node kinds it never sees. Each formula becomes one
# marked element carrying its TeX as text, which `math_script` typesets; until
# it does, or where KaTeX cannot load, the reader sees the source.
#
# Dollar signs follow pandoc's rules, because prose uses `$` for money: an
# opening `$` is followed by a non-space, a closing `$` is preceded by one and
# not followed by a digit, and inline math never contains a backtick. So
# `costs $5 and $10`, `$1,234.00/$56.00` and `$5 (see `x$`)` stay text, while
# `$x$` and `$\alpha_1$` are math. `$$…$$` is display math anywhere, and a
# paragraph that is only `$$…$$` (or `\[…\]`) is a display block, as is a
# ```` ```math ```` fence. `\(…\)` and `\[…\]` are the LaTeX delimiters, tried
# before CommonMark's backslash escapes, which still apply to every other `\`.
struct _MarkdownMathRule end

# The non-space edges of inline dollar math: any character but `$`, a
# backslash, a backtick or whitespace, or a backslash pair (`\$`, `\,`).
const _MD_DOLLAR_EDGE = raw"(?:[^$\\`\s]|\\[^\n])"
const _MD_MATH_INLINE = (
    (Regex("^\\\$\\\$(.+?)\\\$\\\$", "s"), true),
    (Regex("^\\\$(" * _MD_DOLLAR_EDGE * "(?:(?:[^\$\\\\`]|\\\\[^\\n])*?" *
           _MD_DOLLAR_EDGE * ")?)\\\$(?!\\d)", "s"), false),
    (r"^\\\[(.+?)\\\]"s, true),
    (r"^\\\((.+?)\\\)"s, false),
)

function _parse_markdown_math(p::CommonMark.InlineParser, block::CommonMark.Node)
    for (rx, display) in _MD_MATH_INLINE
        m = match(rx, p)
        isnothing(m) && continue
        tex = strip(m.captures[1])
        isempty(tex) && continue
        CommonMark.consume(p, m)
        child = CommonMark.Node(CommonMark.Math(startswith(m.match, '$'), display))
        child.literal = String(tex)
        CommonMark.append_child(block, child)
        return true
    end
    # Not math: the `$` becomes text, and a `\` goes to the escape rule.
    false
end

CommonMark.inline_rule(::_MarkdownMathRule) =
    CommonMark.Rule(_parse_markdown_math, 0.5, "\$\\")

# A paragraph that is exactly one display formula is a block of its own.
const _MD_MATH_BLOCK = (r"^\$\$(.+)\$\$$"s => "\$\$", r"^\\\[(.+)\\\]$"s => "\\]")
CommonMark.block_modifier(::_MarkdownMathRule) =
    CommonMark.Rule(1.5) do parser, node
        if node.t isa CommonMark.CodeBlock && node.t.info == "math"
            node.t = CommonMark.DisplayMath()
            node.literal = strip(node.literal, '\n')
        elseif node.t isa CommonMark.Paragraph
            literal = strip(node.literal)
            for (rx, closer) in _MD_MATH_BLOCK
                m = match(rx, literal)
                isnothing(m) && continue
                # `$$a$$ and $$b$$` is two formulas in prose, not one block.
                tex = strip(m[1])
                (isempty(tex) || occursin(closer, tex)) && continue
                node.t = CommonMark.DisplayMath(startswith(literal, '$'))
                node.literal = String(tex)
                break
            end
        end
        return nothing
    end

# One formula: its TeX as escaped text, between `$` delimiters that only the
# Markdown projection writes, so `?plain` reads `$x$` / `$$…$$` and the HTML
# carries the bare source. Inline display math stays a `span` (a `div` would
# close the paragraph it sits in); a display block is a `div`.
_markdown_math(tex::AbstractString, display::Bool) = display ?
    h.span(markdown_only("\$\$"), String(tex), markdown_only("\$\$");
        class="htmxo-math htmxo-math-display", data_htmxo_math="display") :
    h.span(markdown_only("\$"), String(tex), markdown_only("\$");
        class="htmxo-math", data_htmxo_math="inline")
_markdown_math_block(tex::AbstractString) =
    h.div(markdown_only("\$\$\n"), String(tex), markdown_only("\n\$\$");
        class="htmxo-math htmxo-math-display", data_htmxo_math="display")

markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, t::CommonMark.Math, n, rules, context) =
    _markdown_math(n.literal, t.display)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.DisplayMath, n, rules, context) =
    _markdown_math_block(n.literal)

# Raw HTML in free text is almost never intentional and must never be
# injected into the page. A bare `String` child renders escaped, so inline
# HTML stays visible literal text and block HTML shows as a source block.
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.HtmlInline, n, rules, context) =
    _md_html_inline(n)
markdown_node(::MarkdownRenderer, ::CommonMark.HtmlBlock, n, rules, context) =
    h.pre(n.literal)

# The one exception is an empty link target, `<span id="X"></span>` or
# `<a id="X"></a>`: Markdown has no syntax for an element id, so inline HTML is
# how an author names the destination of a `[text](#X)` link (BRM model
# descriptions mark their prior-table rows this way). The parser hands over
# the two tags as adjacent `HtmlInline` nodes. Only that exact pair, with no
# other attribute and an id made of a letter and then letters, digits, `_` or
# `-`, becomes an empty element; every other tag stays escaped text. The
# opening node renders the element, for the HTML peer only; the closing node
# writes both tags back as they were, for the Markdown projection only, where
# Pandoc reads them as a labelled span. An `<a>` target inside a link stays
# text, because an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML.
const _MD_ID_TARGET = r"^<(span|a) id=\"([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\">$"

_md_html_inline_at(n::CommonMark.Node) =
    n !== CommonMark.NULL_NODE && n.t isa CommonMark.HtmlInline

# The target's opening tag, or `nothing` when `open`/`close` are not one.
function _md_id_target(open::CommonMark.Node, close::CommonMark.Node)
    (_md_html_inline_at(open) && _md_html_inline_at(close)) || return nothing
    m = match(_MD_ID_TARGET, open.literal)
    (isnothing(m) || close.literal != "</$(m[1])>") && return nothing
    m[1] == "a" && _md_in_link(open) && return nothing
    m
end

function _md_html_inline(n::CommonMark.Node)
    target = _md_id_target(n, n.nxt)
    isnothing(target) ||
        return html_only(getproperty(h, Symbol(target[1]))(; id=String(target[2])))
    if isdefined(n, :prv) && !isnothing(_md_id_target(n.prv, n))
        return markdown_only(n.prv.literal * n.literal)
    end
    n.literal
end

# Loud fallback (dev §1 / §4.5): an unhandled node kind warns and still
# renders its children, so prose is surfaced, never silently dropped.
function markdown_node(r::MarkdownRenderer, t, n, rules, context)
    @warn "render_markdown: unhandled CommonMark node kind" kind = typeof(t)
    h.span(markdown_children(r, n, rules, context)...)
end

"""
    markdown_parser(; extra_rules=[], math=true) -> CommonMark.Parser

Fresh parser per render: `Parser` holds mutable rule state. Enables the GFM
trio (pipe tables, strikethrough, task lists), TeX math unless `math=false`
(see [`render_markdown`](@ref)), plus caller-supplied `extra_rules` — rule
instances like `CommonMark.TableRule()`, enabled in order. Pair with the
[`render_markdown`](@ref) AST entry point to inspect the tree before
rendering it.
"""
function markdown_parser(; extra_rules::AbstractVector=[], math::Bool=true)
    parser = CommonMark.Parser()
    CommonMark.enable!(parser, CommonMark.TableRule())
    CommonMark.enable!(parser, CommonMark.StrikethroughRule())
    CommonMark.enable!(parser, CommonMark.TaskListRule())
    math && CommonMark.enable!(parser, _MarkdownMathRule())
    for rule in extra_rules
        CommonMark.enable!(parser, rule)
    end
    parser
end

"""
    render_markdown(text; rules=nothing, context=nothing,
                    renderer=DefaultMarkdownRenderer(), parser_rules=[],
                    math=true) -> Node
    render_markdown(ast::CommonMark.Node; rules=nothing, context=nothing,
                    renderer=DefaultMarkdownRenderer()) -> Node

Parse `text` as Markdown — CommonMark plus the GFM pipe-table,
strikethrough and task-list extensions, and TeX math — and render it into an
`h` node tree: a `div` wrapping one node per block. The AST form renders an
already-parsed tree instead, so a caller can inspect it first (parse with
[`markdown_parser`](@ref) for the same stock extensions).

Math is `\$…\$` or `\\(…\\)` inline and `\$\$…\$\$`, `\\[…\\]` or a
```` ```math ```` fence for display. The TeX is kept byte for byte (`\\_`
stays `\\_`), and code spans and fences never become math. Dollar signs
follow pandoc's rules, so money stays text: an opening `\$` must be
followed by a non-space and a closing `\$` preceded by one and not followed
by a digit (`costs \$5 and \$10` is text, `\$x_1\$` is math); inline
math holds no backtick, and `\\\$` is a literal dollar. Each formula renders
as an element marked `htmxo-math` that carries its TeX as text; the
[`htmx`](@ref) shell's [`math_script`](@ref) typesets it with KaTeX, and
until then, or without KaTeX, the reader sees the source. The Markdown
projection writes it back as `\$…\$` / `\$\$…\$\$`. `math=false` parses no
math, so every `\$` and `\\(` is text as before.

`rules` is the inline text-rule registry: a [`MarkdownRule`](@ref), or a
vector of them, matched against each joined text run outside links, code
and fences (earliest match wins; ties go to the earliest rule). `nothing`
(the default) renders with [`MARKDOWN_URL_RULE`](@ref), so bare URLs link;
pass `rules=MarkdownRule[]` for pure CommonMark with no linking.

`context` is passed to every rule `build` call, so one value scopes the
whole render.

`renderer` is the [`MarkdownRenderer`](@ref) the walk recurses through: pass
an app subtype with [`markdown_node`](@ref) overrides for the node kinds the
app renders its own way. `parser_rules` adds CommonMark parser rule
instances (like `CommonMark.TableRule()`) beyond the stock ones.

The `?plain` projection stays faithful: rules and overrides build ordinary
nodes, which the tag-dispatched markdown serializer projects back
(`h.a` → `[text](url)`, emphasis → `*text*`, and so on).

Raw HTML stays escaped text, except an empty link target:
`<span id="X"></span>` or `<a id="X"></a>`, with no other attribute and an id
of a letter followed by letters, digits, `_` or `-`, renders as that empty
element, so a `[text](#X)` link reaches it. The Markdown projection writes the
tags back as written.

Two deliberate cuts: a rule sees
escape-RESOLVED text (a `\\\\` marker is zero-width, so `\\\\#12` and `#12` are
indistinguishable downstream of the parser), and link destinations are NOT
scheme-filtered — a `javascript:` author link renders as written, exactly as
the HTML the parser would emit.
"""
function render_markdown(text::AbstractString; rules=nothing, context=nothing,
                         renderer::MarkdownRenderer=DefaultMarkdownRenderer(),
                         parser_rules::AbstractVector=[], math::Bool=true)
    render_markdown(markdown_parser(; extra_rules=parser_rules, math)(String(text));
        rules=rules, context=context, renderer=renderer)
end

function render_markdown(ast::CommonMark.Node; rules=nothing, context=nothing,
                         renderer::MarkdownRenderer=DefaultMarkdownRenderer())
    active = isnothing(rules) ? (MARKDOWN_URL_RULE,) :
        rules isa MarkdownRule ? (rules,) : tuple(rules...)
    markdown_node(renderer, ast, active, context)
end

"""
    decorated_link(label, href, entry; class, attrs, base=(;)) -> Node

Render one inline reference that gains metadata once its background batch
lands — the read-through half of deferred batched decoration. The fetch half
is a DynamicObjects `BackgroundCache` in batch mode (single-flighted drain,
TTL, backoff, logged failures); a [`MarkdownRule`](@ref) build function reads
`cache[key]` on the render path and hands the entry here:

```julia
MarkdownRule(r"([\\w-]+)#(\\d+)", (m, ctx) -> begin
    key = "\$(m.captures[1])#\$(m.captures[2])"
    decorated_link(m.match, "/items/\$(m.captures[2])", CACHE[key];
        class="app-ref", attrs=e -> (; data_state=e.state, title=e.title))
end)
```

While `entry` is `nothing` (batch not back — or the key failed and keeps no
previous value) the link is plain: an anchor with `class` and `base` only,
so first paint never waits. Once known, `attrs(entry)` — a `NamedTuple` of
extra attributes, or `nothing` for none — merges over `base` (a state colour,
a hover title). Anything else from `attrs` is an `ArgumentError`.

`base` is a `NamedTuple` of entry-independent attributes — a full-URL hover
title behind a compact label, `target`/`rel` on an external link — rendered
both before the entry lands and after. On a key collision the entry's
attribute wins, so `base` doubles as the unknown-state fallback (a bare-URL
title that becomes `"<state>: <title>\n<url>"` once known).

Live metadata is point-in-time, so the `?plain` projection carries the stable
part only: `[label](href)`, decorated or not.
"""
function decorated_link(label, href, entry; class::AbstractString, attrs::Function,
                        base::NamedTuple=(;))
    if isnothing(entry)
        extra = base
    else
        known = attrs(entry)
        isnothing(known) && (known = (;))
        known isa NamedTuple || throw(ArgumentError(
            "decorated_link attrs must return a NamedTuple (or nothing), got $(typeof(known))"))
        extra = merge(base, known)
    end
    h.a(label; href=string(href), class=String(class), extra...)
end

# Prose is authored as Markdown, so the HTML peer RENDERS it rather than showing
# its source. `render_markdown` builds real nodes (never `Raw` markup), and `h`
# escapes ordinary text children exactly once, so prose cannot smuggle HTML in.
_semantic_prose_node(prose::SemanticProse) = h.div(
    render_markdown(prose.markdown; math=prose.math); class="htmxo-semantic-prose")

# Delegates to the one table renderer rather than hand-building a second
# `h.table`. Sorting and download are interactive chrome, not part of a semantic
# projection, so both are off.
function _semantic_table_node(table::SemanticTable)
    isnothing(_semantic_table_columns(table.source)) &&
        return h.div(table.source; class="htmxo-semantic-table")
    render_table(table.source; sortable=false, download=false,
        class="striped htmxo-semantic-table")
end

_semantic_html_node(fields::SemanticFields) = _semantic_fields_node(fields)
_semantic_html_node(code::SemanticCode) = _semantic_code_node(code)
_semantic_html_node(status::SemanticStatus) = _semantic_status_node(status)
_semantic_html_node(unavailable::SemanticUnavailable) =
    _semantic_unavailable_node(unavailable)
_semantic_html_node(prose::SemanticProse) = _semantic_prose_node(prose)
_semantic_html_node(table::SemanticTable) = _semantic_table_node(table)
_semantic_html_node(metric::SemanticMetric) = h.div(
    h.span(metric.label; class="htmxo-semantic-metric-label"),
    h.strong(_semantic_metric_text(metric); class="htmxo-semantic-metric-value");
    class="htmxo-semantic-metric")
function _semantic_html_node(link::SemanticLink)
    if link.code
        label = h.code(link.label)
        link.external || return h.a(
            label; href=string(link.target), class="htmxo-semantic-link")
        # The glyph stays outside the code span: it marks the exit, it is
        # not part of the identifier — the same split the Markdown peer draws.
        return h.a(label, " ↗"; href=string(link.target), target="_blank",
            rel="noopener", class="htmxo-semantic-link")
    end
    link.external || return h.a(
        link.label; href=string(link.target), class="htmxo-semantic-link")
    # An external link leaves the app: new tab, safe attrs, and a trailing
    # glyph marking the exit — the same meaning the text peers carry.
    h.a(link.label * " ↗"; href=string(link.target), target="_blank",
        rel="noopener", class="htmxo-semantic-link")
end
# An action is an OPERATION, so the HTML peer carries the `hx-*` attributes that
# make it an in-place swap. `href` stays set so it degrades to a plain link.
_semantic_html_node(action::SemanticAction) = h.a(
    action.label; href=string(action.target), hx_get=string(action.target),
    hx_target="this", hx_swap="outerHTML", aria_live="polite",
    class="htmxo-semantic-action")
# An artifact is one download control: icon, label, then format and size in a
# `small`. The spaces between them separate the parts on a page without
# `pico_bridge()`, whose flex layout drops them. The icon is an SVG drawn in the
# text colour, not `⬇`, which Chrome draws as a blue colour emoji.
function _semantic_html_node(artifact::SemanticArtifact)
    facts = _semantic_artifact_facts(artifact, nothing)
    h.a(_semantic_artifact_icon(), " ", artifact.label,
        (isempty(facts) ? () : (" ", h.small(join(facts, " · "))))...;
        href=_semantic_artifact_href(artifact), download=artifact.name,
        type=artifact.mime, class="htmxo-semantic-artifact",
        (artifact.label == artifact.name ? (;) : (; title=artifact.name))...)
end
_semantic_artifact_icon() = h.svg(h.path(; d="M8 2v8M4.5 6.5 8 10l3.5-3.5M2.5 13.5h11");
    viewBox="0 0 16 16", width="1em", height="1em", fill="none", stroke="currentColor",
    stroke_width="1.6", stroke_linecap="round", stroke_linejoin="round", aria_hidden="true")
# The heading level a `SemanticSection` opens where it renders: 2 at the top of
# a document (below its title), one more inside each enclosing heading. It is a
# dynamic scope rather than an `IOContext` key, because it must reach every
# division a section holds: through an `h.div` (HTMX.jl's Markdown serializer
# re-wraps an `IO` it is handed, splitting its stream state), into a table cell
# rendered to a buffer of its own, and through the static-export walk, which
# builds HTML peers with no `IO` at all.
const _SEMANTIC_HEADING_LEVEL = ScopedValues.ScopedValue{Int}(2)
# Whether a section's or card's heading encloses the render: the level alone
# cannot tell the top of a document from the inside of a `level=1` section.
const _SEMANTIC_UNDER_HEADING = ScopedValues.ScopedValue{Bool}(false)

_semantic_heading_level() = _SEMANTIC_HEADING_LEVEL[]

# The level a section opens at here: its own `level`, or the one the nesting gives.
_semantic_opening_level(section::SemanticSection) =
    something(section.level, _semantic_heading_level())

# Runs `f(level)` for a heading at `level`, with everything below it one deeper.
_semantic_heading(f, level) = ScopedValues.with(() -> f(level),
    _SEMANTIC_HEADING_LEVEL => level + 1, _SEMANTIC_UNDER_HEADING => true)

# A card's Markdown title sits at the level a section would open there, and at
# `###` outside any section, below the page's `##` headings (an operation's
# title, say). Its HTML title is not a heading, so only Markdown asks for this.
_semantic_card_level() = _SEMANTIC_UNDER_HEADING[] ?
    _semantic_heading_level() : max(_semantic_heading_level(), 3)

# HTML and Markdown have six heading levels; a deeper division keeps the sixth.
_semantic_heading_tag(level) = Symbol(:h, min(level, 6))
_semantic_heading_sigil(level) = "#"^min(level, 6)

_semantic_html_node(card::SemanticCard) = h.article(
    h.header(h.strong(card.title)), card.children...;
    class="htmxo-semantic-card-body", _semantic_anchor_attrs(card.anchor)...)
_semantic_html_node(section::SemanticSection, level=_semantic_opening_level(section)) = h.section(
    h(_semantic_heading_tag(level), section.title), section.children...;
    class="htmxo-semantic-section", _semantic_anchor_attrs(section.anchor)...)
_semantic_html_node(group::SemanticGroup) = h.div(
    group.children...; class="htmxo-semantic-group")
# A disclosure with nothing to open for its reader has no peer at all, rather
# than a label over an empty body (`_shows_nothing`).
_semantic_html_node(disclosure::SemanticDisclosure) =
    _shows_nothing(disclosure, _ambient_audience()) ? nothing : h.details(
        h.summary(disclosure.summary), disclosure.children...;
        class="htmxo-semantic-disclosure")

# The default view renders directly; every later view sits collapsed in a
# native `<details>` under its label — switching with no client JavaScript.
# A later view with nothing to show its reader is left out with its label.
# Native elements already render the meaning (Pico styles them), so like the
# other containers this owes no framework CSS: stacked blocks need no layout.
function _semantic_html_node(alt::SemanticAlternatives)
    audience = _ambient_audience()
    h.div(
        first(alt.views),
        (h.details(h.summary(String(first(v))), last(v);
            class="htmxo-semantic-alternative")
         for v in alt.views[2:end] if !_shows_nothing(last(v), audience))...;
        class="htmxo-semantic-alternatives")
end

# HXML is a peer projection. Construct the target node tree explicitly so a
# semantic child is classified as a view/text node before HTMX's strict HXML
# serializer groups children into contexts.
_semantic_hxml_child(value::SemanticNode) = _semantic_hxml_node(value)
_semantic_hxml_child(value::Node) = value
_semantic_hxml_child(value) = h.span(value)

# Every leaf projects the same tree in both markup formats; only the containers
# differ, because their children need classifying first.
_semantic_hxml_node(value::SemanticNode) = _semantic_html_node(value)
# The exception: prose's HTML peer is `Raw` rendered markup, which is not HXML.
# Its HXML peer is the source as plain, escaped text.
_semantic_hxml_node(prose::SemanticProse) = h.span(prose.markdown)
_semantic_hxml_node(card::SemanticCard) = h.article(
    h.header(h.strong(card.title)),
    map(_semantic_hxml_child, card.children)...;
    class="htmxo-semantic-card-body", _semantic_anchor_attrs(card.anchor)...)
# Children are classified here, before serializing, so a nested section must
# already see the level below this one.
_semantic_hxml_node(section::SemanticSection, level=_semantic_opening_level(section)) = h.section(
    h(_semantic_heading_tag(level), section.title),
    _semantic_heading(_ -> map(_semantic_hxml_child, section.children), level)...;
    class="htmxo-semantic-section", _semantic_anchor_attrs(section.anchor)...)
_semantic_hxml_node(group::SemanticGroup) = h.div(
    map(_semantic_hxml_child, group.children)...; class="htmxo-semantic-group")
_semantic_hxml_node(disclosure::SemanticDisclosure) =
    _shows_nothing(disclosure, _ambient_audience()) ? nothing : h.details(
        h.summary(disclosure.summary),
        map(_semantic_hxml_child, disclosure.children)...;
        class="htmxo-semantic-disclosure")
# HXML readers cannot expand a `<details>` either, so like the text peers the
# HXML peer is the default view alone, classified before serializing.
_semantic_hxml_node(alt::SemanticAlternatives) =
    _semantic_hxml_child(first(alt.views))

# `SemanticPlot` is absent on purpose: it owns no markup of its own and
# delegates every format to its layer's own `show` (below).
for T in (SemanticCard, SemanticFields, SemanticCode, SemanticStatus,
          SemanticUnavailable, SemanticProse, SemanticTable, SemanticMetric,
          SemanticLink, SemanticAction, SemanticArtifact,
          SemanticGroup, SemanticDisclosure, SemanticAlternatives)
    @eval Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/html", value::$T) =
        _show_semantic_peer(io, mime, _semantic_html_node(value))
    @eval Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", value::$T) =
        _show_semantic_peer(io, mime, _semantic_hxml_node(value))
end
# An element without a peer (a disclosure with nothing to open) writes nothing.
_show_semantic_peer(io::IO, mime::MIME, ::Nothing) = nothing
_show_semantic_peer(io::IO, mime::MIME, peer) = show(io, mime, peer)
# A section's peer is built at the level it opens here and shown with its
# children one level below: they render inside its `show`, not before it.
for (mime, peer) in ((MIME"text/html", :_semantic_html_node),
                     (MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", :_semantic_hxml_node))
    @eval Base.show(io::IO, mime::$mime, section::SemanticSection) =
        _semantic_heading(_semantic_opening_level(section)) do level
            show(io, mime, $peer(section, level))
        end
end

_semantic_show_leaf(io::IO, mime::MIME, value) =
    showable(mime, value) ? show(io, mime, value) : print(io, value)
_semantic_show_leaf(io::IO, ::MIME, value::AbstractString) = print(io, value)

# A plot owns no markup: every format is its layer's own projection. That is
# what lets an AlgebraOfVega layer drop in with no registration here and no
# dependency — including its bounded `text/markdown` summary, so a markdown read
# of a plot is real content rather than an empty script node.
for mime in (MIME"text/html", MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml",
             MIME"text/markdown", MIME"text/plain")
    @eval Base.show(io::IO, mime::$mime, plot::SemanticPlot) =
        _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, plot.layer)
end

# A child that shows its reader nothing (`_shows_nothing`) takes no separator.
function _semantic_show_children(io::IO, mime::MIME, children, separator)
    audience = _ambient_audience()
    wrote = false
    for child in children
        _shows_nothing(child, audience) && continue
        wrote && print(io, separator)
        wrote = true
        _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, child)
    end
end

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", fields::SemanticFields)
    for (key, value) in fields.pairs
        print(io, "- **", key, ":** ")
        _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, value)
        print(io, '\n')
    end
end

function _semantic_code_fence(text)
    longest = maximum((length(match.match) for match in eachmatch(r"`+", text)); init=0)
    repeat("`", max(3, longest + 1))
end

# An inline code span that survives a label containing backticks: the fence
# run is one longer than the longest run inside, and a label touching the
# fence gets padded — the inline analogue of `_semantic_code_fence`.
function _semantic_inline_code(text)
    longest = maximum((length(match.match) for match in eachmatch(r"`+", text)); init=0)
    fence = repeat("`", max(1, longest + 1))
    if startswith(text, "`") || endswith(text, "`")
        return fence * " " * text * " " * fence
    end
    fence * text * fence
end

# A code block has no heading to carry its anchor, and Pandoc's Typst writer
# drops an identifier on the fence itself, so an anchored block is wrapped in a
# fenced div that carries it.
function Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", code::SemanticCode)
    fence = _semantic_code_fence(code.text)
    isempty(code.anchor) || print(io, "::: ", _markdown_anchor_attr(code.anchor), '\n')
    print(io, fence, code.language, '\n', code.text, '\n', fence)
    isempty(code.anchor) || print(io, "\n:::")
end

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", status::SemanticStatus) = print(
    io, _semantic_status_glyph(status.state), " **", status.label, "**",
    isempty(status.detail) ? "" : " — $(status.detail)")

Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", card::SemanticCard) =
    _semantic_heading(_semantic_card_level()) do level
        print(io, _semantic_heading_sigil(level), " ", card.title,
            _markdown_heading_anchor(card.anchor))
        _shows_nothing(card.children, _ambient_audience()) || print(io, "\n\n")
        _semantic_show_children(io, mime, card.children, "\n\n")
    end

# Same shape as the status peer above, for the same reason: the marker carries
# the meaning, the reason carries the why.
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", unavailable::SemanticUnavailable) =
    print(io, "∅ **", unavailable.texts.unavailable, "**",
        isempty(unavailable.reason) ? "" : " — $(unavailable.reason)")

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", prose::SemanticProse) =
    print(io, prose.markdown)

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", metric::SemanticMetric) =
    print(io, "**", metric.label, ":** ", _semantic_metric_text(metric))

# The exit glyph stays outside the code span: it marks the exit, not the
# identifier — the same split the HTML peer draws with `<code>`.
function Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", link::SemanticLink)
    label = link.code ? _semantic_inline_code(link.label) : link.label
    print(io, "[", label, link.external ? " ↗" : "", "](", string(link.target), ")")
end

# An action degrades to an ordinary link. No format but HTML can express "swap
# this in place", and the target is the honest remainder of the meaning — which
# is the whole reason the operation is held as data rather than as `hx-get`.
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", action::SemanticAction) =
    print(io, "[", action.label, "](", string(action.target), ")")

function Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", artifact::SemanticArtifact)
    facts = _semantic_artifact_facts(artifact, string("`", artifact.mime, "`"))
    print(io, "[", artifact.label, "](", _semantic_artifact_href(artifact), ")")
    isempty(facts) || print(io, " (", join(facts, ", "), ")")
end

# A Markdown table row is one line, and GFM splits a cell at every `|`, code
# spans included. So a cell's line breaks fold into an inline `<br>` and its
# `|` is escaped as `\|` — the rule HTMX.jl applies to an `h.table`'s cells, so
# a `SemanticTable` reads the same as a `render_table` of the same source, a
# float included (`format_number`). The tab-separated plain peer is data, like
# the ⬇ CSV, and keeps the exact value.
_semantic_table_cell(value) =
    replace(replace(strip(string(value)), r"\s*\n\s*" => "<br>"), "|" => "\\|")
_semantic_table_cell(value::AbstractFloat) = format_number(value)

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", table::SemanticTable)
    cells = _semantic_table_columns(table.source)
    isnothing(cells) && return _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, table.source)
    names, columns = cells
    print(io, "| ", join(map(_semantic_table_cell, names), " | "), " |\n")
    print(io, "|", repeat(" --- |", length(names)), "\n")
    for row in eachindex(first(columns))
        print(io, "| ",
            join((_semantic_table_cell(column[row]) for column in columns), " | "), " |\n")
    end
end

Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", section::SemanticSection) =
    _semantic_heading(_semantic_opening_level(section)) do level
        print(io, _semantic_heading_sigil(level), " ", section.title,
            _markdown_heading_anchor(section.anchor))
        _shows_nothing(section.children, _ambient_audience()) || print(io, "\n\n")
        _semantic_show_children(io, mime, section.children, "\n\n")
    end

Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", group::SemanticGroup) =
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, group.children, "\n\n")

# Not a header: a disclosure's label names a body, it does not open a document
# level. Collapsing is a viewport affordance HTML has and these formats do not,
# so the body is simply present. With nothing to open, there is no label either.
function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown",
                   disclosure::SemanticDisclosure)
    _shows_nothing(disclosure, _ambient_audience()) && return
    print(io, "**", disclosure.summary, "**\n\n")
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, disclosure.children, "\n\n")
end

# Collapsed views have no text analogue: the default view is the whole
# projection, and the alternatives' labels with it stay in HTML alone.
Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/markdown", alt::SemanticAlternatives) =
    _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, first(alt.views))

# Each projection above writes its element as if it opened the document, so
# among siblings HTMX.jl's serializer must delimit it, or they run together:
# `**A:** 1**B:** 2` for two metrics in a layout `div`, `### T2` mid-line after
# a card, and a closing code fence followed on its own line by the next
# element's text, which leaves the fence open. The block elements are the ones
# whose HTML peer is a block (div, p, pre, dl, table, article, section,
# details); a status, link, action or artifact is a `span` or an `<a>` in HTML
# and stays in its line here too.
HTMX.markdown_block(::SemanticNode) = true
HTMX.markdown_block(::Union{SemanticStatus,SemanticLink,SemanticAction,SemanticArtifact}) = false
# A disclosure that writes nothing delimits nothing.
HTMX.markdown_block(disclosure::SemanticDisclosure) =
    !_shows_nothing(disclosure, _ambient_audience())

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", fields::SemanticFields)
    for (key, value) in fields.pairs
        print(io, key, ": ")
        _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, value)
        print(io, '\n')
    end
end

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", code::SemanticCode) = print(io, code.text)
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", status::SemanticStatus) = print(
    io, '[', status.label, ']', isempty(status.detail) ? "" : " $(status.detail)")

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", card::SemanticCard)
    print(io, card.title)
    _shows_nothing(card.children, _ambient_audience()) || print(io, "\n\n")
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, card.children, "\n\n")
end

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", unavailable::SemanticUnavailable) = print(
    io, '[', unavailable.texts.unavailable, ']',
    isempty(unavailable.reason) ? "" : " $(unavailable.reason)")

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", prose::SemanticProse) =
    print(io, prose.markdown)

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", metric::SemanticMetric) =
    print(io, metric.label, ": ", _semantic_metric_text(metric))

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", link::SemanticLink) = print(
    io, link.label, link.external ? " ↗" : "", " (", string(link.target), ")")

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", action::SemanticAction) =
    print(io, action.label, " (", string(action.target), ")")

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/plain", artifact::SemanticArtifact) = print(
    io, artifact.label, " [", join(_semantic_artifact_facts(artifact, artifact.mime), ", "), "]",
    isnothing(artifact.target) ? "" : " ($(artifact.target))")

# The tab-separated peer keeps exact values, as the ⬇ CSV does: a column of
# `IntervalEstimate`s is three columns, the estimate and its two bounds.
function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", table::SemanticTable)
    cells = _semantic_table_columns(table.source)
    isnothing(cells) && return _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, table.source)
    names, columns = cells
    wide = [any(v -> v isa IntervalEstimate, column) for column in columns]
    texts = _EN_TABLE_TEXTS
    print(io, join((w ? join((n, "$n / $(texts.sort_lower)", "$n / $(texts.sort_upper)"), '\t') : string(n)
                    for (n, w) in zip(names, wide)), '\t'), '\n')
    for row in eachindex(first(columns))
        print(io, join((w ? _semantic_interval_fields(column[row]) : string(column[row])
                        for (column, w) in zip(columns, wide)), '\t'), '\n')
    end
end
_semantic_interval_fields(x) = string(x, "\t\t")

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", section::SemanticSection)
    print(io, section.title)
    _shows_nothing(section.children, _ambient_audience()) || print(io, "\n\n")
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, section.children, "\n\n")
end

Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", group::SemanticGroup) =
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, group.children, "\n\n")

function Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain",
                   disclosure::SemanticDisclosure)
    _shows_nothing(disclosure, _ambient_audience()) && return
    print(io, disclosure.summary, "\n\n")
    _semantic_show_children(io, mime, disclosure.children, "\n\n")
end

Base.show(io::IO, mime::MIME"text/plain", alt::SemanticAlternatives) =
    _semantic_show_leaf(io, mime, first(alt.views))

# Normalize a route return value into something `auto` can render, leaving
# every value that already has an HTML representation untouched. Arrays stay
# element-wise (an array of Nodes is still a fragment) and OOB-swap Pairs keep
# their target id, so this only ever adds rendering where there was none.
_html_value(val) = showable(MIME"text/html"(), val) ? val : generic_html(val)
_html_value(val::AbstractString) = val
_html_value(val::AbstractArray) = map(_html_value, val)
_html_value((content, id)::Pair) = _html_value(content) => id

# A matrix of plain data renders as a grid. Flattening it element-wise like any
# other array would emit its cells in column-major order with the shape gone —
# a `Matrix{Float64}` result reaching the client as an unlabelled, mis-ordered
# run of numbers. A matrix holding anything already renderable (a matrix of
# Nodes) keeps the element-wise fragment behavior, so this only changes the
# shapes that previously had no rendering at all.
_html_value(val::AbstractMatrix) =
    any(x -> showable(MIME"text/html"(), x), val) ? map(_html_value, val) : generic_html(val)

"""
    to_response(val)

Convert any Julia value to an `HTTP.Response`. Handles Nodes, plain strings,
arrays of either, and HTMX OOB-swap Pairs (via `auto`). Values that are
already an `HTTP.Response` pass through unchanged. Values with no `text/html`
`show` method render via [`generic_html`](@ref) instead of raising.
"""
to_response(val::HTTP.Response) = val
to_response(val) = auto(_html_value(val); wrap=_html_response)

"""
    MIMEResponse(content_type, body)

Small wrapper for routes that return non-HTML content (JS, JSON, CSS,
SVG, plain text, etc.) without hand-rolling an `HTTP.Response`.
`_resolve_response` converts it to `HTTP.Response(200,
["Content-Type" => content_type]; body)`. The recording flow's
`_save_typed_response` then dispatches on Content-Type to pick the
right file extension (`.js` for `application/javascript`, `.json` for
`application/json`, etc.). One line, fully recordable, MIME-aware.

```julia
@get aov_runtime_js = MIMEResponse("application/javascript",
    strip_script(sprint(show, MIME"text/html"(), vega_runtime())))
```
"""
struct MIMEResponse
    content_type::String
    body
end

to_response(m::MIMEResponse) =
    HTTP.Response(200, ["Content-Type" => m.content_type]; body=m.body)

# Dispatch hook used by `_resolve_response` / `_resolve_response_nested`
# to bypass the HTML-page pipeline for handler return values that are
# already a final HTTP response shape. Methods return the finalized
# `HTTP.Response` (or `nothing` for values that should still flow
# through the normal HTML / markdown / HX pipeline). Add a method to
# this function to teach the framework about a new escape-hatch type.
_finalized_response(::Any) = nothing
_finalized_response(r::HTTP.Response) = r
_finalized_response(m::MIMEResponse) = to_response(m)

"""
    to_markdown_string(val; audience=nothing, lang=nothing) -> String

The Markdown of `val`, as a `?plain` or `Accept: text/markdown` answer holds it:
`show(io, MIME"text/markdown"(), val)` once interactive chrome (forms, inputs,
buttons) is dropped, or `""` when nothing else is left.

`audience` (`:public` or `:private`) is who the Markdown is for:
[`private`](@ref) content shows its stand-in, or nothing, for `:public`, and in
full for `:private`. Without it, `val` renders for the audience of the answer
it is rendered in (a route's `export_audience(__req__)`), and for the public
outside any request.

`lang` (a BCP 47 tag such as `"de-DE"`) is the language of the words
HTMXObjects writes itself, such as the label of private content ("nicht
öffentlich"). Without it they follow the answer `val` is rendered in
([`HTMXObjects.page_lang`](@ref)), and are English outside any route. The
Markdown keeps the language it is rendered in.

Library code that assembles a document from values it renders itself passes
the document's audience and language, also when it runs inside a live request,
which is `:private`:

```julia
chunks = [to_markdown_string(section; audience, lang="de-DE") for section in sections]
pdf_report(join(chunks, "\\n\\n"), "konzept.pdf"; title="Konzept", lang="de-DE", audience)
```

An `audience` other than `:public` and `:private`, and a `lang` that is not a
language tag (`"German"`), are an `ArgumentError`.
"""
to_markdown_string(val; audience=nothing, lang=nothing) =
    _with_page_lang(() -> _to_markdown_string(val, audience), _check_lang(lang, "to_markdown_string"))
# HTMX.jl defines show for Node; users can extend with show(io, MIME"text/markdown", val::MyType).
# Interactive chrome (form/input/textarea/button) is dropped first by
# `_strip_md_chrome` so a `?plain`/`?markdown` read never serializes a form's
# fields into prose (e.g. an edit-form textarea pre-filled with the raw body).
# An all-chrome val strips to `nothing` → empty markdown.
_to_markdown_string(val, ::Nothing) = let stripped = _strip_md_chrome(val)
    isnothing(stripped) ? "" : sprint(show, MIME"text/markdown"(), stripped)
end
_to_markdown_string(val, audience) =
    _with_audience(() -> _to_markdown_string(val, nothing), _check_audience(audience, "to_markdown_string"))

# --- Dual-view wrappers: html_only / markdown_only ---

"""
    html_only(content)

Wrap content that should only appear in HTML responses. In markdown mode,
this produces an empty string. Use for interactive elements, styling, badges,
etc. that have no meaningful text representation.
"""
struct HtmlOnly
    content
end
html_only(content) = HtmlOnly(content)

"""
    markdown_only(text)

Wrap text that should only appear in markdown responses. In HTML mode,
this is invisible (renders as empty string). Use for structured markdown
output (code blocks with markers, custom formatting) that has no HTML equivalent.
"""
struct MarkdownOnly
    text::String
end
markdown_only(text) = MarkdownOnly(text)

# HTML rendering: HtmlOnly renders its content, MarkdownOnly is invisible
to_response(val::HtmlOnly) = to_response(val.content)
to_response(::MarkdownOnly) = to_response("")
auto(val::HtmlOnly; wrap) = auto(val.content; wrap)
auto(::MarkdownOnly; wrap) = ""
# Primitive semantic results (for example a materialized Float64 from an
# @mmap property) have no MIME"text/html" show method. Render them as escaped
# text through the existing String path instead of leaking a caught 200.
auto(val::Union{Number,Symbol,Bool,Char}; wrap) = auto(string(val); wrap)

# Semantic operations may return a small NamedTuple carrying an HTML fragment
# alongside structured metadata. Treat its values as an HTML fragment sequence
# instead of falling through to `repr("text/html", ::NamedTuple)`, which has no
# MIME renderer and turns an otherwise valid HX response into a caught error.
auto(val::NamedTuple; wrap) = auto(collect(values(val)); wrap)

# HTML rendering via show dispatch — HtmlOnly renders content, MarkdownOnly is invisible
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", val::HtmlOnly) = show(io, m, val.content)
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/html", ::MarkdownOnly) = nothing

# Markdown rendering via show dispatch — HtmlOnly skipped, MarkdownOnly prints text
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", ::HtmlOnly) = nothing
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", val::MarkdownOnly) = print(io, val.text)

# --- Audience: content that public views leave out ---

"""
    private(content; public=nothing, mark=true)
    private(; public=nothing, mark=true) do … end

Content that a public reader must not see, such as a model's full
specification. The live app and a private export show `content`. A public
answer shows `public` in its place: nothing (the default), or a stand-in such
as a short summary of the model.

A private reader sees which parts no public answer holds: `content` is
outlined and labelled "not public" ("nicht öffentlich" on a German page). In
HTML it sits in a `div` or `span` of class `htmxo-private`, or carries that
class itself where a wrapper would break the markup (a table row or cell, a
list item, a `summary`); the outline and the label, in the language of the
element's `lang`, come from [`htmxo_utility_styles`](@ref), which every
[`htmx`](@ref) page carries. In Markdown a block is a fenced div
`::: {.htmxo-private}` that opens with the label, `*not public*`, and inline
content a span `[*not public:* …]{.htmxo-private}`. The label is in the
language of `HTMXObjects.page_lang()`: a route's `__lang__`, or the `lang` of
[`to_markdown_string`](@ref)`(x; lang)`, of `dispatch(…; lang)` and of a
[`pdf_report`](@ref) of a value. A Markdown string keeps the language it was
rendered in. Private content inside private content is marked once.
`mark=false` shows `content` without the mark.

Public answers are those of an export, unless it asks for `audience=:private`:
a [`StaticExport`](@ref) or [`export_site`](@ref) bundle, a
[`pdf_report`](@ref) of a value, a `dispatch` made outside any request (the
Markdown a concept PDF inlines), and a `dispatch(...; audience=:public)`.
Anything rendered outside any request is public too, unless it is rendered
with [`to_markdown_string`](@ref)`(x; audience=:private)` or inside
[`with_audience`](@ref)`(f, :private)`. The live app always shows everything.
[`export_audience`](@ref) tells a route which audience it answers for.

`private` works wherever a value is rendered, in HTML, HXML, Markdown and plain
text: as a child of an element, in a semantic element, or as a route's whole
answer. A [`SemanticDisclosure`](@ref) left with nothing to open renders
nothing, label included, and so does a [`SemanticAlternatives`](@ref) view left
with nothing to show. A route whose whole answer is `private(x)` without a
stand-in is a private page.
A public answer to it is a `404` carrying the header `X-HTMXO-Private: true`
and nothing else; [`export_site`](@ref) writes no file for it and leaves out
every link to it. A static export never follows a link inside content it
leaves out.

A public export renders `public` while it walks the answer, so anything
rendered before the walk cannot be left out: markup a route renders into a
`Raw` string or a JSON payload. The live page marks private content with the
HTML comments `<!--htmxo-private-->` and `<!--/htmxo-private-->`, so such
markup inside a public answer makes the export fail
([`PrivateContentEscaped`](@ref)) instead of publishing it.

`content` can be a function (a `do` block) that builds it: it is called only
when the content is shown to a private reader, so a public answer neither
computes nor reads what only a private reader sees, and nothing it throws can
fail a public answer. A route's whole answer is built once, while the answer is
made: a [`CallerFault`](@ref) it throws answers with its status, and any other
exception is a recorded route error, as if the route body had thrown it. A
part of a page is built whenever it is rendered for a private reader, so keep
the function free of side effects. It runs after the route's operation, so the
`:auto` transport does not poll it.

```julia
@get model() = h.article(
    h.h2("Model"),
    private(SemanticCode(:stan, stan_code(spec));
            public = h.p("A hierarchical model of the measured doses.")))
@get specification() = private(SemanticCode(:stan, stan_code(spec)))  # a private page
@get document(id::String) = private() do  # read for a private reader only
    file = get(DOCUMENTS, id, nothing)
    isnothing(file) && throw(CallerFault(404, "no document \$(repr(id))"))
    MIMEResponse("application/pdf", read(file))
end
```
"""
struct Private
    content::Any
    public::Any
    mark::Bool
end
private(content; public=nothing, mark::Bool=true) = Private(content, public, mark)

# Content a function builds is built when it is shown.
_built(content) = content
_built(build::Function) = build()

# --- The navigation of `pico_page`'s header ---
#
# Here, after `Private`, because a private entry stays private.

# The nav entries as list items, the link to the page being read marked.
function _site_nav_items(nav)
    meta = _page_meta()
    current = _site_nav_current(nav, get(meta, :route, nothing), get(meta, :prefix, nothing))
    Any[_site_nav_item(entry, current) for entry in nav]
end

_site_nav_item(entry::Pair, current) = h.li(h.a(first(entry); href=last(entry),
    (!isnothing(current) && _site_nav_path(last(entry)) == current ? (; aria_current="page") : (;))...))
# A private entry stays private: its content, built when it is shown, and its
# stand-in are entries too.
_site_nav_item(entry::Private, current) =
    Private(_site_nav_item(entry.content, current), _site_nav_item(entry.public, current), entry.mark)
_site_nav_item(build::Function, current) = () -> _site_nav_item(build(), current)
_site_nav_item(entry, current) = entry

# The path of the entry that names the page being read: of the entries whose
# path is `route` or above it, the longest; the app's home (its mount prefix,
# `/` without one) names only itself. `nothing` outside a request.
_site_nav_current(nav, ::Nothing, prefix) = nothing
function _site_nav_current(nav, route::AbstractString, prefix)
    here = _site_nav_path(route)
    home = rstrip(something(prefix, ""), '/')
    best = nothing
    for path in _site_nav_paths(nav)
        path == here || (path != home && startswith(here, path * "/")) || continue
        (isnothing(best) || length(path) > length(best)) && (best = path)
    end
    best
end

_site_nav_paths(nav) = String[p for entry in nav for p in _site_nav_entry_paths(entry) if !isnothing(p)]
_site_nav_entry_paths(entry::Pair) = (_site_nav_path(last(entry)),)
_site_nav_entry_paths(entry::Private) = (_site_nav_entry_paths(entry.content)..., _site_nav_entry_paths(entry.public)...)
# Content built when it is shown is not built to find the page being read.
_site_nav_entry_paths(entry) = ()

# An address as the nav compares it: a path on this site, without its query
# and trailing slash, percent-encoding undone (`""` is the site's root).
# `nothing` for an address with a fragment, a relative one or another site's.
_site_nav_path(href) = nothing
function _site_nav_path(href::AbstractString)
    (startswith(href, "/") && !startswith(href, "//") && !occursin('#', href)) || return nothing
    path = first(split(href, '?'; limit=2))
    rstrip(_percent_decode_lenient(path), '/')
end

# The audience of the answer being rendered: `:public` or `:private`, and
# `:public` outside any request, export and `with_audience`, so a value
# rendered without asking cannot show private content. A route handler sets it
# to its request's `export_audience` for the whole request, and every task
# started for it inherits it: HTMXObjects' own and DynamicObjects' deferred and
# `@queued` work; `pdf_report` and `to_markdown_string` set their own while
# they render a value.
const _AUDIENCE = ScopedValues.ScopedValue{Symbol}(:public)
_ambient_audience() = _AUDIENCE[]
_with_audience(f, audience::Symbol) = ScopedValues.with(f, _AUDIENCE => audience)

_check_audience(audience, who) = audience in (:public, :private) ? audience :
    throw(ArgumentError("$who audience must be :public or :private (got $(repr(audience)))"))

"""
    with_audience(f, audience)

Call `f()` and return its value, rendering everything it renders for
`audience`: `:public` or `:private`. [`private`](@ref) content shows its
stand-in, or nothing, for `:public`, and in full for `:private`.

Rendering already follows the answer it is part of: a route renders for
[`export_audience`](@ref)`(__req__)`, and so does the work it runs in other
tasks: an `:auto` or `@queued` operation's body and a task the route spawns.
Outside any request it is for the public. Use `with_audience` where code
renders for someone else: HTML it builds itself (`repr("text/html", x)`, an
[`HTMLSnapshot`](@ref)) for a given reader. For Markdown,
[`to_markdown_string`](@ref)`(x; audience)` does the same.

```julia
html = with_audience(:private) do
    repr("text/html", page)          # everything, as the live app shows it
end
public_summary = with_audience(() -> repr("text/html", summary), :public)
```

An `audience` other than `:public` and `:private` is an `ArgumentError`.
"""
with_audience(f, audience) = _with_audience(f, _check_audience(audience, "with_audience"))

# What an answer for `audience` shows of `p`: for a private reader its content,
# marked as no public answer's part.
_shown(p::Private, audience) = audience === :public ? p.public : _marked(p, _built(p.content))
_shown(p::Private) = _shown(p, _ambient_audience())

# Private content as a private reader sees it: marked, so the reader can tell
# what a public export leaves out (snag mark-a-tender-pr-fd4b7782). Every
# projection treats it as its content, plus the mark.
struct _PrivateMark
    content::Any
end

# Inside marked content a private part is not marked again. The scope covers
# rendering the content and every walk that rebuilds it for a later render.
const _IN_PRIVATE_MARK = ScopedValues.ScopedValue{Bool}(false)
_in_private_mark() = _IN_PRIVATE_MARK[]
_in_private_mark(f) = ScopedValues.with(f, _IN_PRIVATE_MARK => true)

_marked(p::Private, ::Nothing) = nothing
_marked(p::Private, content) = p.mark && !_in_private_mark() ? _PrivateMark(content) : content

# The label, in the language of the answer; the page's stylesheet writes it in
# each element's (`htmxo_utility_styles`).
const _PRIVATE_LABELS = (en = "not public", de = "nicht öffentlich")
# A CSS string literal, ASCII only, so it reads the same in a linked stylesheet
# of any encoding.
_css_string(s) = string('"', join(isascii(c) ? (c in ('"', '\\') ? "\\" * c : string(c)) :
    "\\" * lpad(string(UInt32(c); base=16), 6, '0') for c in s), '"')
_private_label(lang) = _lang_entry(_PRIVATE_LABELS, lang)
# A table of words by language (`(en = …, de = …)`): the entry of `lang`'s
# primary language, English for any other and outside a page.
_lang_entry(table::NamedTuple, ::Nothing) = table.en
_lang_entry(table::NamedTuple, lang::AbstractString) =
    get(table, Symbol(lowercase(first(split(lang, '-')))), table.en)

# Elements that must stay a direct child of their parent carry the mark
# themselves; anything else sits in a `span` when it is phrasing content (text
# included), else in a `div`.
const _PRIVATE_IN_PLACE_TAGS = Set{Symbol}((:tr, :td, :th, :thead, :tbody, :tfoot, :caption,
    :col, :colgroup, :li, :dt, :dd, :option, :optgroup, :summary, :legend, :figcaption,
    :source, :track, :html, :head, :body, :title, :meta, :link, :base, :script, :style))
const _PRIVATE_INLINE_TAGS = Set{Symbol}((:a, :abbr, :b, :bdi, :bdo, :br, :cite, :code, :data,
    :del, :dfn, :em, :i, :img, :input, :ins, :kbd, :label, :mark, :meter, :output, :progress,
    :q, :s, :samp, :select, :small, :span, :strong, :sub, :sup, :textarea, :time, :u, :var,
    :wbr, :button, :svg, :math))
_private_block(x) = HTMX.markdown_block(x)
_private_block(node::Node) = !(Symbol(lowercase(string(HTMX.tag(node)))) in _PRIVATE_INLINE_TAGS)
_private_block(xs::Union{AbstractVector,Tuple}) = any(_private_block, xs)
_private_block(x::Union{HtmlOnly,_PrivateMark}) = _private_block(x.content)
_private_block(p::Private) = _private_block(_shown(p))

_private_in_place(x) = false
_private_in_place(node::Node) = Symbol(lowercase(string(HTMX.tag(node)))) in _PRIVATE_IN_PLACE_TAGS
_private_wrapped(content) = (_private_block(content) ? h.div : h.span)(content; class="htmxo-private")

_private_mark_node(doc::HTMLDocument) = doc
_private_mark_node(content) = _private_wrapped(content)
# Rows of a table body, items of a list: each carries the mark.
_private_mark_node(xs::AbstractVector) =
    !isempty(xs) && all(_private_in_place, xs) ? map(_private_mark_node, xs) : _private_wrapped(xs)
function _private_mark_node(node::Node)
    _private_in_place(node) || return _private_wrapped(node)
    attrs = copy(HTMX.attrs(node))
    class = get(attrs, :class, nothing)
    attrs[:class] = class isa AbstractString && !isempty(class) ? class * " htmxo-private" : "htmxo-private"
    Node(HTMX.tag(node), attrs, copy(HTMX.children(node)))
end

Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", x::_PrivateMark) =
    _in_private_mark(() -> _show_html_content(io, m, _private_mark_node(x.content)))
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", x::_PrivateMark) =
    _in_private_mark(() -> _show_html_content(io, m, x.content))
# A block is a fenced div that opens with the label, inline content (and a
# block in a table cell, which a fence would break) a span; Pandoc keeps both
# (Quarto shows the label), HTMX.jl writes its language parts the same way. An
# element that marks itself in HTML (a row, a cell, a list item) starts with
# the label instead, so its table or list stays one.
const _PRIVATE_LABELLED_TAGS = Set{Symbol}((:li, :td, :th, :dt, :dd, :summary, :caption,
    :legend, :figcaption))
_private_md_labelled(x, label) = x
_private_md_labelled(xs::AbstractVector, label) = map(x -> _private_md_labelled(x, label), xs)
function _private_md_labelled(node::Node, label)
    tag = Symbol(lowercase(string(HTMX.tag(node))))
    kids = HTMX.children(node)
    if tag in _PRIVATE_LABELLED_TAGS
        kids = Any[h.em(label * ":"), " ", kids...]
    else
        part = tag === :tr ? (:td, :th) : tag in (:thead, :tbody, :tfoot) ? (:tr,) : ()
        i = findfirst(c -> c isa Node && Symbol(lowercase(string(HTMX.tag(c)))) in part, kids)
        isnothing(i) && return node
        kids = copy(kids)
        kids[i] = _private_md_labelled(kids[i], label)
    end
    Node(HTMX.tag(node), copy(HTMX.attrs(node)), kids)
end
_private_md_in_place(x) = _private_in_place(x)
_private_md_in_place(xs::AbstractVector) = !isempty(xs) && all(_private_in_place, xs)
# HTMX.jl's Markdown stream knows where a block starts and a line ends, so the
# fence is laid out as its own fenced divs (`_md_fenced_div`, which writes an
# id and a language but no class). Outside a stream the mark enters one.
function Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/markdown", x::_PrivateMark)
    io isa HTMX._MDStream || return show(io, m, h.div(x))
    label = _private_label(page_lang())
    _in_private_mark() do
        if _private_md_in_place(x.content)
            foreach(c -> show(io, m, c), vcat(_private_md_labelled(x.content, label)))
        elseif _private_block(x.content) && !get(io, :md_table_cell, false)
            HTMX._md_block_start(io)
            HTMX._md_blank_line(io)
            print(io, "::: {.htmxo-private}\n\n*", label, "*")
            show(io, m, h.div(x.content))
            HTMX._md_end_line(io)
            print(io, ":::\n\n")
        else
            print(io, "[*", label, ":* ")
            show(io, m, h.span(x.content))
            print(io, "]{.htmxo-private}")
        end
    end
end
# Plain text names the mark in brackets, as `SemanticUnavailable`'s plain peer
# names its own.
function Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/plain", x::_PrivateMark)
    print(io, "[", _private_label(page_lang()), "] ")
    _in_private_mark(() -> foreach(c -> _semantic_show_leaf(io, m, c), vcat(x.content)))
end
HTMX.markdown_block(x::_PrivateMark) = _private_block(x.content)
_finalized_response(x::_PrivateMark) = _finalized_response(x.content)
filter_errors(x::_PrivateMark) = _in_private_mark(() -> filter_errors(x.content))
_filter_errors_child(x::_PrivateMark) = _in_private_mark(() -> _filter_errors_child(x.content))
# An element labelled in place is that element again, so the table or list it
# belongs to still holds it (HTMX.jl's table writer reads only nodes).
function _strip_md_chrome(x::_PrivateMark)
    stripped = _in_private_mark(() -> _strip_md_chrome(x.content))
    isnothing(stripped) && return nothing
    _private_md_in_place(stripped) ? _private_md_labelled(stripped, _private_label(page_lang())) :
        _PrivateMark(stripped)
end
_strip_md_chrome_child(x::_PrivateMark) = _strip_md_chrome(x)
# Semantic content rescued out of dropped chrome stays marked.
function _rescue_semantic!(out, x::_PrivateMark)
    rescued = Any[]
    _in_private_mark(() -> _rescue_semantic!(rescued, x.content))
    isempty(rescued) || push!(out, _PrivateMark(rescued))
    nothing
end

# Whether `x` shows a reader of `audience` nothing at all, judged by its
# structure: `nothing`, private content without a stand-in for a public reader,
# or a vector, tuple, group or disclosure holding only such values. Anything
# else is content, an empty string or element included, and so is a
# `private() do … end` block for a private reader: it is not run to find out.
# A disclosure with nothing to open renders nothing, label included, and so
# does a collapsed alternative.
_shows_nothing(x, audience) = false
_shows_nothing(::Nothing, audience) = true
_shows_nothing(xs::Union{AbstractVector,Tuple}, audience) =
    all(x -> _shows_nothing(x, audience), xs)
_shows_nothing(p::Private, audience) = audience === :public ?
    _shows_nothing(p.public, audience) :
    !(p.content isa Function) && _shows_nothing(p.content, audience)
_shows_nothing(group::SemanticGroup, audience) = _shows_nothing(group.children, audience)
_shows_nothing(disclosure::SemanticDisclosure, audience) =
    _shows_nothing(disclosure.children, audience)
_shows_nothing(x::_PrivateMark, audience) = _shows_nothing(x.content, audience)

const _PRIVATE_OPEN = "<!--htmxo-private-->"
const _PRIVATE_CLOSE = "<!--/htmxo-private-->"

function Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", p::Private)
    _ambient_audience() === :public && return _show_html_content(io, m, p.public)
    print(io, _PRIVATE_OPEN)
    _show_html_content(io, m, _shown(p, :private))
    print(io, _PRIVATE_CLOSE)
end
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", p::Private) =
    _show_html_content(io, m, _shown(p))
# Without this, plain text would print the struct, private content included.
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/plain", p::Private) = _show_html_content(io, m, _shown(p))
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/markdown", p::Private) =
    let shown = _shown(p)
        isnothing(shown) || show(io, m, shown)
    end
HTMX.markdown_block(p::Private) = HTMX.markdown_block(_shown(p))
# A whole answer, as the route had returned what it shows.
auto(p::Private; wrap) = let shown = _shown(p)
    isnothing(shown) ? wrap("") : auto(_html_value(shown); wrap)
end
_finalized_response(p::Private) = _finalized_response(_shown(p))
filter_errors(p::Private) = filter_errors(_shown(p))
_filter_errors_child(p::Private) = _filter_errors_child(_shown(p))
_strip_md_chrome(p::Private) = _strip_md_chrome(_shown(p))
_strip_md_chrome_child(p::Private) = _strip_md_chrome_child(_shown(p))
_rescue_semantic!(out, p::Private) = _rescue_semantic!(out, _shown(p))

# Content renders as an element's child does: its own markup, else escaped
# text; several values in order. Plain text is a semantic element's leaf.
_show_html_content(io, m, ::Nothing) = nothing
_show_html_content(io, m, xs::Union{AbstractVector,Tuple}) =
    foreach(x -> _show_html_content(io, m, x), xs)
_show_html_content(io, m, x) = _show_content_leaf(io, m, x)
_show_content_leaf(io, m, x) =
    showable(m, x) ? show(io, m, x) : print(io, HTMX.escape(string(x)))
_show_content_leaf(io, m::MIME"text/plain", x) = _semantic_show_leaf(io, m, x)

# A route whose whole answer is private content without a stand-in is a
# private page; a public answer to it holds nothing of it.
_private_page(_) = false
_private_page(p::Private) = isnothing(p.public)
_private_page(req::HTTP.Request, val) = export_audience(req) === :public && _private_page(val)
_private_page_response() = HTTP.Response(404, ["X-HTMXO-Private" => "true"]; body="")
_private_page_response(resp::HTTP.Response) = HTTP.header(resp, "X-HTMXO-Private") == "true"
# A whole answer's private content is built once, for a private reader, before
# the answer is made from it; a public reader's answer builds none.
_built_answer(req::HTTP.Request, val) = val
_built_answer(req::HTTP.Request, p::Private) =
    export_audience(req) === :public ? p : Private(_built(p.content), p.public, p.mark)

"""
    PrivateContentEscaped <: Exception

Thrown when a public answer (a public static export or `dispatch(...;
audience=:public)`, a recording, a public `pdf_report`) still holds content
marked [`private`](@ref): markup rendered for the live app before the export
could leave it out, such as a `Raw` string built from `repr("text/html", …)` or
an `HTMLSnapshot` held inside a value the export cannot walk. Render that
markup from the value instead, or build it for the request's
[`export_audience`](@ref).
"""
struct PrivateContentEscaped <: Exception
    what::String
end

Base.showerror(io::IO, e::PrivateContentEscaped) = print(io,
    "PrivateContentEscaped: ", e.what, " is for a public audience but holds content marked ",
    "`private` (", _PRIVATE_OPEN, "), rendered before the export could leave it out. ",
    "Render that markup from the value, or for the request's `export_audience`.")

# Refuse a public answer that still holds private markup.
_check_public(body, what) =
    _body_contains(body, _PRIVATE_OPEN) ? throw(PrivateContentEscaped(what)) : body
_check_public(resp::HTTP.Response, what) = (_check_public(_response_bytes(resp), what); resp)

"""
    HTMLSnapshot(value)

Serialize `value`'s HTML once, at construction, and keep `value` for every
other projection. Use it when an application retains a generated surface across
responses: an HTML response or HX swap writes the stored bytes, while
`?plain`/`?markdown`/`Accept: text/markdown`, `?error` and static export project
the retained `value` exactly as if the route had returned `value` itself. One
build serves every projection.

The snapshot is projection-transparent wherever it sits: returned from a route,
or nested as a child of a larger Node (`h.div(header, snapshot)`). The Node
error filter, Markdown chrome stripping and static export all descend into it.
The `repr("text/html", value)` + `HTMX.Raw` recipe cannot do this: Raw bytes
have no Markdown or error projection.

It is a value, not a cache. It holds no key, invalidation or request identity,
and captures whatever context `value` was built under. Retain it only under the
application-owned identity documented in "Reusing generated markup" (root and
provider lifetime, external prefix, selected indices, request inputs, controls
and domains, presentation settings, and the [`export_audience`](@ref) the
stored bytes show [`private`](@ref) content for).
"""
struct HTMLSnapshot
    value::Any
    html::String
    HTMLSnapshot(value) = new(value, repr(MIME"text/html"(), _html_value(value)))
end

Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/html", s::HTMLSnapshot) = print(io, s.html)
Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", s::HTMLSnapshot) =
    print(io, to_markdown_string(s.value))
Base.show(io::IO, s::HTMLSnapshot) =
    print(io, "HTMLSnapshot(", ncodeunits(s.html), " bytes)")
# Every projection other than HTML reads the retained value. The walker
# methods for static export sit beside `_static_walk_child`.
filter_errors(s::HTMLSnapshot) = filter_errors(s.value)
_private_block(s::HTMLSnapshot) = _private_block(s.value)
_filter_errors_child(s::HTMLSnapshot) = _filter_errors_child(s.value)
_strip_md_chrome(s::HTMLSnapshot) = _strip_md_chrome(s.value)
_strip_md_chrome_child(s::HTMLSnapshot) = _strip_md_chrome_child(s.value)
_rescue_semantic!(out, s::HTMLSnapshot) = _rescue_semantic!(out, s.value)

# A control whose label belongs to the document: a sortable column's name, a
# `detail_toggle=:label` row's name, a branch row's label. HTML writes the
# control; Markdown writes its label with every serializer. `_strip_md_chrome`
# (behind `to_markdown_string`) already keeps the label of a `data-htmxo-label`
# control, but a consumer's own `show(io, MIME"text/markdown"(), x)` that
# delegates to a table node reaches HTMX.jl's serializer directly, which drops a
# `<button>` whole and would leave the table's header row empty. The other
# projections see through to the control (walker methods for static export sit
# beside `_static_walk_child`).
struct _LabelledControl
    control::Node
end
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", c::_LabelledControl) = show(io, m, c.control)
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", c::_LabelledControl) =
    show(io, m, c.control)
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/markdown", c::_LabelledControl) =
    let label = _strip_md_chrome(c.control)
        isnothing(label) || show(io, m, label)
    end
filter_errors(c::_LabelledControl) = filter_errors(c.control)
_filter_errors_child(c::_LabelledControl) = _filter_errors_child(c.control)
_strip_md_chrome(c::_LabelledControl) = _strip_md_chrome(c.control)
_strip_md_chrome_child(c::_LabelledControl) = _strip_md_chrome_child(c.control)
_rescue_semantic!(out, c::_LabelledControl) = _rescue_semantic!(out, c.control)

# A `detail-<key>` companion row in a `sortable_table` body. HTML (and HXML)
# write the row; Markdown writes nothing, with every serializer: HTMX.jl's
# table writer makes a pipe-table line of each `<tr>` child of a `<tbody>` and
# skips anything else, so a consumer's own `show(io, MIME"text/markdown"(), x)`
# leaves the companion out too. A master/detail table writes its eager details
# after the table instead (`_MDDetailsMarkdown`). `?error` and static export see
# through to the row.
struct _MDCompanionRow
    row::Node
end
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"text/html", c::_MDCompanionRow) = show(io, m, c.row)
Base.show(io::IO, m::MIME"application/vnd.hyperview+xml", c::_MDCompanionRow) =
    show(io, m, c.row)
Base.show(::IO, ::MIME"text/markdown", ::_MDCompanionRow) = nothing
filter_errors(c::_MDCompanionRow) = filter_errors(c.row)
_filter_errors_child(c::_MDCompanionRow) = _filter_errors_child(c.row)


"""
    _save_typed_response(record_dir, url_path, response)

Save an `HTTP.Response` body using a file extension derived from its
Content-Type header. Used by the recording flow to capture routes that
hand-roll their response (`/aov_runtime_js` → `.js`, `/spec/<id>` → `.json`,
etc.) so the deployed static site serves them with the right MIME.
"""
function _save_typed_response(record_dir::String, url_path::String, response::HTTP.Response)
    ct = HTTP.header(response, "Content-Type", "")
    ext = if occursin("application/javascript", ct) || occursin("text/javascript", ct)
        ".js"
    elseif occursin("application/json", ct)
        ".json"
    elseif occursin("text/css", ct)
        ".css"
    elseif occursin("text/markdown", ct) || occursin("text/plain", ct)
        ".md"
    elseif occursin("image/svg", ct)
        ".svg"
    else
        ".html"
    end
    save_response(record_dir, url_path, response; ext)
end

# The file a recording writes for `url_path`, as a path below the recording's
# root: the path plus `ext`, except that a trailing slash means "directory
# entry", so `/foo/` and `/` (and an empty path) map to an `index<ext>` in the
# matching directory. Without that, `/hx/` would become `hx/<ext>`, which a
# static server cannot serve. A recorded `hx-get` names the same file
# (`_recorded_fragment_url`).
_record_file_path(url_path::AbstractString, ext::AbstractString) =
    isempty(url_path) || endswith(url_path, '/') ? url_path * "index" * ext : url_path * ext

"""
    save_response(record_dir, url_path, response; ext=".html")

Save a response body to disk, mirroring the URL path structure
(`/post/42` → `record_dir/post/42<ext>`). Enables later replay via a
static file server. Pass `ext=".md"` for markdown recordings (under the
`/md/` subtree); see also [`_save_typed_response`](@ref) which picks
the extension from the response's Content-Type.
"""
function save_response(record_dir::String, url_path::String, response::HTTP.Response; ext::String=".html")
    dest = joinpath(record_dir, lstrip(_record_file_path(url_path, ext), '/'))
    _guard_record_destination!(record_dir, dest)
    mkpath(dirname(dest))
    write(dest, _response_bytes(response))
    dest
end

# A response body as what a file holds (bytes, or the `String` a route
# answered), whichever body type HTTP.jl gave it.
function _response_bytes(response::HTTP.Response)
    body = response.body
    if isdefined(HTTP, :BytesBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :BytesBody))
        Vector{UInt8}(body)
    elseif isdefined(HTTP, :EmptyBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :EmptyBody))
        UInt8[]
    else
        body
    end
end

# A record! call is synchronous, but multiple calls may run on independent
# Tasks. Keep collision state task-local so a second requested URL cannot
# silently overwrite a file emitted earlier in the same recording session.
const _RECORDING_SESSION_KEY = :HTMXObjects_recording_session

struct _RecordDestinationCollision <: Exception
    previous::String
    source::String
    destination::String
end

function Base.showerror(io::IO, err::_RecordDestinationCollision)
    print(io, "record!: requested paths ", repr(err.previous), " and ",
          repr(err.source), " resolve to the same static destination ",
          repr(err.destination))
end

function _guard_record_destination!(record_dir::String, dest::String)
    session = get(task_local_storage(), _RECORDING_SESSION_KEY, nothing)
    isnothing(session) && return
    session.record_dir == abspath(record_dir) || return

    destination = abspath(dest)
    source = session.source[]
    previous = get(session.destinations, destination, nothing)
    if !isnothing(previous) && previous != source
        throw(_RecordDestinationCollision(previous, source, destination))
    end
    session.destinations[destination] = source
    nothing
end

# Queues a `url_params` value a recording walk met (`_static_variant_target`)
# for the running `record!`, which records its fragment after the requested
# paths, and returns the address whose fragment file holds it: its own, or a
# requested path or value recorded already with the same parameters in another
# order. `nothing` when no `record!` runs, so nothing will record it.
function _record_variant!(address::AbstractString)
    session = get(task_local_storage(), _RECORDING_SESSION_KEY, nothing)
    isnothing(session) && return nothing
    get!(session.recorded, _record_query_identity(address)) do
        push!(session.variants, String(address))
        String(address)
    end
end

# The address whose fragment file the running `record!` writes for an `hx-get`
# with a query, or `nothing`: a requested path's or a declared value's, met so
# far (a value counts once a response recorded before declared it, or the
# same response did earlier in its markup).
function _recorded_query_address(address::AbstractString)
    session = get(task_local_storage(), _RECORDING_SESSION_KEY, nothing)
    isnothing(session) && return nothing
    get(session.recorded, _record_query_identity(address), nothing)
end

# What `record!` knows an address with a query by: its path and its
# parameters in a fixed order, without a `#` fragment.
function _record_query_identity(address::AbstractString)
    path, query = _split_once(first(_split_once(address, '#')), '?')
    isnothing(query) ? path : path * "?" * _sorted_query(query)
end
_sorted_query(query::AbstractString) = join(sort!(split(query, '&'; keepempty=false)), '&')

# Where a recorded response is saved: its path without the query, except an
# address with a query that `record!` drives (a requested path or a
# `url_params` value), whose query names it.
_record_save_path(req::HTTP.Request) = get(req.context, :htmxo_record_query, false) ?
    _record_variant_save_path(String(req.target)) : String(first(split(String(req.target), "?"; limit=2)))

# Paths that use kwargs (query params) and thus can't vary by query string on static servers.
# Populated by route! when record_dir is set; consulted only by the recording form of the
# static transform (`static_transform(val; record_base)`), never by a request-scoped export.
const _static_kwargs_paths = Set{String}()

const _STATIC_DISABLED_STYLE = Node("style", "[data-static-disabled]{opacity:.45;pointer-events:none;cursor:not-allowed}")

# Non-GET hx attribute names that are non-functional on a static server.
# HTMX converts underscores to hyphens in attribute names, so these use hyphens.
const _HX_NONGET_ATTRS = Set([Symbol("hx-post"), Symbol("hx-put"), Symbol("hx-patch"), Symbol("hx-delete")])

"""
    StaticExport(; url_map=nothing, controls=:disable, record_base="", runtime=:inline,
                 audience=:public)

Request-scoped static-export spec: pass it as `dispatch(...; static=spec)` to
receive one route's response in static form from inside a live server. For
that request only:

- execution is forced blocking — the operation's finished value, never a
  Treebars poller or a page-load placeholder — and
- the static transform ([`static_transform`](@ref)) is applied to the route's
  value before serialization; for a full-page request it runs after the
  `__page__` wrap, so the page chrome is rewritten too.

The caller receives the transformed `HTTP.Response` and writes it wherever it
wants. Nothing is saved, and no `route!` registration, operation policy, or
process-global recording state is read or changed: the spec rides the request
alone, so concurrent ordinary requests are unaffected.

- `url_map` — `nothing`, or a function from an address (the full attribute
  value, query included: `"/report/figure?doc=a&name=b"`) to a static target
  `String` or `nothing`. It is consulted for every `hx-get`, `href`, and `src`,
  semantic elements' included (a [`SemanticLink`](@ref)'s `href`, `external=true`
  too, and a [`SemanticAction`](@ref)'s `hx-get`), and for every address a
  route body passes to [`static_url`](@ref) (one inside a script or a JSON
  payload, which the walk cannot see).
  A `String` replaces the attribute verbatim — relative targets such as
  `"embeds/fig-a.html"` are emitted as given — and the element stays live.
  `nothing` falls back to the default rules below. Any other return value
  throws an `ArgumentError`. It is also called with the address of each value
  a [`url_params`](@ref) element declares, whose target becomes that value's
  file in the bundle, and with the path of an `hx-push-url`/`hx-replace-url`
  address, which keeps its query.
- `controls` — what happens to an element a static server cannot serve: one
  carrying `hx-post`/`hx-put`/`hx-patch`/`hx-delete`, or an unmapped `hx-get`
  whose request carries a query string. That is one whose address has a
  query, and one that sends values, which htmx puts in the query of a GET: a
  `<form>` (its fields), a named `input`/`select`/`textarea`/`button` (its own
  value), and an element with `hx-include`, `hx-vals` or `hx-vars` on it or
  on an enclosing element, which it inherits (an `unset` value stops that).
  A [`url_params`](@ref) element is not one of them: the export gives it the
  file of each value it declares. `:disable` (default; the recording
  behaviour) strips those attributes and greys the element out
  (`data-static-disabled` + `disabled`, plus the dimming style in a full
  page's `<head>`). `:remove` drops the element and its subtree instead.
- `record_base` — the prefix the default rules prepend to rooted addresses an
  unmapped attribute keeps (`hx-get` → `<record_base>/hx/…`, `href`/`src` →
  `<record_base>/…`). Empty (default) leaves them unchanged.
- `runtime` — what happens to a page's linked runtime (`htmx(...;
  runtime=:linked)`). `:inline` (default) puts each block back inline, so the
  exported page needs no other file. `:linked` keeps the links, their
  addresses rewritten by `url_map` and `record_base` like any other `src` or
  `href`, for a bundle that carries the runtime once:
  [`copy_vendorfiles`](@ref) writes those files next to the libraries, so a
  page whose `assets` addresses the copied libraries addresses the runtime
  too. A many-page export then holds one copy instead of one per page.
- `audience` — who reads the export. `:public` (default) shows the stand-in
  of each [`private`](@ref) part, or nothing, and answers a private page with
  an empty `404` (header `X-HTMXO-Private: true`); links inside what it leaves
  out never reach `url_map`. `:private` shows everything, as the live app does.
  The route sees it as [`export_audience`](@ref)`(__req__)`, and Markdown
  requests follow it too. A public answer that still holds private markup
  rendered before the export (a `Raw` string) throws
  [`PrivateContentEscaped`](@ref).

A route that returns a finished `HTTP.Response`, [`MIMEResponse`](@ref) or
[`JSONResponse`](@ref) has no value to transform, so a static dispatch of it throws
[`StaticExportRefused`](@ref) instead of handing back live markup; fetch those
with a plain `dispatch`. Markdown requests (`Accept: text/markdown`, `?plain`)
are forced blocking too but carry no attributes to rewrite.

```julia
spec = StaticExport(; controls=:remove,
    url_map = url -> startswith(url, "/report/figure?") ?
        "embeds/" * figure_name(url) * ".html" : nothing)
resp = dispatch(:GET, "/report/figure?doc=a&name=qoi";
                headers=["HX-Request" => "true"], parent=job, static=spec)
write(joinpath(bundle, "embeds", "qoi.html"), resp.body)
```
"""
struct StaticExport{F}
    url_map::F
    controls::Symbol
    record_base::String
    runtime::Symbol
    audience::Symbol
    function StaticExport(url_map::F, controls::Symbol,
            record_base::AbstractString, runtime::Symbol=:inline, audience=:public) where {F}
        controls in (:disable, :remove) || throw(ArgumentError(
            "StaticExport controls must be :disable or :remove (got $(repr(controls)))"))
        runtime in (:inline, :linked) || throw(ArgumentError(
            "StaticExport runtime must be :inline or :linked (got $(repr(runtime)))"))
        new{F}(url_map, controls, String(record_base), runtime, _check_audience(audience, "StaticExport"))
    end
end

StaticExport(; url_map=nothing, controls::Symbol=:disable,
        record_base::AbstractString="", runtime::Symbol=:inline, audience=:public) =
    StaticExport(url_map, controls, record_base, runtime, audience)

"""
    StaticExportRefused <: Exception

Thrown by `dispatch(...; static=spec)` when the route returned a finished
`HTTP.Response`, `MIMEResponse` or `JSONResponse`. Such a response bypasses the static
transform, so returning it would put live markup into a static bundle; the
handler rethrows instead of rendering an ordinary route error, so the export
fails at the `dispatch` call.
"""
struct StaticExportRefused <: Exception
    method::String
    target::String
    response_type::String
end

StaticExportRefused(req::HTTP.Request, finalized) =
    StaticExportRefused(req.method, String(req.target), string(typeof(finalized)))

Base.showerror(io::IO, e::StaticExportRefused) = print(io,
    "StaticExportRefused: static dispatch of ", e.method, " ", e.target,
    " — the route returned a finished ", e.response_type, ", which bypasses ",
    "the static transform. Fetch it with a plain `dispatch` (no `static=`), ",
    "or return an HTML value from the route.")

"""
    static_export(req::HTTP.Request) -> Union{StaticExport,Nothing}

The [`StaticExport`](@ref) spec a `dispatch(...; static=spec)` call attached
to this request, or `nothing` for every other request (real HTTP requests
never carry it: the request context is server-side).

HTMXObjects forces its own operation transport blocking for such a request.
A route body that hand-rolls `Treebars.polling_fetchindex` decides its own
`sync`, so it must block as well — otherwise the export captures a live
poller:

```julia
polling_fetchindex(ip, key; sync=wants_markdown(__req__) ||
                                 static_export(__req__) !== nothing) do rv
    ...
end
```
"""
static_export(req::HTTP.Request) = get(req.context, :htmxo_static_export, nothing)

"""
    export_audience(req::HTTP.Request) -> Symbol

Who the answer to this request is for: `:public` or `:private`. A request of
the live app is `:private`, since the live app shows everything. A `dispatch`
answers for the `audience` it was given, else its `StaticExport`'s (`:public`
unless asked otherwise), else the audience of the request it is made in, and
`:public` outside any request. [`private`](@ref) content shows its public
stand-in, or nothing, in a `:public` answer.

A `dispatch` made while the route runs inherits it, in the route's own task and
in the tasks it starts. Pass it on to a function with its own default, so what
the route embeds is for the same reader, such as `pdf_report` (public unless
asked otherwise):

```julia
@get concept() = pdf_report(concept_page(), "konzept.pdf"; title="Konzept",
                            lang="de-DE", audience=export_audience(__req__))
```

Markup a route builds and keeps beyond the request (an `HTMLSnapshot`, a cached
`Raw` string) holds what it showed for the audience it was built for, so key it
by `export_audience(__req__)` as well.
"""
export_audience(req::HTTP.Request) = get(req.context, :htmxo_audience, :private)

# Who names the file of each value a `url_params` element declares, and of an
# unmapped rooted `hx-get`: the spec's `url_map` and default rule for a
# request-scoped export, the recording rule (the file `record!` writes) for a
# recording.
struct _MappedVariants end
struct _RecordedVariants end

# What htmx adds to the request of an element with one of these attributes,
# and of every element inside it, which inherits it: the inputs an
# `hx-include` names, and `hx-vals`/`hx-vars` values. An `unset` value stops
# what the element would inherit.
const _HX_INCLUDE_ATTRS = (Symbol("hx-include"), Symbol("data-hx-include"))
const _HX_VALUES_ATTRS = (Symbol("hx-vals"), Symbol("hx-vars"),
                          Symbol("data-hx-vals"), Symbol("data-hx-vars"))

# Whether enclosing elements add inputs (`include`) or values (`vals`) to every
# request inside them.
struct _StaticSent
    include::Bool
    vals::Bool
end
const _STATIC_SENT_NOTHING = _StaticSent(false, false)

# What an element with `attrs` and everything inside it inherit, given what
# its enclosing elements add.
_static_sent(sent::_StaticSent, attrs) = _StaticSent(
    _static_inherits(sent.include, attrs, _HX_INCLUDE_ATTRS),
    _static_inherits(sent.vals, attrs, _HX_VALUES_ATTRS))
function _static_inherits(inherited::Bool, attrs, syms)
    found = false
    for sym in syms
        value = get(attrs, sym, nothing)
        isnothing(value) && continue
        strip(string(value)) == "unset" || return true
        found = true
    end
    found ? false : inherited
end

# Whether an element's `hx-get` request sends values besides its address, as
# htmx builds a GET: a form's fields, a named control's own value, and what
# an `hx-include` or `hx-vals`/`hx-vars` on it or an enclosing element adds.
# They become the request's query, which a static file cannot answer.
_static_sends_values(tag::Symbol, attrs, sent::_StaticSent) =
    sent.include || sent.vals || tag === :form ||
    tag in (:input, :select, :textarea, :button) && !isempty(string(get(attrs, :name, "")))

# What a static walk consults: the spec, the rooted `hx-get` paths a static
# server cannot vary by query — `route!`'s recording registry for the recording
# form of `static_transform`, nothing for a request-scoped export — who
# names the files of a declared URL parameter's values and of fragments, and
# what the elements enclosing the walked one add to its requests.
struct _StaticRules{S<:StaticExport,V}
    spec::S
    unservable::Set{String}
    variants::V
    sent::_StaticSent
end
_StaticRules(spec, unservable, variants) =
    _StaticRules(spec, unservable, variants, _STATIC_SENT_NOTHING)

# Returned by the walker for an element `controls=:remove` drops; containers
# filter it out of their children.
struct _StaticRemoved end
const _STATIC_REMOVED = _StaticRemoved()

_static_kept(xs) = filter(x -> x !== _STATIC_REMOVED, xs)

# The caller's target for one attribute value, or `nothing` for the default
# rules. Only string values are addresses; without a `url_map` every address
# takes the default rules. `kind` says how a static site loads the address:
# `:fragment` for an `hx-get` (and a `url_params` value, which the element's
# `hx-get` loads), `:full` for everything else. An ordinary `url_map` sees only
# the address; `export_site`'s own map (`_SiteMap`) takes the kind as well.
_static_url_target(::StaticExport, url, kind::Symbol=:full) = nothing
_static_url_target(::StaticExport{Nothing}, ::AbstractString, kind::Symbol=:full) = nothing
function _static_url_target(spec::StaticExport, url::AbstractString, kind::Symbol=:full)
    target = _url_map_target(spec.url_map, url, kind)
    target === nothing && return nothing
    target isa AbstractString && return String(target)
    throw(ArgumentError("StaticExport url_map must return a String or nothing; " *
                        "got $(repr(target)) for $(repr(url))"))
end
_url_map_target(url_map, url, kind) = url_map(url)

"""
    escape_html(s) -> String

Escape `&`, `"`, `'`, `<`, `>`. Thin re-export of `HTMX.escape`.

For building a **raw HTML string by hand** — not for values passed
through `h.*`. As of the escape-by-default flip, `h.*` escapes text
children and all attribute values exactly once, so **never** pre-escape
a value you hand to a node builder (that double-escapes). Reach for
`escape_html` only when you assemble HTML text yourself; for deliberate
trusted markup inside a node use [`Raw`](@ref) instead.
"""
escape_html(s::AbstractString) = HTMX.escape(s)

"""
    html_escape(s) -> String

Escape `&`, `<`, `>` in `s` for safe interpolation into a **hand-built
raw HTML string** (e.g. `replace(_, => "<a href=...>...")` for a `<pre>`
block). Never pre-escape a value passed through `h.*` — node builders
escape text and attributes themselves; use [`Raw`](@ref) for trusted
markup inside a node.

For values that may contain quotes / apostrophes, use
[`escape_html`](@ref) (which delegates to `HTMX.escape`) for the full
5-character escape set.
"""
html_escape(s) = replace(string(s), "&" => "&amp;", "<" => "&lt;", ">" => "&gt;")

"""
    _static_walk(val, rules::_StaticRules) -> val

Walk a Node tree and make it static-safe under `rules`:
- `hx-get`, `href`, `src`: a `url_map` target replaces the value verbatim;
  otherwise rooted addresses gain `record_base` (`hx-get` under `/hx`; in a
  recording, the fragment file it records, `<record_base>/hx/<path>.html`)
- Strip `hx-post`, `hx-put`, `hx-patch`, `hx-delete` attributes
- Strip unmapped `hx-get` attributes whose URL contains `?` (query-param
  routes; in a `record!` recording, unless it records that address,
  `_static_query_hx_url`), whose element sends values that become the query
  (a form, a named control, an inherited `hx-include`/`hx-vals`/`hx-vars`;
  `_static_sends_values`), or that name one of `rules.unservable` (kwargs
  routes)
- Mark affected elements with `data-static-disabled` and `disabled`, or —
  under `controls=:remove` — drop them (`_STATIC_REMOVED`)
- Give a [`url_params`](@ref) element the file of each value it declares
  (`data-htmxo-url-files`), its `hx-get` the file of the value it shows
- Map the path of an `hx-push-url`/`hx-replace-url` address like an `href`,
  keeping its query
- Put a linked page-runtime block (`htmx(...; runtime=:linked)`) back inline,
  unless the spec keeps it linked (`runtime=:linked`)

- Leave out what [`private`](@ref) content does not show the spec's
  `audience`, and every element that links a page the export leaves out as
  private

Documents, arrays, tuples, `content => id` pairs, [`html_only`](@ref) and
[`private`](@ref) content and [`HTMLSnapshot`](@ref) values are walked
through; other values pass through unchanged.
"""
_static_walk(val, rules::_StaticRules) = val
# A full page arrives as an `HTMLDocument`; unwrap so the `<html>` tree is
# walked and re-wrap so the recorded file keeps its doctype. Without this
# method the catch-all above would return the document untouched and every
# recorded page would keep its live (non-static) hx attributes.
_static_walk(doc::HTMLDocument, rules::_StaticRules) =
    HTMLDocument(_static_walk(doc.root, rules))
_static_walk(val::AbstractArray, rules::_StaticRules) =
    _static_kept([_static_walk(x, rules) for x in val])
_static_walk(val::Tuple, rules::_StaticRules) =
    Tuple(_static_kept([_static_walk(x, rules) for x in val]))
function _static_walk((content, id)::Pair, rules::_StaticRules)
    walked = _static_walk(content, rules)
    walked === _STATIC_REMOVED ? walked : walked => id
end

# Rewrite an absolute-from-root URL (`/foo/bar`) by prepending `prefix`.
# External URLs (`https://…`), anchors (`#…`), schemes (`mailto:…`), and
# protocol-relative (`//…`) pass through unchanged. Empty `prefix` is a no-op.
function _rewrite_static_url(url::AbstractString, prefix::AbstractString)
    isempty(prefix) && return url
    isempty(url) && return url
    if startswith(url, "//") || startswith(url, "http://") || startswith(url, "https://") ||
       startswith(url, "#") || occursin(':', url) && !startswith(url, "/")
        return url
    end
    startswith(url, "/") || return url
    rstrip(prefix, '/') * url
end

# `hx-get` URLs in recordings should fetch fragments — they live under
# the `/hx/` subtree of `record_base`. Convenience wrapper.
_rewrite_hx_url(url::AbstractString, record_base::AbstractString) =
    _rewrite_static_url(url, isempty(record_base) ? "" : (rstrip(record_base, '/') * "/hx"))

# What an `href`/`src` address becomes under `spec`: the `url_map` target, or
# the default rule. `static_url` applies the same rule to addresses the walk
# cannot see.
function _static_url(spec::StaticExport, url)
    target = _static_url_target(spec, url)
    isnothing(target) ? _rewrite_static_url(url, spec.record_base) : target
end

"""
    static_url(req::HTTP.Request, address::AbstractString) -> String

The address a response should embed for `address` where a static export
cannot rewrite it: inside a JSON payload, an inline script, or a Vega spec's
`data.url` (an AlgebraOfVega `geoshape(url)` or `data(url)`). A static export
rewrites only the `hx-get`, `href` and `src` attributes it walks.

For a `dispatch(...; static=spec)` request this is what an `href` or `src`
with that value becomes: the target `spec.url_map(address)` returns, or else
the default rule (`record_base` before a rooted address). The `url_map` is
called with `address`, so an exporter that follows every address its
`url_map` sees exports this one too. Every other request gets `address`
unchanged, so the same route body serves live pages and exports:

```julia
@direct @get raster() = MIMEResponse("application/geo+json", read(path, String))
@get map() = data(cells) * mapping(:gid; color=:value) *
             geoshape(static_url(__req__, __self__ / "raster"))
```

A `record!` recording saves the same value it serves live, so there the
address stays as given.
"""
static_url(req::HTTP.Request, address::AbstractString) =
    _static_url(static_export(req), address)
_static_url(::Nothing, address::AbstractString) = String(address)

"""
    url_params(; name = value, …, values = (name = domain, …)) -> NamedTuple

Attributes that let parameters in the page address drive an element that loads
its content with `hx-get`. Splat them onto that element:

```julia
h.div(id="voralarme", hx_get=base * "/voralarme", hx_trigger="none", hx_swap="outerHTML";
      url_params(k = k; values = (k = (3.0, 4.0, 5.0),))...)(alarms_view(k))
```

An element that shows its content from the start, as this one does, needs a
trigger that never fires (`hx_trigger="none"`): an element with `hx-get` and
no trigger loads on every click inside it. One that loads itself
(`hx_trigger="load"`) sends the address's values with that first request.
`hx-vals` that carry a value from an event leave the parameter out when there
is none (`"js:{...(event?.detail?.kenn ? {kenn: event.detail.kenn} : {})}"`);
an `undefined` value would be sent as the text `undefined`. An element a
control reloads (`hx_trigger="change from:#ortsteil"`) wants
`hx_sync="this:replace"` as well: htmx otherwise queues a second change behind
the request of the first and drops it once that swap replaces the element.

`k = k` says that the address parameter `k` drives this element and that the
element was rendered for `k` (`nothing`: rendered without one).
`messstelle = kenn => :kenn` renames: the address says `messstelle`, the
element's requests send `kenn`. Values are compared and sent as their
[`option_wire_value`](@ref). `values` names each parameter's domain; a
parameter without one follows the address on a live page only. `values` is
therefore not a parameter name.

The page's runtime ([`url_params_script`](@ref), which [`htmx`](@ref) pages
carry) does the rest in the browser, on a live page and in a static export
alike:

- **Address → element.** When the element enters the page (page load, an htmx
  swap, Back/Forward) and the address names another value than the one it shows,
  it requests its `hx-get` again with the address's value. A request the
  element makes while that one loads (a selector's change, a poll) takes its
  place. Its own requests (a `load` trigger, polling) carry the address's
  value too, unless the request carries the parameter itself, such as a value
  an `hx-vals` takes from a click.
  A value outside the declared domain keeps the element as it is, with a
  console warning.
- **Element → address.** When a request loads the element with a new value — its
  own request, or another element's whose `hx-target` is this one, such as a
  link that swaps a new version of it in — the value is written into the
  address (`history.replaceState`; other parameters and htmx's history state
  stay). A first load that shows the rendered value leaves the address as it
  is. The element's declaration then names the value it shows, so htmx's
  history cache keeps that value with the element: Back or Forward to the
  address requests nothing. A request whose route answers through a deferred operation's poller
  writes the value it asked for when the poller takes the element's place, so
  the element the polls bring matches the address. Reload, Back and a copied
  link then show the same view; no `hx-push-url` is needed for it.
- **Element → controls.** A control outside the element that reloads it — a
  `select`, a group of radio buttons or another input that holds a value,
  named as the element's requests send the parameter, at or inside a `from:`
  source of its `hx-trigger` or an `hx-include` target — shows the element's
  value: the address's value as soon as the element requests it, and the
  value of each version that enters the page. Setting it fires no `change`.
  A request that fails puts it back, unless the reader changed it meanwhile;
  a value it has no option for leaves it as it is, with a console warning.
  Controls inside the element are rendered with it and left alone.
- **Static exports.** A static export calls `url_map` with the address of every
  declared value (the element's `hx-get` with that value) and gives the element
  each value's file, so an exporter that follows mapped addresses writes them and
  the bundle's page loads the right one with no app script. `record!` records
  them itself, named `<record_base>/hx/<path>@<query>.html`. A value whose
  address maps to no file keeps the element as it is.

Inside a docs embed (`.htmxo-embed`) the address belongs to the host page and is
left alone.
"""
function url_params(; values=(;), params...)
    isempty(params) && throw(ArgumentError(
        "url_params: name at least one address parameter, e.g. `url_params(k = 3.0)`"))
    for name in keys(values)
        haskey(params, name) || throw(ArgumentError(
            "url_params: `values` names `$name`, which is not one of the parameters ($(join(keys(params), ", ")))"))
    end
    entries = [_url_param(name, spec, get(values, name, nothing)) for (name, spec) in pairs(params)]
    sent = [p.as for p in entries]
    allunique(sent) || throw(ArgumentError(
        "url_params: two parameters send the same request name ($(join(sent, ", ")))"))
    (; data_htmxo_url_params = "[" * join((_url_param_json(p) for p in entries), ",") * "]")
end

# One declared parameter: its address name, the request name its value is sent
# under, the value the element was rendered with and its domain (`nothing`:
# none), all as wire strings.
_url_param(name, spec, domain) = _url_param(name, spec => name, domain)
function _url_param(name, (value, as)::Pair, domain)
    rendered = _url_param_wire(value)
    values = _url_param_domain(domain)
    isnothing(values) || isnothing(rendered) || rendered in values || throw(ArgumentError(
        "url_params: $name = $(repr(rendered)) is not among its values ($(join(repr.(values), ", ")))"))
    (; name=string(name), as=string(as), value=rendered, values)
end
_url_param_wire(::Nothing) = nothing
_url_param_wire(value) = _option_wire_string(value)
_url_param_domain(::Nothing) = nothing
_url_param_domain(domain) = String[_option_wire_string(v) for v in domain]

_url_param_json(p) = "[" * _md_json_string(p.name) * "," * _md_json_string(p.as) * "," *
    _json_or_null(_md_json_string, p.value) * "," * _json_or_null(_md_json_list, p.values) * "]"
_json_or_null(f, ::Nothing) = "null"
_json_or_null(f, x) = f(x)

# The parameters `url_params` declared, read back from its attribute.
function _url_params_decode(s::AbstractString)
    bad() = throw(ArgumentError("data-htmxo-url-params: not what url_params writes: $(repr(s))"))
    tree = _json_string_tree(s, bad)
    tree isa Vector || bad()
    map(tree) do p
        p isa Vector && length(p) == 4 && p[1] isa String && p[2] isa String &&
            p[3] isa Union{String,Nothing} && (isnothing(p[4]) || p[4] isa Vector && all(v -> v isa String, p[4])) || bad()
        (; name=p[1], as=p[2], value=p[3], values=isnothing(p[4]) ? nothing : String.(p[4]))
    end
end

# `address` with the query parameter `name` set to `value` in place of any it
# carries (`nothing`: removed); the other parameters and a fragment stay.
function _url_with_param(address::AbstractString, name::AbstractString, value)
    rest, fragment = _split_once(address, '#')
    path, query = _split_once(rest, '?')
    key = HTTP.URIs.escapeuri(name)
    kept = filter(split(something(query, ""), '&'; keepempty=false)) do pair
        HTTP.URIs.unescapeuri(first(split(pair, '='; limit=2))) != name
    end
    isnothing(value) || push!(kept, key * "=" * HTTP.URIs.escapeuri(value))
    path * (isempty(kept) ? "" : "?" * join(kept, "&")) * (isnothing(fragment) ? "" : "#" * fragment)
end
_split_once(s, c) = (i = findfirst(c, s); isnothing(i) ? (String(s), nothing) : (String(s[1:prevind(s, i)]), String(s[nextind(s, i):end])))

# Where a declared value's content lives in a static export, or `nothing`:
# the `url_map` target of its address for a request-scoped export; for a
# recording, the recording rule's file, which the running `record!` is told
# to record (none outside `record!`: nothing would write the file).
_static_variant_target(rules::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_MappedVariants}, address) =
    _static_url_target(rules.spec, address, :fragment)
_static_variant_target(rules::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_RecordedVariants}, address) =
    _recorded_query_url(rules, _record_variant!(address))

# What an unmapped `hx-get` with a query becomes, or `nothing` (it is
# disabled): for a recording, the file `record!` writes for its address, when
# it records that address; a request-scoped export names files only through
# its `url_map`.
_static_query_hx_url(::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_MappedVariants}, url) = nothing
_static_query_hx_url(rules::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_RecordedVariants}, url) =
    _recorded_query_url(rules, _recorded_query_address(url))
_recorded_query_url(rules, ::Nothing) = nothing
_recorded_query_url(rules, address::AbstractString) =
    _recorded_fragment_url(rules.spec.record_base, _record_variant_save_path(address))

# The address of the fragment a recording saves for `save_path`: the file
# `save_response` writes below `hx/`, under `record_base` (empty: the site
# root). A `%` in the path is part of the file's name, so it is escaped for
# the static server that decodes the address.
_recorded_fragment_url(record_base::AbstractString, save_path::AbstractString) =
    rstrip(record_base, '/') * replace(_record_file_path("/hx" * save_path, ".html"), "%" => "%25")

# What an unmapped, servable `hx-get` becomes: for a request-scoped export the
# default rule (`record_base` and `/hx` before a rooted address); for a
# recording the file it records for that address, so the bundle loads it from
# any static server, not only from one that serves `/foo` as `foo.html`.
_static_hx_url(rules::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_MappedVariants}, url) =
    _rewrite_hx_url(url, rules.spec.record_base)
_static_hx_url(rules::_StaticRules{<:StaticExport,_RecordedVariants}, url) =
    startswith(url, "/") && !startswith(url, "//") ?
        _recorded_fragment_url(rules.spec.record_base, _record_variant_save_path(url)) : url

# A recorded `url_params` value's file, below the recording's `hx/`: its
# path, then `@` and its query, so each value has its own file beside the
# path's (`/voralarme?k=4.0` → `/voralarme@k=4.0`).
function _record_variant_save_path(address::AbstractString)
    path, query = _split_once(first(_split_once(address, '#')), '?')
    isnothing(query) ? path : path * "@" * replace(query, "/" => "%2F")
end

# The file of each value a `url_params` element declares, as the JSON the
# runtime reads (one object level per parameter with a domain, in declared
# order), and the file of the values it was rendered with, or `nothing`.
function _static_url_files(declared, address::AbstractString, rules::_StaticRules)
    for p in declared
        isnothing(p.values) && (address = _url_with_param(address, p.as, p.value))
    end
    keyed = [p for p in declared if !isnothing(p.values)]
    isempty(keyed) && return "{}", nothing
    own = any(p -> isnothing(p.value), keyed) ? nothing :
        _static_variant_target(rules, foldl((a, p) -> _url_with_param(a, p.as, p.value), keyed; init=address))
    something(_static_url_files_json(keyed, 1, address, rules), "{}"), own
end
_static_url_files(declared, ::Nothing, rules::_StaticRules) = "{}", nothing
function _static_url_files_json(keyed, i, address, rules)
    if i > length(keyed)
        target = _static_variant_target(rules, address)
        return isnothing(target) ? nothing : _md_json_string(target)
    end
    p = keyed[i]
    entries = String[]
    for v in p.values
        sub = _static_url_files_json(keyed, i + 1, _url_with_param(address, p.as, v), rules)
        isnothing(sub) || push!(entries, _md_json_string(v) * ":" * sub)
    end
    isempty(entries) ? nothing : "{" * join(entries, ",") * "}"
end

# A page address htmx writes into the address bar (`hx-push-url`,
# `hx-replace-url`): its path maps like a link's, and its query and fragment
# stay, since the page reads them in the browser. `true`, `false` and
# addresses that are not rooted pass through.
function _static_page_url(spec::StaticExport, value::AbstractString)
    startswith(value, "/") && !startswith(value, "//") || return value
    i = findfirst(in(('?', '#')), value)
    isnothing(i) && return _static_url(spec, value)
    target, rest = _static_url(spec, value[1:prevind(value, i)]), value[i:end]
    occursin('?', target) && startswith(rest, "?") ? target * "&" * rest[2:end] : target * rest
end
_static_page_url(spec::StaticExport, value) = value

function _static_walk(node::Node, rules::_StaticRules)
    spec = rules.spec
    # A linked page-runtime block goes back inline, so an exported page needs
    # nothing from the live vendor mount (`htmx(...; runtime=:linked)`) —
    # unless the bundle carries the runtime files and the spec keeps the link,
    # whose address is then rewritten below like any other.
    runtime = get(HTMX.attrs(node), Symbol("data-htmxo-runtime"), nothing)
    isnothing(runtime) || spec.runtime === :linked || return _runtime_block(Symbol(runtime))
    attrs = HTMX.attrs(node)
    _static_links_private(spec, attrs) && return _STATIC_REMOVED
    new_attrs = copy(attrs)
    sent = _static_sent(rules.sent, attrs)
    # Set when the element carries a request a static server cannot answer.
    unservable = false

    # Remove non-GET hx attributes
    for attr in _HX_NONGET_ATTRS
        if haskey(new_attrs, attr)
            delete!(new_attrs, attr)
            unservable = true
        end
    end

    # A `url_params` element carries the export's file of each value it
    # declares, and its `hx-get` names the file of the value it was rendered
    # with (`_static_url_files`).
    hx_get_sym = Symbol("hx-get")
    declared = get(new_attrs, Symbol("data-htmxo-url-params"), nothing)
    own = nothing
    if !isnothing(declared)
        files, own = _static_url_files(_url_params_decode(declared), get(new_attrs, hx_get_sym, nothing), rules)
        new_attrs[Symbol("data-htmxo-url-files")] = files
    end

    # A mapped `hx-get` takes the caller's target verbatim, and an unmapped
    # one with a query string the file a recording writes for its address
    # (`_static_query_hx_url`). Any other with a query string, sending values
    # that become its query (`_static_sends_values`; a `url_params` element's
    # runtime loads its declared values' files instead) or pointing to a
    # kwargs route is removed; the rest name their fragment under
    # `record_base` (`_static_hx_url`).
    if !isnothing(own)
        new_attrs[hx_get_sym] = own
    elseif haskey(new_attrs, hx_get_sym)
        url = new_attrs[hx_get_sym]
        sends = isnothing(declared) && _static_sends_values(HTMX.tag(node), attrs, sent)
        target = _static_url_target(spec, url, :fragment)
        isnothing(target) && !sends && occursin('?', url) && (target = _static_query_hx_url(rules, url))
        if !isnothing(target)
            new_attrs[hx_get_sym] = target
        elseif sends || occursin('?', url) || url in rules.unservable
            delete!(new_attrs, hx_get_sym)
            unservable = true
        else
            new_attrs[hx_get_sym] = _static_hx_url(rules, url)
        end
    end

    # Rewrite `<a href="/foo">` to `record_base * /foo` so links from a
    # recorded page land on the matching full-page recording (not the
    # `/hx/` fragment subtree — full pages link to other full pages).
    # A mapped address takes the caller's target instead.
    for sym in (:href, :src)
        haskey(new_attrs, sym) || continue
        new_attrs[sym] = _static_url(spec, new_attrs[sym])
    end
    # A page address htmx writes into the address bar keeps its query, which
    # the page reads in the browser (`_static_page_url`).
    for sym in (Symbol("hx-push-url"), Symbol("hx-replace-url"))
        haskey(new_attrs, sym) || continue
        new_attrs[sym] = _static_page_url(spec, new_attrs[sym])
    end

    if unservable
        spec.controls === :remove && return _STATIC_REMOVED
        new_attrs[Symbol("data-static-disabled")] = "true"  # renders as data-static-disabled
        new_attrs[:disabled] = "true"                        # renders as boolean attribute
    end

    # Recurse into children, which inherit what this element adds to their
    # requests; private content shown as several values takes its place
    # among them.
    inner = sent === rules.sent ? rules :
        _StaticRules(spec, rules.unservable, rules.variants, sent)
    new_children = Any[]
    for child in HTMX.children(node)
        _static_append!(new_children, _static_walk_child(child, inner))
    end

    Node(HTMX.tag(node), new_attrs, new_children)
end

_static_append!(out, child) = (child === _STATIC_REMOVED || push!(out, child); out)
_static_append!(out, children::AbstractVector) =
    (foreach(child -> _static_append!(out, child), children); out)

# An element that loads or links an address the export leaves out as a private
# page (`export_site` knows them) goes, whatever `controls` says: a disabled
# link would still name the page.
const _STATIC_ADDRESS_ATTRS = (Symbol("hx-get") => :fragment, :href => :full, :src => :full,
    Symbol("hx-push-url") => :full, Symbol("hx-replace-url") => :full)
_static_links_private(spec::StaticExport, attrs) = any(_STATIC_ADDRESS_ATTRS) do (sym, kind)
    address = get(attrs, sym, nothing)
    address isa AbstractString && _url_map_private(spec.url_map, address, kind)
end
_url_map_private(url_map, address, kind) = false

_static_walk_child(child, rules) = child
_static_walk_child(child::Node, rules) = _static_walk(child, rules)
# Private content is walked as what the export's audience reads, so the
# content a public export leaves out is gone from its answer, whatever later
# renders it, and the links inside it never reach `url_map`.
function _static_walk(p::Private, rules::_StaticRules)
    shown = _shown(p, rules.spec.audience)
    isnothing(shown) ? _STATIC_REMOVED : _static_walk(shown, rules)
end
_static_walk_child(p::Private, rules) = _static_walk(p, rules)
# A private export keeps the mark around what it walks.
function _static_walk(x::_PrivateMark, rules::_StaticRules)
    walked = _in_private_mark(() -> _static_walk(x.content, rules))
    walked === _STATIC_REMOVED ? walked : _PrivateMark(walked)
end
_static_walk_child(x::_PrivateMark, rules) = _static_walk(x, rules)
# A snapshot's stored bytes predate the rewrite, so export walks its value.
_static_walk(s::HTMLSnapshot, rules::_StaticRules) = _static_walk(_html_value(s.value), rules)
_static_walk_child(s::HTMLSnapshot, rules) = _static_walk(s, rules)
# `html_only` content is HTML like any other subtree (the table toolbars and
# grouped-header tables put live controls there), so it is walked and
# re-wrapped to keep its empty Markdown projection. A removed control takes
# the wrapper with it.
function _static_walk(val::HtmlOnly, rules::_StaticRules)
    walked = _static_walk(val.content, rules)
    walked === _STATIC_REMOVED ? walked : HtmlOnly(walked)
end
_static_walk_child(val::HtmlOnly, rules) = _static_walk(val, rules)
function _static_walk(c::_LabelledControl, rules::_StaticRules)
    walked = _static_walk(c.control, rules)
    walked === _STATIC_REMOVED ? walked : _LabelledControl(walked)
end
_static_walk_child(c::_LabelledControl, rules) = _static_walk(c, rules)
function _static_walk(c::_MDCompanionRow, rules::_StaticRules)
    walked = _static_walk(c.row, rules)
    walked === _STATIC_REMOVED ? walked : _MDCompanionRow(walked)
end
_static_walk_child(c::_MDCompanionRow, rules) = _static_walk(c, rules)
# A semantic element has no attributes until its HTML peer is built, so the
# export walks that peer: a `SemanticLink`'s `href` (`external=true` included),
# a `SemanticAction`'s `hx-get`, prose links, and every element a semantic
# container holds follow the same rules as a hand-built element. An export is
# HTML alone, so nothing is lost (the same reason a snapshot walks its value).
# The peer is built for the export's audience, which decides what a disclosure
# or a collapsed alternative leaves out.
_static_peer(value::SemanticNode, rules::_StaticRules) =
    _with_audience(() -> _semantic_html_node(value), rules.spec.audience)
_static_walk(value::SemanticNode, rules::_StaticRules) =
    _static_walk(_static_peer(value, rules), rules)
_static_walk_child(value::SemanticNode, rules) = _static_walk(value, rules)
# A disclosure with nothing to open has no peer. The walk can also leave its
# summary alone, by removing every control in it (`controls=:remove`).
function _static_walk(disclosure::SemanticDisclosure, rules::_StaticRules)
    peer = _static_peer(disclosure, rules)
    isnothing(peer) && return _STATIC_REMOVED
    walked = _static_walk(peer, rules)
    walked isa Node && length(HTMX.children(walked)) == 1 ? _STATIC_REMOVED : walked
end
# The sections a section holds are built while its children are walked, so the
# walk runs one heading level below it, as its `show` does.
_static_walk(section::SemanticSection, rules::_StaticRules) =
    _semantic_heading(_semantic_opening_level(section)) do level
        _static_walk(_semantic_html_node(section, level), rules)
    end
# A plot owns no markup: its layer renders itself, and an address inside its
# spec goes through `static_url`.
_static_walk(plot::SemanticPlot, ::_StaticRules) = plot

"""
    _inject_static_style(val)

If val is a full HTML page (contains a `<head>`), inject the disabled-element style block.
"""
_inject_static_style(val) = val
# Same unwrap/re-wrap as `_static_walk`: the `<head>` to inject into
# lives inside the document's `<html>` root.
_inject_static_style(doc::HTMLDocument) = HTMLDocument(_inject_static_style(doc.root))
function _inject_static_style(node::Node)
    if HTMX.tag(node) == :head
        new_children = vcat(HTMX.children(node), [_STATIC_DISABLED_STYLE])
        return Node(:head, copy(HTMX.attrs(node)), new_children)
    end
    # Recurse into children looking for <head>
    new_children = map(_inject_child, HTMX.children(node))
    Node(HTMX.tag(node), copy(HTMX.attrs(node)), new_children)
end

_inject_child(child) = child
_inject_child(child::Node) = _inject_static_style(child)

"""
    static_transform(val; record_base="")
    static_transform(val, spec::StaticExport)

Transform a value for static serving: disable non-functional elements,
rewrite surviving `hx-get` URLs to point at the recorded fragment subtree
under `record_base`, and inject the disabled-element style block (full
pages only).

The keyword form is the recording form used by `record!` / `route!(;
record_dir)`: it also strips `hx-get` addresses naming a kwargs route that
recording registered. `record_base` is the URL prefix where the recording
will be served (e.g. `/HTMXObjects.jl/dev/examples/counter`); empty (the
default) means the site root. Each rooted `hx-get="/foo"` names the fragment
file the recording writes, `hx-get="<record_base>/hx/foo.html"` (`/foo/`:
`<record_base>/hx/foo/index.html`), so HTMX-driven sub-fetches load the
recorded fragment `<record_dir>/hx/foo.html` from any static server,
including one that does not serve `/foo` from `foo.html`. Rooted
`href`/`src` addresses gain `record_base` and keep their path as written.

The [`StaticExport`](@ref) form reads nothing but the spec — its `url_map`,
`controls`, and `record_base` — and is what `dispatch(...; static=spec)`
applies. Under `controls=:remove` a value that is itself a removed control
comes back as an empty fragment.
"""
static_transform(val; record_base::String="") =
    _static_transform(val, _StaticRules(StaticExport(; record_base),
                                        _static_kwargs_paths, _RecordedVariants()))
static_transform(val, spec::StaticExport) =
    _static_transform(val, _StaticRules(spec, Set{String}(), _MappedVariants()))

function _static_transform(val, rules::_StaticRules)
    result = _static_walk(val, rules)
    result === _STATIC_REMOVED && return Any[]
    # Removed controls leave nothing to dim.
    rules.spec.controls === :disable ? _inject_static_style(result) : result
end

# A request-scoped static export (`dispatch(...; static=spec)`) transforms the
# value the response pipeline is about to serialize; every other request
# passes it through untouched.
_static_response_value(req::HTTP.Request, val) =
    _static_response_value(static_export(req), val)
_static_response_value(::Nothing, val) = val
_static_response_value(spec::StaticExport, val) =
    static_transform(_html_value(val), spec)

# What a rendered response serializes: the static export's transform, then the
# prestart of its load-triggered fragments.
_prepared_response_value(req::HTTP.Request, val) =
    _prestart_fragments(req, _static_response_value(req, val))

# --- Static sites (export_site) ---
#
# The whole app as a static bundle, built on the per-response export: a
# crawl that dispatches every address the written answers reference, with a
# `StaticExport` whose `url_map` (`_SiteMap`) queues each address and stands in
# for the file it will become. A file's extension can depend on the answer
# (`/api/x` answers JSON), which is known only once that address has been
# rendered, after the pages linking it, so the map writes a marker naming the
# address and the crawl replaces the markers once every file is named.

"""
    static_file_name(address; fragment=false, ext=".html") -> String

The file [`export_site`](@ref) writes for an app address, as a path inside the
bundle.

- **Pages and fragments** get flat names: the path's segments joined by `-`,
  then each query parameter as `key-value`, in alphabetical order of the keys
  (`/rohdaten/personen` → `rohdaten-personen.html`, `/voralarme?k=3` →
  `voralarme-k-3.html`, `/` → `index.html`). Every page of a bundle therefore
  sits in its root, so one relative link works in every page, and a fragment's
  links (which resolve against the page that loads it) are right wherever it
  is loaded. A fragment (`fragment=true`) goes below `hx/`, so a page and the
  fragment its navigation loads from the same address are two files.
- A static server decodes an address before it looks up the file, so `%XX`
  escapes are decoded and every character other than `A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ , -`
  becomes `_` (`pfad=%2Fap1%2Fk40` → `pfad-_ap1_k40`).
- **An address whose last segment has a file extension** keeps its path
  (`/datei/bericht.pdf` → `datei/bericht.pdf`); its query names no file, since
  a static server ignores it (`/vendor/htmx.min.js?v=2.0.8` →
  `vendor/htmx.min.js`).
- `ext` is the extension of a page, fragment or extensionless data address;
  `export_site` passes the one the answer's `Content-Type` calls for.

An app that names files itself passes its own function with this signature as
`export_site(...; file_name)`, usually wrapping this one.
"""
function static_file_name(address::AbstractString; fragment::Bool=false, ext::AbstractString=".html")
    path, query = _split_once(first(_split_once(address, '#')), '?')
    name = if !isnothing(_static_own_extension(path))
        _static_unescape(lstrip(path, '/'))
    else
        stem = strip(path, '/')
        params = sort!(split(something(query, ""), '&'; keepempty=false); by=p -> first(split(p, '='; limit=2)))
        parts = [isempty(stem) ? "index" : replace(stem, '/' => '-'); replace.(params, '=' => '-')]
        join(_static_name_part.(parts), '-') * ext
    end
    fragment ? "hx/" * name : name
end

# The file extension an address's last path segment names (`.pdf`), or
# `nothing`. A segment such as `A1.5` or `v1.2` is a name, not a file.
function _static_own_extension(path::AbstractString)
    m = match(r"(\.[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{0,7})$", last(split(path, '/')))
    isnothing(m) ? nothing : lowercase(m.captures[1])
end

# `%XX` escapes decoded as the bytes they stand for (a static server does the
# same before it looks a file up); a malformed `%` stays as it is.
_static_unescape(s::AbstractString) =
    replace(s, r"%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}" => m -> String([parse(UInt8, m[2:3]; base=16)]))

_static_name_char(c::Char) = isvalid(c) && isascii(c) && (isletter(c) || isdigit(c) || c in "._,-")
_static_name_part(s::AbstractString) = map(c -> _static_name_char(c) ? c : '_', _static_unescape(s))

# A file name as the address a page links it by: each character a URL path
# cannot carry as it is, percent-escaped (the server decodes it back).
function _static_link(name::AbstractString)
    io = IOBuffer()
    for c in name
        if isascii(c) && (isletter(c) || isdigit(c) || c in "/._,-~")
            print(io, c)
        else
            foreach(b -> print(io, '%', uppercase(string(b; base=16, pad=2))), codeunits(string(c)))
        end
    end
    String(take!(io))
end

"""
    StaticSiteIncomplete <: Exception

Thrown by [`export_site`](@ref) once its crawl has finished, when the bundle
is not complete: `failed` lists each address whose answer could not go into it
(`address => reason`: a status other than 200, an `X-HTMXO-Error-Id` header,
no file extension for its `Content-Type`, a file another address already
took), `unresolved` each reference in a written HTML file that names no file
of the bundle (`file => address`). `files` and `bytes` count what was written
into `dir`.
"""
struct StaticSiteIncomplete <: Exception
    dir::String
    failed::Vector{Pair{String,String}}
    unresolved::Vector{Pair{String,String}}
    files::Int
    bytes::Int
end

function Base.showerror(io::IO, e::StaticSiteIncomplete)
    print(io, "StaticSiteIncomplete: export_site wrote ", e.files, " files into ", e.dir,
          ", but ", length(e.failed), " addresses failed and ", length(e.unresolved),
          " references name no file of the bundle")
    foreach(((a, why),) -> print(io, "\n  failed: ", a, " — ", why), e.failed)
    foreach(((f, a),) -> print(io, "\n  unresolved: ", a, " in ", f), e.unresolved)
end

# One address the crawl writes, and how a static site loads it.
struct _SiteEntry
    address::String
    fragment::Bool
end

struct _SiteCrawl
    file_name::Any
    aliases::Dict{String,String}
    marker::String
    strict::Bool
    entries::Vector{_SiteEntry}
    keys::Dict{String,Int}
    failed::Vector{Pair{String,String}}
    private::Dict{Int,Int}  # entry answered as a private page → how many were found by then
    lock::ReentrantLock
end

# The `url_map` of one dispatch in the crawl: `base` is the address being
# rendered, against which a relative address resolves.
struct _SiteMap
    crawl::_SiteCrawl
    base::String
end
_url_map_target(m::_SiteMap, url, kind) = _site_target!(m.crawl, m.base, url, kind)
_url_map_private(m::_SiteMap, url, kind) = _site_private(m.crawl, m.base, url, kind)

_site_marker(crawl::_SiteCrawl, i::Integer) = string(crawl.marker, i, 'x')

# Whether `url` is an address of this app the bundle carries (not an anchor, a
# query of the page itself, another site or a scheme such as `data:`).
_site_internal(url::AbstractString) =
    !(isempty(url) || startswith(url, '#') || startswith(url, '?') || startswith(url, "//") ||
      occursin(r"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:", url))

# A relative address resolves against the address being rendered, as a
# browser resolves it against the page.
function _site_resolve(base::AbstractString, address::AbstractString)
    startswith(address, '/') && return String(address)
    uri = HTTP.URIs.resolvereference(HTTP.URI("http://site" * base), address)
    isempty(uri.query) ? uri.path : uri.path * "?" * uri.query
end

# What the default names deliberately fold together: the order of query
# parameters, a trailing slash, and a file address's query.
function _site_identity(address::AbstractString)
    path, query = _split_once(address, '?')
    path = isempty(path) || path == "/" ? "/" : rstrip(path, '/')
    isnothing(query) || !isnothing(_static_own_extension(path)) ? path :
        path * "?" * _sorted_query(query)
end

# The address `url` names from the page at `base`, and the key of its entry
# (its file name before a `?`) with the query a link to it keeps.
function _site_address(crawl::_SiteCrawl, base::AbstractString, url::AbstractString, kind::Symbol)
    rest, id = _split_once(url, '#')
    address = _site_resolve(base, rest)
    address = get(crawl.aliases, address, address)
    fragment = kind === :fragment
    key, linked = _split_once(crawl.file_name(address; fragment, ext=".html"), '?')
    (; address, fragment, key, linked, id)
end

# Whether `url` names an entry that answered as a private page.
function _site_private(crawl::_SiteCrawl, base::AbstractString, url::AbstractString, kind::Symbol)
    lock(() -> isempty(crawl.private), crawl.lock) && return false
    _site_internal(url) || return false
    key = _site_address(crawl, base, url, kind).key
    lock(crawl.lock) do
        i = get(crawl.keys, key, nothing)
        !isnothing(i) && haskey(crawl.private, i)
    end
end

# Queue `url` (if new) and return what the page writes for it: the marker of
# its file, then the query the link keeps (one `file_name` names after a `?`,
# or a file address's own) and the `#id`.
function _site_target!(crawl::_SiteCrawl, base::AbstractString, url::AbstractString, kind::Symbol)
    _site_internal(url) || return nothing
    (; address, fragment, key, linked, id) = _site_address(crawl, base, url, kind)
    path, query = _split_once(address, '?')
    own = _static_own_extension(path)
    i = lock(crawl.lock) do
        i = get(crawl.keys, key, nothing)
        if isnothing(i)
            push!(crawl.entries, _SiteEntry(address, fragment))
            i = crawl.keys[key] = length(crawl.entries)
        elseif crawl.strict && _site_identity(crawl.entries[i].address) != _site_identity(address) &&
               !any(f -> first(f) == address, crawl.failed)
            push!(crawl.failed, address => "has the same file ($key) as $(crawl.entries[i].address); " *
                  "pass export_site a file_name that tells them apart")
        end
        i
    end
    kept = something(linked, isnothing(own) ? nothing : query, Some(nothing))
    _site_marker(crawl, i) * (isnothing(kept) ? "" : "?" * kept) * (isnothing(id) ? "" : "#" * id)
end

# The file extension an answer's `Content-Type` calls for, or `nothing`.
const _SITE_EXTENSIONS = Dict(
    "text/html" => ".html", "application/json" => ".json", "application/geo+json" => ".geojson",
    "text/markdown" => ".md", "text/plain" => ".txt", "text/csv" => ".csv", "text/css" => ".css",
    "text/javascript" => ".js", "application/javascript" => ".js", "application/xml" => ".xml",
    "application/pdf" => ".pdf", "application/zip" => ".zip", "image/svg+xml" => ".svg",
    "image/png" => ".png", "image/jpeg" => ".jpg", "image/webp" => ".webp",
    "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet" => ".xlsx")
_site_extension(resp::HTTP.Response) =
    get(_SITE_EXTENSIONS, lowercase(strip(first(split(HTTP.header(resp, "Content-Type"), ';')))), nothing)

# Why an answer must not go into the bundle, or `nothing`. Under htmx a failed
# fragment still answers 200 with its error article (so htmx swaps it in);
# only its `X-HTMXO-Error-Id` header tells it apart.
function _site_failure(resp::HTTP.Response)
    resp.status == 200 || return "HTTP $(resp.status)"
    id = HTTP.header(resp, "X-HTMXO-Error-Id")
    isempty(id) ? nothing : "error $id (X-HTMXO-Error-Id)"
end

# One address's answer. A file address (one with an extension other than
# `.html`) is fetched as its route answers it; anything else is rendered as a
# static export, and a route that returns a finished response (a download, an
# API's JSON) is fetched as it answers too.
function _site_fetch(crawl::_SiteCrawl, e::_SiteEntry, controls::Symbol, parent, audience::Symbol)
    headers = e.fragment ? ["HX-Request" => "true"] : Pair{String,String}[]
    plain() = dispatch(:GET, e.address; headers, parent, operation_policy=:blocking, audience)
    own = _static_own_extension(first(_split_once(e.address, '?')))
    isnothing(own) || own == ".html" || own == ".htm" || return plain()
    try
        dispatch(:GET, e.address; headers, parent,
                 static=StaticExport(_SiteMap(crawl, e.address), controls, "", :linked, audience))
    catch err
        err isa StaticExportRefused || rethrow()
        plain()
    end
end

"""
    export_site(dir; pages, file_name=static_file_name, controls=:remove,
                aliases=Dict{String,String}(), parent=nothing, audience=:public)
        -> (; files, bytes)

Write the mounted app into the new or empty directory `dir` as a static site
that any static file server shows as it is, from the bundle's root or from a
subdirectory (`https://<user>.github.io/<repo>/`), with no server-side code.
`route!` must have registered the app; no listener is needed, and the export
can run inside a serving app (it renders in process with
`dispatch(...; static=StaticExport(...))`, which re-registers nothing).

- **What is written.** Starting from `pages` (app addresses), every address
  the written answers reference, until nothing new turns up: links (`href`),
  `hx-get` fragments, each value a [`url_params`](@ref) element declares, each
  address a route passes to [`static_url`](@ref) (a Vega spec's `data.url`),
  `hx-push-url` pages, and the head's script and style links, so the browser
  libraries and the linked HTMXObjects runtime come from the app's own
  [`vendorfiles`](@ref) or `staticfiles` routes, with every file a stylesheet
  loads through `url(...)` (KaTeX's fonts) and every language grammar the
  PrismJS autoloader can fetch. A relative address resolves
  against the address being rendered. Addresses on other sites, `#id`s and
  `?query` links of the page itself stay as they are.
- **Page or fragment.** An address in `pages`, or reached through a link,
  `hx-push-url`, `src` or `static_url`, is fetched as a full answer (a page in
  its page chrome); one reached through `hx-get` or as a `url_params` value is
  fetched as a fragment (`HX-Request: true`), into its own file. A route that
  returns a finished response (a [`MIMEResponse`](@ref) download, a
  [`JSONResponse`](@ref)) is written as it answers.
- **File names** come from `file_name` ([`static_file_name`](@ref) by default):
  flat names in the bundle's root for pages, below `hx/` for fragments, the
  path for an address with a file extension, and otherwise the extension the
  answer's `Content-Type` calls for (`/insitu/raster` answering GeoJSON →
  `insitu-raster.geojson`). Every link is written relative to the bundle's
  root. Under the default names two addresses never silently share a file: a
  collision is a failure naming both. Addresses an app's own `file_name` gives
  one name share one file, rendered once from the first one reached (list the
  plain address in `pages` to render that one). A name with a query
  (`"lieferflaechen.html?ortsteil=0101"`) names the file before the `?`, and
  links to that address keep the query.
- **A page that applies its address in the browser is one file.** A page
  address with a query (`/?region=09&jahr=2024`, a zoom link) shares the file
  of its page (`/`) when that page is in the bundle too and its
  [`url_params`](@ref) elements declare every parameter of the query with
  `values` that hold the query's value. Links to the address name the page's
  file with the query (`index.html?region=09&jahr=2024`); the page's runtime
  loads the view the query names from the fragment written for it, and the
  rest of the page is as its own address renders it. Any other page address
  with a query is a file of its own, rendered with that query: one with a
  parameter no element declares, or declares without `values`, a value
  outside them, or a parameter named twice. These addresses are decided once
  nothing else is left to render, so their page has been met if anything
  links it; list the page in `pages` when only its addresses with a query
  are. A `file_name` that names such an address with a query has named it
  already.
- **Controls** a static site cannot serve (a non-GET `hx-*`, an unmapped
  `hx-get` with a query) follow `controls`: `:remove` (default) leaves them
  out, `:disable` greys them out ([`StaticExport`](@ref)). A page's linked
  runtime stays linked, so the bundle carries it once.
- `aliases` points an address that needs a server (an interactive API viewer)
  at another address, whose file is linked instead:
  `Dict("/api/v1/docs" => "/api/v1/openapi.json")`.
- `parent` is the progress node each route's work hangs under (`dispatch`).
- **Private content.** `audience` says who reads the site: `:public` (default)
  or `:private`. A public site shows the stand-in of each [`private`](@ref)
  part, or nothing, and never follows a link inside what it leaves out. A page
  or fragment whose whole answer is private gets no file, and every element
  that links or loads it is left out; a page written before such a page was
  found is rendered again for that. An address a public answer names where
  the export cannot leave it out (a script, a JSON payload) is a failure, as
  is private markup rendered before the export ([`PrivateContentEscaped`](@ref)).
  `audience=:private` writes everything, as the live app shows it.
- A route can tell it is being exported with `static_export(__req__) !== nothing`
  (to show a snapshot notice, say), and for whom with
  [`export_audience`](@ref)`(__req__)`.

`.nojekyll` is written too (GitHub Pages otherwise drops `_`-prefixed files
such as `_hyperscript.min.js`). Then every `href`, `src`, `hx-get` and
`hx-push-url`/`hx-replace-url` of an element in the written HTML, and every
Vega `"url"` in its scripts, is checked to name a file of the bundle, which
also catches an address a route wrote into raw markup the walk never sees. The
text of a `<style>` or `<script>` and an HTML comment are not markup, so an
`href="…"` there (a CSS comment of the inline runtime) is not checked. An
answer with a status other than 200 or an `X-HTMXO-Error-Id` header (a failed
fragment answers 200 under htmx) is not written; the crawl carries on, so one
run finds every failure, and then throws
[`StaticSiteIncomplete`](@ref) listing each failed address and each unresolved
reference. Otherwise it returns the number of files and bytes written.

```julia
route!(App())
vendorfiles()
export_site("site"; pages = ["/", "/bericht", "/daten"])
```
"""
function export_site(dir::AbstractString; pages, file_name=static_file_name, controls::Symbol=:remove,
                     aliases=Dict{String,String}(), parent=nothing, audience=:public)
    # refuses a `controls` or `audience` it does not know
    audience = StaticExport(nothing, controls, "", :linked, audience).audience
    isdir(dir) && !isempty(readdir(dir)) && throw(ArgumentError(
        "export_site writes into a new or empty directory; $(repr(dir)) is not empty"))
    aliases = Dict{String,String}(String(a) => String(b) for (a, b) in aliases)
    for address in Iterators.flatten((pages, keys(aliases), values(aliases)))
        startswith(address, '/') && !startswith(address, "//") || throw(ArgumentError(
            "export_site: $(repr(address)) is not an address of this app (it must start with `/`)"))
    end
    mkpath(dir)
    root = normpath(abspath(dir))
    crawl = _SiteCrawl(file_name, aliases, "htmxo" * Random.randstring('a':'z', 12) * "f",
        file_name === static_file_name, _SiteEntry[], Dict{String,Int}(), Pair{String,String}[],
        Dict{Int,Int}(), ReentrantLock())
    foreach(p -> _site_target!(crawl, "/", p, :full), pages)
    written = _SiteWritten(root)
    i = 0
    # Page addresses with a query wait until nothing else is left, so the page
    # whose file they may share is written by then (`_site_shares_page!`). One
    # whose page the crawl has not met waits for the others, which may reach it.
    queried = Int[]
    while true
        while i < length(crawl.entries) || !isempty(queried)
            if i < length(crawl.entries)
                i += 1
                _site_query_page(crawl, i) ? push!(queried, i) :
                    _site_write!(crawl, written, i, controls, parent, audience)
            else
                k = popat!(queried, something(findfirst(k -> !isnothing(_site_page_entry(crawl, k)), queried), 1))
                _site_shares_page!(crawl, written, k) ||
                    _site_write!(crawl, written, k, controls, parent, audience)
            end
        end
        # A file rendered before a page it refers to answered as private still
        # refers to it; rendered again, the walk leaves those references out.
        stale = _site_stale(crawl, written)
        isempty(stale) && break
        foreach(k -> _site_write!(crawl, written, k, controls, parent, audience), stale)
    end
    for k in sort!(collect(written.marked)), j in unique(_site_markers(crawl, read(written.paths[k], String)))
        haskey(crawl.private, j) && push!(crawl.failed, crawl.entries[j].address =>
            "is private, but $(relpath(written.paths[k], root)) refers to it where the export " *
            "cannot leave the reference out (a script or a JSON payload)")
    end
    # A failed address keeps its own path, which the reference check below
    # does not report again.
    failed = Set(first(_split_once(a, '?')) for (a, _) in crawl.failed)
    unmarked(m) = (k = parse(Int, m[length(crawl.marker)+1:end-1]);
                   get(() -> first(_split_once(crawl.entries[k].address, '?')), written.links, k))
    marker = Regex(crawl.marker * "[0-9]+x")
    for k in written.marked
        file = written.paths[k]
        write(file, _site_unmark(read(file, String), marker, unmarked, endswith(file, ".html")))
    end
    touch(joinpath(root, ".nojekyll"))
    unresolved = _site_unresolved(root, failed)
    bytes = sum(filesize, values(written.paths); init=0)
    files = length(written.paths)
    isempty(crawl.failed) && isempty(unresolved) ||
        throw(StaticSiteIncomplete(root, crawl.failed, unresolved, files, bytes))
    (; files, bytes)
end

# What the crawl has written, by entry: its file, the address pages link it
# by, how many private pages were known when it was rendered, and whether its
# answer carries markers.
struct _SiteWritten
    root::String
    paths::Dict{Int,String}
    links::Dict{Int,String}
    rendered::Dict{Int,Int}
    marked::Set{Int}
    owners::Dict{String,Int}  # file → entry
    declared::Dict{Int,Vector{Any}}  # page → the parameters its url_params elements declare
end
_SiteWritten(root) = _SiteWritten(root, Dict{Int,String}(), Dict{Int,String}(), Dict{Int,Int}(),
                                  Set{Int}(), Dict{String,Int}(), Dict{Int,Vector{Any}}())

# Whether entry `i` is a page address with a query that `file_name` names
# without one, and so may share its page's file.
function _site_query_page(crawl::_SiteCrawl, i::Int)
    e = crawl.entries[i]
    path, query = _split_once(e.address, '?')
    !e.fragment && !isnothing(query) && !isempty(query) && isnothing(_static_own_extension(path)) &&
        isnothing(last(_split_once(crawl.file_name(e.address; fragment=false, ext=".html"), '?')))
end

# The entry of the page of entry `i` (its address without the query), or
# `nothing` while the crawl has not met that page.
function _site_page_entry(crawl::_SiteCrawl, i::Int)
    key = _site_address(crawl, "/", first(_split_once(crawl.entries[i].address, '?')), :full).key
    j = lock(() -> get(crawl.keys, key, nothing), crawl.lock)
    isnothing(j) || crawl.entries[j].fragment ? nothing : j
end

# Entry `i`, a page address with a query, shares the file of its page when
# that page is in the bundle and applies the query in the browser
# (`_site_applies`): links to it name the page's file with the query.
function _site_shares_page!(crawl::_SiteCrawl, written::_SiteWritten, i::Int)
    j = _site_page_entry(crawl, i)
    (isnothing(j) || !haskey(written.paths, j)) && return false
    query = last(_split_once(crawl.entries[i].address, '?'))
    declared = get!(written.declared, j) do
        file = written.paths[j]
        endswith(file, ".html") ? _site_url_params(read(file, String)) : Any[]
    end
    _site_applies(declared, query) || return false
    written.links[i] = written.links[j] * "?" * _static_query_link(query)
    true
end

# Whether a page whose `url_params` elements declare `declared` shows, from its
# own file, what the address query `query` names: each parameter, named once,
# is declared with values (the export wrote a fragment for each), and the
# query's value is among them in every element that declares it. The runtime
# reads the address as `URLSearchParams` does (`+` is a space).
function _site_applies(declared, query::AbstractString)
    names = String[]
    for part in split(query, '&'; keepempty=false)
        name, value = (_static_unescape(replace(something(s, ""), '+' => ' ')) for s in _split_once(part, '='))
        name in names && return false
        push!(names, name)
        ps = filter(p -> p.name == name, declared)
        !isempty(ps) && all(p -> !isnothing(p.values) && value in p.values, ps) || return false
    end
    !isempty(names)
end

# A query as a link keeps it: each character a URL cannot carry as it is, or
# that would end a quoted string around it, percent-escaped (the page's
# runtime decodes it back); `%` escapes stay as they are.
_static_query_link(query::AbstractString) =
    replace(query, r"[^A-Za-z0-9\-._~!$&()*+,;=:@/?%]" =>
                   c -> join('%' * uppercase(string(b; base=16, pad=2)) for b in codeunits(c)))

# Fetch entry `i` and write its file, or record why not.
function _site_write!(crawl::_SiteCrawl, written::_SiteWritten, i::Int, controls, parent, audience)
    e = crawl.entries[i]
    known = lock(() -> length(crawl.private), crawl.lock)
    resp = try
        _site_fetch(crawl, e, controls, parent, audience)
    catch err
        err isa PrivateContentEscaped || rethrow()
        return lock(() -> push!(crawl.failed, e.address => sprint(showerror, err)), crawl.lock)
    end
    if _private_page_response(resp)
        return lock(() -> (crawl.private[i] = length(crawl.private) + 1), crawl.lock)
    end
    reason = _site_failure(resp)
    path = first(_split_once(e.address, '?'))
    ext = isnothing(reason) ? something(_static_own_extension(path), _site_extension(resp), Some(nothing)) : nothing
    if isnothing(reason) && isnothing(ext)
        reason = "no file extension for its Content-Type $(repr(HTTP.header(resp, "Content-Type"))); " *
                 "give the address one, or pass export_site a file_name"
    end
    name = isnothing(reason) ? first(_split_once(crawl.file_name(e.address; fragment=e.fragment, ext), '?')) : ""
    file = normpath(joinpath(written.root, name))
    if isnothing(reason) && (isabspath(name) || !startswith(file, joinpath(written.root, "")))
        reason = "its file name $(repr(name)) leaves the bundle"
    elseif isnothing(reason) && get(written.owners, file, i) != i
        reason = "has the same file ($name) as $(crawl.entries[written.owners[file]].address)"
    end
    isnothing(reason) || return lock(() -> push!(crawl.failed, e.address => reason), crawl.lock)
    body = _response_bytes(resp)
    mkpath(dirname(file))
    write(file, body)
    written.owners[file] = i
    written.paths[i] = file
    written.links[i] = _static_link(name)
    written.rendered[i] = known
    delete!(written.declared, i)
    _body_contains(body, crawl.marker) ? push!(written.marked, i) : delete!(written.marked, i)
    ext == ".css" && _site_follow_stylesheet!(crawl, e.address, body)
    endswith(path, "/" * _PRISM_AUTOLOADER) && _site_follow_autoloader!(crawl, e.address)
end

# The entries each marker in `text` names.
_site_markers(crawl::_SiteCrawl, text::AbstractString) =
    (parse(Int, m[1]) for m in eachmatch(Regex(crawl.marker * "([0-9]+)x"), text))

# Written entries that refer to a page found private after they were rendered.
function _site_stale(crawl::_SiteCrawl, written::_SiteWritten)
    isempty(crawl.private) && return Int[]
    sort!([k for k in written.marked
           if any(j -> get(crawl.private, j, 0) > written.rendered[k],
                  _site_markers(crawl, read(written.paths[k], String)))])
end

# A stylesheet's `url(...)`s resolve against the stylesheet itself, and its
# file keeps its path, so each file it loads (KaTeX's fonts) is queued and
# keeps its own path, and the stylesheet is written as it answers.
function _site_follow_stylesheet!(crawl::_SiteCrawl, address::AbstractString, body)
    css = body isa AbstractString ? body : String(copy(body))
    for m in eachmatch(r"url\(\s*['\"]?([^'\")\s]+)['\"]?\s*\)", css)
        url = m.captures[1]
        _site_internal(url) || continue
        loaded = _site_resolve(first(_split_once(address, '?')), first(_split_once(url, '#')))
        if isnothing(_static_own_extension(first(_split_once(loaded, '?'))))
            lock(() -> push!(crawl.failed, loaded => "the stylesheet $address loads it, but it has " *
                "no file extension, so its file would not be at the address the stylesheet names"), crawl.lock)
        else
            _site_target!(crawl, address, url, :full)
        end
    end
end

# The PrismJS autoloader fetches each language's grammar by name from the
# `components/` directory beside its `plugins/`, an address no answer names,
# so every grammar it can fetch is queued beside it.
function _site_follow_autoloader!(crawl::_SiteCrawl, address::AbstractString)
    for f in _prism_languages()
        _site_target!(crawl, address, "../../components/" * f, :full)
    end
end

_body_contains(body::AbstractString, s::AbstractString) = occursin(s, body)
_body_contains(body::AbstractVector{UInt8}, s::AbstractString) = !isnothing(findfirst(codeunits(s), body))

const _SITE_ATTRIBUTE = r"\b(?:href|src|hx-get|hx-push-url|hx-replace-url)=\"([^\"]*)\""
const _SITE_SCRIPT_URL = r"\"url\":\"([^\"]*)\""
# An HTML comment, or a `<script>`/`<style>` element: its tag's attributes,
# then its text, which ends at the first closing tag (as a browser reads it).
const _SITE_RAW_TEXT = r"<!--.*?-->|<(script|style)\b((?:[^>\"']|\"[^\"]*\"|'[^']*')*)>(.*?)</\1\b[^>]*>"si

# The addresses an HTML file names, in order: each `href`, `src`, `hx-get`,
# `hx-push-url` and `hx-replace-url` attribute of an element (its character
# references decoded), and each `"url"` in a script (a Vega spec's
# `data.url`). The text of a `<script>` or `<style>` and an HTML comment hold
# no elements, so an `href="…"` written there (a comment of the inline
# runtime's styles) is not one.
function _site_references(html::AbstractString)
    references = String[]
    _site_scan(text -> for m in eachmatch(_SITE_ATTRIBUTE, text)
                           push!(references, _site_attribute_text(m[1]))
                       end,
               text -> foreach(u -> push!(references, u[1]), eachmatch(_SITE_SCRIPT_URL, text)),
               html)
    references
end

# Calls `markup` with each stretch of `html` that holds elements and `script`
# with the text of each `<script>`, in order. An HTML comment and the text of
# a `<script>` or `<style>` hold no elements; the tag's own attributes do.
function _site_scan(markup, script, html::AbstractString)
    at = 1
    for m in eachmatch(_SITE_RAW_TEXT, html)
        markup(SubString(html, at, prevind(html, m.offset)))
        if !isnothing(m[1])
            markup(m[2])
            lowercase(m[1]) == "script" && script(m[3])
        end
        at = m.offset + ncodeunits(m.match)
    end
    markup(SubString(html, at))
    nothing
end

# An attribute's value as the element holds it: its character references decoded.
_site_attribute_text(s::AbstractString) =
    replace(s, "&quot;" => "\"", "&#39;" => "'", "&lt;" => "<", "&gt;" => ">", "&amp;" => "&")

const _SITE_URL_PARAMS = r"\bdata-htmxo-url-params=\"([^\"]*)\""

# The parameters the `url_params` elements of an HTML file declare.
function _site_url_params(html::AbstractString)
    declared = Any[]
    _site_scan(text -> for m in eachmatch(_SITE_URL_PARAMS, text)
                           append!(declared, _url_params_decode(_site_attribute_text(m[1])))
                       end,
               _ -> nothing, html)
    declared
end

# `text` with each `marker` replaced by `link(marker)`: as an attribute value
# where an HTML file holds elements (a link that keeps a query writes `&amp;`),
# as it is elsewhere (a script's text, a JSON file).
function _site_unmark(text::AbstractString, marker::Regex, link, html::Bool)
    html || return replace(text, marker => link)
    io = IOBuffer()
    at = 1
    for m in eachmatch(_SITE_RAW_TEXT, text)
        print(io, replace(SubString(text, at, prevind(text, m.offset)), marker => escape_html ∘ link))
        print(io, replace(m.match, marker => link))
        at = m.offset + ncodeunits(m.match)
    end
    print(io, replace(SubString(text, at), marker => escape_html ∘ link))
    String(take!(io))
end

# Every reference in the bundle's HTML that names no file of it, as
# `file => address`: a rooted address (it escaped the export, and would point
# outside a bundle served from a subdirectory) or a relative one whose file is
# missing. Addresses whose export failed are already reported as failures.
function _site_unresolved(root::AbstractString, failed::Set{String})
    unresolved = Pair{String,String}[]
    for (dirpath, _, files) in walkdir(root), f in sort(files)
        endswith(f, ".html") || continue
        file = joinpath(dirpath, f)
        for address in _site_references(read(file, String))
            (_site_internal(address) && address != "true" && address != "false") || continue
            path = first(split(address, r"[?#]"; limit=2))
            ok = startswith(path, '/') ? path in failed :
                 isfile(joinpath(root, _static_unescape(path)))
            ok || push!(unresolved, relpath(file, root) => address)
        end
    end
    unresolved
end

# --- Prestarted fragments ---
#
# A load-triggered fragment (`lazy(url)`, `hx-get` with `hx-trigger="load"`) is
# requested as soon as htmx starts on the page, which is only after the page
# arrived and its scripts ran. While rendering a response for a browser, the
# server starts the operations of those fragments itself and adds their token
# to each `hx-get`, so the browser's request joins the running operation (as
# an attach, `_execute_operation_resume`) instead of starting it. Nothing runs
# that the browser would not request: only an `hx-get` the server can repeat
# exactly qualifies — a load-only trigger, an address on this server, and no
# `hx-vals`, `hx-vars`, `hx-include`, `hx-headers` or `hx-request` on it or an
# enclosing element in the response. A request that does not match the
# prestart (other arguments, other session) is computed fresh, as before.

# The request pipeline a prestart runs through: the app's `middleware` around
# the router, as `serve` composed it; `nothing` outside `serve` or with
# `serve(; prestart=false)`, which turns prestarts off.
const _PRESTART_APP = Ref{Any}(nothing)
# Prestarts per response, and unclaimed prestarts held at once: past either the
# browser starts the rest itself.
const _PRESTART_LIMIT = 24
const _PRESTART_LIVE_LIMIT = 96

# The app's `middleware` around `handler`, as `serve` composes it, without the
# access log, the runtime tracker or Revise: the pipeline of the requests the
# server makes itself (prestarts, pushed answers).
_inner_pipeline(middleware, handler) =
    foldr((m, app) -> m(app), middleware; init=_fallback_middleware(handler))

_prestart_pipeline(middleware) = _inner_pipeline(middleware, _prestart_router)

# `ROUTER` for a prestart: an `@htmx` route only. Any other handler (an `@sse`
# or `@ws` route, one an app registered on `ROUTER` itself) would run inline
# while the page renders, so the browser's own request goes there.
function _prestart_router(req::HTTP.Request)
    handler = first(HTTP.Handlers.gethandler(ROUTER, req))
    handler isa _OperationRoute || return HTTP.Response(404)
    ROUTER(req)
end

_prestart_slot(req::HTTP.Request) = get(req.context, :htmxo_prestart, nothing)
_prestart_slot(_) = nothing

# A response is prestarted only when it goes to a browser that will run its
# htmx: a page navigation (`Accept: text/html`, as the page-load deferral reads
# it) or an htmx request. A script, a static export, an in-process `dispatch`
# and the server's own prewarm requests get the response as rendered.
function _prestart_wanted(req::HTTP.Request)
    _PRESTART_APP[] === nothing && return false
    get(req.context, :htmxo_served, false) === true || return false
    _prestart_slot(req) === nothing || return false
    static_export(req) === nothing || return false
    _operation_prewarm_request(req) && return false
    is_htmx(req) || _operation_rich_page_request(req)
end

struct _PrestartWalk
    req::HTTP.Request
    current_url::String
    count::Base.RefValue{Int}
end

function _prestart_fragments(req::HTTP.Request, val)
    _prestart_wanted(req) || return val
    value = _html_value(val)
    walk = _PrestartWalk(req, _prestart_current_url(req), Ref(0))
    walked = _prestart_walk(value, walk, false)
    walked === nothing ? val : walked
end

# Each method returns the rewritten value, or `nothing` when nothing in it
# changed, so an untouched subtree is returned as it was.
_prestart_walk(val, walk, blocked) = nothing
_prestart_walk(doc::HTMLDocument, walk, blocked) =
    _prestart_rewrap(_prestart_walk(doc.root, walk, blocked), HTMLDocument)
_prestart_walk(val::HtmlOnly, walk, blocked) =
    _prestart_rewrap(_prestart_walk(val.content, walk, blocked), HtmlOnly)
_prestart_walk(p::Private, walk, blocked) = _ambient_audience() === :public ?
    _prestart_rewrap(_prestart_walk(p.public, walk, blocked), x -> Private(p.content, x, p.mark)) :
    _prestart_walk_private(_built(p.content), p, walk, blocked)
# Content built for the walk replaces its function, so the page that renders
# the walked value does not build it again.
function _prestart_walk_private(content, p::Private, walk, blocked)
    walked = _prestart_walk(content, walk, blocked)
    isnothing(walked) && content === p.content && return nothing
    Private(isnothing(walked) ? content : walked, p.public, p.mark)
end
_prestart_walk(val::_LabelledControl, walk, blocked) =
    _prestart_rewrap(_prestart_walk(val.control, walk, blocked), _LabelledControl)
_prestart_walk((content, id)::Pair, walk, blocked) =
    _prestart_rewrap(_prestart_walk(content, walk, blocked), walked -> walked => id)
_prestart_rewrap(::Nothing, wrap) = nothing
_prestart_rewrap(walked, wrap) = wrap(walked)

function _prestart_walk(items::Union{AbstractVector,Tuple}, walk, blocked)
    changed = nothing
    for (i, item) in enumerate(items)
        walked = _prestart_walk(item, walk, blocked)
        walked === nothing && continue
        changed === nothing && (changed = collect(Any, items))
        changed[i] = walked
    end
    changed === nothing ? nothing : (items isa Tuple ? Tuple(changed) : changed)
end

const _PRESTART_INHERITED = (_HX_VALUES_ATTRS..., _HX_INCLUDE_ATTRS...,
    Symbol.(("hx-headers", "hx-request", "data-hx-headers", "data-hx-request"))...)

function _prestart_walk(node::Node, walk::_PrestartWalk, blocked::Bool)
    attrs = HTMX.attrs(node)
    blocked = blocked || any(attr -> haskey(attrs, attr), _PRESTART_INHERITED)
    new_attrs = nothing
    url = blocked ? nothing : _prestart_candidate(attrs)
    if url !== nothing
        token = _prestart!(walk, url, attrs)
        if token !== nothing
            new_attrs = copy(attrs)
            new_attrs[Symbol("hx-get")] =
                _operation_marker_url(url, "__htmxo_operation", token)
        end
    end
    children = HTMX.children(node)
    new_children = _prestart_walk(children, walk, blocked)
    new_attrs === nothing && new_children === nothing && return nothing
    Node(HTMX.tag(node), something(new_attrs, attrs),
         something(new_children, children))
end

# The address of a load-triggered `hx-get` to this server, or `nothing`.
function _prestart_candidate(attrs)
    url = get(attrs, Symbol("hx-get"), nothing)
    trigger = get(attrs, Symbol("hx-trigger"), nothing)
    (url isa AbstractString && trigger isa AbstractString) || return nothing
    _prestart_load_only(trigger) || return nothing
    startswith(url, "/") && !startswith(url, "//") || return nothing
    # Already joined to an operation (a direct page's placeholder, a poller),
    # or a form refresh.
    occursin("__htmxo_", url) && return nothing
    String(url)
end

# Every trigger is `load`, optionally `once` or with a `delay:`. A filter, a
# second event or `every` would send the request again, or not at all.
_prestart_load_only(trigger::AbstractString) =
    all(spec -> occursin(r"^load(\s+(once|delay:\d+(\.\d+)?(ms|s)?))*$", strip(spec)),
        split(trigger, ','))

_prestart_live_count() = lock(_operation_poll_lock) do
    count(entry -> entry.prestarted && entry.polls == 0, values(_operation_polls))
end

# Starts one fragment through the app's pipeline; its token, or `nothing` when
# nothing was started (over a limit, refused by middleware, an unknown route,
# a route answered inline, a failure).
function _prestart!(walk::_PrestartWalk, url::String, attrs)
    app = _PRESTART_APP[]
    app === nothing && return nothing
    walk.count[] >= _PRESTART_LIMIT && return nothing
    _prestart_live_count() >= _PRESTART_LIVE_LIMIT && return nothing
    route = _prestart_route_target(walk.req, url)
    route === nothing && return nothing
    walk.count[] += 1
    request = HTTP.Request("GET", route, _prestart_headers(walk, attrs), UInt8[])
    slot = Ref{Union{Nothing,String}}(nothing)
    request.context[:htmxo_prestart] = slot
    response = try
        app(request)
    catch err
        @warn "Prestarting a load-triggered fragment failed; the browser's request computes it" url exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        return nothing
    end
    response isa HTTP.Response && response.status == 204 ? slot[] : nothing
end

# The address `url` reaches this server under. Behind a proxy that strips the
# mount prefix (`X-Forwarded-Prefix`) on the way in, the rendered address
# carries the prefix and the router knows it without; an address outside the
# prefix goes to another server through that proxy (`nothing`).
function _prestart_route_target(req::HTTP.Request, url::String)
    prefix = _request_prefix(req, "")
    isempty(prefix) && return url
    path = _request_route_path(req)
    (path == prefix || startswith(path, prefix * "/")) && return url
    startswith(url, prefix) || return nothing
    rest = url[nextind(url, lastindex(prefix)):end]
    isempty(rest) && return "/"
    first(rest) == '/' && return rest
    first(rest) == '?' ? "/" * rest : nothing
end

# The headers of the browser's request for this fragment, as far as the server
# can know them: the page request's own (cookies, authorization, language),
# without those that describe that request's body or caching, plus htmx's.
const _PRESTART_DROPPED_HEADERS = Set(["accept", "accept-encoding",
    "content-type", "content-length", "content-encoding", "transfer-encoding",
    "if-none-match", "if-modified-since", "range", "expect", "connection",
    "upgrade", "te", "keep-alive", "last-event-id", "cache-control", "pragma"])

function _prestart_headers(walk::_PrestartWalk, attrs)
    headers = _inner_htmx_headers(walk.req, walk.current_url)
    id = get(attrs, :id, nothing)
    id isa AbstractString && !isempty(id) && push!(headers, "HX-Trigger" => String(id))
    target = _prestart_target_id(get(attrs, Symbol("hx-target"), nothing), id)
    target === nothing || push!(headers, "HX-Target" => target)
    headers
end

# The headers of an htmx GET the page at `current_url` would send, derived from
# `req`, a request of that page's browser: its own headers (cookies,
# authorization, language, a proxy's `X-Forwarded-*`) without those that
# describe that request's body, caching or kind, plus htmx's.
function _inner_htmx_headers(req::HTTP.Request, current_url::AbstractString)
    headers = Pair{String,String}[]
    for (name, value) in req.headers
        key = lowercase(name)
        (key in _PRESTART_DROPPED_HEADERS || startswith(key, "hx-") ||
         startswith(key, "sec-fetch-") || startswith(key, "htmxo-")) && continue
        push!(headers, String(name) => String(value))
    end
    push!(headers, "Accept" => "*/*", "HX-Request" => "true")
    isempty(current_url) || push!(headers, "HX-Current-URL" => String(current_url))
    headers
end

_prestart_target_id(::Nothing, id) = _prestart_target_id("this", id)
function _prestart_target_id(target::AbstractString, id)
    target == "this" && return id isa AbstractString && !isempty(id) ? String(id) : nothing
    occursin(r"^#[A-Za-z][-\w]*$", target) ? String(target[2:end]) : nothing
end
_prestart_target_id(_, id) = nothing

# The page the fragment's request will come from: an htmx request's own page,
# or the address of the page being sent.
function _prestart_current_url(req::HTTP.Request)
    current = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Current-URL")
    isempty(current) || return String(current)
    host = HTTP.header(req, "X-Forwarded-Host", HTTP.header(req, "Host"))
    isempty(host) && return ""
    scheme = HTTP.header(req, "X-Forwarded-Proto", "http")
    target = String(req.target)
    qi = findfirst('?', target)
    query = isnothing(qi) ? "" : target[qi:end]
    string(scheme, "://", host,
           _request_route_path(req, _request_prefix(req, "")), query)
end

# --- Operation stream ---
#
# A running `:auto` poller polls its operation every `poll_interval`, so its
# result reaches the page only at the next poll, and each running poller costs
# the server a request five times a second. Under `serve(; operation_stream=
# true)` a running poller carries `data-htmxo-operation-stream` (the Treebars
# extension adds it to the polling element), and `operation_stream_script()`
# holds one event stream per page for the operations of those pollers. The
# stream only announces that an operation ended; the poller then fetches the
# answer with its own poll, so everything that handles a poll's answer still
# handles it. It is a GET of the page's own address carrying
# `__htmxo_operations=<token>,…`, answered after `middleware` and before
# routing: an auth gateway sees it like the page, and a proxy that mounts the
# app under a prefix passes it like the page.

# Whether `serve` answers operation streams (`serve(; operation_stream=)`).
const _OPERATION_STREAM = Ref(false)
const _OPERATION_STREAM_PARAM = "__htmxo_operations"
# Operations one stream covers. Every token is 64 characters, so 32 keep the
# stream's address near 2 KB; the page's other pollers poll as before.
const _OPERATION_STREAM_LIMIT = 32

# Whether a poller rendered for `req` is marked for the page's stream: only one
# for a request `serve` received, while it answers streams.
_operation_stream_wanted(req::HTTP.Request) =
    _OPERATION_STREAM[] && get(req.context, :htmxo_served, false) === true
_operation_stream_wanted(_) = false

_operation_stream_router(router) = function (req::HTTP.Request)
    replay = _pushed_answer(req)
    replay === nothing || return replay
    tokens = _operation_stream_tokens(req)
    tokens === nothing && return router(req)
    _is_stream_probe(req) && return _stream_probe_response()
    haskey(req.context, :stream) || return router(req)
    push = _operation_stream_push(req)
    _serve_sse(req, sse -> _operation_stream_body(sse, req, tokens; push))
end

# The operation tokens a stream request asks for, or `nothing` for any other
# request: one that is no GET, carries no `__htmxo_operations`, or neither
# accepts an event stream nor is the stream-failure probe.
function _operation_stream_tokens(req::HTTP.Request)
    req.method == "GET" || return nothing
    occursin(_OPERATION_STREAM_PARAM, req.target) || return nothing
    value = get(HTTP.queryparams(HTTP.URI(String(req.target))),
                _OPERATION_STREAM_PARAM, nothing)
    value === nothing && return nothing
    (_is_stream_probe(req) ||
     occursin("text/event-stream", HTTP.header(req, "Accept", ""))) || return nothing
    tokens = String[]
    for token in split(value, ',')
        length(tokens) == _OPERATION_STREAM_LIMIT && break
        occursin(r"^[0-9a-f]{64}$", token) && !(token in tokens) &&
            push!(tokens, String(token))
    end
    tokens
end

# The app's pipeline for the polls the stream makes itself to render the
# answers it pushes (`_inner_pipeline`); `nothing` outside `serve`.
const _OPERATION_PUSH_APP = Ref{Any}(nothing)
# Seconds between the running answers a pushing stream sends. Treebars shows a
# pushed poller as current while it hears from it at least every five seconds.
const _OPERATION_PUSH_INTERVAL = 1.0

# Whether the page asked for answers (`__htmxo_push=1`: it can apply them, see
# `operation_stream_script()`) and `serve` renders them.
_operation_stream_push(req::HTTP.Request) = _OPERATION_PUSH_APP[] !== nothing &&
    get(HTTP.queryparams(HTTP.URI(String(req.target))), "__htmxo_push", "") == "1"

# For each operation the stream covers: `gone <token>` at once when the server
# does not hold it (answered, expired, never started here); otherwise, on a
# pushing stream, its running answer every `_OPERATION_PUSH_INTERVAL` and its
# final answer once its computation has ended (`_operation_push!`), and on any
# other stream `ready <token>` once it has ended. Parts the operation sends
# ahead (`send_ahead`) go out as `ahead` events on either stream: every part so
# far when the stream opens, then each one as it is added (`_send_ahead!`). The
# body returns, closing the stream, when every operation has been sent.
function _operation_stream_body(sse, req, tokens::Vector{String}; push::Bool=false)
    # A token: that operation's computation ended. `""`: the push clock.
    # `+<token>`: that operation sent a part ahead.
    events = Channel{String}(Inf)
    waiting = String[]
    # The parts list of each operation that has one, and how many of its parts
    # this stream has sent.
    ahead = Dict{String,Tuple{_SentAhead,Int}}()
    for token in tokens
        handle = _operation_stream_handle(token)
        if handle === nothing
            HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, token; event="gone") || return nothing
        else
            push!(waiting, token)
            _operation_stream_watch(handle, token, events)
            sink = _sent_ahead_of(something(handle))
            sink === nothing ||
                (ahead[token] = (_watch_sent_ahead!(sink, events, "+" * token), 0))
        end
    end
    # A write to a page that has left fails at the latest with the keep-alive
    # comment; closing `events` then ends a stream whose operations never do.
    left = Timer(1.0; interval=1.0) do timer
        isopen(sse) || (close(events); close(timer))
    end
    ticked = Threads.Atomic{Bool}(false)
    clock = push ? Timer(_OPERATION_PUSH_INTERVAL;
                         interval=_OPERATION_PUSH_INTERVAL) do timer
        # One pending tick at a time: a slow round of renders skips ticks.
        Threads.atomic_xchg!(ticked, true) && return
        try
            put!(events, "")
        catch err
            err isa InvalidStateException || rethrow()
            close(timer)
        end
    end : nothing
    try
        for token in keys(ahead)
            _send_ahead!(sse, ahead, token) || return nothing
        end
        while !isempty(waiting)
            token = try
                take!(events)
            catch err
                err isa InvalidStateException && return nothing
                rethrow()
            end
            if startswith(token, '+')
                sent = token[2:end]
                sent in waiting && (_send_ahead!(sse, ahead, sent) || return nothing)
            elseif isempty(token)
                ticked[] = false
                for running in copy(waiting)
                    outcome = _operation_push!(sse, req, running; final=false)
                    outcome === :closed && return nothing
                    outcome === :ended && filter!(!=(running), waiting)
                end
            elseif token in waiting
                filter!(!=(token), waiting)
                outcome = push ? _operation_push!(sse, req, token; final=true) :
                    (HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, token; event="ready") ? :ended : :closed)
                outcome === :closed && return nothing
            end
        end
    finally
        close(left)
        clock === nothing || close(clock)
        for (sink, _) in values(ahead)
            _unwatch_sent_ahead!(sink, events)
        end
    end
    nothing
end

# Send the parts of `token`'s operation this stream has not sent yet, each as
# an `ahead` event: the token, the part's number and its HTML, a line each.
# `false` once the page has left.
function _send_ahead!(sse, ahead, token::String)
    sink, sent = ahead[token]
    for (i, html) in _sent_ahead_parts(sink, sent)
        HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, string(token, '\n', i, '\n', html);
                             event="ahead") || return false
        ahead[token] = (sink, i)
    end
    true
end

# Push the answer the page's poll of `token` would get now, rendered by
# polling it through the app's pipeline with the stream request's headers:
# `progress` with the running poller, `result` with a final answer the page
# can swap as it is (`_operation_pushable`), `ready` for any other final
# answer, which the page then fetches with its own poll. Every final answer is
# kept for that poll (`_pushed_answer`), so it is never computed twice. With
# nothing to render (no poller rendered yet, the operation gone), `final`
# sends `ready` and a running push sends nothing. Each frame's data is the
# token, a newline, then the answer. Returns `:running`, `:ended`, or `:closed`
# once the page has left.
function _operation_push!(sse, req, token::String; final::Bool)
    send(event, data="") = HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse,
        isempty(data) ? token : string(token, '\n', data); event)
    ended(sent) = sent ? :ended : :closed
    url = _operation_push_url(token)
    target = url === nothing ? nothing : _prestart_route_target(req, url)
    if target === nothing
        final || return :running
        return ended(send("ready"))
    end
    poll = HTTP.Request("GET", target,
        _inner_htmx_headers(req, HTTP.header(req, "Referer", "")), UInt8[])
    poll.context[:htmxo_served] = true
    poll.context[:htmxo_push] = true
    response = try
        _OPERATION_PUSH_APP[](poll)
    catch err
        @warn "Rendering an operation's pushed answer failed; the page polls for it" target exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        return ended(send("ready"))
    end
    if response.status == 200
        return send("progress", String(_message_body_bytes(response))) ? :running : :closed
    end
    _store_pushed_answer!(token, _request_route_path(poll), response)
    _operation_pushable(response) ?
        ended(send("result", String(_message_body_bytes(response)))) :
        ended(send("ready"))
end

# The page's poll address of a held operation whose poller was rendered.
function _operation_push_url(token::String)
    lock(_operation_poll_lock) do
        entry = get(_operation_polls, token, nothing)
        entry isa _OperationPollEntry && !isempty(entry.poll_url) ?
            entry.poll_url : nothing
    end
end

# A final answer the page can swap without htmx's own response handling: an
# HTML poll end with no header htmx or the page runtime acts on (`HX-*`,
# a recorded error's id, a toast). Anything else goes to the page's own poll.
function _operation_pushable(response::HTTP.Response)
    response.status == 286 || return false
    startswith(HTTP.header(response, "Content-Type", ""), "text/html") || return false
    !any(response.headers) do (name, _)
        key = lowercase(name)
        startswith(key, "hx-") || key in ("x-htmxo-error-id", "x-htmxo-toast")
    end
end

# Final answers a pushing stream rendered, by operation token, for the page's
# own poll of that operation: it gets the same answer instead of a second
# computation (a read's heal, a `@fresh` re-run). Bounded and expiring like the
# operation registry.
const _PUSHED_ANSWER_LIMIT = 256
const _PUSHED_ANSWER_TTL = 600.0
const _pushed_answers = Dict{String,@NamedTuple{path::String,response::HTTP.Response,at::Float64}}()

function _store_pushed_answer!(token::String, path::String, response::HTTP.Response)
    now = _operation_poll_now()
    stored = _copy_mutation_terminal(response)
    lock(_operation_poll_lock) do
        filter!(((_, kept),) -> now - kept.at <= _PUSHED_ANSWER_TTL, _pushed_answers)
        while length(_pushed_answers) >= _PUSHED_ANSWER_LIMIT
            delete!(_pushed_answers, argmin(t -> _pushed_answers[t].at, keys(_pushed_answers)))
        end
        _pushed_answers[token] = (; path, response=stored, at=now)
    end
    nothing
end

# A copy of the pushed answer of the operation a GET poll names, if the stream
# rendered one at that poll's path; `nothing` for any other request.
function _pushed_answer(req::HTTP.Request)
    req.method == "GET" && occursin("__htmxo_operation=", req.target) || return nothing
    isempty(_pushed_answers) && return nothing
    _operation_poll_request(req) || return nothing
    token = _operation_poll_token(req)
    token === nothing && return nothing
    path = _request_route_path(req)
    kept = lock(_operation_poll_lock) do
        found = get(_pushed_answers, token, nothing)
        found !== nothing && found.path == path &&
            _operation_poll_now() - found.at <= _PUSHED_ANSWER_TTL ? found : nothing
    end
    kept === nothing ? nothing : _copy_mutation_terminal(kept.response)
end

# What an operation's computation is behind its token, or `nothing`. A
# mutation whose answer is cached for replay has ended.
function _operation_stream_handle(token::String)
    lock(_operation_poll_lock) do
        entry = get(_operation_polls, token, nothing)
        entry isa _OperationPollEntry && return Some(entry.started)
        haskey(_mutation_terminals, token) ? Some(nothing) : nothing
    end
end

# Put `token` into `ended` once `handle`'s computation has ended. One watcher
# per operation and stream, on the interactive pool when there is one, like the
# operation-log watcher (`_watch_operation_finish!`).
function _operation_stream_watch(handle::Some, token::String, ended::Channel{String})
    started = something(handle)
    if !(started isa _OperationHandle)
        put!(ended, token)
        return nothing
    end
    watch = function ()
        try
            _runtime_await(started)
        catch
            # A failed computation has ended too; the poll answers its failure.
        end
        try
            put!(ended, token)
        catch err
            # The page left and its stream closed `ended`.
            err isa InvalidStateException ||
                @error "Operation stream watcher failed" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        end
    end
    Threads.nthreads(:interactive) > 0 ?
        Threads.@spawn(:interactive, watch()) : Threads.@spawn(watch())
    nothing
end

# --- Convenience helpers ---

# The `preload` keyword shared by the navigation components: `nothing`/`false`
# adds nothing, `true` preloads on hover, and a string or symbol passes through
# as the extension's trigger (`"mousedown"`, `"preload:init"`, …). Normalized
# because a bare `preload=true` would render `preload="true"`, which the
# extension reads as an event name that never fires.
_preload_attrs(::Nothing) = (;)
_preload_attrs(preload::Bool) = preload ? (preload="mouseover",) : (;)
_preload_attrs(preload::Union{AbstractString,Symbol}) = (preload=String(preload),)

"""
    hx_link(url; preload=nothing, kwargs...)

Create an `h.a` with both `href` and `hx-get` set to `url`. Extra kwargs
(`hx_target`, `hx_swap`, `class`, etc.) are forwarded to `h.a`. A click swaps
in place; a Ctrl/Cmd-, Shift- or middle-click opens `url` in a new tab or
window (on an [`htmx`](@ref) page, see [`modifier_click_script`](@ref)).

`preload=true` fetches the link's target when the pointer rests on it (a string
such as `"mousedown"` picks another trigger of htmx's
[`preload` extension](https://htmx.org/extensions/preload/), which [`htmx`](@ref)
loads). The route decides what a preload does: a route marked `@preload`
starts its operation so the click finds it done or running; any other route
answers without doing work.
"""
hx_link(url; preload=nothing, kwargs...) =
    h.a(; href=url, hx_get=url, _preload_attrs(preload)..., kwargs...)

"""
    htmx_or(full_page_fn, req, fragment)

Return `fragment` directly for HTMX swaps; call `full_page_fn()` and wrap
its result for direct browser navigation, and for htmx's reload of a history
entry it holds no snapshot of (`HX-History-Restore-Request`), whose answer
replaces the whole page body. Typical usage:

    htmx_or(req, fragment) do
        htmx(h.main(search_input(q), fragment))
    end
"""
function htmx_or(full_page_fn, req::HTTP.Request, fragment)
    to_response(_hx_partial_swap(req) ? fragment : full_page_fn())
end

# --- HTMX request header inspection ---
# All return "" / false when the header is absent (i.e. for non-HTMX requests).

"""Return `true` if the request was made by HTMX (has `HX-Request: true` header)."""
is_htmx(req::HTTP.Request)       = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Request",      "") == "true"
"""Return `true` if the request was boosted by HTMX (`HX-Boosted: true` header)."""
hx_boosted(req::HTTP.Request)    = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Boosted",       "") == "true"
"""Return the `HX-Target` header value (the `id` of the target element), or `""`."""
hx_target(req::HTTP.Request)     = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Target",        "")
"""Return the `HX-Trigger` header value (the `id` of the triggering element), or `""`."""
hx_trigger(req::HTTP.Request)    = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Trigger",       "")
"""Return the `HX-Current-URL` header value (the browser URL at the time of the request), or `""`."""
hx_current_url(req::HTTP.Request)= HTTP.header(req, "HX-Current-URL",   "")
"""Return the `HX-Prompt` header value (the user's response to `hx-prompt`), or `""`."""
hx_prompt(req::HTTP.Request)     = HTTP.header(req, "HX-Prompt",        "")

# htmx's reload of a history entry it holds no `sessionStorage` snapshot for
# (Back/Forward after a miss, `loadHistoryFromServer` in htmx 2): the answer
# replaces the whole history element (`<body>`), so the request asks for the
# page at its address. htmx 2 sends `HX-Request: true` on it too, unless the
# page sets `htmx.config.historyRestoreAsHxRequest = false`.
_hx_history_restore(req::HTTP.Request) =
    HTTP.header(req, "HX-History-Restore-Request", "") == "true"

# An htmx request whose answer replaces a region of a page already on screen:
# every htmx request except a history restore. The fragment-versus-page
# decisions (page chrome, the error page, the operation transport, `htmx_or`)
# read this, never `is_htmx` alone.
_hx_partial_swap(req::HTTP.Request) = is_htmx(req) && !_hx_history_restore(req)

"""
    hx_response(val; trigger, push_url, replace_url, redirect, refresh, retarget, reswap, location, toast) -> HXResponse

Answer with `val` and attach HX-* response headers that instruct the HTMX
client to perform additional actions after swap.

`val` is rendered when HTMXObjects answers the request, for that request's
reader and in its page language, like any other value a route returns: a
`private` part is shown to the live app's reader and left out for a public
one, also from a `@queued` route, whose body runs in the job queue's task.
`to_response(hx_response(…))` gives the `HTTP.Response`, rendered for the
current task's audience (public outside a request; see [`with_audience`](@ref)).

`toast` names the success toast the [`htmx`](@ref) page shell shows once the
request completes (the percent-encoded `X-HTMXO-Toast` header read by
[`toast_script`](@ref)), e.g. `hx_response(row; toast="Invoice saved")`. It
survives a polled `:auto` answer: the terminal poll response carries it.
`retarget`, `reswap` and `location` survive one too: the page shell applies
them when the operation finishes, as on a direct answer (see
`auto_terminal_script()`).
"""
function hx_response(val;
    trigger=nothing, push_url=nothing, replace_url=nothing,
    redirect=nothing, refresh=false,
    retarget=nothing, reswap=nothing, location=nothing, toast=nothing,
)
    hdrs = Pair{String,String}[]
    isnothing(trigger)     || push!(hdrs, "HX-Trigger"     => trigger)
    isnothing(push_url)    || push!(hdrs, "HX-Push-Url"    => push_url)
    isnothing(replace_url) || push!(hdrs, "HX-Replace-Url" => replace_url)
    isnothing(redirect)    || push!(hdrs, "HX-Redirect"    => redirect)
    refresh                && push!(hdrs, "HX-Refresh"     => "true")
    isnothing(retarget)    || push!(hdrs, "HX-Retarget"    => retarget)
    isnothing(reswap)      || push!(hdrs, "HX-Reswap"      => reswap)
    isnothing(location)    || push!(hdrs, "HX-Location"    => location)
    isnothing(toast)       || push!(hdrs, "X-HTMXO-Toast"  => HTTP.escapeuri(toast))
    HXResponse(val, hdrs)
end

"""
    HXResponse

What [`hx_response`](@ref) returns: a value and the HX-* headers to answer it
with. HTMXObjects renders it when it answers; `to_response` turns it into the
`HTTP.Response`.
"""
struct HXResponse
    value::Any
    headers::Vector{Pair{String,String}}
end

to_response(r::HXResponse) = let resp = to_response(r.value)
    HTTP.Response(resp.status, [copy(resp.headers); r.headers]; body=resp.body)
end

# A route answer that is a finished response. An `HXResponse` is rendered
# here, on the task answering the request, which holds that request's audience,
# page language and glossary: a route body may run elsewhere (a `@queued` body
# runs in DynamicObjects' job queue, which carries none of them; snag
# `queued-hx-respon-fb8c1312`). Its value links the glossary's terms first, as
# any other answer does in `_resolve_response`.
_answer_value(value) = value
_answer_value(r::HXResponse) = to_response(_glossary_answer(r))
_finalized_response(r::HXResponse) = to_response(r)

const _REDIRECT_STATUSES = (301, 302, 303, 307, 308)

"""
    redirect_to(req, location; status=303, query=true) -> HTTP.Response

Answer `req` with an HTTP redirect to `location`, typically a moved or aliased
route sending every visitor to its canonical URL:

    @direct @get transactions() = redirect_to(__req__, __self__/"transaction_review_all")

Build `location` with `__self__/"…"`: that URL carries the mount prefix,
including one a reverse proxy announces through `X-Forwarded-Prefix`, so the
redirect stays inside the app behind the KB's `/p/<App>` proxy. A bare
`"/name"` addresses the server root.

Use `@direct` when the route must return the HTTP redirect status and headers
on its first request. Under the default `:auto` transport, a cold HTML load
may answer with a page shell while the route finishes in the background.
With inline transport, a browser follows the `Location` for a direct load.
htmx's request follows it as well and swaps the new route's fragment into the
original target with the original swap. When
that request records history, the [`htmx`](@ref) shell's
[`redirect_history_script`](@ref) records the new URL, so the address bar
and bookmarks show the canonical URL. `?plain`/Markdown and `?error`
requests keep those parameters and are redirected too.

- `status`: `303` (See Other, the default) or `302`, `307`, `301`, `308`.
  `307`/`308` keep the request method; `301`/`308` are permanent, so browsers
  may cache them. Any other status is an `ArgumentError`.
- `query=true` carries the request's query string over (`?year=2025` reaches
  the new route); parameters `location` already names keep `location`'s
  values. `query=false` sends exactly `location`.

The body is a one-line `text/plain` note naming the new URL. A redirect that
reaches an `:auto` poller navigates the page with `HX-Redirect`.
"""
function redirect_to(req::HTTP.Request, location::AbstractString;
        status::Integer=303, query::Bool=true)
    status in _REDIRECT_STATUSES || throw(ArgumentError(
        "redirect_to: status must be one of $(join(_REDIRECT_STATUSES, ", ")); got $status"))
    isempty(location) && throw(ArgumentError("redirect_to: location is empty"))
    target = query ? _carry_query(location, HTTP.URI(req.target).query) :
                     String(location)
    any(c -> c in ('\r', '\n'), target) && throw(ArgumentError(
        "redirect_to: location contains a line break: $(repr(target))"))
    HTTP.Response(status,
        ["Location" => target, "Content-Type" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8"];
        body="Moved to " * target * "\n")
end

# A query parameter's decoded name, for comparing names across encodings.
_query_param_name(pair) =
    HTTP.URIs.unescapeuri(replace(first(split(pair, '='; limit=2)), '+' => ' '))

# `location` with the request's raw `query` appended ahead of any `#fragment`.
# Pairs keep their encoding byte for byte; a name `location` already carries
# keeps `location`'s value.
function _carry_query(location::AbstractString, query::AbstractString)
    isempty(query) && return String(location)
    hash_at = findfirst('#', location)
    base = isnothing(hash_at) ? location : location[1:prevind(location, hash_at)]
    fragment = isnothing(hash_at) ? "" : location[hash_at:end]
    query_at = findfirst('?', base)
    own = isnothing(query_at) ? "" : base[nextind(base, query_at):end]
    taken = Set(_query_param_name(p) for p in split(own, '&'; keepempty=false))
    carried = [p for p in split(query, '&'; keepempty=false)
               if !(_query_param_name(p) in taken)]
    isempty(carried) && return String(location)
    joiner = isnothing(query_at) ? "?" :
             (isempty(own) || endswith(own, '&')) ? "" : "&"
    string(base, joiner, join(carried, '&'), fragment)
end

"""
    route!(obj; prefix="", record_dir=nothing)

Register all `@get`/`@post`/`@put`/`@patch`/`@delete`-marked properties of `obj`
on [`ROUTER`](@ref). The type is stored in `_registered_types` so that `_reroute!`
(called automatically by the `@htmx` macro) can re-register routes when Revise
updates the struct.

- Non-indexed properties map to `METHOD /name` (e.g. `obj.about` → `GET /about`)
- Indexed properties map to `METHOD /name/{p1}/{p2}/...`
- Type annotations auto-parse URL strings: `@get item(id::Int)` parses `id` to `Int`
- Trailing defaults register shortened routes: `@get filter(a, b=1)` registers
  both `GET /filter/{a}/{b}` and `GET /filter/{a}` (with `b=1` filled in)
- Kwargs (via call syntax) auto-extract from query params or form data:
  `@get search(; q="", page::Int=1)` extracts `q` and `page` from the query string
  (`GET` / `DELETE` → `queryparams`, `POST` / `PUT` / `PATCH` → `formparams`).
  Both parsers preserve multi-value keys, so a `Vector`-typed kwarg receives
  every repeated value (`?tag=a&tag=b` or repeated `image=a&image=b` body fields).
- A submitted value its declared type rejects — `?page=abc` for `page::Int`, a
  path segment, `@param`, `@header` or indexed-mount key that does not parse, a
  repeated value for a scalar — is the caller's fault: a `400` (`200` under
  HTMX) naming the parameter, the value and the expected type (for `Bool` or an
  `Enum`, the allowed values), recorded like every error answer with its
  status. A declared type with no `parse` method is the declaration's fault
  and stays a `500`.
- `@post` / `@put` / `@patch` / `@delete` execute fresh on every request,
  including identical submissions on a retained root. `@get`, `@ws`, `@sse`,
  and properties read inside a mutation retain their own declared cache policy.
- Freshness is cache policy, not transport: under the default
  [`OperationPolicy`](@ref) a slow `@fresh` read or HTMX mutation (one whose
  response swaps into a target) is answered by a poller like any other slow
  route. A mutation path also answers the GET resumes of its pollers (a plain
  GET there is still `405`).
- `@direct` (alongside the verb marker, e.g. `@fresh @direct @get rows()`) pins a
  route to direct transport: it always answers inline on the request's own task.
- `@queued` (e.g. `@queued @get results(id::String)`) declares heavy computation.
  An ordinary route's background operation starts on the pool of the request that
  started it; a `@queued` route's computation runs on `:default` instead, admitted
  through [`configure_job_queue!`](@ref), however the request is answered (a
  blocking or `@direct` request waits for it). It is an `ArgumentError` on a `@ws`
  or `@sse` route, whose body runs on its connection's task.
- The `:index` property (with empty prefix) maps to `GET /`

If `record_dir` is given, each response is also written to disk under that
directory, mirroring the URL path structure. This enables later replay via any
static HTTP server.

Returns `obj`.
"""


const _http_verbs = Dict(
    Symbol("@get") => "GET",
    Symbol("@post") => "POST",
    Symbol("@put") => "PUT",
    Symbol("@patch") => "PATCH",
    Symbol("@delete") => "DELETE",
    Symbol("@ws") => "WEBSOCKET",
    Symbol("@sse") => "SSE",
)

# Store registered types so _reroute! can re-register after Revise updates.
# Stores `(prefix, record_dir)` per root type — these are re-supplied to
# `_register_routes` when Revise re-evaluates the struct definition.
# Frozen NamedTuple shape: Julia 1.10 cannot redefine a `const` whose
# stored type changes, so widening this without a restart is impossible.
# Companion dict `_record_bases` carries the `record_base` URL prefix so
# new fields can be added without touching this const.
const _registered_types = Dict{DataType, NamedTuple{(:prefix, :record_dir), Tuple{String, Any}}}()
const _record_bases = Dict{DataType, String}()
# Reverse lookup: included sub-struct type → set of registered parent types
const _included_type_parents = Dict{DataType, Set{DataType}}()

"""
    OperationContext

Request-local context passed to a [`RootProvider`](@ref). `scope` and `key`
describe the provider-selected application lifetime (`:request`, `:session`, or
`:job`); `transport` is `:http`, `:websocket`, or `:sse`. A custom provider owns any
storage behind session/job keys. The managed [`RootRetention`](@ref) form keeps
that common in-process storage inside HTMXObjects.
"""
struct OperationContext{R,K}
    request::R
    prefix::String
    route::String
    transport::Symbol
    scope::Symbol
    key::K
end

"""
    RootRetention(; max_entries=128, ttl=nothing)

Bounded retention policy for an in-process [`RootProvider`](@ref).

- `max_entries` is the maximum number of retained roots. When inserting a new
  root at capacity, the least-recently-used entry is removed.
- `ttl` is an optional maximum idle time in seconds. Expired entries are
  removed opportunistically on the next provider access.

Both policies remove only the provider's reference. An already-running request
or computation may still hold its root; a later request constructs a new root.
The store is process-local, so distributed runtimes should use a custom
`RootProvider(factory; ...)` adapter instead.
"""
struct RootRetention
    max_entries::Int
    ttl::Union{Nothing,Float64}
end

function RootRetention(; max_entries::Integer=128, ttl=nothing)
    max_entries > 0 || throw(ArgumentError(
        "root-retention max_entries must be positive (got $(repr(max_entries)))"))
    ttl_seconds = isnothing(ttl) ? nothing : Float64(ttl)
    (isnothing(ttl_seconds) || (isfinite(ttl_seconds) && ttl_seconds > 0)) ||
        throw(ArgumentError(
            "root-retention ttl must be a positive finite number of seconds or nothing " *
            "(got $(repr(ttl)))"))
    RootRetention(Int(max_entries), ttl_seconds)
end

"""
    RootProvider(factory; scope=:request, key=nothing)
    RootProvider(; scope=:request, key=nothing, retention=nothing)

Construct roots for registered operations. A custom `factory` is called as
`factory(RootT, context)` and must return a `RootT`. For `scope=:session` or
`:job`, pass a `key(req)` function and let the custom factory decide what is
retained behind that key.

For the common in-process case, omit the factory and pass a `RootRetention`:

```julia
RootProvider(
    scope=:job,
    key=req -> HTTP.header(req, "X-Job", ""),
    retention=RootRetention(max_entries=64, ttl=3600),
)
```

HTMXObjects supplies the locked store, caches one root per `(RootT,
context.key)`, and returns a same-type DynamicObjects remount for every request.
Request-derived properties and their transitive dependents—including mounted
children—refresh, while unrelated settled, in-flight, mmap, and indexed cache
identity remains shared. A property counts as request-derived when its body
reads request context directly, through the properties it calls, or through a
`__self__` it passes to a function defined outside the struct; such opaque
`__self__` work that reads no request context is shared (DynamicObjects
`c0c742c`; earlier DynamicObjects pins recompute it per request even when it
reads nothing, snag `remount-opaque-s-2938c22c`). Inline nested children share
it too, including an indexed `@struct child(k)` first realized during a request
(DynamicObjects `822765e`; earlier DynamicObjects pins rebuild such a child per
request, snag `remount-drops-ne-af0c7132`). Keep long-running work that requests
poll on an indexed property whose body reads no request context. The default
`RootProvider()` keeps the historic fresh root-per-request behavior. `retention`
is rejected for `scope=:request` because request scope already has exactly one
request per root.

Both forms without a `factory` build each new root from the instance given to
[`route!`](@ref): its fixed fields and constructor keyword overrides, plus the
request. A custom `factory` builds its own roots.

The managed store is deliberately process-local. A distributed/multipod job
store remains an application/runtime adapter expressed by the factory form.
"""
struct RootProvider{F,K}
    factory::F
    scope::Symbol
    key::K
end

"""
    OperationPolicy(mode=:auto; poll_interval="200ms", keep_progress=true,
        keep_terminal_tree=false, stall_after=600, queued_stall_after=nothing)

Select route execution transport. `:auto` — **the default, applied to every app
whether or not it declares a policy** — polls pending-capable HTMX requests.
A direct rich-page visit spends the same grace budget an HTMX request would:
an operation that finishes within it renders inline in its composed `__page__`
shell — one response, no placeholder, no refetch. Only a slower operation
defers, returning the shell with a load-triggered request that joins the
already-running operation; that fragment then uses the same grace/polling path.
Routes without page chrome stay direct for non-HTMX requests. `:polling`
forces the polling transport. `:blocking` keeps every route direct; it is the
opt-out for a route surface that genuinely must answer inline.

A request answered as Markdown (`?plain`, `?markdown`, `Accept: text/markdown`
or `text/plain`) or filtered by `?error` is answered inline under every policy,
whether or not it is an HTMX request: the Markdown projection drops the
poller's `hx-get` continuation, and `?error` would filter a running poller to
"(no errors)". Such a read of a poll URL joins the operation the URL names and
waits for its result, up to the operation's stall bound, instead of starting
another; with no operation to join, it is answered like a direct request of
that URL.

Cache policy never selects the transport. A `@fresh` route and every mutation
verb (`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE`) recompute on each invocation, and a slow
invocation is still answered by the poller: it runs in the background as an
ephemeral operation that only its poll token retains — never memoized, never
joined by an identical request. The Treebars poller issues GET refreshes, so an
HTMX mutation is resumed by GETs of its own path carrying its token. Such a
resume never re-runs the submission: a token the server no longer holds (a
restart, an expired entry) answers `HTMXObjects.OperationResultUnavailable`
(`410`) instead. A mutation's poller needs a swap target to live in, so a slow
submission is answered by one only when its client declares such a swap: the
`htmx()` shell's `mutation_poll_script()` sends `HTMXO-Swap`. A
swap-less submission (`hx-swap="none"`), a page that does not declare it, and
a non-HTMX submission (a classic form post, curl) are answered by their own
response.

Direct handling is reserved for what genuinely needs it: declared
`HTTP.Response`, `MIMEResponse` and `JSONResponse` outputs (a route whose answer
is its status or headers — `hx_response` included — or a JSON body declares
that return type), WebSocket
and SSE route lambdas, [`record!`](@ref)'s static export (it forces
`:blocking`), and routes marked `@direct` — the per-route opt-out for a
fragment that must answer inline on the request's own task.

A background operation starts on the threadpool of the request that started it:
`:interactive` under HTTP.jl 2 or `serve(; parallel=:interactive)`, so a page keeps
answering while application compute saturates `:default`. Mark heavy computation
`@queued`: it runs on `:default`, admitted through [`configure_job_queue!`](@ref),
whatever the transport — polled, page-load, preload, or inline (`@direct`,
non-HTMX, `:blocking`), where the request waits for it. Only `@queued` work is
queued. Work a route body spawns itself (`Threads.@threads`) runs where it spawns
it.

You never have to write `OperationPolicy` to get non-blocking long routes —
`route!(app)` alone already does. Reach for it to tune (`poll_interval`),
to keep finished progress trees (`keep_terminal_tree=true`), to change the
stall bound (`stall_after`, `queued_stall_after`), or to opt out (`:blocking`).
One in-process request takes a policy of its own through [`dispatch`](@ref)
(`dispatch(...; operation_policy=:blocking)`) without re-registering anything.

A polled operation that is still unfinished `stall_after` seconds after it
started (default `600`, ten minutes) stops being polled: its next poll answers
a terminal `HTMXObjects.OperationStalled` failure instead of the running
poller. It is recorded like any route error (error log,
`__on_error__`/`__error__` hooks, an `X-HTMXO-Error-Id` response header) and
shown beside the open progress tree, so the reader sees what was still
running. A read offers a **Run again** button that starts a fresh computation
with the same arguments; a mutation is never re-submitted. The stalled
computation itself is not cancelled — DynamicObjects has no cancellation — and
may still finish in the background. An operation that finishes or fails always
answers its own result, however long it took. `@queued` operations, which can
legitimately wait in the job queue, use `queued_stall_after` instead (default
`nothing`: unbounded). `nothing` disables either bound. Every resumed poll
carries `X-HTMXO-Operation-Age: <seconds since the operation started>`, so a
script can bound its own polling too.

Nested progress follows source-visible DynamicObjects property reads and
indexed-property calls in generated route/property bodies. Their lowering
carries the caller's progress node explicitly; ordinary Julia calls,
constructors, arithmetic and loops remain ordinary. Use DynamicObjects'
explicit progress markers when work hidden in a foreign helper should attach to
the caller too.

When a request crosses the grace period, HTMXObjects retains that exact
operation behind an independently generated OS-random bearer token. Possession
authorizes polling that operation only while its bindings still match: the
routed root/leaf types, route, typed args, and provider scope/key are checked
before the retained operation is resumed. A poll that cannot resume — an
unknown token (process restart wiped the registry), an expired entry
(TTL/LRU), arguments that drifted since creation, or a missing token — heals
by re-executing a fresh operation with the poll request's current args
instead of failing. The token is a resumption hint: a healed request computes
exactly what a fresh GET of the same URL would, so healing grants no
authority a direct request lacks, and a token bound to one operation never
exposes another — a mismatch resumes nothing and heals only the requesting
route. Successful terminal rendering removes the entry, while a bounded
process-local registry expires abandoned or failed operations. Fresh request
roots can therefore follow in-flight work without making DynamicObjects
caches global.

A resolved `:auto` poll answers the result fragment in a select-matching
terminal node — no kept progress tree, no inspection chrome — and the
`htmx()` shell unwraps that node on swap, so the caller's target ends with
the bare result fragment. `keep_progress` does not change that: it governs
hand-shaped `polling_fetchindex` pollers (which keep their frozen tree),
the direct-page replacement flow, and what a failed `:auto` operation
renders (the recorded error beside its open tree, rather than a bare
route-boundary article). To keep the finished tree below a resolved `:auto`
result, set `keep_terminal_tree=true`: polled operations then resolve
through Treebars' done terminal — the result with the frozen tree in a
collapsed `<details>` — instead of the bare node. Operations that finish
within the grace budget still answer inline bare either way: no poll, no
tree. While loading, the interim poller swaps into
the target and transiently displaces its children; the resolved result then
lands as the route rendered it, a table part (`<tr>`, `<td>`, `<tbody>`, …)
included. A route whose fragment
must be the direct children of a structural element (`details`/`summary`,
`table`/`tr`, `select`/`option`, …) and cannot tolerate that transient
should declare itself `@direct @get` instead — likewise any route that must
stay bare under a tree-keeping policy, since a kept tree is not bare-safe, and
a hand-shaped `polling_fetchindex` wrapper, which owns its transport
(`@fresh @direct @get`).
"""
struct OperationPolicy
    mode::Symbol
    poll_interval::String
    keep_progress::Bool
    keep_terminal_tree::Bool
    # Seconds a polled operation may run before its next poll answers
    # `OperationStalled`; `nothing` = unbounded. `@queued` operations use
    # `queued_stall_after`.
    stall_after::Union{Nothing,Float64}
    queued_stall_after::Union{Nothing,Float64}
end

const _DEFAULT_STALL_AFTER = 600.0

_stall_bound(::Nothing, _name) = nothing
function _stall_bound(seconds::Real, name)
    isfinite(seconds) && seconds > 0 || throw(ArgumentError(
        "operation-policy $(name) must be a positive number of seconds or nothing (got $(repr(seconds)))"))
    Float64(seconds)
end

# One default, spelled once: `route!`'s kwarg default and every
# `get(_operation_policies, T, OperationPolicy())` registry fallback read their
# mode from here, so "what does an app that declares nothing get?" has a single
# answer in the source as well as in the docs.
function OperationPolicy(mode::Symbol=:auto; poll_interval="200ms",
        keep_progress::Bool=true, keep_terminal_tree::Bool=false,
        stall_after::Union{Nothing,Real}=_DEFAULT_STALL_AFTER,
        queued_stall_after::Union{Nothing,Real}=nothing)
    mode in (:blocking, :polling, :auto) || throw(ArgumentError(
        "operation-policy mode must be :blocking, :polling, or :auto (got $(repr(mode)))"))
    OperationPolicy(mode, string(poll_interval), keep_progress,
        keep_terminal_tree, _stall_bound(stall_after, :stall_after),
        _stall_bound(queued_stall_after, :queued_stall_after))
end

# Back-compat: the pre-`keep_terminal_tree` and pre-stall-bound positional
# shapes keep working, with today's default bounds.
OperationPolicy(mode::Symbol, poll_interval, keep_progress::Bool) =
    OperationPolicy(mode; poll_interval, keep_progress)
OperationPolicy(mode::Symbol, poll_interval, keep_progress::Bool,
        keep_terminal_tree::Bool) =
    OperationPolicy(mode; poll_interval, keep_progress, keep_terminal_tree)

# What an `operation_policy=` keyword accepts — `route!`'s and `dispatch`'s
# alike: a policy, or a mode Symbol standing for `OperationPolicy(mode)`.
_operation_policy(policy::OperationPolicy) = policy
_operation_policy(mode::Symbol) = OperationPolicy(mode)
_operation_policy(other) = throw(ArgumentError(
    "operation_policy takes an OperationPolicy or a mode Symbol " *
    "(:blocking, :polling, :auto); got $(repr(other))"))

# The stall bound an operation starting now under `policy` carries.
_operation_stall_after(policy::OperationPolicy, queued::Bool) =
    queued ? policy.queued_stall_after : policy.stall_after

function RootProvider(factory; scope::Symbol=:request, key=nothing)
    scope in (:request, :session, :job) ||
        throw(ArgumentError("root-provider scope must be :request, :session, or :job (got $(repr(scope)))"))
    isnothing(key) && scope !== :request &&
        throw(ArgumentError("root-provider scope $(repr(scope)) requires a key(req) function"))
    keyfn = isnothing(key) ? (_req -> nothing) : key
    RootProvider{typeof(factory),typeof(keyfn)}(factory, scope, keyfn)
end

# Every root HTMXObjects builds for a request — the fresh per-request root and a
# new entry in a managed store — comes from the instance `route!` was given: its
# fixed fields and the keyword overrides it was constructed with
# (`App(; dir=…)`) are passed to the new root alongside the request. Nothing the
# instance computed is carried over; each root computes its own properties, so
# a default the instance did not override is still evaluated per root.
_default_root_factory(RootT, context::OperationContext) =
    _root_from(get(_root_templates, RootT, nothing), RootT, context)

_root_from(::Nothing, RootT, context::OperationContext) =
    RootT(; _root_request_kw(context)...)

function _root_from(template, RootT, context::OperationContext)
    fixed, overrides = _construction_inputs(template)
    RootT(fixed...; overrides..., _root_request_kw(context)...)
end

_root_request_kw(context::OperationContext) =
    (; __req__=context.request, __route__=context.route, _prefix_kw(context.prefix)...)

# Request and mount context, which a root takes from its own request and never
# from the instance it was built from.
const _REQUEST_CONTEXT_INPUTS = (:__req__, :__route__, :__prefix__, :__parent__, :__status__)

# A DynamicObject's construction inputs: its fixed fields (every field but the
# trailing property cache, as `DynamicObjects.remake` copies them) and the
# keyword overrides it was created with (`PropertyCache.seed`). `remake` is not
# used: it would also carry the instance's settled memos into every request
# root. (Before DynamicObjects `d0a0b82` it also kept the overrides only as
# memos, snag `remake-drops-the-295be6c2`.)
function _construction_inputs(obj)
    T = typeof(obj)
    fixed = Any[getfield(obj, name) for name in fieldnames(T)[1:end-1]]
    seed = getfield(obj, :cache).seed
    fixed, Base.structdiff(seed, NamedTuple{_REQUEST_CONTEXT_INPUTS})
end

mutable struct _RetainedRoot
    value::Any
    touched::Float64
end

struct _ManagedRootFactory{C}
    retention::RootRetention
    clock::C
    lock::ReentrantLock
    entries::Dict{Any,_RetainedRoot}
end

_root_retention_time() = time_ns() * 1.0e-9

_ManagedRootFactory(retention::RootRetention; clock=_root_retention_time) =
    _ManagedRootFactory(retention, clock, ReentrantLock(), Dict{Any,_RetainedRoot}())

function _release_managed_root_materialization!(event)
    isdefined(DynamicObjects, :release_materialization!) || return nothing
    context = (;
        scope=event.scope,
        key=event.key,
        retention=(;
            max_entries=event.retention.max_entries,
            ttl=event.retention.ttl,
        ),
    )
    Base.invokelatest(
        getproperty(DynamicObjects, :release_materialization!),
        context, event.root; reason=event.reason)
    isdefined(DynamicObjects, :materialization_gc!) &&
        Base.invokelatest(getproperty(DynamicObjects, :materialization_gc!))
    nothing
end

_managed_root_release_handler = Ref{Any}(_release_managed_root_materialization!)

function _managed_root_release_event(factory::_ManagedRootFactory, store_key,
        entry::_RetainedRoot, scope::Symbol, reason::Symbol)
    root_type, key = store_key
    (; root=entry.value, root_type, scope, key, reason,
       retention=factory.retention)
end

function _notify_managed_root_releases(events)
    isassigned(_managed_root_release_handler) || return nothing
    handler = _managed_root_release_handler[]
    isnothing(handler) && return nothing
    for event in events
        Base.invokelatest(handler, event)
    end
    nothing
end

function _prune_managed_roots!(factory::_ManagedRootFactory, now::Float64,
        scope::Symbol)
    released = Any[]
    ttl = factory.retention.ttl
    if !isnothing(ttl)
        for (key, entry) in collect(factory.entries)
            now - entry.touched >= ttl || continue
            delete!(factory.entries, key)
            push!(released, _managed_root_release_event(
                factory, key, entry, scope, :ttl))
        end
    end
    released
end

function _evict_managed_root!(factory::_ManagedRootFactory, scope::Symbol)
    isempty(factory.entries) && return nothing
    oldest_key = first(keys(factory.entries))
    oldest_time = factory.entries[oldest_key].touched
    for (key, entry) in factory.entries
        entry.touched < oldest_time || continue
        oldest_key = key
        oldest_time = entry.touched
    end
    entry = factory.entries[oldest_key]
    delete!(factory.entries, oldest_key)
    _managed_root_release_event(factory, oldest_key, entry, scope, :lru)
end

# `route!` with another instance of a type already served from this store: the
# retained roots were built from the earlier instance, so the next request
# builds a new one from the current instance instead. A retained root that IS
# the routed instance stays: `semantic_app(app)` before `route!(app)` retains
# `app` itself, and requests then share its caches.
function _forget_managed_roots!(provider::RootProvider, RootT, routed)
    factory = provider.factory
    factory isa _ManagedRootFactory || return nothing
    released = lock(factory.lock) do
        Any[(delete!(factory.entries, key);
             _managed_root_release_event(factory, key, entry, provider.scope, :reroute))
            for (key, entry) in collect(factory.entries)
            if first(key) === RootT && entry.value !== routed]
    end
    _notify_managed_root_releases(released)
    nothing
end

function _prime_managed_root!(root, seen=Base.IdSet())
    root in seen && return root
    push!(seen, root)
    T = typeof(root)
    primed = Set{Symbol}()
    for (name, info) in Base.invokelatest(DynamicObjects.meta, T)
        name in primed && continue
        DynamicObjects.isfixed(info) && continue
        info.indexed && continue
        any(macro_sym in info.macros for macro_sym in keys(_http_verbs)) && continue
        nested_type = _nested_struct_type(T, Val(name))
        isnothing(nested_type) && continue
        isempty(Base.invokelatest(DynamicObjects.meta, nested_type)) && continue
        push!(primed, name)
        child = getproperty(root, name)
        _prime_managed_root!(child, seen)
    end
    root
end

function (factory::_ManagedRootFactory)(RootT, context::OperationContext)
    now = Float64(factory.clock())
    released = Any[]
    root = lock(factory.lock) do
        append!(released, _prune_managed_roots!(factory, now, context.scope))
        store_key = (RootT, context.key)
        if haskey(factory.entries, store_key)
            entry = factory.entries[store_key]
            entry.touched = now
            return entry.value
        end
        while length(factory.entries) >= factory.retention.max_entries
            event = _evict_managed_root!(factory, context.scope)
            isnothing(event) || push!(released, event)
        end
        # Prime only declaration-level, non-indexed mounted children. This
        # gives remount a stable rooted graph whose unrelated child/model caches
        # can be shared. The primed children carry THIS request's context: a
        # later request's remount runs each child's own constructor call and
        # adopts the primed child only when that call builds it alike, so a
        # child whose arguments read request context (the mount prefix, an
        # `@param`) is rebuilt for a request that differs (DynamicObjects
        # `7c3852e`; earlier pins reused it with the first request's
        # arguments). Indexed children are realized lazily because their keys
        # are request/application inputs.
        root = _prime_managed_root!(_default_root_factory(RootT, context))
        factory.entries[store_key] = _RetainedRoot(root, now)
        root
    end
    # Release notifications may hand ownership back to DynamicObjects. Never
    # invoke framework code while holding the provider lock: an executor may
    # synchronously inspect or reacquire the same semantic root.
    _notify_managed_root_releases(released)
    root
end

function RootProvider(; scope::Symbol=:request, key=nothing, retention=nothing)
    if isnothing(retention)
        return RootProvider(_default_root_factory; scope, key)
    end
    retention isa RootRetention || throw(ArgumentError(
        "root-provider retention must be a RootRetention or nothing " *
        "(got $(typeof(retention)))"))
    scope === :request && throw(ArgumentError(
        "root-provider retention requires scope=:session or :job; request roots are already fresh"))
    RootProvider(_ManagedRootFactory(retention); scope, key)
end

_normalize_root_provider(::Nothing) = RootProvider()
_normalize_root_provider(provider::RootProvider) = provider
_normalize_root_provider(factory) = RootProvider(factory)

# Companion registry rather than another field on `_registered_types`: that
# const's NamedTuple value type is frozen under Julia 1.10/Revise.
const _root_providers = Dict{DataType,Any}()
# The instance each root type was routed with (`_default_root_factory`).
const _root_templates = Dict{DataType,Any}()
const _operation_policies = Dict{DataType,OperationPolicy}()
const _semantic_root_types = Set{Type}()
# Convert a string value to the target type. Strings pass through as-is.
# Called from generated _extract_args methods with actual Types (resolved at compile time).
_convert_param(val, ::Nothing) = val
# When the user wrote `@get foo(arg::String)`, the URL parser hands us a
# SubString{String}; without a coercion the typed iscached method dispatch
# misses (it only matches ::String, not ::SubString{String}).
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, T::Type{<:AbstractString}) = convert(T, val)
# HTML form encoding submits a textarea's line breaks as CRLF; the argument
# sees one line-break spelling whatever the transport sent.
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{MultilineText}) =
    MultilineText(replace(val, "\r\n" => "\n", "\r" => "\n"))
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{Symbol}) = Symbol(val)
# Enum controls submit the shared option wire spelling, not an integer storage
# code. Recover the exact instance without requiring an application Base.parse.
function _convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{T}) where {T<:Base.Enum}
    for candidate in instances(T)
        _option_wire_string(candidate) == val && return candidate
    end
    throw(ArgumentError("no $(T) instance matching $(repr(val))"))
end
# `parse` needs a concrete type — `parse(Integer, "3")` fails inside `tryparse`
# on `typemax(::Type{Integer})`. An abstract numeric annotation is the natural
# way to write an index parameter (`@include chains(chain::Integer)`), and it
# says "any integer", so pick the machine default rather than making the
# application name a width it does not care about.
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{Integer}) = parse(Int, val)
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{Signed}) = parse(Int, val)
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{AbstractFloat}) = parse(Float64, val)
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{Real}) = parse(Float64, val)
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, T::Type) = parse(T, val)
_convert_param(val::AbstractVector, ::Nothing) = val  # multi-value, no type annotation → keep as vector
# A repeated value for a scalar parameter is something the request sent, so it
# is an `ArgumentError` like any other value the type rejects (a 400 through
# `_convert_submitted`), not a server fault.
_single_value_error(val) = ArgumentError(string(
    "expected one value, got ", length(val),
    "; a parameter that accepts repeated values declares a Vector type"))
_convert_param(val::AbstractVector, T::Type{<:AbstractString}) =
    throw(_single_value_error(val))
# Multi-value + an element-typed Vector annotation (`::Vector{Int}`): honour the
# element type by running every entry through the SAME scalar conversion the
# `::Int` path uses (`_convert_param` dispatches on the element type `T`, so
# Symbol/Integer/Real/Float element types work too, not just what `parse` covers;
# `Base.broadcastable(::Type)=Ref` makes `T` a scalar in the broadcast), then land
# in the declared container `V`. Without this the element type was silently dropped
# and the raw strings surfaced as a TypeError at the first typed callee.
function _convert_param(val::AbstractVector, ::Type{V}) where {T, V<:AbstractVector{T}}
    val isa V && return val
    convert(V, _convert_param.(val, T))
end
_convert_param(val::AbstractVector, T::Type{<:AbstractVector}) = val  # bare ::Vector / ::AbstractVector (no element type) → keep as-is
_convert_param(val::AbstractVector, T::Type) = throw(_single_value_error(val))
# Single (or empty) value declared as an element-typed Vector: wrap the one
# converted element (empty → an empty `V`).
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{V}) where {T, V<:AbstractVector{T}} =
    isempty(val) ? V() : convert(V, [_convert_param(val, T)])
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, T::Type{<:AbstractVector}) = isempty(val) ? String[] : [val]  # bare ::Vector (no element type): single/empty → vector
# Multipart Upload values flow through the same lookup path. Untyped (`::Nothing`)
# is already covered by the `_convert_param(val, ::Nothing) = val` catch-all above.
_convert_param(val::Upload, ::Type{Upload}) = val
_convert_param(val::Upload, T::Type{<:AbstractVector}) = [val]  # single file declared as a Vector
# A text field where a file was declared (a urlencoded form, a hand-built
# request): the request sent the wrong kind of value.
_convert_param(val::AbstractString, ::Type{Upload}) =
    throw(ArgumentError("expected an uploaded file, got a text value"))

# The declared type rejected the submitted value: its `parse`, or one of the
# decoders above, threw. A type with no `parse` method at all (`MethodError`)
# is the declaration's fault, not the caller's, and stays a server fault.
_rejects_value(::Union{ArgumentError,OverflowError}) = true
_rejects_value(_err) = false
_rejection_reason(err::Union{ArgumentError,OverflowError}) = err.msg

"""
    _convert_submitted(val, T, name) -> value

`_convert_param` for a value the request submitted under `name` (a keyword,
path segment, `@param`, `@header` or indexed-mount segment). A value the
declared type rejects is the caller's fault, answered 400:
`InvalidDomainValue` when the type implies a closed domain (`Bool`, an `Enum`)
so the answer lists the allowed values, otherwise `InvalidParamValue` naming
the expected type.
"""
function _convert_submitted(val, T, name)
    try
        _convert_param(val, T)
    catch err
        _rejects_value(err) || rethrow()
        allowed = _inferred_param_domain(T)
        allowed === nothing || throw(InvalidDomainValue(Symbol(name), val, allowed))
        throw(InvalidParamValue(Symbol(name), val, T, _rejection_reason(err)))
    end
end

# Determine whether kwargs come from queryparams (GET/DELETE) or formparams (POST/PUT/PATCH).
const _queryparams_verbs = Set(["GET", "DELETE"])
function _kwargs_source(req, method)
    method in _queryparams_verbs && return queryparams(req)
    bodyparams(req)
end

# Sentinel for "no default provided" / "key was absent". Distinguishes required
# params from those defaulting to `nothing`, and acts as the missing-marker
# returned by `_lookup_param`.
struct _NoDefault end
const _NO_DEFAULT = _NoDefault()

_inferred_param_domain(::Any) = nothing
_inferred_param_domain(::Type{Bool}) = (false, true)
_inferred_param_domain(::Type{T}) where {T<:Base.Enum} = instances(T)

"""
    _lookup_param(src, fallback, name, T) -> value or _NO_DEFAULT

Low-level parameter lookup shared by `_extract_param` and the route-macro's
generated `_extract_args` methods. Given a precomputed primary source dict and
optional fallback dict (e.g. queryparams for POST), look up `name`, apply
`_convert_submitted(value, T, name)` when present, or return the `_NO_DEFAULT`
sentinel when the key is absent/empty so callers can decide between default or
error.
"""
function _lookup_param(src, fallback, name, T)
    v = _submitted_raw(src, fallback, name)
    v === _NO_DEFAULT ? v : _convert_submitted(v, T, name)
end

# The raw submitted value for `name`, or `_NO_DEFAULT` when it is absent or empty
# in both the primary source and the fallback.
function _submitted_raw(src, fallback, name)
    key = String(name)
    v = get(src, key, nothing)
    if (v === nothing || v == "") && fallback !== nothing
        v = get(fallback, key, nothing)
    end
    (v === nothing || v == "") ? _NO_DEFAULT : v
end

# A submitted option carries only its stable wire value. Recover the
# actual machine object from the bound, lazily evaluated `__options__` property
# before the ordinary parser sees it. This is essential for node-valued domains
# whose abstract/concrete element type deliberately has no `parse` method.
function _declared_param_options(options, name)
    declared = Base.invokelatest(options, Val(Symbol(name)))
    isdefined(DynamicObjects, :option_records) || return nothing
    Base.invokelatest(getproperty(DynamicObjects, :option_records), declared)
end

function _convert_option_param(options, val::AbstractString, name, T)
    records = _declared_param_options(options, name)
    records === nothing && return _convert_submitted(val, T, name)
    allowed = Any[option.value for option in records
                  if !get(option, :disabled, false)]
    for candidate in allowed
        _option_wire_string(candidate) == val && return candidate
    end

    # Keep the established scalar conversion as a fallback for domains whose
    # wire spelling is accepted by the annotated type. The resolved value must
    # still belong to the CURRENT domain; this is the stale-form guard.
    converted = try
        _convert_param(val, T)
    catch err
        err isa Union{ArgumentError,MethodError} || rethrow()
        throw(InvalidDomainValue(Symbol(name), val, allowed))
    end
    any(candidate -> isequal(candidate, converted), allowed) ||
        throw(InvalidDomainValue(Symbol(name), converted, allowed))
    converted
end
_convert_option_param(options, val, name, T) = _convert_submitted(val, T, name)

function _lookup_option_param(options, src, fallback, name, T)
    v = _submitted_raw(src, fallback, name)
    v === _NO_DEFAULT ? v : _convert_option_param(options, v, name, T)
end

"""
    _extract_param(req, name, T, default=_NO_DEFAULT) -> value

Extract a single typed parameter from an HTTP request, mirroring the behavior of
`@get`/`@post` kwarg extraction. Looks up `name` in the method-appropriate source
(`queryparams` for GET/DELETE, `formparams` for POST/PUT/PATCH with a `queryparams`
fallback), converts via `_convert_param(value, T)`, and returns `default` when the
key is absent or empty. Throws `MissingRequiredParam(name)` if no default was supplied.

`T` may be `nothing` for untyped params (raw `String`/`Vector{String}`).
"""
function _extract_param(req, name, T, default=_NO_DEFAULT)
    method = req.method
    src = _kwargs_source(req, method)
    fallback = method in _queryparams_verbs ? nothing : queryparams(req)
    v = _lookup_param(src, fallback, name, T)
    _resolve_extracted(v, default, name)
end
function _extract_option_param(options, req::HTTP.Request, name, T,
        default=_NO_DEFAULT)
    method = req.method
    src = _kwargs_source(req, method)
    fallback = method in _queryparams_verbs ? nothing : queryparams(req)
    v = _lookup_option_param(options, src, fallback, name, T)
    _resolve_extracted(v, default, name)
end
_extract_option_param(_options, ::Nothing, name, _T, default=_NO_DEFAULT) =
    _resolve_extracted(_NO_DEFAULT, default, name)
# Outside a request (a form rendered from a bare instance) a `@param` is its
# default, as one with `@options` is.
_extract_param(::Nothing, name, _T, default=_NO_DEFAULT) =
    _resolve_extracted(_NO_DEFAULT, default, name)
# A `@param` with a `@relevant` condition: its submitted value while the
# condition holds, its default otherwise, so a value that does not apply never
# reaches what reads it (and two requests differing only there compute alike).
_extract_relevant_param(relevant, req, name, T, default) =
    _checked_relevance(relevant, name) ? _extract_param(req, name, T, default) : default
_extract_relevant_option_param(relevant, options, req, name, T, default) =
    _checked_relevance(relevant, name) ?
        _extract_option_param(options, req, name, T, default) : default
_checked_relevance(relevant::Bool, _name) = relevant
_checked_relevance(relevant, name) = throw(ArgumentError(string(
    "`@relevant($name)` must be true or false, got ", repr(relevant), " (",
    typeof(relevant), ")")))
_resolve_extracted(::_NoDefault, ::_NoDefault, name) = throw(MissingRequiredParam(name))
_resolve_extracted(::_NoDefault, default, _) = default
_resolve_extracted(v, _default, _name) = v

# Convert a kwarg-style symbol name to an HTTP header key by replacing `_` with
# `-`. HTTP.jl header lookup is case-insensitive, so `x-kb-agent-id` finds the
# `X-KB-Agent-ID` header that the KB curl wrapper injects.
_kwarg_to_header_name(name::Symbol) = replace(String(name), '_' => '-')

"""
    _extract_header(req, name, T, default=_NO_DEFAULT) -> value

Extract a single typed HTTP request header. The header key is derived from `name`
by replacing `_` with `-` (`x_kb_agent_id` → `x-kb-agent-id`; HTTP.jl lookup is
case-insensitive, so this matches `X-KB-Agent-ID`). Converts the raw string via
`_convert_param(value, T)` and returns `default` when the header is absent or
empty. Throws `MissingRequiredParam(name)` if no default was supplied and the header is absent.

`T` may be `nothing` for untyped headers (raw `String`).
"""
function _extract_header(req, name, T, default=_NO_DEFAULT)
    v = HTTP.header(req, _kwarg_to_header_name(name), "")
    v_or_sentinel = isempty(v) ? _NO_DEFAULT : _convert_submitted(v, T, name)
    _resolve_extracted(v_or_sentinel, default, name)
end

# Register a route handler on `ROUTER`. We extract path params ourselves via
# positional URL segment indexing. Wraps the handler so that pending Revise errors are surfaced as the
# framework's standard error article (see `_check_revise_errors!`) — this is
# the single chokepoint for all HTTP route registrations, so the check
# applies uniformly to plain, indexed, `@include`'d, and WebSocket routes.
function _register_handler(method, path, handler; resume_only::Bool=false,
        operation::Bool=false)
    http_method = get(_TRANSPORT_HTTP_METHODS, method, method)
    wrapped = function(req)
        _runtime_note_route!(req, method, path)
        try
            _check_revise_errors!()
        catch err
            return _route_error_response(req, err, catch_backtrace())
        end
        # The whole answer renders for the request's audience (`private`).
        _with_audience(() -> handler(req), export_audience(req))
    end
    operation && (wrapped = _OperationRoute(wrapped))
    if http_method == "GET" && !resume_only
        _register_get_route!(path, wrapped)
    else
        HTTP.register!(ROUTER, http_method, path, wrapped)
    end
end

# The handler of an `@htmx` route, which runs its property through
# `_run_operation`; a prestart reaches no other handler (`_prestart_router`).
struct _OperationRoute{F}
    handler::F
end
(route::_OperationRoute)(req) = route.handler(req)

# Every GET route goes on `ROUTER` and on its `_GET_ROUTER` mirror; the
# resume-only GETs of mutation paths go on `ROUTER` alone. A GET route an app
# registers on `ROUTER` directly is not mirrored.
function _register_get_route!(path, handler)
    pass = _route_registration_pass()
    pass === nothing || push!(pass.get_shapes, _route_shape(path))
    # Re-registering a route already warns once, from `ROUTER`.
    Base.CoreLogging.with_logger(Base.CoreLogging.NullLogger()) do
        HTTP.register!(_GET_ROUTER, "GET", path, handler)
    end
    HTTP.register!(ROUTER, "GET", path, handler)
end

# The router node a path is filed under. HTTP.jl matches a `{name}` (or `*`)
# segment as a nameless wildcard and a `{name:regex}` segment by its regex, so
# `/a/{x}` and `/a/{y}` are one node and registering either replaces the
# other's same-method handler.
_route_shape(path) = join(map(_route_shape_segment, split(path, '/'; keepempty=false)), '/')

function _route_shape_segment(segment)
    segment == "*" && return "{}"
    (startswith(segment, '{') && endswith(segment, '}')) || return String(segment)
    colon = findfirst(':', segment)
    colon === nothing ? "{}" : "{" * segment[colon:end]
end

# One `_register_routes` pass over a root type. Mutation routes poll through
# GETs of their own path; a path whose type declares no GET at that router
# node gets a resume-only GET once the pass has seen every route, so a GET
# declared after its mutation is never replaced by (or replaces) the resume
# handler. A plain GET reaching a resume-only GET is answered as if it were
# not there (`_resume_only_plain_get`).
const _ROUTE_REGISTRATION_PASS = :htmxo_route_registration_pass

_route_registration_pass() =
    get(task_local_storage(), _ROUTE_REGISTRATION_PASS, nothing)

function _with_route_registration_pass(f)
    pass = (; get_shapes=Set{String}(), mutation_resumes=Dict{String,Any}())
    task_local_storage(f, _ROUTE_REGISTRATION_PASS, pass)
    for (shape, resume) in pass.mutation_resumes
        shape in pass.get_shapes && continue
        resume.register(join(sort!(collect(resume.methods)), ", "))
    end
    nothing
end

# A plain GET of a mutation path gets the GET route `ROUTER` would have matched
# without the path's resume-only GET (a `{param}` route covering the path,
# say), or `405` with the path's mutation verbs as `Allow` when there is none.
function _resume_only_plain_get(req, allow)
    response = _GET_ROUTER(req)
    response isa _NoGetRoute ? HTTP.Response(405, ["Allow" => allow]) : response
end

# The client's `stream_probe_script` asks a stream route whether the page's
# credentials still reach it after a refused handshake. Only the response
# headers matter (an auth gateway in front answers with `HX-Refresh`), so a
# stream route answers the probe with an empty 204 before any upgrade,
# argument parsing or stream start.
const _STREAM_PROBE_HEADER = "X-HTMXO-Probe"
_is_stream_probe(req) = !isempty(HTTP.header(req, _STREAM_PROBE_HEADER, ""))
_stream_probe_response() = HTTP.Response(204, ["Cache-Control" => "no-store"])

# A WebSocket route is a GET route that upgrades the connection, behind the same
# Revise-error chokepoint as every other emitted route. `serve` puts the raw
# stream in `req.context[:stream]`; once the session ends, `_WebSocketClosed`
# tells the pipeline not to write an HTTP response on the upgraded connection.
function _register_websocket_handler(path, handler)
    _register_handler("WEBSOCKET", path, function(req)
        _is_stream_probe(req) && return _stream_probe_response()
        stream = get(req.context, :stream, nothing)
        if !HTTP.WebSockets.isupgrade(req) || stream === nothing
            # Explicit length: HTTP 1.x never chunks a response carrying `Upgrade`.
            body = "426: WebSocket upgrade required"
            return HTTP.Response(426,
                ["Upgrade" => "websocket", "Content-Type" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
                 "Content-Length" => string(sizeof(body))], body)
        end
        check_origin = get(req.context, :ws_check_origin, nothing)
        # HTTP.jl 1.x accepts upgrade keywords but does not check Origin.
        # Apply an explicitly supplied policy to the original stream request
        # before upgrading; HTTP.jl 2.x applies it during the handshake.
        if check_origin !== nothing && pkgversion(HTTP) < v"2"
            _ws_origin_allowed(check_origin, stream.message) ||
                return HTTP.Response(403, ["Content-Type" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8"],
                    "websocket origin rejected")
        end
        try
            HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade(ws -> _run_websocket(handler, ws, req), stream;
                check_origin)
        catch err
            @error "WebSocket session on $(req.target) failed" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        end
        throw(_WebSocketClosed())
    end)
end

function _ws_origin_allowed(checker::Function, request::HTTP.Request)::Bool
    origin = HTTP.header(request, "Origin", nothing)
    result = if applicable(checker, request, origin)
        checker(request, origin)
    elseif applicable(checker, request)
        checker(request)
    else
        throw(ArgumentError("check_origin callback must accept (request) or (request, origin)"))
    end
    result isa Bool || throw(ArgumentError("check_origin callback must return Bool"))
    return result
end

# Once the client has gone away (tab closed, element swapped out by HTMX), the
# `@ws` body's next `send`/`receive` throws a `WebSocketError`. That is how a
# push loop normally ends, not a route error, so it is not reported as one.
function _run_websocket(handler, ws, req)
    try
        handler(ws, req)
    catch err
        err isa HTTP.WebSockets.WebSocketError || rethrow()
    end
    nothing
end

# Transport pseudo-methods and the HTTP method their routes register under.
const _TRANSPORT_HTTP_METHODS = Dict("WEBSOCKET" => "GET", "SSE" => "GET")

# --- Server-sent events ------------------------------------------------------

# `serve(; compress)` on an event stream: one gzip stream (RFC 1952) for the
# whole response, with a zlib sync flush after every write, so each frame
# leaves the compressor whole as soon as it is written. CodecZlib's stream API
# cannot sync-flush (its `flush` keeps the bytes deflate still holds), so this
# drives zlib through CodecZlib's own binding. A separate gzip member per frame
# is no way around it: Chrome decodes only the first member of a response.
mutable struct _GzipFrames
    zstream::CodecZlib.ZStream
    out::Vector{UInt8}
end

function _GzipFrames()
    zstream = CodecZlib.ZStream()
    # Level 6, as `transcode` uses for buffered answers; window bits 15 + 16
    # write the gzip header and trailer.
    code = CodecZlib.deflate_init!(zstream, 6, 15 + 16)
    code == CodecZlib.Z_OK || error("zlib could not start a gzip stream (code $code)")
    _GzipFrames(zstream, Vector{UInt8}(undef, 64 * 1024))
end

# Compress `n` bytes at `p` into `io`, ending with `flush`: `Z_SYNC_FLUSH`
# after a write, `Z_FINISH` for the trailer (`_end_sse_gzip!`).
function _gzip_write(gz::_GzipFrames, io, p::Ptr{UInt8}, n::UInt, flush::Cint)
    z = gz.zstream
    while true
        # zlib counts input in `Cuint`; a larger write goes in pieces.
        piece = min(n, UInt(typemax(Cuint)))
        n -= piece
        mode = n == 0 ? flush : CodecZlib.Z_NO_FLUSH
        z.next_in = p
        z.avail_in = piece
        p += piece
        while true
            code = _gzip_deflate!(gz, io, mode)
            # `Z_BUF_ERROR` only says there was nothing left to do.
            code in (CodecZlib.Z_OK, CodecZlib.Z_STREAM_END, CodecZlib.Z_BUF_ERROR) ||
                error("zlib failed to compress an event stream (code $code)")
            # Done once zlib ends the stream, or leaves output space unused
            # (it then holds nothing back from this flush).
            code == CodecZlib.Z_STREAM_END && return nothing
            mode != CodecZlib.Z_FINISH && z.avail_out > 0 && break
        end
        n == 0 && return nothing
    end
end

# One `deflate` call into `gz.out`, whose output goes to `io`; zlib's code.
function _gzip_deflate!(gz::_GzipFrames, io, mode::Cint)
    z = gz.zstream
    return GC.@preserve gz begin
        z.next_out = pointer(gz.out)
        z.avail_out = length(gz.out)
        code = CodecZlib.deflate!(z, mode)
        produced = length(gz.out) - z.avail_out
        produced > 0 && unsafe_write(io, pointer(gz.out), produced)
        code
    end
end

"""
    SSEStream

The `__sse__` handle inside an `@sse` route body: one live
`text/event-stream` response. Send events with
`HTTP.WebSockets.send(__sse__, x; event, id, retry)` — the same `send` that
`@ws` bodies use — and loop `while isopen(__sse__)` for an open-ended feed.

`send` renders `x` like a route return value (a `Node`, a string, …), splits
it into `data:` lines, and returns `false` instead of throwing once the client
has disconnected. `isopen` turns `false` at the first failed write; the
framework's keep-alive comment (every 15 s) makes sure a quiet feed notices a
departed client too. Every `write` to the handle is serialized with those
comments, so a frame written in one call is never split.

When the body returns, a non-`nothing` value is sent as a final
`event: done` (a thrown error is recorded and its `__error__` rendering sent
instead), followed by `event: close`, which tells the client the stream is
over. `close(__sse__)` instead ends the stream without those frames, so the
browser reconnects (sending [`last_event_id`](@ref)).

Under `serve(; compress=true)` the stream is gzipped for a client that accepts
gzip, and every write is flushed through the compressor, so each frame still
reaches the browser the moment it is written.
"""
mutable struct SSEStream <: IO
    stream::Any
    request::HTTP.Request
    lock::ReentrantLock
    open::Bool
    gzip::Union{Nothing,_GzipFrames}
end

# `gzip=true`: the response announced `Content-Encoding: gzip`
# (`_sse_gzip_headers!`), and every write goes through one gzip stream.
SSEStream(stream, request::HTTP.Request; gzip::Bool=false) =
    SSEStream(stream, request, ReentrantLock(), true, gzip ? _GzipFrames() : nothing)

Base.isopen(sse::SSEStream) = @lock sse.lock sse.open

# Ends the stream from inside the body: later sends return `false` and the
# framework's closing frames are skipped (the client may then reconnect).
function Base.close(sse::SSEStream)
    @lock sse.lock sse.open = false
    nothing
end

function Base.unsafe_write(sse::SSEStream, p::Ptr{UInt8}, n::UInt)
    lock(sse.lock)
    try
        sse.open || throw(Base.IOError("SSE client disconnected", 0))
        try
            sse.gzip === nothing && return unsafe_write(sse.stream, p, n)
            _gzip_write(sse.gzip, sse.stream, p, n, CodecZlib.Z_SYNC_FLUSH)
            return n
        catch
            # A failed write means the client went away; nothing more can
            # reach it, so every later write and `isopen` reflects that.
            sse.open = false
            rethrow()
        end
    finally
        unlock(sse.lock)
    end
end

Base.write(sse::SSEStream, b::UInt8) = write(sse, UInt8[b])

"""
    last_event_id(sse::SSEStream) -> Union{String,Nothing}

The `Last-Event-ID` the browser sent when reconnecting (the `id` of the last
event it received), or `nothing` on a first connection. Lets an `@sse` body
resume a feed instead of replaying it.
"""
function last_event_id(sse::SSEStream)
    value = HTTP.header(sse.request, "Last-Event-ID", "")
    isempty(value) ? nothing : String(value)
end

function _sse_field(name, value)
    s = string(value)
    (occursin('\n', s) || occursin('\r', s) || occursin('\0', s)) &&
        throw(ArgumentError("SSE $name must not contain newlines or NUL: $(repr(s))"))
    s
end

# One complete `text/event-stream` frame. Every line of `data` becomes its own
# `data:` line (the browser joins them back with `\n`), so multi-line HTML
# arrives intact.
function _sse_frame(data::AbstractString; event=nothing, id=nothing, retry=nothing)
    io = IOBuffer()
    event === nothing || print(io, "event: ", _sse_field("event", event), '\n')
    id === nothing || print(io, "id: ", _sse_field("id", id), '\n')
    if retry !== nothing
        retry isa Integer && retry > 0 ||
            throw(ArgumentError("SSE retry must be a positive integer, got $(repr(retry))"))
        print(io, "retry: ", retry, '\n')
    end
    for line in split(data, r"\r\n|\r|\n")
        print(io, "data: ", line, '\n')
    end
    print(io, '\n')
    String(take!(io))
end

_sse_text(body::AbstractString) = String(body)
_sse_text(body::AbstractVector{UInt8}) = String(copy(body))
function _sse_text(body)
    # HTTP 2.x wraps response bodies in explicit body objects.
    if isdefined(HTTP, :BytesBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :BytesBody))
        return String(Vector{UInt8}(body))
    elseif isdefined(HTTP, :EmptyBody) && isa(body, getproperty(HTTP, :EmptyBody))
        return ""
    end
    throw(ArgumentError("cannot send a $(typeof(body)) response body as SSE data"))
end

# Event data is rendered exactly like a route return value.
_sse_data(x::AbstractString) = String(x)
_sse_data(::Nothing) = ""
_sse_data(x) = _sse_text(to_response(x).body)

function HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse::SSEStream, x; event=nothing, id=nothing,
        retry=nothing)
    isopen(sse) || return false
    frame = _sse_frame(_sse_data(x); event, id, retry)
    try
        write(sse, frame)
        true
    catch err
        isopen(sse) && rethrow()
        @debug "SSE client disconnected" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        false
    end
end

const _SSE_HEADERS = (
    "Content-Type" => "text/event-stream",
    "Cache-Control" => "no-cache",
    # Stops nginx and similar reverse proxies from buffering the stream.
    "X-Accel-Buffering" => "no",
)

# Seconds between keep-alive comments; idle proxies drop silent connections.
# `0` disables them.
const _SSE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS = Ref(15.0)

function _start_sse_heartbeat(sse::SSEStream)
    interval = _SSE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS[]
    interval > 0 || return nothing
    Timer(interval; interval) do timer
        try
            isopen(sse) ? write(sse, ": keepalive\n\n") : close(timer)
        catch
            close(timer)
        end
    end
end

# The value an `@sse` body ended with, or the error it failed with (already
# recorded and rendered through `__error__`).
struct _SSEFailure
    rendered::Any
end

function _sse_failure(err, bt, req, error_obj)
    uid, path = _record_error(err, bt, req)
    _SSEFailure(_invoke_error_handler(error_obj, err, uid, path))
end

function _run_sse_body(body, sse::SSEStream, req, error_obj)
    try
        body(sse)
    catch err
        bt = catch_backtrace()
        # A body that dies on a write to a departed client was simply stopped.
        isopen(sse) || return nothing
        _sse_failure(err, bt, req, error_obj)
    end
end

# End-of-stream frames: a non-`nothing` final value (or the rendered failure)
# goes out as `event: done`, then `event: close` tells the client the stream
# is over. Without that marker the browser's EventSource would reconnect and
# run a finished body again; `sse_region` closes the source on it.
function _finish_sse_body(sse::SSEStream, final, req, error_obj)
    isopen(sse) || return nothing
    final isa _SSEFailure && (final = final.rendered)
    if final !== nothing
        try
            HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, final; event="done")
        catch err
            isopen(sse) || return nothing
            HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse,
                _sse_failure(err, catch_backtrace(), req, error_obj).rendered;
                event="done")
        end
    end
    HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, ""; event="close")
    nothing
end

# HTTP 1's stream handler writes the returned response's head once more after
# the handler returns. The stream therefore announces `Connection: close`: the
# body is terminated first, then the request is marked `Connection: close` so
# the server closes the connection right after that late write, which lands
# behind a finished response the client has already agreed not to reuse.
# HTTP 2 terminates the body and then ignores the handler's empty return value.
_sse_http1() = isdefined(HTTP, :payload)

function _end_sse_response(stream)
    try
        HTTP.closewrite(stream)
    catch err
        @debug "SSE response already closed" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
    end
    _sse_http1() && HTTP.setheader(stream.message, "Connection" => "close")
    nothing
end

function _serve_sse(req::HTTP.Request, body; error_obj=nothing)
    stream = req.context[:stream]
    for header in _SSE_HEADERS
        HTTP.setheader(stream, header)
    end
    gzip = _sse_gzip_headers!(stream, req)
    _sse_http1() && HTTP.setheader(stream, "Connection" => "close")
    HTTP.startwrite(stream)
    sse = SSEStream(stream, req; gzip)
    heartbeat = _start_sse_heartbeat(sse)
    try
        final = _run_sse_body(body, sse, req, error_obj)
        _finish_sse_body(sse, final, req, error_obj)
    finally
        heartbeat === nothing || close(heartbeat)
        close(sse)
        _end_sse_gzip!(sse)
        _end_sse_response(stream)
    end
    HTTP.Response(200)
end

# `serve(; compress)` (`_stream_handler` records it on the request) for an
# event stream: `Vary: Accept-Encoding` on every stream, as on a buffered
# answer, and `Content-Encoding: gzip` when the client admits gzip. Whether
# the stream is gzipped.
function _sse_gzip_headers!(stream, req::HTTP.Request)
    get(req.context, :htmxo_compress, false) === true || return false
    HTTP.setheader(stream, "Vary" => "Accept-Encoding")
    _accepts_gzip(HTTP.header(req, "Accept-Encoding", "")) || return false
    HTTP.setheader(stream, "Content-Encoding" => "gzip")
    return true
end

# A gzipped stream's trailer, written before the response ends, and zlib's
# memory freed whether or not the client is still there to take it.
function _end_sse_gzip!(sse::SSEStream)
    @lock sse.lock begin
        gz = sse.gzip
        gz === nothing && return nothing
        sse.gzip = nothing
        try
            _gzip_write(gz, sse.stream, Ptr{UInt8}(C_NULL), UInt(0), CodecZlib.Z_FINISH)
        catch err
            @debug "SSE client left before the gzip trailer" exception=(err, catch_backtrace())
        finally
            CodecZlib.deflate_end!(gz.zstream)
        end
    end
    nothing
end

# An `@sse` route answers its GET with a live event stream. Argument parsing,
# validation, and target resolution run in `prepare` BEFORE any byte is sent,
# so a bad request still gets an ordinary error response (a non-200 status
# also stops the browser's EventSource from reconnecting). `prepare` returns
# `(; body, error_obj)`: the `(__sse__) -> …` route value and the object whose
# `__error__` renders a failure after the stream has started.
function _register_sse_handler(path, prepare)
    _register_handler("SSE", path, function(req)
        _is_stream_probe(req) && return _stream_probe_response()
        haskey(req.context, :stream) || return _route_error_response(req,
            ArgumentError("`@sse` route $(path) needs a live HTTP connection; " *
                          "in-process requests cannot consume an event stream"),
            backtrace())
        prepared = try
            prepare(req)
        catch err
            return _route_error_response(req, err, catch_backtrace())
        end
        _serve_sse(req, prepared.body; error_obj=prepared.error_obj)
    end)
end

"""
    sse_region(url, content...; events="message,done", close="close", swap=nothing)

Client side of an `@sse` route: a `<div hx-ext="sse" sse-connect=url>` that
opens the stream, wrapping a target that swaps in the named `events`
(default: plain `send`s and the body's final `done` value). The source closes
on the framework's `close` event, so a finished stream is not re-run by the
browser's automatic reconnect. `content` is the target's initial content;
`swap` sets its `hx-swap` (htmx's default replaces the inner HTML; pass
`swap="beforeend"` to append a log or feed).

Build `url` from the route struct so mounts keep working, e.g.
`sse_region(query_url(__self__/"feed"; n=3), h.p("waiting…"))`.

Needs the htmx SSE extension, which [`htmx`](@ref) page shells load by default.
"""
function sse_region(url, content...; events="message,done", close="close",
        swap=nothing)
    target = isnothing(swap) ? h.div(; sse_swap=events) :
                               h.div(; sse_swap=events, hx_swap=swap)
    h.div(; hx_ext="sse", sse_connect=string(url), sse_close=close)(
        target(content...))
end

# --- Server-push refresh of fragments by resource key ------------------------

"""
    KeySubscriptions()

Thread-safe registry mapping a resource key (e.g. `"gh:owner/repo#12"`) to
the live [`SSEStream`](@ref)s of the fragments subscribed to it. One
instance per app (usually a `const`): an `@sse` route body feeds it through
[`serve_key_feed!`](@ref), and any server code pushes a refresh with
[`invalidate_key!`](@ref), which re-fetches every subscribed
[`live_fragment`](@ref) on every open page.
"""
struct KeySubscriptions
    lock::ReentrantLock
    streams::Dict{String,Vector{SSEStream}}
end

KeySubscriptions() = KeySubscriptions(ReentrantLock(), Dict{String,Vector{SSEStream}}())

function _push_key(key)
    k = string(key)
    isempty(k) && throw(ArgumentError("fragment subscription keys must be non-empty"))
    k
end

# The event name travels both as an SSE `event:` field and inside an
# `hx-trigger="sse:<event>"` attribute, where whitespace and commas would
# split it into several triggers. One token keeps both sides in agreement.
function _push_event(event)
    e = string(event)
    (isempty(e) || any(c -> c in " ,\t\r\n", e)) &&
        throw(ArgumentError("fragment refresh event must be one non-empty token, got $(repr(e))"))
    e
end

# The key half of a wire event name travels through three layers: the SSE
# `event:` field (no newlines or NUL), `source.addEventListener(name)` (any
# string), and the fragment's `hx-trigger="sse:<name>"`, where htmx's trigger
# parser ends the name at a comma, `[`, or whitespace (a bare `]` also trips
# a syntax error). Percent-encode exactly those plus the escape character,
# so `gh:owner/repo#12`-style keys stay readable while arbitrary keys stay
# one trigger token.
function _escape_event_key(key)
    io = IOBuffer()
    for c in string(key)
        if c == '%' || c == ',' || c == '[' || c == ']' || c == '\0' ||
                c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r' || c == '\v' || c == '\f'
            print(io, '%', uppercase(string(Int(c); base=16, pad=2)))
        else
            print(io, c)
        end
    end
    String(take!(io))
end

# The wire event name for a keyed refresh: the validated `event` prefix plus
# the trigger-safe key. Both `live_fragment` (the `hx-trigger`) and
# `invalidate_key!` (the `event:` frame) compute it, so the two sides agree
# by construction.
_push_event_name(event, key) = _push_event(event) * "-" * _escape_event_key(_push_key(key))

"""
    subscribe_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, sse::SSEStream, key) -> String

Register `sse` for push refreshes of `key` (converted with `string`, which
must be non-empty). Registering the same stream twice is a no-op. Returns
the key. [`serve_key_feed!`](@ref) calls this for `@sse` route bodies;
call it directly only for a hand-rolled feed.
"""
function subscribe_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, sse::SSEStream, key)
    k = _push_key(key)
    lock(subs.lock) do
        vec = get!(Vector{SSEStream}, subs.streams, k)
        any(s -> s === sse, vec) || push!(vec, sse)
    end
    k
end

"""
    unsubscribe_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, sse::SSEStream, key) -> Bool

Drop `sse` from `key`'s subscribers, removing the key when its last stream
leaves. Returns whether the stream was subscribed. Unknown keys and
foreign streams are a quiet no-op (`false`).
"""
function unsubscribe_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, sse::SSEStream, key)
    k = string(key)
    lock(subs.lock) do
        vec = get(subs.streams, k, nothing)
        isnothing(vec) && return false
        before = length(vec)
        filter!(s -> s !== sse, vec)
        isempty(vec) && delete!(subs.streams, k)
        length(vec) != before
    end
end

"""
    serve_key_feed!(sse::SSEStream, subs::KeySubscriptions, key; poll=1.0) -> Nothing
    serve_key_feed!(sse::SSEStream, subs::KeySubscriptions, keys::AbstractVector; poll=1.0) -> Nothing

`@sse` route body for a keyed subscription feed: subscribes `sse` to `key`
(or every key in `keys`), blocks while the client stays connected, and
unsubscribes on the way out. `poll` is the disconnect-check interval in
seconds.

The vector form multiplexes many keys onto ONE stream: the page wraps its
fragments in one [`live_region`](@ref) whose feed URL carries every key
(`query_url(...; key=keys)`), and each [`live_fragment`](@ref) re-fetches
only on its own key's event — one connection per page instead of one per
fragment.

```julia
@sse key_events(; key::Vector{String}=String[]) = serve_key_feed!(__sse__, SUBS, key)
```
"""
function serve_key_feed!(sse::SSEStream, subs::KeySubscriptions, keys::AbstractVector; poll::Real=1.0)
    poll >= 0 || throw(ArgumentError("serve_key_feed! poll must be non-negative, got $poll"))
    ks = unique!([_push_key(k) for k in keys])
    isempty(ks) && throw(ArgumentError("serve_key_feed! needs at least one subscription key"))
    for k in ks
        subscribe_key!(subs, sse, k)
    end
    try
        while isopen(sse)
            sleep(poll)
        end
    finally
        for k in ks
            unsubscribe_key!(subs, sse, k)
        end
    end
    nothing
end

serve_key_feed!(sse::SSEStream, subs::KeySubscriptions, key; poll::Real=1.0) =
    serve_key_feed!(sse, subs, [key]; poll=poll)

"""
    invalidate_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, key; event="refresh") -> Int

Push a refresh of `key` to every subscribed fragment on every open page:
each subscribed stream gets one `<event>-<key>` frame carrying the key,
which re-fetches exactly the fragments showing that key through their
`hx-trigger="sse:<event>-<key>"` (see [`live_fragment`](@ref)) — on a
multiplexed [`live_region`](@ref) stream the other keys' fragments stay
put. Streams whose client is gone are pruned. Returns the number of
streams notified; a key with no subscribers is a quiet no-op (`0`).

This is the push half only: it does not touch whatever cache the fragment
route reads. Refresh the data first (or kick its background rebuild), then
invalidate, so the re-fetch renders the new state.
"""
function invalidate_key!(subs::KeySubscriptions, key; event::AbstractString="refresh")
    k = _push_key(key)
    e = _push_event_name(event, k)
    live = lock(subs.lock) do
        copy(get(subs.streams, k, SSEStream[]))
    end
    notified = 0
    pruned = false
    for sse in live
        if HTTP.WebSockets.send(sse, k; event=e)
            notified += 1
        else
            pruned = true
        end
    end
    if pruned
        lock(subs.lock) do
            vec = get(subs.streams, k, nothing)
            if vec !== nothing
                # Drop streams the push could not reach; a stream that died
                # between the push and this lock is dead either way.
                filter!(isopen, vec)
                isempty(vec) && delete!(subs.streams, k)
            end
        end
    end
    notified
end

"""
    live_fragment(key, content...; fragment_url, events_url=nothing, event="refresh",
                  swap="outerHTML", id=nothing, class=nothing)

A fragment that re-fetches itself when `key` is invalidated. Renders a
`<div>` that re-`GET`s `fragment_url` into itself (`hx-swap`, default
`"outerHTML"`) whenever its stream sends the key's event
(`<event>-<key>`, default `refresh-<key>`). The fragment route answers
with the same `live_fragment` call, so the re-fetch is self-similar.

- With `events_url` the div opens its own [`serve_key_feed!`](@ref) stream
  (`sse-connect` to `events_url`, which must carry the key, e.g.
  `query_url(__self__/"key_events"; key=key)`); the swapped-out element's
  stream is closed by the SSE extension and the fresh element opens a new
  one. One stream per fragment.
- With `events_url=nothing` (the default) the div opens no stream and
  listens to the [`live_region`](@ref) stream of an ancestor instead, so
  many fragments share one connection. Outside a `live_region` the
  fragment renders but never refreshes.

```julia
@get issue_card(; key::String="") = live_fragment(key, _issue_body(key);
    fragment_url=query_url(__self__/"issue_card"; key=key))
```

An invalidation that lands between the page render and the stream connect
is missed: the fragment shows render-time state until the next one. The
same holds for a fragment swapped in later until htmx has processed it
(its triggers attach when the swap settles). In `?plain` the element
degrades to its text content; the refresh machinery is inert there.
"""
function live_fragment(key, content...; fragment_url, events_url=nothing,
        event::AbstractString="refresh", swap::AbstractString="outerHTML",
        id=nothing, class=nothing)
    k = _push_key(key)
    e = _push_event_name(event, k)
    h.div(content...; id, class, hx_ext="sse",
        sse_connect=isnothing(events_url) ? nothing : string(events_url),
        hx_get=string(fragment_url), hx_trigger="sse:$e",
        hx_swap=string(swap), data_key=k)
end

"""
    live_region(events_url, content...; id=nothing, class=nothing, discover=false)

One shared [`serve_key_feed!`](@ref) stream for many
[`live_fragment`](@ref)s. Renders a `<div>` that connects a single event
stream (`sse-connect` to `events_url`, which must carry every key the
region shows, e.g. `query_url(__self__/"key_events"; key=keys)`) and hosts
fragments rendered without their own `events_url`: each re-fetches only on
its own key's event. One connection per region instead of one per
fragment — the shape for per-row or per-reference liveness, where a stream
per fragment would starve the page's other requests under HTTP/1.1's
per-origin connection limit.

```julia
@get panel() = live_region(query_url(__self__/"key_events"; key=all_keys),
    (issue_card(__self__, k) for k in all_keys)...)

@sse key_events(; key::Vector{String}=String[]) =
    serve_key_feed!(__sse__, SUBS, key)
```

With `discover=true`, `events_url` is the feed's BASE url (no keys) and
the region follows the fragments actually present instead of the keys
known at render time: the [`live_region_script`](@ref) runtime gathers
the `data-key`s of descendant fragments — including ones swapped in
later by htmx, a poller, or a modal — opens one stream with `?key=…` for
the union, and reconnects whenever the set changes. An event reaches the
fragments present when it arrives, so a fragment's own `outerHTML`
refresh and a late fragment on a key the stream already carries refresh
like the fragments present at load, with no reconnect. A refused stream
(any non-200, such as a gateway's `502`/`503` while the app restarts) is
reopened with the sse extension's backoff for a static region — 500 ms,
doubling to at most 64 s, reset once a stream opens. Each reconnect is an
ordinary fresh subscription; the previous stream's server task
unsubscribes when it notices the disconnect (within one `poll`
interval), so a push landing in that window re-fetches its fragments
twice — idempotent. With no fragments present the region holds no stream
rather than opening an empty-key feed. A fragment belongs to its nearest
ancestor discover region; do not combine a discover region with the
static `sse-connect` shape (or nest discover regions) over the same
fragments — both halves would refresh them. Pages built without
[`htmx`](@ref) must include [`live_region_script`](@ref) themselves.

In `?plain` the element degrades to its children's text content.
"""
function live_region(events_url, content...; id=nothing, class=nothing,
        discover::Bool=false)
    url = string(events_url)
    isempty(url) && throw(ArgumentError("live_region events_url must be non-empty"))
    discover || return h.div(content...; id, class, hx_ext="sse", sse_connect=url)
    h.div(content...; id, class,
        data_htmxo_live_discover="", data_htmxo_live_base=url)
end

"""
    live_region_script()

Client runtime for [`live_region`](@ref) discover mode (`discover=true`).
Each `[data-htmxo-live-discover]` region keeps one `EventSource` open on
its base url with `?key=…` for the union of its descendant
[`live_fragment`](@ref) `data-key`s — however they reach the DOM (page
load, htmx swap, poller, modal) — and reconnects when the set changes. A
stream that ends `CLOSED` (refused with any non-200) reports to
[`stream_probe_script`](@ref) and reopens after htmx-ext-sse's backoff
(500 ms × 2^n, at most 64 s, reset on `open`); a teardown or key-set change
cancels it. A reconnect is an ordinary fresh [`serve_key_feed!`](@ref)
subscription; the previous stream's server task unsubscribes on disconnect. Each event is
dispatched to the region's fragments for that event at arrival, through
the fragment's own `hx-trigger="sse:<event>"` — the exact call the sse
extension makes for a static region, so a fragment behaves identically
under either shape, including after its own swap replaces it. A fragment belongs to its nearest
ancestor discover region. Exposes `window.htmxoLiveRegion.init(el)` /
`.resync()`. Safe to include more than once. Auto-included by [`htmx`](@ref);
include it yourself only on pages built without the shell.
"""
live_region_script() = h.script(Raw(raw"""
(function () {
  'use strict';
  if (window.htmxoLiveRegion) return;
  var MARK = 'data-htmxo-live-discover';
  var BASE = 'data-htmxo-live-base';
  var DEBOUNCE_MS = 60;
  var FRAG_SELECTOR = '[data-key][hx-trigger], [data-key][data-hx-trigger]';
  var live = [];

  // The `sse:NAME` wire names in one hx-trigger value. A fragment rendered
  // by `live_fragment` carries exactly `sse:<event>-<key>`; the split keeps
  // multi-trigger values (`sse:a, click`) and trims htmx filters/modifiers
  // (`sse:e[foo]`, `sse:e consume`), so the runtime reads the wire names the
  // server rendered instead of reimplementing its key escaping.
  function sseNames(trigger) {
    var out = [];
    if (!trigger) return out;
    trigger.split(',').forEach(function (part) {
      var t = part.trim();
      if (t.slice(0, 4) !== 'sse:') return;
      var name = t.slice(4).split(/[\s\[]/, 1)[0];
      if (name) out.push(name);
    });
    return out;
  }

  function triggersOf(el) {
    return sseNames(el.getAttribute('hx-trigger'))
      .concat(sseNames(el.getAttribute('data-hx-trigger')));
  }

  // Fragments owned by `region`: carrying a key plus an sse trigger, with
  // `region` as their NEAREST discover region (nested regions keep theirs).
  // Other `data-key` carriers (thread items carry no hx-trigger) select out.
  function ownFragments(region) {
    return Array.prototype.filter.call(
      region.querySelectorAll(FRAG_SELECTOR),
      function (el) {
        return el.getAttribute('data-key') &&
          el.closest('[' + MARK + ']') === region && triggersOf(el).length > 0;
      });
  }

  function feedUrl(base, keys) {
    var u = new URL(base, window.location.href);
    u.searchParams.delete('key');   // the runtime owns `key`; other params pass through
    keys.forEach(function (k) { u.searchParams.append('key', k); });
    return u.toString();
  }

  function openSource(url) {
    // The sse extension's factory when it is loaded, so a consumer override
    // covers discover streams too; otherwise a plain EventSource.
    if (window.htmx && htmx.createEventSource) return htmx.createEventSource(url);
    return new EventSource(url, { withCredentials: true });
  }

  function closeState(state) {
    if (state.source) { state.source.close(); state.source = null; }
    state.names = new Set();
  }

  // A browser `EventSource` refused with any non-200 (a gateway's 502/503
  // while the app restarts, a 401) ends `CLOSED` and never retries by itself.
  // Reopen after the sse extension's backoff for a static region (500 ms × 2^n,
  // at most 64 s), counted across consecutive failures and reset on `open`.
  // The reopen is an ordinary `sync`, so it carries the key set present then.
  function retryLater(state) {
    closeState(state);
    state.retries = Math.max(Math.min(state.retries * 2, 128), 1);
    state.retry = setTimeout(function () { state.retry = null; sync(state); },
                             state.retries * 500);
  }

  function open(state, keys) {
    var source = state.source = openSource(feedUrl(state.base, keys));
    source.addEventListener('open', function () {
      if (state.source === source) state.retries = 0;
    });
    source.addEventListener('error', function () {
      // CONNECTING: the browser is re-establishing a dropped stream itself.
      if (source.readyState !== EventSource.CLOSED) return;
      // A refused stream ends `CLOSED` with no status to read; the probe
      // runtime asks whether the page's session still reaches it.
      if (window.htmxoStreamProbe) window.htmxoStreamProbe.failed(source.url);
      // Only the region's current stream retries: one closed by a key-set
      // change or a teardown is no longer `state.source`.
      if (state.source === source) retryLater(state);
    });
  }

  // One listener per wire event name on the open stream. It reads the
  // region's fragments for that name when the event arrives, never a list
  // taken when the stream opened: a fragment's own outerHTML refresh and a
  // fragment swapped in later on a key the stream already carries are new
  // elements, and neither changes the key set that reopens the stream.
  function listen(state, name) {
    if (state.names.has(name)) return;
    state.names.add(name);
    var region = state.region;
    state.source.addEventListener(name, function (evt) {
      if (!document.contains(region)) return;
      ownFragments(region).forEach(function (f) {
        if (triggersOf(f).indexOf(name) < 0) return;
        // The static region shape's exact dispatch: the sse extension
        // turns a wire event into `htmx.trigger(f, 'sse:NAME')`, and htmx
        // core answers through the fragment's `hx-trigger`.
        if (window.htmx) {
          htmx.trigger(f, 'sse:' + name, evt);
          htmx.trigger(f, 'htmx:sseMessage', evt);
        } else {
          f.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('sse:' + name, { bubbles: true, detail: evt }));
        }
      });
    });
  }

  // Reconcile the region's stream with the fragments present: close + reopen
  // on the new union when the sorted key set changed, then listen for any
  // wire name not yet heard on the open stream. While a refused stream's
  // backoff is pending, the reopen waits for it, whatever the key set.
  function sync(state) {
    if (!document.contains(state.region)) { teardown(state.region); return; }
    var keys = [];
    var names = [];
    ownFragments(state.region).forEach(function (el) {
      var key = el.getAttribute('data-key');
      if (keys.indexOf(key) < 0) keys.push(key);
      triggersOf(el).forEach(function (name) {
        if (names.indexOf(name) < 0) names.push(name);
      });
    });
    keys.sort();
    var sig = JSON.stringify(keys);
    if (sig !== state.sig) {
      state.sig = sig;
      closeState(state);
    }
    // No fragments: hold no stream until one arrives.
    if (!state.source && keys.length && !state.retry) open(state, keys);
    if (state.source) names.forEach(function (name) { listen(state, name); });
  }

  function schedule(state) {
    if (state.timer) return;
    state.timer = setTimeout(function () { state.timer = null; sync(state); }, DEBOUNCE_MS);
  }

  function init(region) {
    if (!region || region.nodeType !== 1 || !region.hasAttribute(MARK)) return null;
    if (region.__htmxoLive) return region.__htmxoLive;
    var base = region.getAttribute(BASE);
    if (!base) return null;
    var state = { region: region, base: base, sig: null, source: null, names: new Set(),
                  timer: null, mo: null, retry: null, retries: 0 };
    region.__htmxoLive = state;
    live.push(state);
    state.mo = new MutationObserver(function () { schedule(state); });
    state.mo.observe(region, {
      childList: true, subtree: true, attributes: true,
      attributeFilter: ['data-key', 'hx-trigger', 'data-hx-trigger']
    });
    sync(state);
    return state;
  }

  function teardown(region) {
    var state = region ? region.__htmxoLive : null;
    if (!state) return;
    region.__htmxoLive = null;
    var i = live.indexOf(state);
    if (i >= 0) live.splice(i, 1);
    if (state.timer) { clearTimeout(state.timer); state.timer = null; }
    if (state.retry) { clearTimeout(state.retry); state.retry = null; }
    if (state.mo) { state.mo.disconnect(); state.mo = null; }
    closeState(state);
  }

  function scan(node) {
    if (!node || node.nodeType !== 1) return;
    if (node.hasAttribute(MARK)) init(node);
    if (node.querySelectorAll) node.querySelectorAll('[' + MARK + ']').forEach(init);
  }

  function sweep() {
    // A region dropped with a removed ancestor carries no observer record
    // of its own; anything tracked but no longer in the document is dead.
    live.slice().forEach(function (state) {
      if (!document.contains(state.region)) teardown(state.region);
    });
  }

  window.htmxoLiveRegion = {
    init: init,
    resync: function () { live.slice().forEach(sync); }
  };

  function boot() {
    scan(document.body);
    new MutationObserver(function (records) {
      records.forEach(function (r) {
        r.addedNodes.forEach(function (n) { scan(n); });
        r.removedNodes.forEach(function (n) {
          if (n.nodeType !== 1) return;
          if (n.hasAttribute(MARK)) teardown(n);
          n.querySelectorAll('[' + MARK + ']').forEach(teardown);
        });
      });
      sweep();
    }).observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });
    // Backstop for the observers above: once htmx settles a swap, scan it
    // for new regions and resync the region it landed in. Dispatch reads the
    // fragments when an event arrives, so a resync only settles the stream's
    // key set and wire names; it is a no-op when neither changed.
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSettle', function (e) {
      var t = e.target;
      if (!t || t.nodeType !== 1) return;
      scan(t);
      var r = t.closest('[' + MARK + ']');
      if (r && r.__htmxoLive) schedule(r.__htmxoLive);
    });
  }
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot);
  else boot();
})();
"""))

"""
    stream_probe_script()

Client runtime that lets an auth gateway end a refused stream. A browser
exposes no HTTP status for a refused WebSocket handshake (just `error` and
`close` 1006) or a refused `EventSource` (it ends `CLOSED`), so a stream
behind an expired login cannot tell a `401` from a transient failure: the
htmx ws and sse extensions reconnect with backoff forever, and the page goes
stale until a manual reload.

When a stream fails before it opens — a ws-extension socket that closes
without opening, an sse-extension or [`live_region`](@ref) discover source
that ends `CLOSED` — the runtime sends one htmx-shaped GET (`HX-Request:
true`, `X-HTMXO-Probe: stream`) to that stream's own URL and reads only the
response headers, in htmx's order: `HX-Redirect` navigates, `HX-Refresh:
true` reloads the page (an auth gateway's answer to an expired login,
typically `401` plus `HX-Refresh`). Any other answer leaves the stream's own
reconnect running. `@ws` and `@sse` routes answer the probe with an empty 204
before upgrading or starting the stream; other endpoints answer however they
answer a GET, and the probe aborts once the headers arrive.

Probes are page-wide: at most one in flight and at most one per 5 seconds,
only for same-origin streams. `window.htmxoStreamProbe.failed(url)` reports a
failed stream the runtime does not see itself and returns the probe's
promise (`'refresh'`, `'redirect'`, a status, `'redirected'` or
`'unreachable'`), or `null` when no probe was sent. Safe to include more than
once. Auto-included by [`htmx`](@ref); include it yourself only on pages
built without the shell.
"""
stream_probe_script() = h.script(Raw(raw"""
(function () {
  'use strict';
  if (window.htmxoStreamProbe) return;
  // The gateway checks the page's session, so one answer covers every
  // stream on the page, however many fail together.
  var SPACING_MS = 5000;
  var TIMEOUT_MS = 10000;
  var inflight = null;
  var last = -Infinity;

  // The http(s) form of a stream URL, or null for another origin, whose
  // auth is not the page's session.
  function probeUrl(url) {
    var u;
    try { u = new URL(url, window.location.href); } catch (_) { return null; }
    if (u.protocol === 'wss:') u.protocol = 'https:';
    else if (u.protocol === 'ws:') u.protocol = 'http:';
    return u.origin === window.location.origin ? u.href : null;
  }

  // One htmx-shaped GET; an auth gateway keys its htmx answer on
  // `HX-Request`. Only the headers are read, and the request is aborted as
  // soon as they arrive, so an endpoint that streams anyway never stays open.
  // A redirect is not followed: only the stream URL's own answer counts.
  function probe(url) {
    var ctl = new AbortController();
    var timer = setTimeout(function () { ctl.abort(); }, TIMEOUT_MS);
    return fetch(url, {
      headers: { 'HX-Request': 'true', 'HX-Current-URL': window.location.href,
                 'X-HTMXO-Probe': 'stream' },
      credentials: 'same-origin', cache: 'no-store', redirect: 'manual',
      signal: ctl.signal
    }).then(function (r) {
      var redirect = r.headers.get('HX-Redirect');
      var refresh = r.headers.get('HX-Refresh') === 'true';
      ctl.abort();
      // htmx's own order for these two headers.
      if (redirect) {
        window.location.href = redirect;
        if (refresh) window.location.reload();
        return 'redirect';
      }
      if (refresh) { window.location.reload(); return 'refresh'; }
      return r.type === 'opaqueredirect' ? 'redirected' : r.status;
    }, function () {
      return 'unreachable';
    }).then(function (outcome) {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      return outcome;
    });
  }

  function failed(url) {
    var target = probeUrl(url);
    if (!target || inflight) return null;
    var now = Date.now();
    if (now - last < SPACING_MS) return null;
    last = now;
    inflight = probe(target).then(function (outcome) {
      inflight = null;
      return outcome;
    });
    return inflight;
  }

  // ws extension: a socket that closes without having opened is a refused
  // handshake. One that opened and later dropped is left to the extension's
  // reconnect, whose new socket reports here if its handshake is refused.
  var opened = new WeakSet();
  function socketOf(e) { return e.detail && e.detail.event && e.detail.event.target; }
  document.addEventListener('htmx:wsOpen', function (e) {
    var socket = socketOf(e);
    if (socket) opened.add(socket);
  });
  document.addEventListener('htmx:wsClose', function (e) {
    var socket = socketOf(e);
    if (socket && socket.url && !opened.has(socket)) failed(socket.url);
  });
  // sse extension: the browser gave up on the source (a dropped stream
  // reconnects in CONNECTING and reports here only if that is refused).
  document.addEventListener('htmx:sseError', function (e) {
    var source = e.detail && e.detail.source;
    if (source && source.readyState === EventSource.CLOSED) failed(source.url);
  });

  window.htmxoStreamProbe = { failed: failed };
})();
"""))

"""
    redirect_history_script()

Client runtime that records a redirect's destination in the browser history.
htmx's request follows a redirect ([`redirect_to`](@ref)) and swaps the new
route's fragment, but a link whose `hx-push-url` (or `hx-replace-url`) names
the requested URL itself — as [`nav_sidebar`](@ref)'s links do — would still
record the old URL. When htmx is about to record such a URL for a request
that was redirected within the same origin, this runtime records the
destination instead: its path, and its query unless the recorded URL carried
a query of its own different from the request's. Any other history entry —
`hx-push-url="true"` and boosted links, which htmx already points at the
destination, or a URL naming another page — is left as it is.

Safe to include more than once. Auto-included by [`htmx`](@ref); include it
yourself only on pages built without the shell.
"""
redirect_history_script() = h.script(Raw(raw"""
(function () {
  'use strict';
  if (window.htmxoRedirectHistory) return;
  window.htmxoRedirectHistory = true;
  function parse(href) {
    try { return new URL(href, document.baseURI); } catch (_) { return null; }
  }
  document.addEventListener('htmx:beforeHistoryUpdate', function (e) {
    var d = e.detail || {};
    var info = d.pathInfo, history = d.history, xhr = d.xhr;
    if (!info || !history || !history.path || !xhr || !xhr.responseURL) return;
    var requested = parse(info.finalRequestPath);
    var landed = parse(xhr.responseURL);
    var recorded = parse(history.path);
    if (!requested || !landed || !recorded) return;
    // Only a same-origin redirect, and only an entry naming the requested page.
    if (landed.origin !== requested.origin || recorded.origin !== requested.origin) return;
    if (landed.pathname + landed.search === requested.pathname + requested.search) return;
    if (recorded.pathname !== requested.pathname) return;
    var search = recorded.search === requested.search ? landed.search : recorded.search;
    history.path = landed.pathname + search + recorded.hash;
  });
})();
"""))

"""
    modifier_click_script()

Client runtime that gives Ctrl-, Cmd- and Shift-clicks on htmx links back to
the browser. htmx takes over every click on an `<a href>` that carries
`hx-get` (or another request attribute), modifier clicks included, and swaps
in place; its own exemption covers only Ctrl/Cmd-clicks on boosted links. With
this runtime, a Ctrl/Cmd- or Shift-click on such a link — as
[`nav_sidebar`](@ref), [`htmxo_breadcrumb`](@ref) and [`hx_link`](@ref)
render — opens its `href` in a new tab or window and leaves the page as it
