"""
    auto_terminal_script()

Unwrap a resolved `:auto` poll to the bare route fragment.

A resolved `:auto` poll answers a select-matching
`.treebar-poller-inner.treebar-terminal-content` node carrying
`data-htmxo-auto-terminal` (the response keeps that shape so every deployed
poller `hx-select` generation still matches it). The node is a `div`, or the
table element that can parent a table-part result: a `tbody` around rows, a
`tr` around cells, a `table` around a `tbody`/`thead`/`tfoot`/`caption`/
`colgroup`, a `colgroup` around columns — inside a `div` an HTML parser drops
those tags and keeps their text. On `htmx:afterSwap` this
script replaces the live `.treebar-poller` / `.treebar-terminal` wrapper with
the marked node's bare content, so the caller's target ends with exactly what
the route rendered — a wrapper cannot be a direct child of a structural
element (`details`/`summary`, `table`/`tr`, `select`/`option`, `dl`, `ul`/`li`).

Only marked nodes unwrap: hand-shaped `polling_fetchindex` terminals (frozen
tree kept for inspection), `:auto` terminals under `keep_terminal_tree=true`
(the same kept-tree shape, by design), and the direct-page OOB terminal
carry no marker and are untouched, and terminals diverted into
`.htmxo-live-reporter` stay owned by `live_refresh_script()`. The unwrap
moves the marked node's live children out of the wrapper — no second parse,
cleanup, or swap cycle (scripts ran once, at insert) — then runs htmx's
own settle step on the moved nodes explicitly (`process` + autofocus +
`htmx:load`), since htmx initializes swapped content after `afterSwap`
through tasks that would otherwise only see the detached husk. The mirror
also dispatches a faithful `htmx:afterSettle` per moved node (same swap
detail htmx would have carried): htmx's own settle event fires on the
inserted top-level nodes — the wrapper just removed — so without the
mirror it lands on the detached husk and never reaches document-level
listeners, and every body-level settle consumer (compose rebind,
show-when, live-region resync, …) silently misses `:auto` resolutions
(snag compose-afterswa-9ae50ac9). The moved bare content carries no
marker, so the scan cannot re-trigger, and a nested marked terminal unwraps
in the same pass. Runs in either listener order
against Treebars' `terminalizePoller` (the wrapper may already be renamed
to `.treebar-terminal` when this fires).

It also applies a finalized answer (`hx_response`, a `dispatch`ed
sub-request) whose headers move it away from the poller: `HX-Retarget`,
`HX-Reswap`, `HX-Reselect`, or `HX-Location`. Such a terminal arrives with
htmx's stop-polling status 286, and the script handles it as the original
request would have handled a direct answer. It records what the operation's
poller displaced when the poller arrived. When the terminal arrives, it puts
that content back before htmx resolves the headers. The bare answer then lands
where the headers say, or in the original target with the original swap, and
the spent poller is removed. An `HX-Retarget` that matches nothing raises
htmx's `htmx:targetError` and leaves the page as it was before the request.

It also ends a poll whose poller is gone. htmx keeps an `every` trigger on its
element, and a morph swap (`hx-swap="morph:*"`, including the live refresh's
terminal) can keep a running poller's element while turning it into other
content. htmx then still polls from that element, but never re-initializes
it unless it carries an `hx-*` attribute. A poll whose element no longer names
that operation in its `hx-get` is cancelled, and the element is re-processed,
so htmx drops the stale trigger; an answer already in flight is not swapped
into it. The page keeps what the morph rendered, as it would after a plain
swap that replaced the poller.

It also keeps one poll per operation at a time, and none after the operation's
286. htmx loses a poll element's request lock when it processes an ancestor
while the element's request runs (the element then carries `htmx-request`),
and sends a tick queued during a slow poll once that poll ends, if the element
is still in the page. A poll of an operation whose 286 has arrived is cancelled
and its element stops polling; a poll of an operation that is still waiting for
another of its polls is cancelled, and the next tick polls again.

Automatic — no consumer wiring. Include once per page; the `htmx()` shell
installs it alongside the Treebars assets.
