# The live-refresh interception and the `:auto` terminal unwrap ride alongside
# the Treebars poll assets, but ONLY when they are actually present: without
# the Treebars extension there are no pollers to intercept or unwrap (the core
# fallback answers bare already), and the shell must stay byte-for-byte
# poller-free (the `treebars_assets=false` opt-out and every ext-absent shell
# are unchanged, so `htmx page shells auto-install Treebars assets` still
# holds). Names `_runtime_blocks` entries, so the shell inlines or links them.
function _operation_page_blocks(treebars_assets::Bool)
    treebars_assets || return ()
    isempty(_polling_page_assets()) ? () :
        (:treebars_styles, :treebars_script, :live_refresh, :auto_terminal,
         :mutation_poll, :operation_stream, :sent_ahead)
end

"""
    mutation_poll_script()

Declare, on every HTMX mutation request (`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE`), where
its response will land: the `HTMXO-Swap` request header carries the effective
swap style — `htmx.ajax`'s `swap` option, else the nearest `hx-swap`, else
`htmx.config.defaultSwapStyle`. Under the default `:auto` policy a slow
mutation is answered by a poller only when that header names a swap that can
host one: a poller swapped nowhere (`hx-swap="none"`) would never poll, so the
submission's real answer — its fragment, its `HX-Trigger`, the
`hx-on::after-request` refresh it drives — would be lost. Without the header (a
hand-built page, curl) a mutation is answered directly, as before.

Automatic — no consumer wiring. The `htmx()` shell installs it alongside the
Treebars assets; include it once per page when building a `<head>` by hand.
"""
mutation_poll_script() = h.script(Raw(raw"""
(function() {
  if (window.__htmxoMutationPolls) return;
  window.__htmxoMutationPolls = true;
  document.addEventListener('htmx:beforeRequest', function(e) {
    var d = e.detail, config = d.requestConfig || {}, verb = config.verb;
    if (!verb || verb === 'get' || !d.xhr) return;
    var value = d.etc && d.etc.swapOverride;
    if (!value) {
      var elt = config.elt, source = elt && elt.closest &&
        elt.closest('[hx-swap],[data-hx-swap]');
      value = source && (source.getAttribute('hx-swap') ||
                         source.getAttribute('data-hx-swap'));
    }
    value = value || (window.htmx && htmx.config.defaultSwapStyle) || 'innerHTML';
    d.xhr.setRequestHeader('HTMXO-Swap', String(value).trim().split(/\s+/)[0]);
  });
})();
"""))

"""
    operation_stream_script()

Follow a page's running `:auto` pollers on one event stream, so each poller's
result arrives when its operation ends instead of at its next poll (see
[`serve`](@ref)'s `operation_stream`). The script gathers the operations of
the pollers marked `data-htmxo-operation-stream` (at most 32) and holds one
`EventSource` on the page's own address with `__htmxo_operations=<token>,…`.
When the page's Treebars script provides `treebarUpdatePoller` and
`treebarPollerLost`, the stream is opened with `__htmxo_push=1` and carries
answers, and its pollers send no polls: from the moment the stream is opened
a poller waits for its first answer (the server sends one a second later),
`progress` and `result` frames are applied with `treebarUpdatePoller`, and
the poller's timer polls are held in `htmx:confirm`, so the page's other
listeners never see them as requests. If no answer has arrived three seconds
after the stream opened (a proxy holding the stream back), the poller polls
once a second until one does. A poller goes back to its own polls with
`treebarPollerLost` when the stream drops. A poller inside a live-refresh
reporter keeps polling and fetches its result.
When the stream reports an operation `ready`, the script sends that poller's
poll at once (`htmx.ajax` with the polling element as its source, the request
its own timer would send), so the answer is swapped, ended and recorded as any
poll's is. While a stream that pushes nothing covers a poller, that poller's
timer polls go out at most once a second, for its progress tree; an
operation the stream reports `gone` polls as before. Parts an operation sends ahead ([`send_ahead`](@ref)) arrive as
`ahead` events, which [`sent_ahead_script`](@ref) lands in the poller's
region. A poller that appears later reopens the stream with the
new set. A dropped stream leaves the pollers polling at their own pace while the
browser reconnects; a refused one is reported to `htmxoStreamProbe` and
reopened with the backoff of [`live_region`](@ref)'s discover streams. The
stream closes itself once every operation it covers has ended.

Automatic — no consumer wiring. The `htmx()` shell installs it alongside the
Treebars assets; include it once per page when building a `<head>` by hand.
"""
operation_stream_script() = h.script(Raw(raw"""
(function() {
  if (window.__htmxoOperationStream) return;
  window.__htmxoOperationStream = true;
  // A streamed poller nobody pushes to polls at most this often, for its
  // progress tree; its result is announced on the stream.
  var PROGRESS_MS = 1000;
  // How long a pushing stream's poller waits for its first answer before it
  // polls by itself. The server sends one a second after the stream opens; a
  // proxy that holds the stream back sends none.
  var FIRST_PUSH_MS = 3000;
  // Operations one stream covers (64-character tokens: about 2 KB of address).
  var LIMIT = 32;
  var state = { source: null, tokens: {}, push: false, retries: 0, retry: null, queued: false };
  var streamed = {};   // token -> the open stream covers it
  var pushed = {};     // token -> its poller shows pushed answers
  var awaited = {};    // token -> since when a pushing stream owes it a first answer
  var ended = {};      // token -> the stream sent its result, ready or gone
